const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"Warkworth. Before Northumberland's castle.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Rumour, painted full of tongues.","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"RUMOUR","lines":["Open your ears; for which of you will stop","The vent of hearing when loud Rumour speaks?","I, from the orient to the drooping west,","Making the wind my post-horse, still unfold","The acts commenced on this ball of earth:","Upon my tongues continual slanders ride,","The which in every language I pronounce,","Stuffing the ears of men with false reports.","I speak of peace, while covert enmity","Under the smile of safety wounds the world:","And who but Rumour, who but only I,","Make fearful musters and prepared defence,","Whiles the big year, swoln with some other grief,","Is thought with child by the stern tyrant war,","And no such matter? Rumour is a pipe","Blown by surmises, jealousies, conjectures,","And of so easy and so plain a stop","That the blunt monster with uncounted heads,","The still-discordant wavering multitude,","Can play upon it. But what need I thus","My well-known body to anatomize","Among my household? Why is Rumour here?","I run before King Harry's victory;","Who in a bloody field by Shrewsbury","Hath beaten down young Hotspur and his troops,","Quenching the flame of bold rebellion","Even with the rebels' blood. But what mean I","To speak so true at first? my office is","To noise abroad that Harry Monmouth fell","Under the wrath of noble Hotspur's sword,","And that the king before the Douglas' rage","Stoop'd his anointed head as low as death.","This have I rumour'd through the peasant towns","Between that royal field of Shrewsbury","And this worm-eaten hold of ragged stone,","Where Hotspur's father, old Northumberland,","Lies crafty-sick: the posts come tiring on,","And not a man of them brings other news","Than they have learn'd of me: from Rumour's tongues","They bring smooth comforts false, worse than true wrongs."],"id":2,"m":"Listen closely, for which of you would block your ears when loud Rumour is speaking? Riding the wind like a post-horse, I carry news from east to west of everything that starts on this earth. Slanders ride constantly on my tongues; I speak them in every language and stuff men's ears with false reports. I talk of peace while hidden hatred, smiling behind a mask of safety, wounds the world. And who but Rumour, who but I alone, causes frightened gathering of troops and defences, while the swollen year, pregnant with some other trouble, is thought to be carrying a child fathered by that tyrant, war, when it is nothing of the kind? Rumour is a pipe blown by guesses, suspicions and assumptions, and its finger-holes are so simple that even the dull many-headed monster, the ever-quarrelling, wavering crowd, can play on it. But why should I dissect my famous body for you, my own household? Why is Rumour here? I run ahead of King Henry's victory: at the bloody battle of Shrewsbury he has beaten young Hotspur and his troops and put out the fire of rebellion with the rebels' own blood. But why am I telling the truth at the start? My job is to spread the story that Prince Harry fell to noble Hotspur's sword, and that the King bowed his anointed head as low as death before the furious Douglas. I have spread this rumour through the country towns between that royal battlefield at Shrewsbury and this worm-eaten fortress of rough stone, where Hotspur's father, old Northumberland, is pretending to be ill. The messengers come riding in exhausted, and not one of them brings any news but what they learned from me. From Rumour's tongues they bring false comfort, which is worse than true bad news."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":3},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lord Bardolph.","id":4},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","lines":["Who keeps the gate here, ho?"],"id":5,"m":"Who's keeping the gate here? Hello!"},{"t":"sd","text":"The Porter opens the gate.","id":6},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","cont":1,"lines":["Where is the earl?"],"id":7,"m":"Where is the Earl?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTER","lines":["What shall I say you are?"],"id":8,"m":"Who shall I say you are?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","lines":["Tell thou the earl","That the Lord Bardolph doth attend him here."],"id":9,"m":"Tell the Earl that Lord Bardolph is waiting for him here."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTER","lines":["His lordship is walk'd forth into the orchard:","Please it your honour, knock but at the gate,","And he himself will answer."],"id":10,"m":"His lordship has gone for a walk in the orchard. If your honour will just knock at the gate, he'll answer it himself."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Northumberland.","id":11},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","lines":["Here comes the earl."],"id":12,"m":"Here comes the Earl."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Porter.","id":13},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["What news, Lord Bardolph? every minute now","Should be the father of some stratagem:","The times are wild; contention, like a horse","Full of high feeding, madly hath broke loose","And bears down all before him."],"id":14,"m":"What news, Lord Bardolph? Every minute now should give birth to some new scheme. These are wild times: conflict, like an overfed horse, has broken loose and is trampling everything in its way."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","lines":["Noble earl,","I bring you certain news from Shrewsbury."],"id":15,"m":"Noble Earl, I bring you sure news from Shrewsbury."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Good, an God will!"],"id":16,"m":"Good news, God willing!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","lines":["As good as heart can wish:","The king is almost wounded to the death;","And, in the fortune of my lord your son,","Prince Harry slain outright; and both the Blunts","Kill'd by the hand of Douglas; young Prince John,","And Westmoreland and Stafford fled the field:","And Harry Monmouth's brawn, the hulk Sir John,","Is prisoner to your son: O, such a day,","So fought, so follow'd and so fairly won,","Came not till now to dignify the times,","Since Caesar's fortunes!"],"id":17,"m":"As good as anyone could wish. The King is wounded almost to death. Thanks to your son's success, Prince Harry has been killed outright, and both the Blunts killed by Douglas. Young Prince John, Westmoreland and Stafford ran from the field, and Prince Harry's lump of meat, that hulk Sir John, is your son's prisoner. Oh, such a day, so well fought, so well followed up and so honourably won, has not graced the times since Caesar's victories!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["How is this derived?","Saw you the field? came you from Shrewsbury?"],"id":18,"m":"Where did you get this? Did you see the battle? Have you come from Shrewsbury?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","lines":["I spake with one, my lord, that came from thence,","A gentleman well bred and of good name,","That freely render'd me these news for true."],"id":19,"m":"I talked to a man who came from there, my lord, a well-bred gentleman of good reputation, who freely told me this news as true."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Here comes my servant Travers, whom I sent","On Tuesday last to listen after news."],"id":20,"m":"Here comes my servant Travers, whom I sent out last Tuesday to listen for news."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Travers.","id":21},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","lines":["My lord, I over-rode him on the way;","And he is furnish'd with no certainties","More than he haply may retail from me."],"id":22,"m":"My lord, I overtook him on the road, and he has no sure facts beyond what he may have picked up from me."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Now, Travers, what good tidings comes with you?"],"id":23,"m":"Well, Travers, what good news do you bring?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRAVERS","lines":["My lord, Sir John Umfrevile turn'd me back","With joyful tidings; and, being better horsed,","Out-rode me. After him came spurring hard","A gentleman, almost forspent with speed,","That stopp'd by me to breathe his bloodied horse.","He ask'd the way to Chester; and of him","I did demand what news from Shrewsbury:","He told me that rebellion had bad luck","And that young Harry Percy's spur was cold.","With that, he gave his able horse the head,","And bending forward struck his armed heels","Against the panting sides of his poor jade","Up to the rowel-head, and starting so","He seem'd in running to devour the way,","Staying no longer question."],"id":24,"m":"My lord, Sir John Umfrevile turned me back with happy news and, having a better horse, rode on ahead of me. After him came a gentleman spurring hard, almost worn out with speed, who stopped beside me to rest his bleeding horse. He asked the way to Chester, and I asked him what news there was from Shrewsbury. He told me the rebellion had had bad luck and that young Harry Percy's spur was cold. With that he gave his horse its head, leaned forward and drove his armoured heels into the panting sides of his poor nag right up to the spur-wheel, and set off so fast he seemed to swallow the road, without waiting for more questions."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Ha! Again:","Said he young Harry Percy's spur was cold?","Of Hotspur Coldspur? that rebellion","Had met ill luck?"],"id":25,"m":"What? Say that again: did he say young Harry Percy's spur was cold? That Hotspur was Coldspur? That the rebellion had bad luck?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","lines":["My lord, I'll tell you what;","If my young lord your son have not the day,","Upon mine honour, for a silken point","I'll give my barony: never talk of it."],"id":26,"m":"My lord, I'll tell you this: if your son hasn't won the day, I'll give my whole barony for a silk lace. Don't even talk about it."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Why should that gentleman that rode by Travers","Give then such instances of loss?"],"id":27,"m":"Then why should the gentleman who rode past Travers give such details of a defeat?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","lines":["Who, he?","He was some hilding fellow that had stolen","The horse he rode on, and, upon my life,","Spoke at a venture. Look, here comes more news."],"id":28,"m":"Who, him? He was some worthless fellow who'd stolen the horse he was riding and, I swear, said whatever came into his head. Look, here comes more news."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Morton.","id":29},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Yea, this man's brow, like to a title-leaf,","Foretells the nature of a tragic volume:","So looks the strand whereon the imperious flood","Hath left a witness'd usurpation.","Say, Morton, didst thou come from Shrewsbury?"],"id":30,"m":"Yes, this man's face, like the title page of a book, announces that a tragedy follows. He looks like a beach where the overpowering tide has left the marks of its invasion. Tell me, Morton, have you come from Shrewsbury?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTON","lines":["I ran from Shrewsbury, my noble lord;","Where hateful death put on his ugliest mask","To fright our party."],"id":31,"m":"I ran from Shrewsbury, my noble lord, where hateful death put on its ugliest mask to frighten our side."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["How doth my son and brother?","Thou tremblest; and the whiteness in thy cheek","Is apter than thy tongue to tell thy errand.","Even such a man, so faint, so spiritless,","So dull, so dread in look, so woe-begone,","Drew Priam's curtain in the dead of night,","And would have told him half his Troy was burnt;","But Priam found the fire ere he his tongue,","And I my Percy's death ere thou report'st it.","This thou wouldst say: \"Your son did thus and thus;","Your brother thus: so fought the noble Douglas:\"","Stopping my greedy ear with their bold deeds:","But in the end, to stop my ear indeed,","Thou hast a sigh to blow away this praise,","Ending with \"Brother, son, and all are dead.\""],"id":32,"m":"How are my son and my brother? You're trembling, and the paleness of your cheek tells your message better than your tongue. A man just like you, as faint, as lifeless, as dull, as dreadful in his look and as miserable, opened King Priam's bed-curtains in the dead of night to tell him that half his Troy was burnt. But Priam saw the fire before the man could speak, and I see my Percy's death before you report it. This is what you'd say: \"Your son did this and this; your brother this; this is how the noble Douglas fought,\" filling my greedy ears with their brave deeds. But in the end, to stop my ears for good, you have a sigh to blow all this praise away, and you finish with \"Brother, son, and all are dead.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTON","lines":["Douglas is living, and your brother, yet:","But, for my lord your son,—"],"id":33,"m":"Douglas is alive, and so is your brother, so far. But as for my lord your son —"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Why, he is dead.","See what a ready tongue suspicion hath!","He that but fears the thing he would not know","Hath by instinct knowledge from others' eyes","That what he fear'd is chanced. Yet speak, Morton;","Tell thou an earl his divination lies,","And I will take it as a sweet disgrace","And make thee rich for doing me such wrong."],"id":34,"m":"Why, he's dead. See how quickly suspicion speaks! A man who only fears the thing he doesn't want to know can read in other people's eyes, by instinct, that what he feared has happened. But speak, Morton. Tell an earl that his guess is wrong, and I'll take it as a sweet insult and make you rich for doing me such a wrong."},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTON","lines":["You are too great to be by me gainsaid:","Your spirit is too true, your fears too certain."],"id":35,"m":"You are too great for me to contradict. Your instinct is too true, your fears too certain."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Yet, for all this, say not that Percy's dead.","I see a strange confession in thine eye;","Thou shakest thy head and hold'st it fear or sin","To speak a truth. If he be slain, say so;","The tongue offends not that reports his death:","And he doth sin that doth belie the dead,","Not he which says the dead is not alive","Yet the first bringer of unwelcome news","Hath but a losing office, and his tongue","Sounds ever after as a sullen bell,","Remember'd tolling a departing friend."],"id":36,"m":"Even so, don't say that Percy's dead. I see a strange confession in your eye: you shake your head as if it were frightening or sinful to speak the truth. If he's been killed, say so. The tongue that reports his death does no wrong. It's a sin to lie about the dead, not to say that the dead are no longer alive. And yet the first person to bring bad news has a thankless job, and his voice sounds for ever after like a gloomy bell, remembered for tolling a friend's death."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","lines":["I cannot think, my lord, your son is dead."],"id":37,"m":"I can't believe, my lord, that your son is dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTON","lines":["I am sorry I should force you to believe","That which I would to God I had not seen;","But these mine eyes saw him in bloody state,","Rendering faint quittance, wearied and outbreathed,","To Harry Monmouth; whose swift wrath beat down","The never-daunted Percy to the earth,","From whence with life he never more sprung up.","In few, his death, whose spirit lent a fire","Even to the dullest peasant in his camp,","Being bruited once, took fire and heat away","From the best-temper'd courage in his troops;","For from his metal was his party steel'd;","Which once in him abated, all the rest","Turn'd on themselves, like dull and heavy lead:","And as the thing that's heavy in itself,","Upon enforcement flies with greatest speed,","So did our men, heavy in Hotspur's loss,","Lend to this weight such lightness with their fear","That arrows fled not swifter toward their aim","Than did our soldiers, aiming at their safety,","Fly from the field. Then was that noble Worcester","Too soon ta'en prisoner; and that furious Scot,","The bloody Douglas, whose well-labouring sword","Had three times slain the appearance of the king,","'Gan vail his stomach and did grace the shame","Of those that turn'd their backs, and in his flight,","Stumbling in fear, was took. The sum of all","Is that the king hath won, and hath sent out","A speedy power to encounter you, my lord,","Under the conduct of young Lancaster","And Westmoreland. This is the news at full."],"id":38,"m":"I'm sorry I have to make you believe what I wish to God I had never seen. But these eyes of mine saw him covered in blood, fighting back feebly, exhausted and out of breath, against Prince Harry, whose swift fury beat the never-frightened Percy to the ground, and he never rose from it alive. In short, his death, whose spirit set fire even to the dullest peasant in his camp, once it was known, took the fire and heat from the bravest in his troops. His men took their steel from his metal, and once that was blunted all the rest turned in on themselves like dull, heavy lead. And just as something heavy flies fastest once it's thrown, so our men, made heavy by losing Hotspur, gave their weight such lightness with their fear that arrows never flew faster at their target than our soldiers, aiming at their own safety, fled from the field. Then that noble Worcester was taken prisoner too soon, and that furious Scot, the bloody Douglas, whose hard-working sword had three times killed men disguised as the King, began to lose heart and joined the shame of those who ran, and as he fled he stumbled in his fear and was captured. The sum of it is that the King has won and has quickly sent an army to meet you, my lord, under the command of young Lancaster and Westmoreland. That's the full news."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["For this I shall have time enough to mourn.","In poison there is physic; and these news,","Having been well, that would have made me sick,","Being sick, have in some measure made me well:","And as the wretch, whose fever-weaken'd joints,","Like strengthless hinges, buckle under life,","Impatient of his fit, breaks like a fire","Out of his keeper's arms, even so my limbs,","Weaken'd with grief, being now enraged with grief,","Are thrice themselves. Hence, therefore, thou nice crutch!","A scaly gauntlet now with joints of steel","Must glove this hand: and hence, thou sickly quoif!","Thou art a guard too wanton for the head","Which princes, flesh'd with conquest, aim to hit.","Now bind my brows with iron; and approach","The ragged'st hour that time and spite dare bring","To frown upon the enraged Northumberland!","Let heaven kiss earth! now let not Nature's hand","Keep the wild flood confined! let order die!","And let this world no longer be a stage","To feed contention in a lingering act;","But let one spirit of the first-born Cain","Reign in all bosoms, that, each heart being set","On bloody courses, the rude scene may end,","And darkness be the burier of the dead!"],"id":39,"m":"I'll have plenty of time to mourn this. There's medicine in poison, and this news, which would have made me sick if I'd been well, has, now that I'm sick, made me almost well. And just as a sick wretch, whose fever-weakened joints buckle like feeble hinges under the weight of life, can't bear his fit and breaks out of his nurse's arms like a fire, so my limbs, weakened by grief and now enraged by grief, are three times their strength. Away with you, then, you dainty crutch! A scaly steel gauntlet must cover this hand now. And away, you sickly nightcap! You're too soft a protection for a head that princes, flushed with victory, are aiming at. Now bind my forehead with iron, and let the roughest hour that time and malice dare bring come and frown at the enraged Northumberland! Let heaven and earth collide! Let Nature's hand no longer hold back the wild sea! Let order die! And let this world no longer be a stage where conflict drags on through a slow act; instead let the spirit of Cain, the first murderer, rule in every heart, so that, with every heart set on bloodshed, the brutal play may end and darkness bury the dead!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRAVERS","lines":["This strained passion doth you wrong, my lord."],"id":40,"m":"This violent passion is harming you, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","lines":["Sweet earl, divorce not wisdom from your honour."],"id":41,"m":"Dear Earl, don't divorce your wisdom from your honour."},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTON","lines":["The lives of all your loving complices","Lean on your health; the which, if you give o'er","To stormy passion, must perforce decay.","You cast the event of war, my noble lord,","And summ'd the account of chance, before you said","\"Let us make head.\" It was your presurmise,","That, in the dole of blows, your son might drop:","You knew he walk'd o'er perils, on an edge,","More likely to fall in than to get o'er;","You were advised his flesh was capable","Of wounds and scars and that his forward spirit","Would lift him where most trade of danger ranged:","Yet did you say \"Go forth;\" and none of this,","Though strongly apprehended, could restrain","The stiff-borne action: what hath then befallen,","Or what hath this bold enterprise brought forth,","More than that being which was like to be?"],"id":42,"m":"The lives of all your loyal allies depend on your health, and if you give yourself up to stormy passion it must fail. You weighed the outcome of the war, my noble lord, and counted up the chances, before you said, \"Let's raise an army.\" You foresaw that, in the sharing out of blows, your son might fall. You knew he was walking on a knife-edge over danger, more likely to fall in than to get across. You were told his body could be wounded and scarred and that his bold spirit would carry him wherever the danger was thickest. And yet you said \"Go,\" and none of this, though you understood it well, could hold back the stubborn enterprise. So what has happened, what has this bold venture produced, except what was likely to happen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","lines":["We all that are engaged to this loss","Knew that we ventured on such dangerous seas","That if we wrought out life 'twas ten to one;","And yet we ventured, for the gain proposed","Choked the respect of likely peril fear'd;","And since we are o'erset, venture again.","Come, we will put forth, body and goods."],"id":43,"m":"All of us involved in this loss knew we were sailing such dangerous seas that it was ten to one against coming out alive. And yet we took the risk, because the prize we hoped for smothered our fear of the danger. And since we've been overturned, let's take the risk again. Come, we'll stake everything, our lives and our goods."},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTON","lines":["'Tis more than time: and, my most noble lord,","I hear for certain, and dare speak the truth:","The gentle Archbishop of York is up","With well-appointed powers: he is a man","Who with a double surety binds his followers.","My lord your son had only but the corpse,","But shadows and the shows of men, to fight;","For that same word, rebellion, did divide","The action of their bodies from their souls;","And they did fight with queasiness, constrain'd,","As men drink potions, that their weapons only","Seem'd on our side; but, for their spirits and souls,","This word, rebellion, it had froze them up,","As fish are in a pond. But now the bishop","Turns insurrection to religion:","Supposed sincere and holy in his thoughts,","He's follow'd both with body and with mind;","And doth enlarge his rising with the blood","Of fair King Richard, scraped from Pomfret stones;","Derives from heaven his quarrel and his cause;","Tells them he doth bestride a bleeding land,","Gasping for life under great Bolingbroke;","And more and less do flock to follow him."],"id":44,"m":"It's high time. And, my most noble lord, I hear for certain, and I dare say it's true, that the noble Archbishop of York has risen with well-equipped forces. He is a man who binds his followers with a double bond. My lord your son had only the bodies, the mere shadows and shapes of men, to fight with, because that word \"rebellion\" divided their bodies' actions from their souls. They fought queasily and under pressure, like men taking medicine, so that only their weapons seemed to be on our side; their spirits and souls were frozen by that word \"rebellion\", like fish in a frozen pond. But now the Bishop turns rebellion into religion. Since people believe he is sincere and holy in his thoughts, they follow him in body and mind. He adds weight to his rising with the blood of good King Richard, scraped from the stones of Pomfret Castle; he claims his quarrel and cause come from heaven; he tells them he is standing over a bleeding country that is gasping for life under the tyrant Bolingbroke; and high and low flock to follow him."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["I knew of this before; but, to speak truth,","This present grief had wiped it from my mind.","Go in with me; and counsel every man","The aptest way for safety and revenge:","Get posts and letters, and make friends with speed:","Never so few, and never yet more need."],"id":45,"m":"I knew this before, but to tell the truth, this grief had wiped it from my mind. Come inside with me, and let everyone advise the best way to safety and revenge. Get messengers and letters, and make allies quickly. We've never been so few, and never needed more."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":46},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"London. A street.","id":47},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff, with his Page bearing his sword and buckler.","id":48},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Sirrah, you giant, what says the doctor to my water?"],"id":49,"m":"Now then, you giant, what does the doctor say about my urine?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["He said, sir, the water itself was a good healthy water; but,","for the party that owed it, he might have moe diseases than he","knew for."],"id":50,"m":"He said, sir, the urine itself was good healthy urine, but the person it belonged to might have more diseases than he knew about."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Men of all sorts take a pride to gird at me: the brain of","this foolish-compounded clay, man, is not able to invent any thing","that tends to laughter, more than I invent or is invented on me:","I am not only witty in myself, but the cause that wit is in other men.","I do here walk before thee like a sow that hath overwhelmed all her","litter but one.","If the prince put thee into my service for any other reason than to","set me off, why then I have no judgement. Thou whoreson mandrake, thou","art fitter to be worn in my cap than to wait at my heels. I was never","manned with an agate till now: but I will inset you neither in gold nor","silver, but in vile apparel, and send you back again to your master, for","a jewel,—the juvenal, the prince your master, whose chin is not yet","fledged. I will sooner have a beard grow in the palm of my hand than he","shall get one on his cheek; and yet he will not stick to say his face is","a face-royal: God may finish it when he will, 'tis not a hair amiss yet:","he may keep it still at a face-royal, for a barber shall never earn","sixpence out of it; and yet he'll be crowing as if he had writ man ever","since his father was a bachelor. He may keep his own grace, but he's","almost out of mine, I can assure him. What said Master Dombledon about","the satin for my short cloak and my slops?"],"id":51,"m":"People of every kind take pride in mocking me. The brain of this stupidly made lump of clay, mankind, can't come up with anything funny that isn't invented by me or about me. I'm not only witty myself, I'm the cause of wit in other men. Here I am walking in front of you like a sow that has crushed all her litter except one. If the Prince put you in my service for any reason except to show me off by contrast, then I have no judgment. You son-of-a-whore mandrake root, you'd be better worn as a badge in my cap than following at my heels. I've never had a dwarf for an attendant till now, but I won't set you in gold or silver; I'll dress you in rags and send you back to your master as a jewel — that juvenile, the Prince your master, whose chin hasn't sprouted any feathers yet. I'll grow a beard on the palm of my hand before he gets one on his cheek. And yet he'll happily say his face is a royal face. God can finish it whenever He likes; there isn't a hair out of place yet. He can keep it a royal face, because a barber will never earn sixpence from it. And yet he crows as if he'd been a grown man since his father was a bachelor. He can keep his own grace, but he's almost out of mine, I can tell him. What did Master Dombledon say about the satin for my short cloak and my breeches?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["He said, sir, you should procure him better assurance than Bardolph:","he would not take his band and yours; he liked not the security."],"id":52,"m":"He said, sir, you should get him a better guarantee than Bardolph. He wouldn't accept his bond together with yours; he didn't like the security."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Let him be damned, like the glutton! pray God his tongue be hotter!","A whoreson Achitophel! a rascally yea-forsooth knave! to bear a","gentleman in hand, and then stand upon security! The whoreson","smooth-pates do now wear nothing but high shoes, and bunches of keys","at their girdles; and if a man is through with them in honest taking","up, then they must stand upon security. I had as lief they would","put ratsbane in my mouth as offer to stop it with security.","I looked 'a should have sent me two and twenty yards of satin, as I","am a true knight, and he sends me security. Well, he may sleep in","security; for he hath the horn of abundance, and the lightness of","his wife shines through it: and yet cannot he see, though he have his","own lanthorn to light him. Where's Bardolph?"],"id":53,"m":"Let him be damned like the rich glutton in the Bible! I hope his tongue burns hotter! A son-of-a-whore traitor! A rascally yes-sir knave, to lead a gentleman on and then insist on security! These smooth-headed tradesmen now wear nothing but high shoes and bunches of keys at their belts, and when a man wants to buy honestly on credit, they insist on security. I'd sooner they put rat poison in my mouth than shut it with \"security\". I expected him to send me twenty-two yards of satin, as I'm a true knight, and he sends me \"security\". Well, he can sleep in security, because he has the horn of plenty, and his wife's loose behaviour shines through it; and yet he can't see, though he has his own lantern to light him. Where's Bardolph?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["He's gone into Smithfield to buy your worship a horse."],"id":54,"m":"He's gone to Smithfield to buy your worship a horse."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I bought him in Paul's, and he'll buy me a horse in Smithfield:","an I could get me but a wife in the stews, I were manned, horsed,","and wived."],"id":55,"m":"I bought him in St Paul's, and he'll buy me a horse in Smithfield. If I could just get myself a wife in the brothels, I'd be served, horsed and wived."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Lord Chief-Justice and Servant.","id":56},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Sir, here comes the nobleman that committed the Prince for","striking him about Bardolph."],"id":57,"m":"Sir, here comes the nobleman who sent the Prince to prison for hitting him over Bardolph."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Wait close; I will not see him."],"id":58,"m":"Stay close. I won't see him."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["What's he that goes there?"],"id":59,"m":"Who's that going there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Falstaff, an 't please your lordship."],"id":60,"m":"Falstaff, if it please your lordship."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["He that was in question for the robbery?"],"id":61,"m":"The one who was under investigation for the robbery?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["He, my lord; but he hath since done good service at","Shrewsbury; and, as I hear, is now going with some charge to the","Lord John of Lancaster."],"id":62,"m":"That's him, my lord. But he has since done good service at Shrewsbury and, I hear, is now going with some command to Lord John of Lancaster."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["What, to York? Call him back again."],"id":63,"m":"What, to York? Call him back."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Sir John Falstaff!"],"id":64,"m":"Sir John Falstaff!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Boy, tell him I am deaf."],"id":65,"m":"Boy, tell him I'm deaf."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["You must speak louder; my master is deaf."],"id":66,"m":"You'll have to speak louder; my master is deaf."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["I am sure he is, to the hearing of anything good.","Go, pluck him by the elbow; I must speak with him."],"id":67,"m":"I'm sure he is, to hearing anything good. Go and pull him by the elbow; I must speak to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Sir John!"],"id":68,"m":"Sir John!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What! a young knave, and begging! Is there not wars? is","there not employment? doth not the king lack subjects? do not the","rebels need soldiers? Though it be a shame to be on any side but","one, it is worse shame to beg than to be on the worst side, were","it worse than the name of rebellion can tell how to make it."],"id":69,"m":"What! A young rascal, and begging! Isn't there a war on? Isn't there work? Doesn't the King need subjects? Don't the rebels need soldiers? Though it's a shame to be on any side but one, it's a worse shame to beg than to be on the worst side, even if it were worse than the word \"rebellion\" can make it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["You mistake me, sir."],"id":70,"m":"You've mistaken me, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Why, sir, did I say you were an honest man? setting my knighthood","and my soldiership aside, I had lied in my throat, if I had said so."],"id":71,"m":"Why, sir, did I say you were an honest man? Setting my knighthood and soldiering aside, I'd have lied through my teeth if I'd said so."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["I pray you, sir, then set your knighthood and your soldiership aside;","and give me leave to tell you, you lie in your throat, if you say I","am any other than an honest man."],"id":72,"m":"Then I beg you, sir, set your knighthood and soldiering aside, and allow me to tell you that you lie through your teeth if you say I'm anything other than an honest man."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I give thee leave to tell me so! I lay aside that which grows to me!","If thou gettest any leave of me, hang me; if thou takest leave,","thou wert better be hanged. You hunt counter: hence! avaunt!"],"id":73,"m":"I allow you to tell me so! I set aside something that's part of me! If you get any \"allowing\" from me, hang me; if you take that liberty, you'd be better off hanged. You're on the wrong scent: away! Get lost!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Sir, my lord would speak with you."],"id":74,"m":"Sir, my lord wants to speak with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Sir John Falstaff, a word with you."],"id":75,"m":"Sir John Falstaff, a word with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["My good lord! God give your lordship good time of day. I am glad to","see your lordship abroad: I heard say your lordship was sick:","I hope your lordship goes abroad by advice. Your lordship, though","not clean past your youth, hath yet some smack of age in you, some","relish of the saltness of time; and I most humbly beseech your lordship","to have a reverend care of your health."],"id":76,"m":"My good lord! God give your lordship good day. I'm glad to see your lordship out and about. I heard your lordship was ill; I hope your lordship goes out on doctor's advice. Your lordship, though not quite past your youth, still has some taste of age in you, some flavour of the saltiness of time, and I most humbly beg your lordship to take respectful care of your health."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Sir John, I sent for you before your expedition to Shrewsbury."],"id":77,"m":"Sir John, I sent for you before your expedition to Shrewsbury."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["An 't please your lordship, I hear his majesty is returned","with some discomfort from Wales."],"id":78,"m":"If it please your lordship, I hear his Majesty has come back from Wales somewhat unwell."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["I talk not of his majesty: you would not come when I","sent for you."],"id":79,"m":"I'm not talking about his Majesty. You wouldn't come when I sent for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["And I hear, moreover, his highness is fall'n into this same","whoreson apoplexy."],"id":80,"m":"And I hear, too, that his Highness has fallen into this damned apoplexy."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Well God mend him! I pray you, let me speak with you."],"id":81,"m":"Well, God heal him! Please, let me speak to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["This apoplexy is, as I take it, a kind of lethargy, an 't please","your lordship; a kind of sleeping in the blood, a whoreson tingling."],"id":82,"m":"This apoplexy, as I understand it, is a kind of lethargy, if it please your lordship, a kind of sleeping in the blood, a damned tingling."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["What tell you me of it? be it as it is."],"id":83,"m":"Why are you telling me about it? Let it be what it is."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["It hath it original from much grief, from study and perturbation","of the brain: I have read the cause of his effects in Galen:","it is a kind of deafness."],"id":84,"m":"It has its origin in much grief, in study and disturbance of the brain. I have read about its causes in Galen. It's a kind of deafness."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["I think you are fallen into the disease, for you hear not","what I say to you."],"id":85,"m":"I think you've caught the disease, because you don't hear what I'm saying to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Very well, my lord, very well: rather, an 't please you, it","is the disease of not listening, the malady of not marking, that","I am troubled withal."],"id":86,"m":"Very true, my lord, very true. Or rather, if it please you, it's the disease of not listening, the illness of not paying attention, that I suffer from."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["To punish you by the heels would amend the attention","of your ears; and I care not if I do become your physician."],"id":87,"m":"Putting you in the stocks would cure the inattention of your ears, and I wouldn't mind being your doctor."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I am as poor as Job, my lord, but not so patient: your lordship","may minister the potion of imprisonment to me in respect of poverty;","but how I should be your patient to follow your prescriptions,","the wise may make some dram of a scruple, or indeed a scruple itself."],"id":88,"m":"I'm as poor as Job, my lord, but not as patient. Your lordship may give me the medicine of prison for my poverty, but as for whether I'd be patient enough to follow your prescriptions, a wise man might have a scruple or two of doubt — or a whole scruple."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["I sent for you, when there were matters against you","for your life, to come speak with me."],"id":89,"m":"I sent for you to come and speak to me when there were charges against you that could have cost you your life."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["As I was then advised by my learned counsel in the laws","of this land-service, I did not come."],"id":90,"m":"I was advised by my learned lawyer in the laws of military service at the time not to come."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Well, the truth is, Sir John, you live in great infamy."],"id":91,"m":"Well, the truth is, Sir John, you live in great disgrace."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["He that buckles himself in my belt cannot live in less."],"id":92,"m":"Anyone who wears a belt my size can't live in less."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Your means are very slender, and your waste is great."],"id":93,"m":"Your income is very small, and your waste is great."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I would it were otherwise; I would my means were greater,","and my waist slenderer."],"id":94,"m":"I wish it were the other way round: I wish my income were greater and my waist smaller."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["You have misled the youthful prince."],"id":95,"m":"You have led the young Prince astray."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["The young prince hath misled me: I am the fellow with the","great belly, and he my dog."],"id":96,"m":"The young Prince has led me astray. I'm the blind man with the big belly, and he's my guide dog."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Well, I am loath to gall a new-healed wound: your day's service","at Shrewsbury hath a little gilded over your night's exploit","on Gad's-hill: you may thank the unquiet time for your quiet","o'er-posting that action."],"id":97,"m":"Well, I don't want to rub a newly healed wound. Your day's service at Shrewsbury has slightly gilded over your night's work at Gad's Hill. You can thank these troubled times that your action passed over quietly."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["My lord?"],"id":98,"m":"My lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["But since all is well, keep it so: wake not a sleeping wolf."],"id":99,"m":"But since all is well, keep it that way. Don't wake a sleeping wolf."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["To wake a wolf is as bad as smell a fox."],"id":100,"m":"Waking a wolf is as bad as smelling a fox."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["What! you are as a candle, the better part burnt out."],"id":101,"m":"What! You're like a candle with the better part burnt away."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["A wassail candle, my lord, all tallow: if I did say of wax, my","growth would approve the truth."],"id":102,"m":"A cheap party candle, my lord, all tallow. If I said wax, my size would prove it true."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["There is not a white hair in your face but should have his","effect of gravity."],"id":103,"m":"There isn't a white hair on your face that shouldn't have the effect of making you grave."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["His effect of gravy, gravy, gravy."],"id":104,"m":"The effect of gravy, gravy, gravy."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["You follow the young prince up and down, like his ill angel."],"id":105,"m":"You follow the young Prince around like his bad angel."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Not so, my lord; your ill angel is light; but I hope he that looks","upon me will take me without weighing: and yet, in some respects,","I grant, I cannot go: I cannot tell. Virtue is of so little regard","in these costermonger times that true valour is turned bear-herd;","pregnancy is made a tapster, and hath his quick wit wasted in giving","reckonings: all the other gifts appertinent to man, as the malice of","this age shapes them, are not worth a gooseberry. You that are old","consider not the capacities of us that are young; you do measure the","heat of our livers with the bitterness of your galls: and we that","are in the vaward of our youth, I must confess, are wags too."],"id":106,"m":"Not so, my lord. A bad angel is a light coin, but I hope anyone who looks at me will take me without weighing me. And yet in some ways, I admit, I won't pass as currency; I can't say. Virtue is so little valued in these shopkeeping times that true courage has become a bear-keeper; quick wits have become a barman, wasted on adding up bills. All the other gifts that belong to a man, as this spiteful age shapes them, aren't worth a gooseberry. You old men don't consider what we young men are capable of. You measure the heat of our passions by the bitterness of your bile, and we who are in the front line of our youth, I must admit, are rascals too."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Do you set down your name in the scroll of youth, that are written","down old with all the characters of age? Have you not a moist eye?","a dry hand? a yellow cheek? a white beard? a decreasing leg? an","increasing belly? is not your voice broken? your wind short? your","chin double? your wit single? and every part about you blasted","with antiquity? and will you yet call yourself young? Fie, fie,","fie, Sir John!"],"id":107,"m":"Do you put your name on the list of the young, when you're marked down as old with every sign of age? Don't you have a watery eye? A dry hand? A yellow cheek? A white beard? A shrinking leg? A growing belly? Isn't your voice broken, your breath short, your chin double, your wit single, and every part of you withered with old age? And you still call yourself young? Shame, shame, shame, Sir John!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["My lord, I was born about three of the clock in the afternoon,","with a white head and something a round belly. For my voice, I","have lost it with halloing and singing of anthems. To approve my","youth further, I will not: the truth is, I am only old in judgement","and understanding; and he that will caper with me for a thousand","marks, let him lend me the money, and have at him!","For the box of the ear that the prince gave you, he gave it like a","rude prince, and you took it like a sensible lord. I have checked","him for it, and the young lion repents; marry, not in ashes and","sackcloth, but in new silk and old sack."],"id":108,"m":"My lord, I was born at about three in the afternoon with a white head and a roundish belly. As for my voice, I lost it shouting and singing hymns. I won't try to prove my youth any further. The truth is, I'm only old in judgement and understanding, and anyone who wants to bet me a thousand marks at dancing, let him lend me the money and I'll take him on! As for the box on the ear the Prince gave you, he gave it like a rough prince and you took it like a sensible lord. I've told him off for it, and the young lion is sorry — not in sackcloth and ashes, but in new silk and old sack."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Well, God send the prince a better companion!"],"id":109,"m":"Well, God send the Prince a better companion!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["God send the companion a better prince! I cannot rid my hands of him."],"id":110,"m":"God send the companion a better prince! I can't get rid of him."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Well, the king hath severed you and Prince Harry:","I hear you are going with Lord John of Lancaster against the","Archbishop and the Earl of Northumberland."],"id":111,"m":"Well, the King has separated you and Prince Harry. I hear you're going with Lord John of Lancaster against the Archbishop and the Earl of Northumberland."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Yea; I thank your pretty sweet wit for it. But look you pray, all","you that kiss my lady Peace at home, that our armies join not in a","hot day; for, by the Lord, I take but two shirts out with me, and I","mean not to sweat extraordinarily: if it be a hot day, and I brandish","any thing but a bottle, I would I might never spit white again.","There is not a dangerous action can peep out his head but I am thrust","upon it: well, I cannot last ever: but it was alway yet the trick of","our English nation, if they have a good thing, to make it too common.","If ye will needs say I am an old man, you should give me rest. I","would to God my name were not so terrible to the enemy as it is:","I were better to be eaten to death with a rust than to be scoured to","nothing with perpetual motion."],"id":112,"m":"Yes, and I have your pretty sweet wit to thank for it. But look, all of you who stay home kissing my lady Peace, pray that our armies don't meet on a hot day, because, by God, I'm only taking two shirts with me and I don't intend to sweat more than usual. If it's a hot day and I brandish anything but a bottle, may I never spit white again. No dangerous action can poke its head out without my being pushed into it. Well, I can't last for ever. But it's always been a habit of our English nation that when they have a good thing they overuse it. If you insist on saying I'm an old man, you should let me rest. I wish to God my name weren't so terrifying to the enemy. I'd rather rust to death than be scrubbed to nothing by constant motion."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Well, be honest, be honest; and God bless your expedition!"],"id":113,"m":"Well, be honest, be honest, and God bless your expedition!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Will your lordship lend me a thousand pound to furnish me forth?"],"id":114,"m":"Will your lordship lend me a thousand pounds to equip myself?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Not a penny, not a penny; you are too impatient to bear crosses.","Fare you well: commend me to my cousin Westmoreland."],"id":115,"m":"Not a penny, not a penny. You're too impatient to bear crosses — or coins stamped with them. Goodbye. Give my regards to my cousin Westmoreland."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Chief-Justice and Servant.","id":116},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["If I do, fillip me with a three-man beetle. A man can no more separate","age and covetousness than 'a can part young limbs and lechery: but","the gout galls the one, and the pox pinches the other; and so both the","degrees prevent my curses. Boy!"],"id":117,"m":"If I do, you can flick me with a sledgehammer that takes three men to lift. You can no more separate old age from greed than you can separate young bodies from lust. But gout torments the one and the pox pinches the other, so both conditions make my curses unnecessary. Boy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Sir?"],"id":118,"m":"Sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What money is in my purse?"],"id":119,"m":"How much money is in my purse?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Seven groats and two pence."],"id":120,"m":"Seven groats and two pence."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I can get no remedy against this consumption of the purse:","borrowing only lingers and lingers it out, but the disease is","incurable. Go bear this letter to my Lord of Lancaster; this to the","prince; this to the Earl of Westmoreland; and this to old Mistress","Ursula, whom I have weekly sworn to marry since I perceived the","first white hair of my chin. About it: you know where to find me."],"id":121,"m":"I can't find any cure for this wasting disease of the purse. Borrowing only drags it out; the illness is incurable. Go and take this letter to my Lord of Lancaster, this one to the Prince, this to the Earl of Westmoreland, and this to old Mistress Ursula, whom I've sworn every week to marry since I saw the first white hair on my chin. Off you go. You know where to find me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Page.","id":122},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","cont":1,"lines":["A pox of this gout! or, a gout of this pox! for the one or the other","plays the rogue with my great toe. 'Tis no matter if I do halt; I","have the wars for my colour, and my pension shall seem the more","reasonable. A good wit will make use of any thing: I will turn","diseases to commodity."],"id":123,"m":"A pox on this gout! Or a gout on this pox! One or the other is playing havoc with my big toe. Never mind if I limp: I can blame the war, and my pension will seem all the more justified. A good wit makes use of anything. I'll turn my diseases into profit."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":124},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"III","loc":"York. The Archbishop's palace.","id":125},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Archbishop, the Lords Hastings, Mowbray, Bardolph.","id":126},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["Thus have you heard our cause and known our means;","And, my most noble friends, I pray you all,","Speak plainly your opinions of our hopes:","And first, lord marshal, what say you to it?"],"id":127,"m":"So you've heard our cause and know our resources. And, my noble friends, I ask you all to speak plainly about our hopes. First, Lord Marshal, what do you say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["I well allow the occasion of our arms;","But gladly would be better satisfied","How in our means we should advance ourselves","To look with forehead bold and big enough","Upon the power and puissance of the king."],"id":128,"m":"I fully accept the reasons for taking up arms, but I'd like to be better convinced about how, with our resources, we can face the King's army and power boldly and strongly enough."},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["Our present musters grow upon the file","To five and twenty thousand men of choice;","And our supplies live largely in the hope","Of great Northumberland, whose bosom burns","With an incensed fire of injuries."],"id":129,"m":"Our current roll is growing towards twenty-five thousand picked men, and our reinforcements rest largely on the hope of great Northumberland, whose heart is burning with an angry fire over the wrongs done to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","lines":["The question then, Lord Hastings, standeth thus:","Whether our present five and twenty thousand","May hold up head without Northumberland?"],"id":130,"m":"Then the question, Lord Hastings, is this: can our present twenty-five thousand hold their own without Northumberland?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["With him, we may."],"id":131,"m":"With him, we can."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","lines":["Yea, marry, there's the point:","But if without him we be thought too feeble,","My judgement is, we should not step too far","Till we had his assistance by the hand;","For in a theme so bloody-faced as this","Conjecture, expectation, and surmise","Of aids incertain should not be admitted."],"id":132,"m":"Yes, indeed, that's the point. But if we're thought too weak without him, my judgment is that we shouldn't go too far until we have his help in hand. In a bloody business like this, guesses, expectations and hopes of uncertain help shouldn't be counted on."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["'Tis very true, Lord Bardolph; for indeed","It was young Hotspur's case at Shrewsbury."],"id":133,"m":"That's very true, Lord Bardolph, for that was exactly young Hotspur's situation at Shrewsbury."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","lines":["It was, my lord; who lined himself with hope,","Eating the air on promise of supply,","Flattering himself in project of a power","Much smaller than the smallest of his thoughts:","And so, with great imagination","Proper to madmen, led his powers to death","And winking leap'd into destruction."],"id":134,"m":"It was, my lord. He fed himself on hope, eating air on the promise of reinforcements, flattering himself with the prospect of an army that turned out smaller than the smallest of his expectations. And so, with the kind of wild imagination that belongs to madmen, he led his forces to their deaths and leapt into destruction with his eyes shut."},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["But, by your leave, it never yet did hurt","To lay down likelihoods and forms of hope."],"id":135,"m":"But, with respect, it has never done any harm to set out likely outcomes and grounds for hope."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","lines":["Yes, if this present quality of war,","Indeed the instant action: a cause on foot","Lives so in hope as in an early spring","We see the appearing buds; which to prove fruit,","Hope gives not so much warrant as despair","That frosts will bite them. When we mean to build,","We first survey the plot, then draw the model;","And when we see the figure of the house,","Then we must rate the cost of the erection;","Which if we find outweighs ability,","What do we then but draw anew the model","In fewer offices, or at least desist","To build at all? Much more, in this great work,","Which is almost to pluck a kingdom down","And set another up, should we survey","The plot of situation and the model,","Consent upon a sure foundation,","Question surveyors, know our own estate,","How able such a work to undergo,","To weigh against his opposite; or else","We fortify in paper and in figures,","Using the names of men instead of men;","Like one that draws the model of a house","Beyond his power to build it; who, half through,","Gives o'er and leaves his part-created cost","A naked subject to the weeping clouds","And waste for churlish winter's tyranny."],"id":136,"m":"Yes, it does, in this kind of war, in this immediate action. A cause that's under way lives on hope the way we see buds appear in early spring: hope gives less guarantee that they'll become fruit than despair gives that the frost will nip them. When we plan to build, we first survey the site, then draw the plan, and when we see the shape of the house, we have to estimate the cost of building it. If we find it's more than we can afford, what do we do but draw the plan again with fewer rooms, or give up building altogether? All the more, in this great work, which is almost to pull a kingdom down and set another up, we should survey the site and the plan, agree on a firm foundation, consult the surveyors, know our own resources and how able we are to take on such a work, and weigh them against the opposition. Otherwise we build our defences on paper and in numbers, using the names of men instead of men, like someone who draws the plan of a house beyond his means to build, who gives up halfway and leaves his half-built expense standing bare under the weeping clouds, a ruin for harsh winter to batter."},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["Grant that our hopes, yet likely of fair birth,","Should be still-born, and that we now possess'd","The utmost man of expectation,","I think we are a body strong enough,","Even as we are, to equal with the king."],"id":137,"m":"Suppose our hopes, which still look likely to be born healthy, turned out to be stillborn, and that we already had every man we could expect. I still think we're a force strong enough, just as we are, to match the King."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","lines":["What, is the king but five and twenty thousand?"],"id":138,"m":"What, does the King only have twenty-five thousand?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["To us no more; nay, not so much, Lord Bardolph.","For his divisions, as the times do brawl,","Are in three heads: one power against the French,","And one against Glendower; perforce a third","Must take up us: so is the unfirm king","In three divided; and his coffers sound","With hollow poverty and emptiness."],"id":139,"m":"Against us, no more; in fact not even that, Lord Bardolph. With the times so full of conflict, his forces are split three ways: one army against the French, one against Glendower, and a third must deal with us. So the unsteady King is divided in three, and his treasure chests ring hollow with poverty and emptiness."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["That he should draw his several strengths together","And come against us in full puissance,","Need not be dreaded."],"id":140,"m":"There's no need to fear that he'll gather his separate forces together and come against us at full strength."},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["If he should do so,","He leaves his back unarm'd, the French and Welsh","Baying him at the heels: never fear that."],"id":141,"m":"If he did, he'd leave his back unprotected, with the French and Welsh barking at his heels. Don't worry about that."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","lines":["Who is it like should lead his forces hither?"],"id":142,"m":"Who is likely to lead his forces here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["The Duke of Lancaster and Westmoreland;","Against the Welsh, himself and Harry Monmouth:","But who is substituted 'gainst the French,","I have no certain notice."],"id":143,"m":"The Duke of Lancaster and Westmoreland. The King himself and Prince Harry are going against the Welsh. But who has been appointed against the French I don't know for certain."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["Let us on,","And publish the occasion of our arms.","The commonwealth is sick of their own choice;","Their over-greedy love hath surfeited:","An habitation giddy and unsure","Hath he that buildeth on the vulgar heart.","O thou fond many, with what loud applause","Didst thou beat heaven with blessing Bolingbroke,","Before he was what thou wouldst have him be!","And being now trimm'd in thine own desires,","Thou, beastly feeder, art so full of him,","That thou provokest thyself to cast him up.","So, so, thou common dog, didst thou disgorge","Thy glutton bosom of the royal Richard;","And now thou wouldst eat thy dead vomit up,","And howl'st to find it. What trust is in these times?","They that, when Richard lived, would have him die,","Are now become enamour'd on his grave:","Thou that threw'st dust upon his goodly head","When through proud London he came sighing on","After the admired heels of Bolingbroke,","Criest now \"O earth, yield us that king again,","And take thou this!\" O thoughts of men accursed!","Past and to come seems best; things present worst."],"id":144,"m":"Let's go on and announce the reasons for our taking up arms. The people are sick of their own choice; their over-greedy love has made them overfull. Anyone who builds on the hearts of the common people has a dizzy, unsafe house. Oh, you foolish crowd, how you beat heaven with loud applause, blessing Bolingbroke before he was what you wanted him to be! And now that he's dressed up in your own desires, you greedy beast, you're so full of him that you're making yourself sick to throw him up. That's how you, common dog, vomited royal Richard out of your gluttonous stomach, and now you'd like to eat your dead vomit up again, and you howl to find it. What loyalty is there in these times? Those who wanted Richard dead when he was alive have now fallen in love with his grave. You who threw dust on his noble head when he came sighing through proud London behind the admired heels of Bolingbroke now cry, \"Oh earth, give us that king back, and take this one!\" Oh, the cursed thoughts of men! The past and future seem best; the present always worst."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["Shall we go draw our numbers, and set on?"],"id":145,"m":"Shall we go and gather our troops and set off?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["We are time's subjects, and time bids be gone."],"id":146,"m":"We're time's subjects, and time tells us to go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":147},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"London. A street.","id":148},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Hostess, Fang and his Boy with her, and Snare following.","id":149},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Master Fang, have you entered the action?"],"id":150,"m":"Master Fang, have you filed the lawsuit?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FANG","lines":["It is entered."],"id":151,"m":"It's filed."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Where's your yeoman? Is 't a lusty yeoman? will 'a stand to 't?"],"id":152,"m":"Where's your assistant? Is he a strong assistant? Will he stand his ground?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FANG","lines":["Sirrah, where's Snare?"],"id":153,"m":"You there, where's Snare?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["O Lord, ay! good Master Snare."],"id":154,"m":"Oh Lord, yes! Good Master Snare."},{"t":"sp","who":"SNARE","lines":["Here, here."],"id":155,"m":"Here, here."},{"t":"sp","who":"FANG","lines":["Snare, we must arrest Sir John Falstaff."],"id":156,"m":"Snare, we have to arrest Sir John Falstaff."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Yea, good Master Snare; I have entered him and all."],"id":157,"m":"Yes, good Master Snare, I've filed a case against him and everything."},{"t":"sp","who":"SNARE","lines":["It may chance cost some of our lives, for he will stab."],"id":158,"m":"It might cost some of us our lives, because he'll stab."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Alas the day! take heed of him; he stabbed me in mine own house,","and that most beastly: in good faith, he cares not what","mischief he does, if his weapon be out: he will foin like any","devil; he will spare neither man, woman, nor child."],"id":159,"m":"Oh dear! Watch out for him. He stabbed me in my own house, and most beastly too. Honestly, he doesn't care what damage he does once his weapon's out. He'll thrust like a devil; he won't spare man, woman or child."},{"t":"sp","who":"FANG","lines":["If I can close with him, I care not for his thrust."],"id":160,"m":"If I can get to grips with him, I don't care about his thrusting."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["No, nor I neither: I'll be at your elbow."],"id":161,"m":"No, nor do I. I'll be right beside you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FANG","lines":["An I but fist him once; an 'a come but within my vice,—"],"id":162,"m":"If I can just get my fist on him once, if he just comes within my grip —"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["I am undone by his going; I warrant you, he's an","infinitive thing upon my score. Good Master Fang, hold him sure:","good Master Snare, let him not 'scape. A' comes continuantly to","Pie-corner—saving your manhoods—to buy a saddle; and he is","indited to dinner to the Lubber's-head in Lumbert Street, to","Master Smooth's the silkman: I pray ye, since my exion is","entered and my case so openly known to the world, let him be","brought in to his answer. A hundred mark is a long one for a poor","lone woman to bear: and I have borne, and borne, and borne; and","have been fubbed off, and fubbed off, and fubbed off, from this","day to that day, that it is a shame to be thought on. There is no","honesty in such dealing; unless a woman should be made an ass and","a beast, to bear every knave's wrong. Yonder he comes; and that","arrant malmsey-nose knave, Bardolph, with him. Do your offices,","do your offices, Master Fang and Master Snare, do me, do me, do me","your offices."],"id":163,"m":"I'm ruined if he goes. I promise you, he owes me an infinite amount. Good Master Fang, hold him tight; good Master Snare, don't let him escape. He's always going to Pie Corner — excuse me, gentlemen — to buy a saddle, and he's invited to dinner at the Lubber's Head in Lombard Street, at Master Smooth's the silk merchant's. Please, since my case has been filed and is so publicly known, let him be brought to answer for it. A hundred marks is a long score for a poor lonely woman to carry, and I've carried it and carried it, and been fobbed off and fobbed off from one day to the next, so it's shameful to think about. There's no honesty in dealing like that, unless a woman is supposed to be a donkey and a beast of burden for every rogue's wrongs. There he comes, and that out-and-out red-nosed rogue Bardolph with him. Do your duty, do your duty, Master Fang and Master Snare. Do me, do me, do me your duty."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff, Page, and Bardolph.","id":164},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["How now! whose mare's dead? what's the matter?"],"id":165,"m":"What's going on? Who's dead? What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FANG","lines":["Sir John, I arrest you at the suit of Mistress Quickly."],"id":166,"m":"Sir John, I arrest you on the complaint of Mistress Quickly."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Away, varlets! Draw, Bardolph: cut me off the villain's","head: throw the quean in the channel."],"id":167,"m":"Get away, you rascals! Draw, Bardolph. Cut off the villain's head, and throw the slut in the gutter."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Throw me in the channel! I'll throw thee in the channel.","Wilt thou? wilt thou? thou bastardly rogue! Murder, murder! Ah,","thou honey-suckle villain! wilt thou kill God's officers and the","king's?","Ah, thou honey-seed rogue! thou art a honey-seed, a man-queller,","and a woman-queller."],"id":168,"m":"Throw me in the gutter! I'll throw you in the gutter. Will you? Will you? You bastard rogue! Murder, murder! Oh, you honeysuckle villain! Will you kill God's officers and the King's? Oh, you honey-seed rogue! You're a honey-seed, a man-killer and a woman-killer."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Keep them off, Bardolph."],"id":169,"m":"Keep them off, Bardolph."},{"t":"sp","who":"FANG","lines":["A rescue! a rescue!"],"id":170,"m":"Help! Help!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Good people, bring a rescue or two. Thou wo't, wo't thou?","thou wo't, wo't ta? do, do, thou rogue! do, thou hemp-seed!"],"id":171,"m":"Good people, bring help. You will, will you? You will, will you? Go on, you rogue! Go on, you gallows-bird!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Away, you scullion! you rampallian! you fustilarian! I'll tickle","your catastrophe."],"id":172,"m":"Get away, you kitchen slut! You scruffy wench! You frump! I'll tickle your backside."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Lord Chief-Justice, and his men.","id":173},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["What is the matter? keep the peace here, ho!"],"id":174,"m":"What's the matter? Keep the peace here!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Good my lord, be good to me. I beseech you, stand to me."],"id":175,"m":"My good lord, be good to me. I beg you, stand by me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["How now, Sir John! what are you brawling here?","Doth this become your place, your time and business?","You should have been well on your way to York.","Stand from him, fellow: wherefore hang'st thou upon him?"],"id":176,"m":"What now, Sir John! Are you brawling here? Is this fitting for your position, your age and your business? You should be well on your way to York by now. Get away from him, fellow. Why are you hanging on him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["O my most worshipful lord, an't please your grace, I am a","poor widow of Eastcheap, and he is arrested at my suit."],"id":177,"m":"Oh, my most worshipful lord, if it please your grace, I'm a poor widow from Eastcheap, and he's arrested on my complaint."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["For what sum?"],"id":178,"m":"For what sum?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["It is more than for some, my lord; it is for all, all I have.","He hath eaten me out of house and home; he hath put all my substance","into that fat belly of his: but I will have some of it out again,","or I will ride thee o' nights like the mare."],"id":179,"m":"It's more than for some, my lord; it's for all, all I have. He's eaten me out of house and home; he's put everything I own into that fat belly of his. But I'll have some of it out again, or I'll ride you at night like a nightmare."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I think I am as like to ride the mare, if I have any","vantage of ground to get up."],"id":180,"m":"I think I'm as likely to ride the mare, if I have any high ground to climb up from."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["How comes this, Sir John? Fie! what man of good temper would","endure this tempest of exclamation? Are you not ashamed to enforce","a poor widow to so rough a course to come by her own?"],"id":181,"m":"What's this, Sir John? Shame! What man of good character would put up with this storm of shouting? Aren't you ashamed to force a poor widow to such rough measures to get back what's hers?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What is the gross sum that I owe thee?"],"id":182,"m":"What's the total I owe you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Marry, if thou wert an honest man, thyself and the money too.","Thou didst swear to me upon a parcel-gilt goblet, sitting in","my Dolphin-chamber, at the round table, by a sea-coal fire, upon","Wednesday in Wheeson week, when the prince broke thy head for","liking his father to a singing-man of Windsor, thou didst swear to","me then, as I was washing thy wound, to marry me and make me my","lady thy wife. Canst thou deny it? Did not goodwife Keech, the","butcher's wife, come in then and call me gossip Quickly? Coming","in to borrow a mess of vinegar; telling us she had a good dish of","prawns, whereby thou didst desire to eat some, whereby I told","thee they were ill for green wound? And didst thou not, when she","was gone down stairs, desire me to be no more so familiarity with","such poor people; saying that ere long they should call me madam?","And didst thou not kiss me, and bid me fetch thee thirty shillings?","I put thee now to thy book-oath: deny it, if thou canst."],"id":183,"m":"Indeed, if you were an honest man, yourself and the money too. You swore to me on a partly gilded goblet, sitting in my Dolphin room at the round table by a coal fire, on the Wednesday of Whitsun week, when the Prince cracked your head for comparing his father to a singer from Windsor — you swore to me then, while I was washing your wound, that you'd marry me and make me my lady your wife. Can you deny it? Didn't goodwife Keech, the butcher's wife, come in then and call me friend Quickly? She came in to borrow a dish of vinegar and told us she had a good dish of prawns, and you wanted to eat some, and I told you they were bad for a fresh wound. And didn't you, when she'd gone downstairs, tell me not to be so familiar with such poor people, saying that before long they'd be calling me madam? And didn't you kiss me and tell me to fetch you thirty shillings? I put you on your oath now: deny it if you can."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["My lord, this is a poor mad soul; and she says up and down the","town that her eldest son is like you: she hath been in good case,","and the truth is, poverty hath distracted her. But for these","foolish officers, I beseech you I may have redress against them."],"id":184,"m":"My lord, this is a poor mad soul, and she goes all over town saying her eldest son looks like you. She used to be well off, and the truth is poverty has driven her out of her mind. But as for these foolish officers, I ask for compensation against them."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Sir John, Sir John, I am well acquainted with your","manner of wrenching the true cause the false way. It is not a","confident brow, nor the throng of words that come with such more","than impudent sauciness from you, can thrust me from a level","consideration: you have, as it appears to me, practised upon the","easy-yielding spirit of this woman, and made her serve your uses","both in purse and in person."],"id":185,"m":"Sir John, Sir John, I know very well your way of twisting a true case into a false one. It isn't a confident face, nor the flood of words that pours out of you with more than shameless cheek, that can push me off a fair judgement. It seems to me you've worked on this woman's easily persuaded nature and used her both for her money and her body."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Yea, in truth, my lord."],"id":186,"m":"Yes, truly, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Pray thee, peace. Pay her the debt you owe her, and unpay the","villany you have done her: the one you may do with sterling","money, and the other with current repentance."],"id":187,"m":"Please, be quiet. Pay her the debt you owe her, and undo the wrong you've done her. The first you can do with good money, the second with sincere repentance."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["My lord, I will not undergo this sneap without reply.","You call honourable boldness impudent sauciness: if a man will make","courtesy and say nothing, he is virtuous: no, my lord, my humble","duty remembered, I will not be your suitor. I say to you, I do desire","deliverance from these officers, being upon hasty employment in the","king's affairs."],"id":188,"m":"My lord, I won't take this telling-off without replying. You call honourable boldness shameless cheek. If a man bows and says nothing, he's virtuous! No, my lord, with all due respect, I won't come begging to you. I tell you, I want to be freed from these officers, as I'm on urgent business for the King."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["You speak as having power to do wrong: but answer","in the effect of your reputation, and satisfy the poor woman."],"id":189,"m":"You speak as if you had a licence to do wrong. But act as your reputation demands, and pay the poor woman."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Come hither, hostess."],"id":190,"m":"Come here, hostess."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Gower.","id":191},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Now, Master Gower, what news?"],"id":192,"m":"Well, Master Gower, what news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["The king, my lord, and Harry Prince of Wales","Are near at hand: the rest the paper tells."],"id":193,"m":"The King, my lord, and Harry Prince of Wales are close by. The letter tells the rest."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["As I am a gentleman."],"id":194,"m":"As I am a gentleman."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Faith, you said so before."],"id":195,"m":"Honestly, you said that before."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["As I am a gentleman. Come, no more words of it."],"id":196,"m":"As I am a gentleman. Come, not another word about it."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["By this heavenly ground I tread on, I must be fain to pawn","both my plate and the tapestry of my dining-chambers."],"id":197,"m":"By this holy ground I walk on, I'll have to pawn my silver plate and the tapestries in my dining rooms."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Glasses, glasses, is the only drinking: and for thy walls, a pretty","slight drollery, or the story of the Prodigal, or the German hunting","in water-work, is worth a thousand of these bed-hangings and","these fly-bitten tapestries. Let it be ten pound, if thou canst.","Come, an 'twere not for thy humours, there's not a better wench in","England. Go, wash thy face, and draw the action. Come, thou must not be","in this humour with me; dost not know me? come, come, I know thou wast","set on to this."],"id":198,"m":"Glasses, glasses are the only thing to drink from. And for your walls, a pretty little comic picture, or the story of the Prodigal Son, or the German hunt painted in watercolour is worth a thousand of these bed-curtains and fly-spotted tapestries. Make it ten pounds if you can. Come on, if it weren't for your moods, there wouldn't be a better girl in England. Go and wash your face, and withdraw the lawsuit. Come, you mustn't be in this mood with me. Don't you know me? Come, come, I know someone put you up to this."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Pray thee, Sir John, let it be but twenty nobles: i' faith,","I am loath to pawn my plate, so God save me, la!"],"id":199,"m":"Please, Sir John, make it just twenty nobles. Honestly, I hate to pawn my plate, God help me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Let it alone; I'll make other shift: you'll be a fool still."],"id":200,"m":"Forget it; I'll find another way. You'll always be a fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Well, you shall have it, though I pawn my gown. I hope","you'll come to supper. You'll pay me all together?"],"id":201,"m":"Well, you'll have it, even if I have to pawn my gown. I hope you'll come to supper. You'll pay me back all at once?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Will I live? [To Bardolph] Go, with her, with her;","hook on, hook on."],"id":202,"m":"As sure as I live. (To Bardolph) Go with her, go with her. Hook her, hook her."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Will you have Doll Tearsheet meet you at supper?"],"id":203,"m":"Do you want Doll Tearsheet to meet you at supper?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["No more words; let's have her."],"id":204,"m":"No more talk; let's have her."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Hostess, Bardolph, Officers, and Boy.","id":205},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["I have heard better news."],"id":206,"m":"I've heard better news."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What's the news, my lord?"],"id":207,"m":"What's the news, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Where lay the king last night?"],"id":208,"m":"Where did the King stay last night?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["At Basingstoke, my lord."],"id":209,"m":"At Basingstoke, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I hope, my lord, all's well: what is the news, my lord?"],"id":210,"m":"I hope all's well, my lord. What's the news, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Come all his forces back?"],"id":211,"m":"Are all his forces coming back?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["No; fifteen hundred foot, five hundred horse,","Are march'd up to my Lord of Lancaster,","Against Northumberland and the Archbishop."],"id":212,"m":"No. Fifteen hundred foot soldiers and five hundred horsemen have marched to join my Lord of Lancaster against Northumberland and the Archbishop."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Comes the king back from Wales, my noble lord?"],"id":213,"m":"Is the King coming back from Wales, my noble lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["You shall have letters of me presently:","Come, go along with me, good Master Gower."],"id":214,"m":"You'll have letters from me shortly. Come with me, good Master Gower."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["My lord!"],"id":215,"m":"My lord!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["What's the matter?"],"id":216,"m":"What is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Master Gower, shall I entreat you with me to dinner?"],"id":217,"m":"Master Gower, may I invite you to dinner with me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GOWER","lines":["I must wait upon my good lord here; I thank you, good Sir John."],"id":218,"m":"I have to attend my good lord here. Thank you, good Sir John."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Sir John, you loiter here too long, being you are to","take soldiers up in counties as you go."],"id":219,"m":"Sir John, you're hanging about here too long, since you're supposed to be recruiting soldiers in the counties on your way."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Will you sup with me, Master Gower?"],"id":220,"m":"Will you have supper with me, Master Gower?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["What foolish master taught you these manners, Sir John?"],"id":221,"m":"What foolish teacher taught you these manners, Sir John?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Master Gower, if they become me not, he was a fool that","taught them me. This is the right fencing grace, my lord; tap for","tap, and so part fair."],"id":222,"m":"Master Gower, if they don't suit me, the man who taught them to me was a fool. This is proper fencing style, my lord: tit for tat, and part on equal terms."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Now the Lord lighten thee! thou art a great fool."],"id":223,"m":"May the Lord enlighten you! You're a great fool."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":224},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"London. Another street.","id":225},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Prince Henry and Poins.","id":226},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Before God, I am exceeding weary."],"id":227,"m":"By God, I'm extremely tired."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Is 't come to that? I had thought weariness durst not have","attach'd one of so high blood."],"id":228,"m":"Has it come to that? I'd have thought tiredness wouldn't dare to seize anyone of such high blood."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Faith, it does me; though it discolours the complexion of","my greatness to acknowledge it. Doth it not show vilely in me to","desire small beer?"],"id":229,"m":"Well, it seizes me, though it spoils the colour of my greatness to admit it. Doesn't it look shameful in me to want weak beer?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Why, a prince should not be so loosely studied as to","remember so weak a composition."],"id":230,"m":"Well, a prince shouldn't be so badly educated as to remember such a feeble drink."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Belike then my appetite was not princely got; for, by my troth,","I do now remember the poor creature, small beer. But, indeed,","these humble considerations make me out of love with my greatness.","What a disgrace is it to me to remember thy name! or to know thy","face to-morrow! or to take note how many pair of silk stockings thou","hast, viz. these, and those that were thy peach-coloured ones! or to","bear the inventory of thy shirts, as, one for superfluity, and another","for use!","But that the tennis-court-keeper knows better than I; for it is a low","ebb of linen with thee when thou keepest not racket there; as thou hast","not done a great while, because the rest of thy low countries have made","a shift to eat up thy holland: and God knows, whether those that bawl","out of the ruins of thy linen shall inherit his kingdom: but the","midwives say the children are not in the fault; whereupon the world","increases, and kindreds are mightily strengthened."],"id":231,"m":"Then I suppose my appetite wasn't born royal, because, honestly, I do now remember that poor creature, weak beer. But really, these humble thoughts make me fall out of love with my greatness. What a disgrace it is for me to remember your name! Or to recognise your face tomorrow! Or to keep count of how many pairs of silk stockings you have — these, and the ones that used to be your peach-coloured ones! Or to keep a list of your shirts: one spare and one for wearing! But the tennis-court keeper knows that better than I do, because your linen is at a low ebb when you don't play there, as you haven't for a long time, because your lowlands have managed to eat up your fine Dutch linen. And God knows whether the babies who bawl out of the remains of your shirts will inherit His kingdom; but the midwives say the children aren't to blame, and so the world grows and families are mightily increased."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["How ill it follows, after you have laboured so hard, you","should talk so idly! Tell me, how many good young princes would","do so, their fathers being so sick as yours at this time is?"],"id":232,"m":"How badly it follows, after you've worked so hard, that you should talk such nonsense! Tell me, how many good young princes would carry on like this when their fathers were as ill as yours is now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Shall I tell thee one thing, Poins?"],"id":233,"m":"Shall I tell you one thing, Poins?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Yes, faith; and let it be an excellent good thing."],"id":234,"m":"Yes, indeed, and make it an excellent thing."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["It shall serve among wits of no higher breeding than thine."],"id":235,"m":"It will do for wits no better bred than yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Go to; I stand the push of your one thing that you will tell."],"id":236,"m":"Go on. I'm ready to take the blow of this one thing you're going to tell me."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Marry, I tell thee it is not meet that I should be sad, now my father","is sick: albeit I could tell to thee, as to one it pleases me, for","fault of a better, to call my friend, I could be sad, and sad indeed too."],"id":237,"m":"Well, I tell you it isn't proper for me to be sad now that my father is ill — although I could tell you, as one I'm pleased to call my friend for lack of anyone better, that I could be sad, and truly sad too."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Very hardly upon such a subject."],"id":238,"m":"Hardly, about such a subject."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["By this hand, thou thinkest me as far in the devil's book as thou","and Falstaff for obduracy and persistency: let the end try the man.","But I tell thee, my heart bleeds inwardly that my father is so sick:","and keeping such vile company as thou art hath in reason taken from","me all ostentation of sorrow."],"id":239,"m":"By this hand, you think I'm as deep in the devil's book as you and Falstaff for stubbornness and persistence. Let the outcome test the man. But I tell you, my heart bleeds inside that my father is so ill, and keeping such low company as you has rightly robbed me of any outward show of grief."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["The reason?"],"id":240,"m":"Why's that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["What wouldst thou think of me, if I should weep?"],"id":241,"m":"What would you think of me if I cried?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["I would think thee a most princely hypocrite."],"id":242,"m":"I'd think you a most princely hypocrite."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["It would be every man's thought; and thou art a blessed fellow to","think as every man thinks: never a man's thought in the world keeps","the road-way better than thine: every man would think me an","hypocrite indeed. And what accites your most worshipful thought to","think so?"],"id":243,"m":"That's what everyone would think, and you're a blessed fellow to think what everyone thinks. No man's thinking in the world keeps to the main road better than yours. Everyone would think me a hypocrite indeed. And what prompts your most worshipful mind to think so?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Why, because you have been so lewd and so much engraffed","to Falstaff."],"id":244,"m":"Why, because you've been so wild and so attached to Falstaff."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["And to thee."],"id":245,"m":"And to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["By this light, I am well spoke on; I can hear it with mine own","ears: the worst that they can say of me is that I am a second","brother and that I am a proper fellow of my hands; and those two","things, I confess, I cannot help. By the mass, here comes Bardolph."],"id":246,"m":"By this light, people speak well of me; I can hear it with my own ears. The worst they can say of me is that I'm a younger son and that I'm handy with my fists, and those two things, I admit, I can't help. By the mass, here comes Bardolph."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Bardolph and Page.","id":247},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["And the boy that I gave Falstaff: 'a had him from me Christian;","and look, if the fat villain have not transformed him ape."],"id":248,"m":"And the boy I gave Falstaff. He got him from me a Christian, and look if the fat villain hasn't turned him into an ape."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["God save your grace!"],"id":249,"m":"God save your grace!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["And yours, most noble Bardolph!"],"id":250,"m":"And yours, most noble Bardolph!"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Come, you virtuous ass, you bashful fool, must you be blushing?","wherefore blush you now? What a maidenly man-at-arms are you become!","Is 't such a matter to get a pottle-pot's maidenhead?"],"id":251,"m":"Come on, you virtuous ass, you bashful fool, must you be blushing? Why are you blushing now? What a maidenly soldier you've become! Is it such a big deal to take the virginity of a two-quart tankard?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["'A calls me e'en now, my lord, through a red lattice, and I could","discern no part of his face from the window: at last I spied his","eyes, and methought he had made two holes in the ale-wife's new","petticoat and so peep'd through."],"id":252,"m":"He called me just now, my lord, through a red window lattice, and I couldn't make out any part of his face from the window. In the end I spotted his eyes, and I thought he'd made two holes in the ale-woman's new petticoat and was peeping through."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Has not the boy profited?"],"id":253,"m":"Hasn't the boy come on?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Away, you whoreson upright rabbit, away!"],"id":254,"m":"Get away, you son-of-a-whore upright rabbit, get away!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Away, you rascally Althaea's dream, away!"],"id":255,"m":"Get away, you rascally Althaea's dream, get away!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Instruct us, boy; what dream, boy?"],"id":256,"m":"Teach us, boy. What dream, boy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Marry, my lord, Althaea dreamt she was delivered of a","fire-brand; and therefore I call him her dream."],"id":257,"m":"Well, my lord, Althaea dreamt she gave birth to a burning torch, so I call him her dream."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["A crown's worth of good interpretation: there 'tis, boy."],"id":258,"m":"That's a crown's worth of good interpretation. There it is, boy."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["O, that this blossom could be kept from cankers! Well,","there is sixpence to preserve thee."],"id":259,"m":"Oh, if only this blossom could be kept from the worms! Well, here's sixpence to protect you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["An you do not make him hanged among you, the gallows","shall have wrong."],"id":260,"m":"If you lot don't get him hanged between you, the gallows will be cheated."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["And how doth thy master, Bardolph?"],"id":261,"m":"And how's your master, Bardolph?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Well, my lord. He heard of your grace's coming to town:","there's a letter for you."],"id":262,"m":"Well, my lord. He heard your grace was coming to town. Here's a letter for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Deliver'd with good respect. And how doth the martlemas,","your master?"],"id":263,"m":"Delivered with proper ceremony. And how is your master, that fat Martinmas ox?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["In bodily health, sir."],"id":264,"m":"In good bodily health, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Marry, the immortal part needs a physician; but that moves","not him: though that be sick, it dies not."],"id":265,"m":"Well, his immortal part needs a doctor, but that doesn't bother him. Though that's sick, it doesn't die."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I do allow this wen to be as familiar with me as my dog;","and he holds his place; for look you how he writes."],"id":266,"m":"I allow this growth to be as familiar with me as my dog, and he keeps his place. Look how he writes."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["[Reads] \"John Falstaff, knight,\"—every man must know that, as oft","as he has occasion to name himself: even like those that are kin","to the king; for they never prick their finger but they say,","\"There's some of the king's blood spilt.\"","\"How comes that?\" says he, that takes upon him not to conceive.","The answer is as ready as a borrower's cap,","\"I am the king's poor cousin, sir.\""],"id":267,"m":"(Reads) \"John Falstaff, knight\" — everyone has to know that, every time he has the chance to name himself. He's just like those distant relatives of the King: they never prick their finger without saying, \"There's some of the King's blood spilt.\" \"How's that?\" says someone who pretends not to understand. And the answer comes as quickly as a borrower doffs his cap: \"I'm the King's poor cousin, sir.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Nay, they will be kin to us, or they will fetch it from Japhet.","But to the letter:"],"id":268,"m":"Yes, they'll be related to us even if they have to trace it back to Noah's son Japhet. But to the letter."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["[Reads] \"Sir John Falstaff, knight, to the son of the king,","nearest his father, Harry Prince of Wales, greeting.\" Why, this","is a certificate."],"id":269,"m":"(Reads) \"Sir John Falstaff, knight, to the son of the King nearest his father, Harry Prince of Wales, greeting.\" Why, this is a legal certificate."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Peace!"],"id":270,"m":"Quiet!"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["[Reads] \"I will imitate the honourable Romans in brevity:\" he sure","means brevity in breath, short-winded. \"I commend me to thee, I commend","thee, and I leave thee. Be not too familiar with Poins; for he misuses","thy favours so much, that he swears thou art to marry his sister Nell.","Repent at idle times as thou mayest; and so, farewell.","\"Thine, by yea and no, which is as much as to say, as thou","usest him,","JACK FALSTAFF with my familiars, JOHN with my brothers and","sisters, and SIR JOHN with all Europe.\"","My lord, I'll steep this letter in sack and make him eat it."],"id":271,"m":"(Reads) \"I will imitate the honourable Romans in brevity.\" He must mean shortness of breath, being short-winded. \"I commend myself to you, I commend you, and I leave you. Don't be too familiar with Poins, because he abuses your favours so much that he swears you're going to marry his sister Nell. Repent in your spare time as you can, and so farewell. Yours, yes and no (which is as much as to say, depending on how you treat him), Jack Falstaff to my friends, John to my brothers and sisters, and Sir John to all Europe.\" My lord, I'll soak this letter in sack and make him eat it."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["That's to make him eat twenty of his words. But do you use","me thus, Ned? must I marry your sister?"],"id":272,"m":"That would make him eat twenty of his words. But do you treat me like this, Ned? Must I marry your sister?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["God send the wench no worse fortune! But I never said so."],"id":273,"m":"God send the girl no worse luck! But I never said so."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Well, thus we play the fools with the time, and the spirits of the","wise sit in the clouds and mock us. Is your master here in London?"],"id":274,"m":"Well, this is how we play the fool with our time, while the spirits of the wise sit in the clouds and laugh at us. Is your master here in London?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Yea, my lord."],"id":275,"m":"Yes, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Where sups he? doth the old boar feed in the old frank?"],"id":276,"m":"Where does he eat? Does the old boar feed in the old pigsty?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["At the old place, my lord, in Eastcheap."],"id":277,"m":"At the old place, my lord, in Eastcheap."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["What company?"],"id":278,"m":"Who with?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Ephesians, my lord, of the old church."],"id":279,"m":"Drinking companions, my lord, of the old congregation."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Sup any women with him?"],"id":280,"m":"Are any women eating with him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["None, my lord, but old Mistress Quickly and Mistress Doll Tearsheet."],"id":281,"m":"None, my lord, but old Mistress Quickly and Mistress Doll Tearsheet."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["What pagan may that be?"],"id":282,"m":"What heathen might that be?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["A proper gentlewoman, sir, and a kinswoman of my master's."],"id":283,"m":"A decent gentlewoman, sir, and a relative of my master's."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Even such kin as the parish heifers are to the town bull. Shall","we steal upon them, Ned, at supper?"],"id":284,"m":"About as related as the parish cows are to the town bull. Shall we creep up on them at supper, Ned?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["I am your shadow, my lord; I'll follow you."],"id":285,"m":"I'm your shadow, my lord. I'll follow you."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Sirrah, you boy, and Bardolph, no word to your master that","I am yet come to town: there's for your silence."],"id":286,"m":"You there, boy, and Bardolph, not a word to your master that I've arrived in town yet. Here's something for your silence."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["I have no tongue, sir."],"id":287,"m":"I have no tongue, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["And for mine, sir, I will govern it."],"id":288,"m":"And as for mine, sir, I'll keep it under control."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Fare you well; go."],"id":289,"m":"Goodbye. Go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Bardolph and Page.","id":290},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","cont":1,"lines":["This Doll Tearsheet should be some road."],"id":291,"m":"This Doll Tearsheet must be a common road."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["I warrant you, as common as the way between Saint Alban's and London."],"id":292,"m":"I promise you, as common as the road between St Albans and London."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["How might we see Falstaff bestow himself to-night in his true","colours, and not ourselves be seen?"],"id":293,"m":"How could we see Falstaff behave tonight in his true colours without being seen ourselves?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Put on two leathern jerkins and aprons, and wait upon him at","his table as drawers."],"id":294,"m":"Put on two leather jackets and aprons and serve him at his table as waiters."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["From a God to a bull? a heavy descension! it was Jove's case.","From a prince to a prentice? a low transformation! that shall be","mine; for in everything the purpose must weigh with the folly.","Follow me, Ned."],"id":295,"m":"From a god to a bull? A heavy comedown! That's what happened to Jove. From a prince to an apprentice? A low transformation! That shall be mine, because in everything the purpose has to balance the foolishness. Follow me, Ned."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":296},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"III","loc":"Warkworth. Before the castle.","id":297},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Northumberland, Lady Northumberland, and Lady Percy.","id":298},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["I pray thee, loving wife, and gentle daughter,","Give even way unto my rough affairs;","Put not you on the visage of the times","And be like them to Percy troublesome."],"id":299,"m":"Please, loving wife and gentle daughter, let my rough business go smoothly. Don't put on the face of the times and be as troublesome to Percy as they are."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["I have given over, I will speak no more:","Do what you will; your wisdom be your guide."],"id":300,"m":"I've given up; I won't say any more. Do what you like; let your wisdom guide you."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Alas, sweet wife, my honour is at pawn;","And, but my going, nothing can redeem it."],"id":301,"m":"Alas, dear wife, my honour is pledged, and nothing but my going can redeem it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY PERCY","lines":["O yet, for God's sake, go not to these wars!","The time was, father, that you broke your word,","When you were more endear'd to it than now!","When your own Percy, when my heart's dear Harry,","Threw many a northward look to see his father","Bring up his powers; but he did long in vain.","Who then persuaded you to stay at home?","There were two honours lost, yours and your son's.","For yours, the God of heaven brighten it!","For his, it stuck upon him as the sun","In the grey vault of heaven; and by his light","Did all the chivalry of England move","To do brave acts: he was indeed the glass","Wherein the noble youth did dress themselves:","He had no legs that practis'd not his gait;","And speaking thick, which nature made his blemish,","Became the accents of the valiant;","For those who could speak low and tardily","Would turn their own perfection to abuse,","To seem like him: so that in speech, in gait,","In diet, in affections of delight,","In military rules, humours of blood,","He was the mark and glass, copy and book,","That fashion'd others. And him, O wondrous him!","O miracle of men! him did you leave,","Second to none, unseconded by you,","To look upon the hideous god of war","In disadvantage; to abide a field","Where nothing but the sound of Hotspur's name","Did seem defensible: so you left him.","Never, O never, do his ghost the wrong","To hold your honour more precise and nice","With others than with him! let them alone:","The marshal and the archbishop are strong:","Had my sweet Harry had but half their numbers,","To-day might I, hanging on Hotspur's neck,","Have talk'd of Monmouth's grave."],"id":302,"m":"Oh, even so, for God's sake, don't go to these wars! There was a time, father, when you broke your word when you were more bound to keep it than now — when your own Percy, my heart's dear Harry, kept looking north to see his father bring up his army, and waited in vain. Who persuaded you to stay at home then? Two honours were lost there, yours and your son's. As for yours, may God in heaven polish it bright! As for his, it was fixed on him like the sun in the grey sky, and by its light all the knights of England were moved to brave deeds. He was truly the mirror in which the noble young men dressed themselves. Every young man copied his walk, and his quick, thick speech, a flaw nature gave him, became the way the brave talked, so that those who could speak slowly and quietly spoiled their own perfection to seem like him. In speech, in walk, in diet, in pleasures, in military discipline and in temperament, he was the target and the mirror, the copy and the book, that shaped everyone else. And him — oh, wonderful him! oh, miracle of men! — you left him, second to none, without your support, to face the hideous god of war at a disadvantage, to hold a battlefield where only the sound of Hotspur's name seemed to give any defence. That's how you left him. Never, oh never, do his ghost the wrong of keeping your honour more strictly and carefully with others than you did with him! Leave them alone. The Marshal and the Archbishop are strong. If my sweet Harry had had even half their numbers, I might today be hanging on Hotspur's neck and talking about Prince Harry's grave."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Beshrew your heart,","Fair daughter, you do draw my spirits from me","With new lamenting ancient oversights.","But I must go and meet with danger there,","Or it will seek me in another place,","And find me worse provided."],"id":303,"m":"Curse you, fair daughter, you drain my spirit from me by lamenting old mistakes all over again. But I must go and meet the danger there, or it will come looking for me somewhere else and find me less prepared."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["O, fly to Scotland,","Till that the nobles and the armed commons","Have of their puissance made a little taste."],"id":304,"m":"Oh, escape to Scotland until the nobles and the armed common people have had a little taste of their strength."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY PERCY","lines":["If they get ground and vantage of the king,","Then join you with them, like a rib of steel,","To make strength stronger; but, for all our loves,","First let them try themselves. So did your son;","He was so suffer'd: so came I a widow;","And never shall have length of life enough","To rain upon remembrance with mine eyes,","That it may grow and sprout as high as heaven,","For recordation to my noble husband."],"id":305,"m":"If they gain ground and an advantage over the King, then join them like a steel rib to make their strength stronger. But, for the love of all of us, let them test themselves first. That's what your son was allowed to do; that's how I became a widow. And I'll never live long enough to water his memory with my tears so that it grows and sprouts as high as heaven as a memorial to my noble husband."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Come, come, go in with me. 'Tis with my mind","As with the tide swell'd up unto his height,","That makes a still-stand, running neither way:","Fain would I go to meet the archbishop,","But many thousand reasons hold me back.","I will resolve for Scotland: there am I,","Till time and vantage crave my company."],"id":306,"m":"Come, come, go in with me. My mind is like the tide when it has swelled to its height and stands still, flowing neither way. I'd like to go and meet the Archbishop, but many thousand reasons hold me back. I'll decide on Scotland. I'll stay there until the right time and advantage call for me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":307},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"IV","loc":"London. The Boar's-head Tavern in Eastcheap.","id":308},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter two Drawers.","id":309},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST DRAWER","lines":["What the devil hast thou brought there? apple-johns?","thou knowest Sir John cannot endure an apple-john."],"id":310,"m":"What the devil have you brought there? Dried-up apples? You know Sir John can't stand old wrinkled apples."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND DRAWER","lines":["Mass, thou sayest true. The prince once set a dish of apple-johns","before him, and told him there were five more Sir Johns, and, putting","off his hat, said \"I will now take my leave of these six dry, round,","old, withered knights.\" It angered him to the heart: but he hath","forgot that."],"id":311,"m":"By the mass, that's true. The Prince once put a dish of them in front of him, told him there were five more Sir Johns, and, taking off his hat, said, \"I'll now take my leave of these six dry, round, old, withered knights.\" It made him furious, but he's forgotten that now."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST DRAWER","lines":["Why, then, cover, and set them down: and see if thou canst find out","Sneak's noise; Mistress Tearsheet would fain hear some music.","Dispatch: The room where they supped is too hot; they'll come in","straight."],"id":312,"m":"Well then, lay the cloth and put them down, and see if you can find Sneak's band; Mistress Tearsheet would like to hear some music. Hurry. The room where they had supper is too hot; they'll come in here in a moment."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND DRAWER","lines":["Sirrah, here will be the prince and Master Poins anon; and they","will put on two of our jerkins and aprons; and Sir John must","not know of it: Bardolph hath brought word."],"id":313,"m":"Listen, the Prince and Master Poins will be here soon, and they'll put on two of our jackets and aprons, and Sir John mustn't know about it. Bardolph has brought word."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST DRAWER","lines":["By the mass, here will be old Utis: it will be an excellent","stratagem."],"id":314,"m":"By the mass, we'll have a grand old time. It'll be an excellent trick."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND DRAWER","lines":["I'll see if I can find out Sneak."],"id":315,"m":"I'll see if I can find Sneak."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":316},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Hostess and Doll Tearsheet.","id":317},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["I' faith, sweetheart, methinks now you are in an excellent good","temperality: your pulsidge beats as extraordinarily as heart would","desire; and your colour, I warrant you, is as red as any rose, in","good truth, la! But, i' faith, you have drunk too much canaries; and","that's a marvellous searching wine, and it perfumes the blood ere one","can say \"What's this?\" How do you now?"],"id":318,"m":"Honestly, sweetheart, I think you're in an excellent good temper now. Your pulse is beating as extraordinarily as anyone could wish, and your colour, I promise you, is as red as any rose, truly! But honestly, you've drunk too much Canary wine, and that's a wonderfully penetrating wine; it perfumes the blood before you can say \"What's this?\" How are you now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["Better than I was: hem!"],"id":319,"m":"Better than I was. (She hiccups.)"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Why, that's well said; a good heart's worth gold. Lo, here","comes Sir John."],"id":320,"m":"Well, that's good to hear; a good heart's worth gold. Look, here comes Sir John."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff.","id":321},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["[Singing] \"When Arthur first in court\"—Empty the jordan.","[Exit First Drawer]—[Singing] \"And was a worthy king.\"","How now, Mistress Doll!"],"id":322,"m":"(Singing) \"When Arthur first in court\" — Empty the chamber pot. (Exit First Drawer) — (Singing) \"And was a worthy king.\" How are you, Mistress Doll?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Sick of a calm; yea, good faith."],"id":323,"m":"Sick from too much calm; yes, truly."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["So is all her sect; an they be once in a calm, they are sick."],"id":324,"m":"So are all women of her sort: once they're calm, they're sick."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["You muddy rascal, is that all the comfort you give me?"],"id":325,"m":"You dirty rascal, is that all the comfort you give me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["You make fat rascals, Mistress Doll."],"id":326,"m":"You make rascals fat, Mistress Doll."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["I make them! gluttony and diseases make them; I make them not."],"id":327,"m":"I make them! Gluttony and diseases make them; I don't."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["If the cook help to make the gluttony, you help to make the diseases,","Doll: we catch of you, Doll, we catch of you; grant that, my poor","virtue, grant that."],"id":328,"m":"If the cook helps cause the gluttony, you help cause the diseases, Doll. We catch them from you, Doll, we catch them from you. Admit it, my poor virtue, admit it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["Yea, joy, our chains and our jewels."],"id":329,"m":"Yes, my darling, you catch our chains and our jewels."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["\"Your brooches, pearls, and ouches:\" for to serve bravely is to come","halting off, you know: to come off the breach with his pike bent","bravely, and to surgery bravely; to venture upon the charged chambers","bravely,—"],"id":330,"m":"\"Your brooches, pearls and ornaments\" — because to serve bravely, you know, is to come away limping: to come away from the breach with your pike bravely bent, and bravely off to the surgeon, to venture bravely against loaded guns —"},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["Hang yourself, you muddy conger, hang yourself!"],"id":331,"m":"Go hang yourself, you slimy eel, hang yourself!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["By my troth, this is the old fashion; you two never meet but you","fall to some discord: you are both, i' good truth, as rheumatic","as two dry toasts; you cannot one bear with another's confirmities.","What the good-year! one must bear, and that must be you: you are the","weaker vessel, as as they say, the emptier vessel."],"id":332,"m":"Honestly, it's the same old story. You two never meet without falling out. Truly, you're both as irritable as two dry pieces of toast; you can't put up with each other's infirmities. What the devil! One of you has to put up with it, and it has to be you. You're the weaker vessel, as they say, the emptier vessel."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["Can a weak empty vessel bear such a huge full hogshead? there's a whole","merchant's venture of Bourdeaux stuff in him; you have not seen a hulk","better stuffed in the hold. Come, I'll be friends with thee, Jack:","thou art going to the wars; and whether I shall ever see thee again or","no, there is nobody cares."],"id":333,"m":"Can a weak empty vessel carry such a huge full barrel? There's a whole merchant's cargo of Bordeaux wine in him; you've never seen a ship's hold better stuffed. Come on, I'll be friends with you, Jack. You're going off to the wars, and whether I'll ever see you again or not, nobody cares."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter First Drawer.","id":334},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST DRAWER","lines":["Sir, Ancient Pistol's below, and would speak with you."],"id":335,"m":"Sir, Ensign Pistol's downstairs and wants to speak to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["Hang him, swaggering rascal! let him not come hither: it is the","foul-mouthed'st rogue in England."],"id":336,"m":"Hang him, the swaggering rascal! Don't let him come up here. He's the foulest-mouthed rogue in England."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["If he swagger, let him not come here: no, by my faith; I must live","among my neighbours; I'll no swaggerers: I am in good name and fame","with the very best: shut the door; there comes no swaggerers here:","I have not lived all this while, to have swaggering now: shut the","door, I pray you."],"id":337,"m":"If he swaggers, don't let him come in here. No, by my faith; I have to live among my neighbours. I won't have swaggerers. I have a good name and reputation with the very best people. Shut the door. No swaggerers come in here. I haven't lived all this time to have swaggering now. Shut the door, please."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Dost thou hear, hostess?"],"id":338,"m":"Are you listening, hostess?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Pray ye, pacify yourself, Sir John: there comes no swaggerers here."],"id":339,"m":"Please calm yourself, Sir John. No swaggerers come in here."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Dost thou hear? it is mine ancient."],"id":340,"m":"Are you listening? He's my ensign."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Tilly-fally, Sir John, ne'er tell me: your ancient swaggerer comes","not in my doors. I was before Master Tisick, the debuty, t'other day;","and, as he said to me, 'twas no longer ago than Wednesday last,","\"I' good faith, neighbour Quickly,\" says he; Master Dumbe, our","minister, was by then; \"neighbour Quickly,\" says he, \"receive those","that are civil; for\" said he \"you are in an ill name:\" now a' said","so, I can tell whereupon; \"for,\" says he, \"you are an honest woman,","and well thought on; therefore take heed what guests you receive:","receive,\" says he, \"no swaggering companions.\" There comes none here:","you would bless you to hear what he said: no, I'll no swaggerers."],"id":341,"m":"Fiddlesticks, Sir John, don't tell me. Your ensign swaggerer isn't coming through my doors. I was up before Master Tisick, the deputy, the other day, and he said to me — it was no longer ago than last Wednesday — \"Honestly, neighbour Quickly,\" he says (Master Dumbe, our minister, was there at the time), \"neighbour Quickly,\" he says, \"only let in people who are civil, because,\" he said, \"you have a bad name.\" Now, he said that, I can tell you why. \"Because,\" he says, \"you're an honest woman and well thought of, so be careful what guests you let in. Don't let in,\" he says, \"any swaggering fellows.\" None come in here. You'd be amazed to hear what he said. No, I won't have swaggerers."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["He's no swaggerer, hostess; a tame cheater, i' faith; you may stroke","him as gently as a puppy greyhound: he'll not swagger with a Barbary","hen, if her feathers turn back in any show of resistance. Call","him up, drawer."],"id":342,"m":"He's no swaggerer, hostess. He's a tame cardsharp, honestly; you can stroke him as gently as a greyhound puppy. He wouldn't swagger at a guinea hen if her feathers stood up as though she meant to fight. Call him up, waiter."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit First Drawer.","id":343},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Cheater, call you him? I will bar no honest man my house, nor no","cheater: but I do not love swaggering, by my troth; I am the worse,","when one says swagger: feel, masters, how I shake; look you, I","warrant you."],"id":344,"m":"Cheater, you call him? I won't bar any honest man from my house, nor any escheator. But I don't like swaggering, honestly; I feel worse when anyone says \"swagger\". Feel how I'm shaking, gentlemen. Look, I promise you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["So you do, hostess."],"id":345,"m":"So you are, hostess."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Do I? yea, in very truth, do I, an 'twere an aspen leaf: I","cannot abide swaggerers."],"id":346,"m":"Am I? Yes, in very truth I am, like an aspen leaf. I can't stand swaggerers."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pistol, Bardolph, and Page.","id":347},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["God save you, Sir John!"],"id":348,"m":"God save you, Sir John!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Welcome, Ancient Pistol. Here, Pistol, I charge you with","a cup of sack: do you discharge upon mine hostess."],"id":349,"m":"Welcome, Ensign Pistol. Here, Pistol, I load you with a cup of sack: you fire it off at my hostess."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["I will discharge upon her, Sir John, with two bullets."],"id":350,"m":"I will fire at her, Sir John, with two bullets."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["She is pistol-proof, sir; you shall hardly offend her."],"id":351,"m":"She's pistol-proof, sir. You'll hardly hurt her."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Come, I'll drink no proofs nor no bullets: I'll drink no","more than will do me good, for no man's pleasure, I."],"id":352,"m":"Come on, I won't drink any proofs or bullets. I'll drink no more than does me good, for no man's pleasure, not I."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Then to you, Mistress Dorothy; I will charge you."],"id":353,"m":"Then to you, Mistress Dorothy; I'll charge you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["Charge me! I scorn you, scurvy companion. What! you poor,","base, rascally, cheating, lack-linen mate! Away, you mouldy","rogue, away!","I am meat for your master."],"id":354,"m":"Charge me! I scorn you, you scabby fellow. What! You poor, low, rascally, cheating, shirtless mate! Get away, you mouldy rogue, get away! I'm meat for your master."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["I know you, Mistress Dorothy."],"id":355,"m":"I know you, Mistress Dorothy."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["Away, you cut-purse rascal! you filthy bung, away! by this wine,","I'll thrust my knife in your mouldy chaps, an you play the saucy","cuttle with me. Away, you bottle-ale rascal! you basket-hilt stale","juggler, you! Since when, I pray you, sir? God's light, with two","points on your shoulder? much!"],"id":356,"m":"Get away, you pickpocket rascal! You filthy purse, get away! By this wine, I'll stick my knife in your mouldy jaws if you play the cheeky cutthroat with me. Get away, you flat-beer rascal! You worn-out sword-swinging fraud! Since when, may I ask, sir? God's light, with two laces on your shoulder? Really!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["God let me not live, but I will murder your ruff for this."],"id":357,"m":"God strike me dead if I don't tear your ruff to pieces for this."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["No more, Pistol; I would not have you go off here:","discharge yourself of our company, Pistol."],"id":358,"m":"No more, Pistol. I don't want you going off in here. Discharge yourself from our company, Pistol."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["No, good Captain Pistol; not here, sweet captain."],"id":359,"m":"No, good Captain Pistol; not here, sweet captain."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["Captain! thou abominable damned cheater, art thou not ashamed","to be called captain? An captains were of my mind, they would","truncheon you out, for taking their names upon you before you","have earned them. You a captain! you slave, for what? for tearing","a poor whore's ruff in a bawdy-house? He a captain! hang him,","rogue! he lives upon mouldy stewed prunes and dried cakes. A","captain! God's light, these villains will make the word as odious","as the word \"occupy;\" which was an excellent good word before it","was ill sorted: therefore captains had need look to't."],"id":360,"m":"Captain! You disgusting damned cheat, aren't you ashamed to be called captain? If captains thought like me, they'd beat you out with their batons for taking their title before you've earned it. You, a captain! You slave, for what? For tearing a poor whore's ruff in a brothel? Him, a captain! Hang him, the rogue! He lives on mouldy stewed prunes and stale cakes. A captain! God's light, these villains will make the word as disgusting as the word \"occupy\", which was an excellent good word before it got into bad company. So captains had better watch out."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Pray thee, go down, good ancient."],"id":361,"m":"Please go downstairs, good ensign."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Hark thee hither, Mistress Doll."],"id":362,"m":"Come here, Mistress Doll."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Not I: I tell thee what, Corporal Bardolph, I could tear","her: I'll be revenged of her."],"id":363,"m":"Not me. I tell you what, Corporal Bardolph, I could tear her apart. I'll get my revenge on her."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Pray thee go down."],"id":364,"m":"Please go downstairs."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["I'll see her damned first; to Pluto's damned lake, by this","hand, to the infernal deep, with Erebus and tortures vile also.","Hold hook and line, say I. Down, down, dogs! down, faitors!","Have we not Hiren here?"],"id":365,"m":"I'll see her damned first — to Pluto's damned lake, by this hand, to the bottomless pit, with Erebus and vile tortures too. Hold the hook and line, I say! Down, down, dogs! Down, traitors! Haven't we got Irene here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Good Captain Peesel, be quiet; 'tis very late, i' faith: I","beseek you now, aggravate your choler."],"id":366,"m":"Good Captain Peesel, be quiet. It's very late, honestly. I beg you now, aggravate your anger."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["These be good humours, indeed! Shall packhorses","And hollow pamper'd jades of Asia,","Which cannot go but thirty mile a-day,","Compare with Caesars, and with Cannibals,","And Trojan Greeks? nay, rather damn them with","King Cerberus; and let the welkin roar.","Shall we fall foul for toys?"],"id":367,"m":"These are fine moods indeed! Shall packhorses and hollow pampered nags of Asia, which can't go more than thirty miles a day, compare themselves with Caesars, and with Cannibals, and Trojan Greeks? No, rather damn them with King Cerberus, and let the heavens roar. Shall we fall out over trifles?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["By my troth, captain, these are very bitter words."],"id":368,"m":"Honestly, captain, these are very bitter words."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Be gone, good ancient: this will grow to a brawl anon."],"id":369,"m":"Go, good ensign. This will turn into a brawl in a moment."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Die men like dogs! give crowns like pins! Have we not Hiren","here?"],"id":370,"m":"Let men die like dogs! Give away crowns like pins! Haven't we got Irene here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["O' my word, captain, there's none such here. What the","good-year! do you think I would deny her? For God's sake, be","quiet."],"id":371,"m":"On my word, captain, there's no one like that here. What the devil! Do you think I'd deny her? For God's sake, be quiet."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Then feed, and be fat, my fair Calipolis.","Come, give's some sack.","\"Si fortune me tormente, sperato me contento.\"","Fear we broadsides? no, let the fiend give fire:","Give me some sack: and, sweetheart, lie thou there."],"id":372,"m":"Then eat and grow fat, my fair Calipolis. Come, give us some sack. \"If fortune torments me, hope contents me.\" Are we afraid of broadsides? No, let the devil fire! Give me some sack. And, sweetheart, you lie there."},{"t":"sd","text":"Laying down his sword.","id":373},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","cont":1,"lines":["Come we to full points here, and are etceteras nothing?"],"id":374,"m":"Have we come to a full stop here, and are \"et ceteras\" nothing?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Pistol, I would be quiet."],"id":375,"m":"Pistol, I'd like some quiet."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Sweet knight, I kiss thy neif: what! we have seen the seven","stars."],"id":376,"m":"Sweet knight, I kiss your fist. What! We've seen the seven stars together."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["For God's sake, thrust him down stairs: I cannot endure such a","fustian rascal."],"id":377,"m":"For God's sake, throw him downstairs. I can't stand such a ranting rascal."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Thrust him down stairs! know we not Galloway nags?"],"id":378,"m":"Throw him downstairs! Don't we know the Galloway ponies?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Quoit him down, Bardolph, like a shove-groat shilling:","nay, an a' do nothing but speak nothing, a' shall be nothing","here."],"id":379,"m":"Slide him down, Bardolph, like a coin in shove-ha'penny. No, if he does nothing but talk nonsense, he'll be nothing here."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Come, get you down stairs."],"id":380,"m":"Come on, get yourself downstairs."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["What! shall we have incision? shall we imbrue?"],"id":381,"m":"What! Shall we have cutting? Shall we draw blood?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Snatching up his sword.","id":382},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","cont":1,"lines":["Then death rock me asleep, abridge my doleful days!","Why, then, let grievous, ghastly, gaping wounds","Untwine the Sisters Three! Come, Atropos, I say!"],"id":383,"m":"Then let death rock me to sleep and cut short my miserable days! Why then, let grievous, ghastly, gaping wounds untwist the thread of the three Fates! Come, Atropos, I say!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Here's goodly stuff toward!"],"id":384,"m":"Here's fine stuff coming!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Give me my rapier, boy."],"id":385,"m":"Give me my rapier, boy."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["I pray thee, Jack, I pray thee, do not draw."],"id":386,"m":"Please, Jack, please, don't draw."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Get you down stairs."],"id":387,"m":"Get yourself downstairs."},{"t":"sd","text":"Drawing, and driving Pistol out.","id":388},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Here's a goodly tumult! I'll forswear keeping house, afore","I'll be in these tirrits and frights. So; murder, I warrant now.","Alas, alas! put up your naked weapons, put up your naked weapons."],"id":389,"m":"Here's a fine uproar! I'll give up keeping a tavern before I'll have these terrors and frights. There, murder, I'm sure of it now. Alas, alas! Put away your naked weapons, put away your naked weapons."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Pistol and Bardolph.","id":390},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["I pray thee, Jack, be quiet; the rascal's gone. Ah, you whoreson","little valiant villain, you!"],"id":391,"m":"Please, Jack, be calm. The rascal's gone. Oh, you son-of-a-whore brave little villain, you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Are you not hurt i' the groin? methought a' made a shrewd","thrust at your belly."],"id":392,"m":"Aren't you hurt in the groin? I thought he made a nasty thrust at your belly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Bardolph.","id":393},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Have you turned him out o' doors?"],"id":394,"m":"Have you thrown him out?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Yea, sir. The rascal's drunk: you have hurt him, sir, i'","the shoulder."],"id":395,"m":"Yes, sir. The rascal's drunk. You've wounded him, sir, in the shoulder."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["A rascal! to brave me!"],"id":396,"m":"A rascal! To defy me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["Ah, you sweet little rogue, you! Alas, poor ape, how thou","sweatest! come, let me wipe thy face; come on, you whoreson chops:","ah, rogue! i' faith, I love thee: thou art as valorous as Hector","of Troy, worth five of Agamemnon, and ten times better than the Nine","Worthies: ah, villain!"],"id":397,"m":"Oh, you sweet little rogue, you! Alas, poor monkey, how you're sweating! Come, let me wipe your face. Come on, you son-of-a-whore fat cheeks. Oh, rogue! Honestly, I love you. You're as brave as Hector of Troy, worth five Agamemnons, and ten times better than the Nine Worthies. Oh, villain!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["A rascally slave! I will toss the rogue in a blanket."],"id":398,"m":"A rascally slave! I'll toss the rogue in a blanket."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["Do, an thou darest for thy heart: an thou dost, I'll canvass","thee between a pair of sheets."],"id":399,"m":"Do it, if you dare for your life. If you do, I'll toss you between a pair of sheets."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Music.","id":400},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["The music is come, sir."],"id":401,"m":"The musicians have come, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Let them play. Play, sirs. Sit on my knee, Doll. A rascal","bragging slave! The rogue fled from me like quicksilver."],"id":402,"m":"Let them play. Play, gentlemen. Sit on my knee, Doll. A rascally bragging slave! The rogue ran away from me like quicksilver."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["I' faith, and thou followedst him like a church. Thou whoreson","little tidy Bartholomew boar-pig, when wilt thou leave fighting","o' days and foining o' nights, and begin to patch up thine old body","for heaven?"],"id":403,"m":"Honestly, and you chased him like a church. You son-of-a-whore neat little roast piglet from Bartholomew Fair, when are you going to stop fighting by day and thrusting by night, and start patching up your old body for heaven?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter, behind, Prince Henry and Poins, disguised as drawers.","id":404},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Peace, good Doll! do not speak like a death's-head; do","not bid me remember mine end."],"id":405,"m":"Quiet, good Doll! Don't talk like a skull; don't tell me to remember my death."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["Sirrah, what humour's the prince of?"],"id":406,"m":"Tell me, what sort of man is the Prince?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["A good shallow young fellow: 'a would have made a good","pantler; a' would ha' chipped bread well."],"id":407,"m":"A good shallow young fellow. He'd have made a good pantry servant; he'd have cut the crusts off bread well."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["They say Poins has a good wit."],"id":408,"m":"They say Poins has a good wit."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["He a good wit! hang him, baboon! his wit's as thick as","Tewksbury mustard; there's no more conceit in him than is in a","mallet."],"id":409,"m":"Him, a good wit! Hang him, the baboon! His wit's as thick as Tewkesbury mustard. There's no more imagination in him than in a mallet."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["Why does the prince love him so, then?"],"id":410,"m":"Then why does the Prince love him so much?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Because their legs are both of a bigness, and a' plays at quoits","well, and eats conger and fennel, and drinks off candles' ends for","flap-dragons, and rides the wild-mare with the boys, and jumps upon","joined-stools, and swears with a good grace, and wears his boots very","smooth, like unto the sign of the leg, and breeds no bate with telling","of discreet stories; and such other gambol faculties a' has, that show","a weak mind and an able body, for the which the prince admits him: for","the prince himself is such another; the weight of a hair will turn the","scales between their avoirdupois."],"id":411,"m":"Because their legs are both the same size, and he plays quoits well, and eats eel and fennel, and drinks off burning candle-ends in flaming brandy, and rides piggyback with the boys, and jumps over stools, and swears elegantly, and wears his boots very smooth, like the sign over a bootmaker's shop, and causes no trouble by telling tactful stories; and he has other such playful skills that show a weak mind and a fit body, and that's why the Prince puts up with him. Because the Prince is just the same: a hair's weight would tip the scales between them."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Would not this nave of a wheel have his ears cut off?"],"id":412,"m":"Shouldn't this hub of a wheel have his ears cut off?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Let's beat him before his whore."],"id":413,"m":"Let's beat him in front of his whore."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Look, whether the withered elder hath not his poll clawed","like a parrot."],"id":414,"m":"Look at the withered old tree having his head scratched like a parrot."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Is it not strange that desire should so many years outlive","performance?"],"id":415,"m":"Isn't it strange that desire should outlive performance by so many years?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Kiss me, Doll."],"id":416,"m":"Kiss me, Doll."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Saturn and Venus this year in conjunction! what says the","almanac to that?"],"id":417,"m":"Saturn and Venus in conjunction this year! What does the almanac say about that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["And, look, whether the fiery Trigon, his man, be not lisping","to his master's old tables, his note-book, his counsel-keeper."],"id":418,"m":"And look how his man, the fiery triangle, is whispering to his master's old notebook, his diary, his confidante."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Thou dost give me flattering busses."],"id":419,"m":"You're giving me flattering kisses."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["By my troth, I kiss thee with a most constant heart."],"id":420,"m":"Honestly, I kiss you with a completely faithful heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I am old, I am old."],"id":421,"m":"I'm old, I'm old."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["I love thee better than I love e'er a scurvy young boy of","them all."],"id":422,"m":"I love you better than any scabby young boy of them all."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What stuff wilt have a kirtle of? I shall receive money o'","Thursday: shalt have a cap to-morrow. A merry song, come: it","grows late; we'll to bed. Thou'lt forget me when I am gone."],"id":423,"m":"What material do you want a skirt made of? I'll get some money on Thursday; you'll have a cap tomorrow. A merry song, come on. It's getting late; we'll go to bed. You'll forget me when I've gone."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["By my troth, thou'lt set me a-weeping, an thou sayest so:","prove that ever I dress myself handsome till thy return: well,","hearken at the end."],"id":424,"m":"Honestly, you'll make me cry if you say that. See if I ever dress myself up nicely before you come back. Well, wait and see how it ends."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Some sack, Francis."],"id":425,"m":"Some sack, Francis."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL AND POINS","lines":["Anon, anon, sir."],"id":426,"m":"Coming, coming, sir."},{"t":"sd","text":"Coming forward.","id":427},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Ha! a bastard son of the king's? And art thou not Poins","his brother?"],"id":428,"m":"Ha! A bastard son of the King's? And aren't you Poins's brother?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Why, thou globe of sinful continents, what a life dost thou lead!"],"id":429,"m":"Why, you globe of sinful continents, what a life you lead!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["A better than thou: I am a gentleman; thou art a drawer."],"id":430,"m":"A better one than yours. I'm a gentleman; you're a waiter."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Very true, sir; and I come to draw you out by the ears."],"id":431,"m":"Very true, sir, and I've come to draw you out by the ears."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["O, the Lord preserve thy grace! by my troth, welcome to","London. Now, the Lord bless that sweet face of thine! O Jesu,","are you come from Wales?"],"id":432,"m":"Oh, the Lord preserve your grace! Honestly, welcome to London. Now the Lord bless that sweet face of yours! Oh Jesus, have you come from Wales?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Thou whoreson mad compound of majesty, by this light","flesh and corrupt blood, thou art welcome."],"id":433,"m":"You son-of-a-whore mad mixture of majesty, by this light — flesh and corrupt blood — you're welcome."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["How, you fat fool! I scorn you."],"id":434,"m":"What, you fat fool! I scorn you."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["My lord, he will drive you out of your revenge and turn all","to a merriment, if you take not the heat."],"id":435,"m":"My lord, he'll talk you out of your revenge and turn it all into a joke if you don't strike while the iron's hot."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["You whoreson candle-mine, you, how vilely did you speak of","me even now before this honest, virtuous, civil gentlewoman!"],"id":436,"m":"You son-of-a-whore tallow mine, you, how vilely you talked about me just now in front of this honest, virtuous, respectable gentlewoman!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["God's blessing of your good heart! and so she is, by my troth."],"id":437,"m":"God bless your good heart! And so she is, honestly."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Didst thou hear me?"],"id":438,"m":"Did you hear me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Yea, and you knew me, as you did when you ran away by","Gad's-hill: you knew I was at your back, and spoke it on purpose","to try my patience."],"id":439,"m":"Yes, and you knew it was me, just as you did when you ran away at Gad's Hill. You knew I was behind you and said it on purpose to test my patience."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["No, no, no; not so; I did not think thou wast within hearing."],"id":440,"m":"No, no, no, not so. I didn't think you were within earshot."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I shall drive you then to confess the wilful abuse; and then I","know how to handle you."],"id":441,"m":"Then I'll force you to confess the deliberate insult, and then I'll know how to deal with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["No abuse, Hal, o' mine honour; no abuse."],"id":442,"m":"No insult, Hal, on my honour, no insult."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Not to dispraise me, and call me pantler and bread-chipper and I","know not what!"],"id":443,"m":"Not to run me down, and call me a pantry boy and a bread-cutter and I don't know what!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["No abuse, Hal."],"id":444,"m":"No insult, Hal."},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["No abuse!"],"id":445,"m":"No insult!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["No abuse, Ned, i' the world; honest Ned, none. I dispraised him before","the wicked, that the wicked might not fall in love with him; in which","doing, I have done the part of a careful friend and a true subject,","and thy father is to give me thanks for it. No abuse, Hal: none,","Ned, none: no, faith, boys, none."],"id":446,"m":"No insult in the world, Ned; honest Ned, none. I ran him down in front of the wicked so that the wicked wouldn't fall in love with him. In doing that I've acted as a caring friend and a loyal subject, and your father ought to thank me for it. No insult, Hal; none, Ned, none. No, honestly, boys, none."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["See now, whether pure fear and entire cowardice doth not make thee","wrong this virtuous gentlewoman to close with us. Is she of the wicked?","is thine hostess here of the wicked? or is thy boy of the wicked?","or honest Bardolph, whose zeal burns in his nose, of the wicked?"],"id":447,"m":"Now see whether pure fear and total cowardice don't make you wrong this virtuous gentlewoman to make it up with us. Is she one of the wicked? Is your hostess here one of the wicked? Or is your boy one of the wicked? Or honest Bardolph, whose zeal burns in his nose, one of the wicked?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POINS","lines":["Answer, thou dead elm, answer."],"id":448,"m":"Answer, you dead elm tree, answer."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["The fiend hath pricked down Bardolph irrecoverable; and his","face is Lucifer's privy-kitchen, where he doth nothing but roast","malt-worms.","For the boy, there is a good angel about him; but the devil","outbids him too."],"id":449,"m":"The devil has marked Bardolph down as past saving, and his face is Lucifer's private kitchen, where he does nothing but roast drunkards. As for the boy, he has a good angel with him, but the devil outbids him too."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["For the women?"],"id":450,"m":"And the women?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["For one of them, she is in hell already, and burns poor souls.","For the other, I owe her money; and whether she be damned for","that, I know not."],"id":451,"m":"One of them is in hell already, and burns poor souls with disease. As for the other, I owe her money, and whether she's damned for that I don't know."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["No, I warrant you."],"id":452,"m":"No, I promise you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["No, I think thou art not; I think thou art quit for that. Marry, there","is another indictment upon thee, for suffering flesh to be eaten in","thy house, contrary to the law; for the which I think thou wilt howl."],"id":453,"m":"No, I don't think you are; I think you're let off for that. But there's another charge against you, for allowing meat to be eaten in your house during Lent, against the law. I think you'll howl for that."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["All victuallers do so: what's a joint of mutton or two in a","whole Lent?"],"id":454,"m":"All innkeepers do it. What's a joint of mutton or two in a whole Lent?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["You, gentlewoman,—"],"id":455,"m":"You, gentlewoman —"},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["What says your grace?"],"id":456,"m":"What does your grace say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["His grace says that which his flesh rebels against."],"id":457,"m":"His grace says what his flesh rebels against."},{"t":"sd","text":"Knocking within.","id":458},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Who knocks so loud at door? Look to the door there, Francis."],"id":459,"m":"Who's knocking so loud at the door? See to the door there, Francis."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Peto.","id":460},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Peto, how now! what news?"],"id":461,"m":"Peto, what's up? What news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETO","lines":["The king your father is at Westminster;","And there are twenty weak and wearied posts","Come from the north: and, as I came along,","I met and overtook a dozen captains,","Bare-headed, sweating, knocking at the taverns,","And asking every one for Sir John Falstaff."],"id":462,"m":"The King your father is at Westminster, and twenty weak and exhausted messengers have come from the north. And on my way here I met and passed a dozen captains, bare-headed and sweating, knocking at the taverns and asking everyone for Sir John Falstaff."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["By heaven, Poins, I feel me much to blame,","So idly to profane the precious time,","When tempest of commotion, like the south","Borne with black vapour, doth begin to melt","And drop upon our bare unarmed heads.","Give me my sword and cloak. Falstaff, good night."],"id":463,"m":"By heaven, Poins, I feel very much to blame for wasting precious time so idly, when a storm of rebellion, like the south wind loaded with black cloud, is starting to melt and pour down on our bare, unarmed heads. Give me my sword and cloak. Falstaff, good night."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Prince, Poins, Peto, and Bardolph.","id":464},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Now comes in the sweetest morsel of the night, and we must","hence, and leave it unpicked.","[Knocking within] More knocking at the door!"],"id":465,"m":"Now comes the sweetest morsel of the night, and we have to go and leave it untasted. (Knocking within) More knocking at the door!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Bardolph.","id":466},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","cont":1,"lines":["How now! what's the matter?"],"id":467,"m":"What now? What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["You must away to court, sir, presently;","A dozen captains stay at door for you."],"id":468,"m":"You have to go to court at once, sir. A dozen captains are waiting at the door for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["[To the Page] Pay the musicians, sirrah. Farewell, hostess; farewell, Doll.","You see, my good wenches, how men of merit are sought after:","the undeserver may sleep, when the man of action is called on.","Farewell, good wenches: if I be not sent away post, I will see","you again ere I go."],"id":469,"m":"(To the Page) Pay the musicians, boy. Farewell, hostess; farewell, Doll. You see, my good girls, how men of merit are sought after. The undeserving can sleep, while the man of action gets called. Farewell, good girls. If I'm not sent off at once, I'll see you again before I go."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["I cannot speak; if my heart be not ready to burst,—well, sweet","Jack, have a care of thyself."],"id":470,"m":"I can't speak. If my heart isn't ready to burst — well, sweet Jack, take care of yourself."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Farewell, farewell."],"id":471,"m":"Farewell, farewell."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Falstaff and Bardolph.","id":472},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Well, fare thee well: I have known thee these twenty-nine years,","come peascod-time; but an honester and truer-hearted man,——","well, fare thee well."],"id":473,"m":"Well, goodbye to you. I've known you twenty-nine years, come pea-pod time, but a more honest and truer-hearted man — well, goodbye to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["[Within] Mistress Tearsheet!"],"id":474,"m":"(Offstage) Mistress Tearsheet!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["What's the matter?"],"id":475,"m":"What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["[Within] Bid Mistress Tearsheet come to my master."],"id":476,"m":"(Offstage) Tell Mistress Tearsheet to come to my master."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["O, run, Doll, run; run, good Doll: come. [She comes blubbered]","Yea, will you come, Doll?"],"id":477,"m":"Oh, run, Doll, run. Run, good Doll. Come on. (She comes in, her face swollen with tears) Yes, will you come, Doll?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":478},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"Westminster. The palace.","id":479},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the King in his nightgown, with a Page.","id":480},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Go call the Earls of Surrey and of Warwick;","But, ere they come, bid them o'er-read these letters,","And well consider of them: make good speed."],"id":481,"m":"Go and call the Earls of Surrey and Warwick. But before they come, tell them to read these letters through and think them over carefully. Be quick."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Page.","id":482},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","cont":1,"lines":["How many thousands of my poorest subjects","Are at this hour asleep! O sleep, O gentle sleep,","Nature's soft nurse, how have I frighted thee,","That thou no more wilt weigh my eyelids down","And steep my senses in forgetfulness?","Why rather, sleep, liest thou in smoky cribs,","Upon uneasy pallets stretching thee","And hush'd with buzzing night-flies to thy slumber","Than in the perfumed chambers of the great,","Under the canopies of costly state,","And lull'd with sound of sweetest melody?","O thou dull god, why liest thou with the vile","In loathsome beds, and leavest the kingly couch","A watch-case or a common 'larum-bell?","Wilt thou upon the high and giddy mast","Seal up the ship-boy's eyes, and rock his brains","In cradle of the rude imperious surge","And in the visitation of the winds,","Who take the ruffian billows by the top,","Curling their monstrous heads and hanging them","With deafening clamour in the slippery clouds,","That, with the hurly, death itself awakes?","Canst thou, O partial sleep, give thy repose","To the wet sea-boy in an hour so rude;","And in the calmest and most stillest night,","With all appliances and means to boot,","Deny it to a king? Then happy low, lie down!","Uneasy lies the head that wears a crown."],"id":483,"m":"How many thousands of my poorest subjects are asleep at this hour! Oh sleep, oh gentle sleep, nature's soft nurse, how have I frightened you, that you'll no longer weigh my eyelids down and soak my senses in forgetfulness? Why, sleep, do you prefer to lie in smoky hovels, stretched out on uncomfortable straw beds and lulled to sleep by buzzing night-flies, rather than in the perfumed rooms of great men, under expensive canopies, lulled by the sound of the sweetest music? Oh, you dull god, why do you lie with the lowly in their disgusting beds and leave the king's bed like a sentry box or an alarm bell? Will you seal the ship's boy's eyes on top of the high, swaying mast, and rock his brain in the cradle of the rough, overpowering sea, in the onslaught of the winds, which seize the violent waves by the crests, curling their monstrous heads and hanging them in the slippery clouds with such a deafening roar that the uproar wakes death itself? Can you, unfair sleep, give your rest to the soaked ship's boy in such a violent hour, and in the calmest, stillest night, with every comfort and aid besides, deny it to a king? Then lie down, you happy humble people! The head that wears a crown lies uneasy."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Warwick and Surrey.","id":484},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Many good morrows to your majesty!"],"id":485,"m":"Many good mornings to your Majesty!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Is it good morrow, lords?"],"id":486,"m":"Is it morning, lords?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["'Tis one o'clock, and past."],"id":487,"m":"It's past one o'clock."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Why then, good morrow to you all, my lords.","Have you read o'er the letters that I sent you?"],"id":488,"m":"Well then, good morning to you all, my lords. Have you read through the letters I sent you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["We have, my liege."],"id":489,"m":"We have, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Then you perceive the body of our kingdom","How foul it is; what rank diseases grow,","And with what danger, near the heart of it."],"id":490,"m":"Then you see how diseased the body of our kingdom is, what rotten illnesses are growing in it, and how dangerously close to its heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["It is but as a body yet distemper'd;","Which to his former strength may be restored","With good advice and little medicine:","My Lord Northumberland will soon be cool'd."],"id":491,"m":"It's only like a body that's out of sorts, which can be restored to its former strength with good advice and a little medicine. My Lord Northumberland will soon be cooled down."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["O God! that one might read the book of fate,","And see the revolution of the times","Make mountains level, and the continent,","Weary of solid firmness, melt itself","Into the sea! and, other times, to see","The beachy girdle of the ocean","Too wide for Neptune's hips; how chances mock,","And changes fill the cup of alteration","With divers liquors! O, if this were seen,","The happiest youth, viewing his progress through,","What perils past, what crosses to ensue,","Would shut the book, and sit him down and die.","'Tis not ten years gone","Since Richard and Northumberland, great friends,","Did feast together, and in two years after","Were they at wars: it is but eight years since","This Percy was the man nearest my soul,","Who like a brother toil'd in my affairs","And laid his love and life under my foot,","Yea, for my sake, even to the eyes of Richard","Gave him defiance. But which of you was by—","You, cousin Nevil, as I may remember—"],"id":492,"m":"Oh God! If only one could read the book of fate and see how the turning of the times makes mountains flat, and the dry land, tired of being solid, melt into the sea; and at other times see the sandy belt of the shore grow too wide for the sea-god's hips; see how chance mocks us, and how changes fill the cup of alteration with different drinks! Oh, if this could be seen, the happiest young man, looking through the course of his life, what dangers past, what troubles still to come, would shut the book, sit down and die. It's not ten years ago that Richard and Northumberland were great friends and feasted together, and two years after that they were at war. It's only eight years since this Percy was the man closest to my heart, who worked like a brother on my behalf and laid his love and life at my feet — yes, and for my sake even defied Richard to his face. But which of you was there — you, cousin Nevil, as I remember —"},{"t":"sd","text":"To Warwick.","id":493},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","cont":1,"lines":["When Richard, with his eye brimful of tears,","Then check'd and rated by Northumberland,","Did speak these words, now proved a prophecy?","\"Northumberland, thou ladder by the which","My cousin Bolingbroke ascends my throne;\"","Though then, God knows, I had no such intent,","But that necessity so bow'd the state","That I and greatness were compell'd to kiss:","\"The time shall come,\" thus did he follow it,","\"The time will come, that foul sin, gathering head,","Shall break into corruption:\" so went on,","Foretelling this same time's condition","And the division of our amity."],"id":494,"m":"when Richard, his eyes brimming with tears, being rebuked and scolded by Northumberland, spoke these words, which have now proved a prophecy? \"Northumberland, you ladder by which my cousin Bolingbroke climbs to my throne\" — though at that time, God knows, I had no such intention, except that necessity so bent the state that greatness and I were forced to kiss. \"The time will come,\" he went on, \"the time will come when foul sin, coming to a head, will burst into rottenness.\" And so he went on, foretelling the state of things now and the breaking of our friendship."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["There is a history in all men's lives,","Figuring the natures of the times deceased;","The which observed, a man may prophesy,","With a near aim, of the main chance of things","As yet not come to life, who in their seeds","And weak beginning lie intreasured.","Such things become the hatch and brood of time;","And by the necessary form of this","King Richard might create a perfect guess","That great Northumberland, then false to him,","Would of that seed grow to a greater falseness;","Which should not find a ground to root upon,","Unless on you."],"id":495,"m":"There's a pattern in every man's life that reflects the nature of times gone by. By observing it, a man can predict fairly accurately the likely course of things that haven't yet come to life, which lie stored up in their seeds and weak beginnings. Such things are hatched and bred by time. And from this inevitable pattern King Richard could make a perfect guess that great Northumberland, false to him then, would grow from that seed to a greater falseness, which would find no ground to take root in except you."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Are these things then necessities?","Then let us meet them like necessities:","And that same word even now cries out on us:","They say the bishop and Northumberland","Are fifty thousand strong."],"id":496,"m":"Are these things necessities, then? Then let's face them as necessities. And that same word is calling out to us now. They say the Bishop and Northumberland are fifty thousand strong."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["It cannot be, my lord;","Rumour doth double, like the voice and echo,","The numbers of the fear'd. Please it your grace","To go to bed. Upon my soul, my lord,","The powers that you already have sent forth","Shall bring this prize in very easily.","To comfort you the more, I have received","A certain instance that Glendower is dead.","Your majesty hath been this fortnight ill,","And these unseason'd hours perforce must add","Unto your sickness."],"id":497,"m":"That can't be so, my lord. Rumour doubles the numbers of the people we fear, like a voice and its echo. Please, your grace, go to bed. Upon my soul, my lord, the forces you've already sent out will win this prize very easily. To comfort you further, I've received reliable news that Glendower is dead. Your Majesty has been ill this fortnight, and these late hours are bound to make your sickness worse."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I will take your counsel:","And were these inward wars once out of hand,","We would, dear lords, unto the Holy Land."],"id":498,"m":"I'll take your advice. And once these civil wars were dealt with, dear lords, we would go to the Holy Land."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":499},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"Gloucestershire. Before Justice Shallow's house.","id":500},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Shallow and Silence, meeting; Mouldy, Shadow, Wart, Feeble, Bullcalf, a Servant or two with them.","id":501},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Come on, come on, come on, sir; give me your hand, sir,","give me your hand, sir: an early stirrer, by the rood! And how","doth my good cousin Silence?"],"id":502,"m":"Come on, come on, come on, sir. Give me your hand, sir, give me your hand, sir. An early riser, by the cross! And how's my good cousin Silence?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["Good morrow, good cousin Shallow."],"id":503,"m":"Good morning, good cousin Shallow."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["And how doth my cousin, your bedfellow? and your fairest","daughter and mine, my god-daughter Ellen?"],"id":504,"m":"And how's my cousin, your wife? And your lovely daughter and mine, my god-daughter Ellen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["Alas, a black ousel, cousin Shallow!"],"id":505,"m":"Alas, as dark as a blackbird, cousin Shallow!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["By yea and nay, sir, I dare say my cousin William is become","a good scholar: he is at Oxford still, is he not?"],"id":506,"m":"By yes and no, sir, I'd say my cousin William has become a good scholar. He's still at Oxford, isn't he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["Indeed, sir, to my cost."],"id":507,"m":"He is, sir, at my expense."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["A' must, then, to the inns o' court shortly. I was once of","Clement's Inn, where I think they will talk of mad Shallow yet."],"id":508,"m":"Then he'll have to go to the law schools in London soon. I was at Clement's Inn once, where I think they still talk about mad Shallow."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["You were called \"lusty Shallow\" then, cousin."],"id":509,"m":"You were called \"lusty Shallow\" then, cousin."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["By the mass, I was called any thing; and I would have done any thing","indeed too, and roundly too. There was I, and little John Doit of","Staffordshire, and black George Barnes, and Francis Pickbone, and","Will Squele, a Cotswold man; you had not four such swinge-bucklers in","all the inns o' court again: and I may say to you, we knew where the","bona-robas were and had the best of them all at commandment. Then was","Jack Falstaff, now Sir John, boy, and page to Thomas Mowbray, Duke of","Norfolk."],"id":510,"m":"By the mass, I was called anything, and I'd have done anything too, and boldly. There was me, and little John Doit of Staffordshire, and black George Barnes, and Francis Pickbone, and Will Squele, a Cotswold man; you'd never find four such swashbucklers in all the law schools again. And I can tell you, we knew where the best whores were and had the pick of them at our command. Jack Falstaff, now Sir John, was a boy then, page to Thomas Mowbray, Duke of Norfolk."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["This Sir John, cousin, that comes hither anon about soldiers?"],"id":511,"m":"This Sir John, cousin, who's coming here soon about soldiers?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["The same Sir John, the very same. I see him break Skogan's head at the","court-gate, when a' was a crack not thus high: and the very same","day did I fight with one Sampson Stockfish, a fruiterer, behind","Gray's Inn.","Jesu, Jesu, the mad days that I have spent! and to see how many of my","old acquaintance are dead!"],"id":512,"m":"The same Sir John, the very same. I saw him crack Skogan's head at the court gate when he was a little lad this high. And that very same day I fought a fruit-seller called Sampson Stockfish behind Gray's Inn. Jesus, Jesus, the mad days I've spent! And to see how many of my old friends are dead!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["We shall all follow, cousin."],"id":513,"m":"We'll all follow, cousin."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Certain, 'tis certain; very sure, very sure: death, as the Psalmist","saith, is certain to all; all shall die. How a good yoke of bullocks at","Stamford fair?"],"id":514,"m":"Certainly, it's certain; very sure, very sure. Death, as the Psalmist says, is certain for everyone; everyone will die. How much is a good pair of bullocks at Stamford fair?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["By my troth, I was not there."],"id":515,"m":"Honestly, I wasn't there."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Death is certain. Is old Double of your town living yet?"],"id":516,"m":"Death is certain. Is old Double from your town still alive?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["Dead, sir."],"id":517,"m":"Dead, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Jesu, Jesu, dead! a' drew a good bow; and dead! a' shot a fine shoot:","John a Gaunt loved him well, and betted much money on his head.","Dead! a' would have clapped i' the clout at twelve score; and carried","you a forehand shaft a fourteen and fourteen and a half, that it","would have done a man's heart good to see. How a score of ewes now?"],"id":518,"m":"Jesus, Jesus, dead! He drew a good bow, and dead! He shot a fine shot. John of Gaunt was very fond of him and bet a lot of money on him. Dead! He'd hit the bull's-eye at two hundred and forty yards, and shoot a straight arrow two hundred and eighty or two hundred and ninety yards, so it would do your heart good to see it. How much is a score of ewes now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["Thereafter as they be: a score of good ewes may be worth ten","pounds."],"id":519,"m":"That depends on what they're like. A score of good ewes might be worth ten pounds."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["And is old Double dead?"],"id":520,"m":"And is old Double dead?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["Here come two of Sir John Falstaffs men, as I think."],"id":521,"m":"Here come two of Sir John Falstaff's men, I think."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Bardolph, and one with him.","id":522},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Good morrow, honest gentlemen: I beseech you, which is justice","Shallow?"],"id":523,"m":"Good morning, honest gentlemen. Please, which of you is Justice Shallow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["I am Robert Shallow, sir; a poor esquire of this county, and one","of the king's justices of the peace: what is your good pleasure","with me?"],"id":524,"m":"I'm Robert Shallow, sir, a poor gentleman of this county and one of the King's justices of the peace. What can I do for you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["My captain, sir, commends him to you; my captain, Sir John","Falstaff, a tall gentleman, by heaven, and a most gallant leader."],"id":525,"m":"My captain, sir, sends you his regards: my captain, Sir John Falstaff, a fine brave gentleman, by heaven, and a most gallant leader."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["He greets me well, sir. I knew him a good backsword man. How","doth the good knight? may I ask how my lady his wife doth?"],"id":526,"m":"That's kind of him, sir. I knew him as a good swordsman with the broadsword. How is the good knight? May I ask how my lady his wife is?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Sir, pardon; a soldier is better accommodated than with a wife."],"id":527,"m":"Excuse me, sir, a soldier is better accommodated than with a wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["It is well said, in faith, sir; and it is well said indeed too.","Better accommodated! it is good; yea, indeed, is it: good phrases are","surely, and ever were, very commendable. Accommodated! it comes of","\"accommodo:\" very good; a good phrase."],"id":528,"m":"Well said, sir, honestly; very well said indeed. \"Better accommodated\"! That's good; yes, it is indeed. Good phrases are certainly, and always were, very admirable. \"Accommodated\"! It comes from \"accommodo\". Very good; a good phrase."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Pardon me, sir; I have heard the word. Phrase call you it? By this","day, I know not the phrase; but I will maintain the word with my sword","to be a soldier-like word, and a word of exceeding good command, by","heaven.","Accommodated; that is, when a man is, as they say, accommodated; or","when a man is, being, whereby a' may be thought to be accommodated;","which is an excellent thing."],"id":529,"m":"Pardon me, sir, I've heard the word. Do you call it a phrase? By this day, I don't know about phrases, but I'll defend the word with my sword as a soldierly word, a word of extremely good command, by heaven. \"Accommodated\": that is, when a man is, as they say, accommodated; or when a man is, being — whereby he may be thought to be accommodated — which is an excellent thing."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["It is very just."],"id":530,"m":"That's very true."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff.","id":531},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","cont":1,"lines":["Look, here comes good Sir John. Give me your good hand, give me your","worship's good hand: by my troth, you like well and bear your years","very well: welcome, good Sir John."],"id":532,"m":"Look, here comes good Sir John. Give me your good hand, give me your worship's good hand. Honestly, you look well and carry your years very well. Welcome, good Sir John."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I am glad to see you well, good Master Robert Shallow: Master","Surecard, as I think?"],"id":533,"m":"I'm glad to see you well, good Master Robert Shallow. Master Surecard, I think?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["No, Sir John; it is my cousin Silence, in commission with me."],"id":534,"m":"No, Sir John, it's my cousin Silence, who's a magistrate with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Good Master Silence, it well befits you should be of the peace."],"id":535,"m":"Good Master Silence, it's very fitting that you should be a justice of the peace."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["Your good worship is welcome."],"id":536,"m":"Your good worship is welcome."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Fie! this is hot weather, gentlemen. Have you provided me here","half a dozen sufficient men?"],"id":537,"m":"Phew! This is hot weather, gentlemen. Have you got me half a dozen suitable men here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Marry, have we, sir. Will you sit?"],"id":538,"m":"Indeed we have, sir. Will you sit down?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Let me see them, I beseech you."],"id":539,"m":"Let me see them, please."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Where's the roll? where's the roll? where's the roll? Let me see,","let me see, let me see.","So, so, so, so, so, so, so: yea, marry, sir: Ralph Mouldy!","Let them appear as I call; let them do so, let them do so.","Let me see; where is Mouldy?"],"id":540,"m":"Where's the list? Where's the list? Where's the list? Let me see, let me see, let me see. So, so, so, so, so, so, so. Yes, indeed, sir. Ralph Mouldy! Let them come forward as I call them; let them do that, let them do that. Let me see, where's Mouldy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOULDY","lines":["Here, an't please you."],"id":541,"m":"Here, if it please you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["What think you, Sir John? a good-limbed fellow; young, strong,","and of good friends."],"id":542,"m":"What do you think, Sir John? A well-built fellow: young, strong, and from a good family."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Is thy name Mouldy?"],"id":543,"m":"Is your name Mouldy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOULDY","lines":["Yea, an't please you."],"id":544,"m":"Yes, if it please you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["'Tis the more time thou wert used."],"id":545,"m":"Then it's high time you were used."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Ha, ha, ha! most excellent, i' faith! things that are mouldy lack use:","very singular good! in faith, well said, Sir John, very well said."],"id":546,"m":"Ha, ha, ha! Most excellent, honestly! Things that are mouldy haven't been used. Exceptionally good! Honestly, well said, Sir John, very well said."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Prick him."],"id":547,"m":"Tick him."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOULDY","lines":["I was prick'd well enough before, an you could have let me alone:","my old dame will be undone now for one to do her husbandry and her","drudgery: you need not to have pricked me; there are other men fitter","to go out than I."],"id":548,"m":"I was pricked often enough before, if you'd just left me alone. My old woman will be ruined now, with no one to do her farm work and her chores. You didn't need to pick me; there are other men better suited to go than me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Go to: peace, Mouldy; you shall go. Mouldy, it is time you were spent."],"id":549,"m":"Come on, quiet, Mouldy. You'll go. Mouldy, it's time you were used up."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOULDY","lines":["Spent!"],"id":550,"m":"Used up!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Peace, fellow, peace; stand aside: know you where you are? For","the other, Sir John: let me see: Simon Shadow!"],"id":551,"m":"Quiet, fellow, quiet. Stand aside. Do you know where you are? Now for the next one, Sir John. Let me see: Simon Shadow!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Yea, marry, let me have him to sit under: he's like to be a","cold soldier."],"id":552,"m":"Yes, indeed, let me have him to sit under. He's likely to be a cool soldier."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Where's Shadow?"],"id":553,"m":"Where's Shadow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHADOW","lines":["Here, sir."],"id":554,"m":"Here, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Shadow, whose son art thou?"],"id":555,"m":"Shadow, whose son are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHADOW","lines":["My mother's son, sir."],"id":556,"m":"My mother's son, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Thy mother's son! like enough; and thy father's shadow: so the son of","the female is the shadow of the male: it is often so indeed; but","much of the father's substance!"],"id":557,"m":"Your mother's son! Likely enough, and your father's shadow. So the son of the female is the shadow of the male. That's often true indeed, but not much of the father's substance!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Do you like him, Sir John?"],"id":558,"m":"Do you like him, Sir John?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Shadow will serve for summer; prick him; for we have a number of","shadows to fill up the muster-book."],"id":559,"m":"Shadow will do for summer. Tick him, because we have a number of shadows to fill up the roll."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Thomas Wart!"],"id":560,"m":"Thomas Wart!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Where's he?"],"id":561,"m":"Where is he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WART","lines":["Here, sir."],"id":562,"m":"Here, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Is thy name Wart?"],"id":563,"m":"Is your name Wart?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WART","lines":["Yea, sir."],"id":564,"m":"Yes, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Thou art a very ragged wart."],"id":565,"m":"You're a very ragged wart."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Shall I prick him down, Sir John?"],"id":566,"m":"Shall I tick him, Sir John?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["It were superfluous; for his apparel is built upon his back and","the whole frame stands upon pins: prick him no more."],"id":567,"m":"That would be unnecessary, because his clothes are built on his back and the whole structure is held together with pins. Don't prick him any more."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Ha, ha, ha! you can do it, sir; you can do it: I commend you","well.","Francis Feeble!"],"id":568,"m":"Ha, ha, ha! You can do it, sir, you can do it. I congratulate you. Francis Feeble!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FEEBLE","lines":["Here, sir."],"id":569,"m":"Here, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What trade art thou, Feeble?"],"id":570,"m":"What's your trade, Feeble?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FEEBLE","lines":["A woman's tailor, sir."],"id":571,"m":"A ladies' tailor, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Shall I prick him, sir?"],"id":572,"m":"Shall I tick him, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["You may: but if he had been a man's tailor, he'ld ha' prick'd you.","Wilt thou make as many holes in an enemy's battle as thou hast done in","a woman's petticoat?"],"id":573,"m":"You may. But if he'd been a men's tailor, he'd have pricked you. Will you make as many holes in an enemy army as you've made in a woman's petticoat?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FEEBLE","lines":["I will do my good will, sir; you can have no more."],"id":574,"m":"I'll do my best, sir. You can't ask for more."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Well said, good woman's tailor! well said, courageous Feeble! thou wilt","be as valiant as the wrathful dove or most magnanimous mouse.","Prick the woman's tailor: well, Master Shallow, deep, Master Shallow."],"id":575,"m":"Well said, good ladies' tailor! Well said, courageous Feeble! You'll be as brave as the furious dove or the most noble mouse. Tick the ladies' tailor — firmly, Master Shallow, deep, Master Shallow."},{"t":"sp","who":"FEEBLE","lines":["I would Wart might have gone, sir."],"id":576,"m":"I wish Wart could have gone, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I would thou wert a man's tailor, that thou mightst mend him and make","him fit to go. I cannot put him to a private soldier that is the leader","of so many thousands; let that suffice, most forcible Feeble."],"id":577,"m":"I wish you were a men's tailor, so you could mend him and make him fit to go. I can't make a common soldier of a man who leads so many thousands of lice. Let that be enough, most forcible Feeble."},{"t":"sp","who":"FEEBLE","lines":["It shall suffice, sir."],"id":578,"m":"It will be enough, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I am bound to thee, reverend Feeble. Who is next?"],"id":579,"m":"I'm obliged to you, reverend Feeble. Who's next?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Peter Bullcalf o' th' green!"],"id":580,"m":"Peter Bullcalf from the village green!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Yea, marry, let's see Bullcalf."],"id":581,"m":"Yes, indeed, let's see Bullcalf."},{"t":"sp","who":"BULLCALF","lines":["Here, sir."],"id":582,"m":"Here, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["'Fore God, a likely fellow! Come, prick me Bullcalf till he roar","again."],"id":583,"m":"By God, a likely fellow! Come, tick Bullcalf until he bellows."},{"t":"sp","who":"BULLCALF","lines":["O Lord! good my lord captain,—"],"id":584,"m":"Oh Lord! My good lord captain —"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What, dost thou roar before thou art prick'd?"],"id":585,"m":"What, are you bellowing before you're pricked?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BULLCALF","lines":["O Lord, sir! I am a diseased man."],"id":586,"m":"Oh Lord, sir! I'm a sick man."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What disease hast thou?"],"id":587,"m":"What disease do you have?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BULLCALF","lines":["A whoreson cold, sir, a cough, sir, which I caught with ringing","in the king's affairs upon his coronation-day, sir."],"id":588,"m":"A damned cold, sir, a cough, sir, which I caught bell-ringing in the King's service on his coronation day, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Come, thou shalt go to the wars in a gown; we will have away thy cold;","and I will take such order that thy friends shall ring for thee.","Is here all?"],"id":589,"m":"Come, you'll go to the wars in a dressing gown. We'll get rid of your cold, and I'll make sure your friends ring the bells for your funeral. Is this everyone?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Here is two more called than your number; you must have but four here,","sir; and so, I pray you, go in with me to dinner."],"id":590,"m":"Two more have been called than your quota. You're only to have four here, sir. And so, please, come in and have dinner with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Come, I will go drink with you, but I cannot tarry dinner. I am","glad to see you, by my troth, Master Shallow."],"id":591,"m":"Come, I'll go and have a drink with you, but I can't stay for dinner. I'm glad to see you, honestly, Master Shallow."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["O, Sir John, do you remember since we lay all night in the windmill","in Saint George's field?"],"id":592,"m":"Oh, Sir John, do you remember when we spent the whole night in the windmill in St George's Fields?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["No more of that, Master Shallow, no more of that."],"id":593,"m":"No more of that, Master Shallow, no more of that."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Ha, 'twas a merry night. And is Jane Nightwork alive?"],"id":594,"m":"Ha, it was a merry night. And is Jane Nightwork still alive?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["She lives, Master Shallow."],"id":595,"m":"She's alive, Master Shallow."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["She never could away with me."],"id":596,"m":"She never could put up with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Never, never; she would always say she could not abide Master","Shallow."],"id":597,"m":"Never, never. She always used to say she couldn't stand Master Shallow."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["By the mass, I could anger her to the heart. She was then a bona-roba.","Doth she hold her own well?"],"id":598,"m":"By the mass, I could make her furious. She was a fine-looking whore then. Is she keeping well?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Old, old, Master Shallow."],"id":599,"m":"Old, old, Master Shallow."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Nay, she must be old; she cannot choose but be old; certain she's old;","and had Robin Nightwork by old Nightwork before I came to Clement's Inn."],"id":600,"m":"Well, she must be old; she can't help being old. Certainly she's old. She had Robin Nightwork by old Nightwork before I went to Clement's Inn."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["That's fifty-five year ago."],"id":601,"m":"That's fifty-five years ago."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Ha, cousin Silence, that thou hadst seen that that this knight and I","have seen! Ha, Sir John, said I well?"],"id":602,"m":"Ha, cousin Silence, if only you'd seen what this knight and I have seen! Ha, Sir John, am I right?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["We have heard the chimes at midnight, Master Shallow."],"id":603,"m":"We've heard the chimes at midnight, Master Shallow."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["That we have, that we have, that we have; in faith, Sir John, we have:","our watchword was \"Hem boys!\" Come, let's to dinner; come, let's","to dinner: Jesus, the days that we have seen! Come, come."],"id":604,"m":"That we have, that we have, that we have. Honestly, Sir John, we have. Our watchword was \"Hey, boys!\" Come, let's go to dinner; come, let's go to dinner. Jesus, the days we've seen! Come, come."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Falstaff and the Justices.","id":605},{"t":"sp","who":"BULLCALF","lines":["Good Master Corporate Bardolph, stand my friend; and here's four","Harry ten shillings in French crowns for you.","In very truth, sir, I had as lief be hanged, sir, as go: and yet,","for mine own part, sir, I do not care; but rather, because I am","unwilling, and, for mine own part, have a desire to stay with my","friends; else, sir, I did not care, for mine own part, so much."],"id":606,"m":"Good Master Corporate Bardolph, be my friend. Here's forty shillings in French crowns for you. In very truth, sir, I'd as soon be hanged, sir, as go. And yet, for my own part, sir, I don't care; but rather because I'm unwilling and, for my own part, want to stay with my friends. Otherwise, sir, I didn't care so much, for my own part."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Go to; stand aside."],"id":607,"m":"Go on, stand aside."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOULDY","lines":["And, good master corporal captain, for my old dame's sake, stand my","friend: she has nobody to do any thing about her when I am gone;","and she is old, and cannot help herself: you shall have forty, sir."],"id":608,"m":"And, good master corporal captain, for my old woman's sake, be my friend. She'll have nobody to do anything for her when I'm gone, and she's old and can't help herself. You'll have forty shillings, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Go to; stand aside."],"id":609,"m":"Go on, stand aside."},{"t":"sp","who":"FEEBLE","lines":["By my troth, I care not; a man can die but once; we owe God a death:","I'll ne'er bear a base mind: an 't be my destiny, so; an 't be not, so:","no man's too good to serve's prince; and let it go which way it will, he","that dies this year is quit for the next."],"id":610,"m":"Honestly, I don't care. A man can only die once. We owe God a death. I'll never have a cowardly mind. If it's my destiny, so be it; if it isn't, so be it. No man's too good to serve his prince, and whichever way it goes, a man who dies this year is let off for the next."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Well said; th'art a good fellow."],"id":611,"m":"Well said. You're a good fellow."},{"t":"sp","who":"FEEBLE","lines":["Faith, I'll bear no base mind."],"id":612,"m":"Honestly, I won't have a cowardly mind."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Falstaff and the Justices.","id":613},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Come, sir, which men shall I have?"],"id":614,"m":"Come, sir, which men shall I have?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Four of which you please."],"id":615,"m":"Any four you like."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Sir, a word with you: I have three pound to free Mouldy and","Bullcalf."],"id":616,"m":"Sir, a word with you. I've got three pounds to let Mouldy and Bullcalf off."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Go to; well."],"id":617,"m":"Go on, then; all right."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Come, Sir John, which four will you have?"],"id":618,"m":"Come, Sir John, which four will you have?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Do you choose for me."],"id":619,"m":"You choose for me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Marry, then, Mouldy, Bullcalf, Feeble, and Shadow."],"id":620,"m":"Well then: Mouldy, Bullcalf, Feeble and Shadow."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Mouldy and Bullcalf: for you, Mouldy, stay at home till you are past","service; and for your part, Bullcalf, grow till you come unto it:","I will none of you."],"id":621,"m":"Mouldy and Bullcalf. As for you, Mouldy, stay at home till you're too old to serve; and as for you, Bullcalf, grow until you're old enough. I'll have neither of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Sir John, Sir John, do not yourself wrong: they are your likeliest","men, and I would have you served with the best."],"id":622,"m":"Sir John, Sir John, don't do yourself wrong. They're your most promising men, and I want you served by the best."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Will you tell me, Master Shallow, how to choose a man? Care I for the","limb, the thewes, the stature, bulk, and big assemblance of a man!","Give me the spirit, Master Shallow. Here's Wart; you see what a ragged","appearance it is: a' shall charge you and discharge you with the","motion of a pewterer's hammer, come off and on swifter than he that","gibbets on the brewer's bucket.","And this same half-faced fellow, Shadow; give me this man: he","presents no mark to the enemy; the foeman may with as great aim level","at the edge of a penknife.","And for a retreat; how swiftly will this Feeble the woman's tailor","run off! O, give me the spare men, and spare me the great ones.","Put me a caliver into Wart's hand, Bardolph."],"id":623,"m":"Are you going to tell me how to choose a man, Master Shallow? Do I care about a man's limbs, muscles, height, size and big appearance? Give me spirit, Master Shallow. Here's Wart; you see how scruffy he looks. He'll load and fire for you as fast as a pewterer's hammer, advance and retreat faster than the man who hangs the brewer's buckets on the yoke. And this half-faced fellow, Shadow: give me this man. He gives the enemy no target; the enemy might as well aim at the edge of a penknife. And as for a retreat, how fast this Feeble the ladies' tailor will run! Oh, give me the thin men and spare me the big ones. Put a musket in Wart's hand, Bardolph."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Hold, Wart, traverse; thus, thus, thus."],"id":624,"m":"Here, Wart, march; like this, like this, like this."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Come, manage me your caliver. So: very well: go to: very good,","exceeding good. O, give me always a little, lean, old, chapt,","bald shot. Well said, i' faith, Wart; thou'rt a good scab: hold,","there's a tester for thee."],"id":625,"m":"Come, handle your musket. That's it. Very good. Go on. Very good, excellent. Oh, always give me a little, thin, old, wrinkled, bald gunner. Well done, honestly, Wart; you're a good scab. Here's sixpence for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["He is not his craft's master; he doth not do it right. I remember at","Mile-end Green, when I lay at Clement's Inn,—I was then Sir Dagonet in","Arthur's show,—there was a little quiver fellow, and a' would manage","you his piece thus; and a' would about and about, and come you in and","come you in: \"rah, tah, tah,\" would a' say; \"bounce\" would a' say; and","away again would a' go, and again would 'a come: I shall ne'er see","such a fellow."],"id":626,"m":"He's not a master of his craft; he doesn't do it right. I remember at Mile End Green, when I was at Clement's Inn — I played Sir Dagonet in the Arthur pageant then — there was a nimble little fellow, and he'd handle his gun like this, and he'd turn about and about, and come in and come in: \"rah, tah, tah,\" he'd say; \"bang,\" he'd say; and away he'd go again, and back he'd come again. I'll never see such a fellow again."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["These fellows will do well. Master Shallow, God keep you, Master Silence:","I will not use many words with you. Fare you well, gentlemen both:","I thank you: I must a dozen mile to-night. Bardolph, give the soldiers","coats."],"id":627,"m":"These fellows will do well. Master Shallow, God keep you. Master Silence, I won't waste many words on you. Goodbye, gentlemen both. Thank you. I must do a dozen miles tonight. Bardolph, give the soldiers uniforms."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Sir John, the Lord bless you! God prosper your affairs! God send us","peace! At your return visit our house; let our old acquaintance be","renewed: peradventure I will with ye to the court."],"id":628,"m":"Sir John, the Lord bless you! God prosper your business! God send us peace! When you come back, visit our house and let's renew our old friendship. Perhaps I'll go with you to court."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["'Fore God, I would you would."],"id":629,"m":"By God, I wish you would."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Go to; I have spoke at a word. God keep you."],"id":630,"m":"Go on, I mean what I say. God keep you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Fare you well, gentle gentlemen."],"id":631,"m":"Goodbye, gentle gentlemen."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Justices.","id":632},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","cont":1,"lines":["On, Bardolph; lead the men away."],"id":633,"m":"On, Bardolph; lead the men away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Bardolph, Recruits, &c.","id":634},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","cont":1,"lines":["As I return, I will fetch off these justices: I do see the bottom","of Justice Shallow.","Lord, Lord, how subject we old men are to this vice of lying!","This same starved justice hath done nothing but prate to me of the","wildness of his youth, and the feats he hath done about Turnbull","Street; and every third word a lie, duer paid to the hearer than the","Turk's tribute. I do remember him at Clement's Inn like a man made","after supper of a cheese-paring: when a' was naked, he was, for all","the world, like a fork'd radish, with a head fantastically carved upon","it with a knife: a' was so forlorn, that his dimensions to any thick","sight were invincible: a' was the very genius of famine; yet lecherous","as a monkey, and the whores called him mandrake: a' came ever in the","rearward of the fashion, and sung those tunes to the overscutch'd","huswifes that he heard the carmen whistle, and sware they were his","fancies or his good-nights.","And now is this Vice's dagger become a squire, and talks as familiarly","of John a Gaunt as if he had been sworn brother to him; and I'll be","sworn a' ne'er saw him but once in the Tilt-yard; and then he burst","his head for crowding among the marshal's men.","I saw it, and told John a Gaunt he beat his own name; for you might","have thrust him and all his apparel into an eel-skin; the case of a","treble hautboy was a mansion for him, a court: and now has he land","and beefs.","Well, I'll be acquainted with him, if I return; and it shall go hard","but I'll make him a philosopher's two stones to me: if the young dace","be a bait for the old pike, I see no reason in the law of nature but I","may snap at him.","Let time shape, and there an end."],"id":635,"m":"On my way back I'll fleece these justices. I can see right to the bottom of Justice Shallow. Lord, Lord, how prone we old men are to this vice of lying! This starved justice has done nothing but babble to me about the wildness of his youth and the things he did around Turnbull Street, and every third word a lie, paid to the listener more punctually than the Sultan's taxes. I remember him at Clement's Inn looking like a man carved out of a cheese-rind after supper. When he was naked he was for all the world like a forked radish with a head fantastically carved on it with a knife. He was so scrawny that anyone with poor eyesight couldn't see him at all. He was the very spirit of famine, yet as lecherous as a monkey, and the whores called him \"mandrake\". He was always behind the fashion, and he sang tunes he'd heard the carters whistle to worn-out hussies and swore they were his own compositions or serenades. And now this stage clown's wooden dagger has become a squire and talks as familiarly about John of Gaunt as if he'd been his sworn brother, and I'll swear he only ever saw him once, at the jousting ground, and then he got his head cracked for pushing in among the marshal's guards. I saw it, and told John of Gaunt he was beating his own name, gaunt, because you could have stuffed Shallow and all his clothes into an eel-skin; the case of a treble oboe was a mansion for him, a palace. And now he has land and cattle. Well, I'll get to know him if I come back, and I'll make him my philosopher's stone, turning everything to gold for me, or my name's not Falstaff. If the young fish is bait for the old pike, I see no reason in the law of nature why I shouldn't snap at him. Let time decide, and that's the end of it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":636},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"Yorkshire. Gaultree Forest.","id":637},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Archbishop of York, Mowbray, Hastings, and others.","id":638},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["What is this forest call'd?"],"id":639,"m":"What is this forest called?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["'Tis Gaultree Forest, an 't shall please your grace."],"id":640,"m":"It's Gaultree Forest, if it please your grace."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["Here stand, my lords; and send discoverers forth","To know the numbers of our enemies."],"id":641,"m":"Stop here, my lords, and send scouts out to find out how many enemies there are."},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["We have sent forth already."],"id":642,"m":"We've sent them already."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["'Tis well done.","My friends and brethren in these great affairs,","I must acquaint you that I have received","New-dated letters from Northumberland;","Their cold intent, tenour and substance, thus:","Here doth he wish his person, with such powers","As might hold sortance with his quality,","The which he could not levy; whereupon","He is retired, to ripe his growing fortunes,","To Scotland: and concludes in hearty prayers","That your attempts may overlive the hazard","And fearful meeting of their opposite."],"id":643,"m":"That's well done. My friends and brothers in this great enterprise, I have to tell you that I've received recently dated letters from Northumberland. Their cold message, gist and substance is this: he wishes he were here in person, with an army fitting his rank, which he couldn't raise. So he has withdrawn to Scotland to let his growing fortunes ripen, and he ends with heartfelt prayers that your efforts may survive the danger of a fearful meeting with the enemy."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["Thus do the hopes we have in him touch ground","And dash themselves to pieces."],"id":644,"m":"So the hopes we had in him hit the ground and smash to pieces."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":645},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["Now, what news?"],"id":646,"m":"Well, what news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["West of this forest, scarcely off a mile,","In goodly form comes on the enemy;","And, by the ground they hide, I judge their number","Upon or near the rate of thirty thousand."],"id":647,"m":"West of this forest, barely a mile away, the enemy is advancing in good order, and judging by the ground they cover, I'd put their number at or near thirty thousand."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["The just proportion that we gave them out.","Let us sway on and face them in the field."],"id":648,"m":"Exactly the number we estimated. Let's move forward and face them in the field."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["What well-appointed leader fronts us here?"],"id":649,"m":"What well-equipped leader is coming to meet us here?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Westmoreland.","id":650},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["I think it is my Lord of Westmoreland."],"id":651,"m":"I think it's my Lord of Westmoreland."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["Health and fair greeting from our general,","The prince, Lord John and Duke of Lancaster."],"id":652,"m":"Health and kind greetings from our general, the Prince, Lord John, Duke of Lancaster."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["Say on, my Lord of Westmoreland, in peace:","What doth concern your coming?"],"id":653,"m":"Speak on in peace, my Lord of Westmoreland. What is the reason for your coming?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["Then, my lord,","Unto your grace do I in chief address","The substance of my speech. If that rebellion","Came like itself, in base and abject routs,","Led on by bloody youth, guarded with rags,","And countenanced by boys and beggary,","I say, if damn'd commotion so appear'd,","In his true, native, and most proper shape,","You, reverend father, and these noble lords","Had not been here, to dress the ugly form","Of base and bloody insurrection","With your fair honours. You, lord archbishop,","Whose see is by a civil peace maintain'd,","Whose beard the silver hand of peace hath touch'd,","Whose learning and good letters peace hath tutor'd,","Whose white investments figure innocence,","The dove and very blessed spirit of peace,","Wherefore you do so ill translate yourself","Out of the speech of peace that bears such grace,","Into the harsh and boisterous tongue of war;","Turning your books to graves, your ink to blood,","Your pens to lances and your tongue divine","To a loud trumpet and a point of war?"],"id":654,"m":"Then, my lord, I address my speech chiefly to your grace. If rebellion came looking like itself, in low, wretched mobs led by violent youths, dressed in rags and supported by boys and beggars — I say, if damned rebellion appeared like that, in its true, natural and proper shape, you, reverend father, and these noble lords wouldn't be here dressing up the ugly shape of low and bloody revolt with your honourable reputations. You, lord Archbishop, whose diocese is kept up by civil peace, whose beard the silver hand of peace has touched, whose learning and scholarship peace has taught, whose white robes stand for innocence, the dove and the very blessed spirit of peace — why do you so badly translate yourself out of the gracious language of peace into the harsh, noisy tongue of war, turning your books into graves, your ink into blood, your pens into lances and your holy tongue into a loud trumpet sounding the call to battle?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["Wherefore do I this? so the question stands.","Briefly to this end: we are all diseased,","And with our surfeiting and wanton hours","Have brought ourselves into a burning fever,","And we must bleed for it; of which disease","Our late king, Richard, being infected, died.","But, my most noble Lord of Westmoreland,","I take not on me here as a physician,","Nor do I as an enemy to peace","Troop in the throngs of military men;","But rather show awhile like fearful war,","To diet rank minds sick of happiness,","And purge the obstructions which begin to stop","Our very veins of life. Hear me more plainly.","I have in equal balance justly weigh'd","What wrongs our arms may do, what wrongs we suffer,","And find our griefs heavier than our offences.","We see which way the stream of time doth run,","And are enforced from our most quiet there","By the rough torrent of occasion;","And have the summary of all our griefs,","When time shall serve, to show in articles;","Which long ere this we offer'd to the king,","And might by no suit gain our audience:","When we are wrong'd and would unfold our griefs,","We are denied access unto his person","Even by those men that most have done us wrong.","The dangers of the days but newly gone,","Whose memory is written on the earth","With yet appearing blood, and the examples","Of every minute's instance, present now,","Hath put us in these ill-beseeming arms,","Not to break peace or any branch of it,","But to establish here a peace indeed,","Concurring, both in name and quality."],"id":655,"m":"Why am I doing this? That's the question. Briefly, for this reason: we are all diseased, and with our overeating and our wild hours we have brought ourselves into a burning fever, and we must be bled for it. Our late King Richard caught this disease and died of it. But, my most noble Lord of Westmoreland, I'm not setting myself up here as a doctor, nor do I march among crowds of soldiers as an enemy of peace. Rather, I put on the look of fearful war for a while, to put on a diet minds that are swollen and sick of too much happiness, and to clear the blockages that are beginning to stop our very veins of life. Let me speak more plainly. I have weighed fairly in the balance the wrongs our army may do and the wrongs we suffer, and I find our grievances heavier than our offences. We see which way the current of the times is running, and we're forced out of our peaceful lives by the rough torrent of events. We have a list of all our grievances, to show in written articles when the time comes, which we offered to the King long ago and could get no hearing for, however we pleaded. When we are wronged and want to set out our grievances, we're refused access to him by the very men who have wronged us most. The dangers of the days just past, whose memory is still written on the earth in blood that can be seen, and the examples that every minute now presents, have put us in this unfitting armour — not to break the peace or any branch of it, but to set up here a real peace, true both in name and in substance."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["When ever yet was your appeal denied?","Wherein have you been galled by the king?","What peer hath been suborn'd to grate on you,","That you should seal this lawless bloody book","Of forged rebellion with a seal divine","And consecrate commotion's bitter edge?"],"id":656,"m":"When was your appeal ever refused? How has the King hurt you? What lord has been bribed to harass you, that you should seal this lawless, bloody book of fraudulent rebellion with a holy seal and bless the bitter edge of rebellion?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["My brother general, the commonwealth,","To brother born an household cruelty,","I make my quarrel in particular."],"id":657,"m":"I make it my personal quarrel on behalf of my brother, the people, and the cruelty done at home to my born brother."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["There is no need of any such redress;","Or if there were, it not belongs to you."],"id":658,"m":"There's no need for any such remedy; or if there were, it isn't your business."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["Why not to him in part, and to us all","That feel the bruises of the days before,","And suffer the condition of these times","To lay a heavy and unequal hand","Upon our honours?"],"id":659,"m":"Why isn't it partly his, and all of ours, who feel the bruises of the days gone by and suffer under these times, which lay a heavy and unjust hand on our honours?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["O, my good Lord Mowbray,","Construe the times to their necessities,","And you shall say indeed, it is the time,","And not the king, that doth you injuries.","Yet for your part, it not appears to me","Either from the king or in the present time","That you should have an inch of any ground","To build a grief on: were you not restored","To all the Duke of Norfolk's signories,","Your noble and right well rememb'red father's?"],"id":660,"m":"Oh, my good Lord Mowbray, interpret the times by what they make necessary, and you'll indeed say it's the times, not the King, that are injuring you. And yet for your part, it doesn't seem to me that either the King or the present times give you an inch of ground to build a grievance on. Weren't you given back all the estates of the Duke of Norfolk, your noble and well-remembered father?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["What thing, in honour, had my father lost,","That need to be revived and breathed in me?","The king that loved him, as the state stood then,","Was force perforce compell'd to banish him:","And then that Henry Bolingbroke and he,","Being mounted and both roused in their seats,","Their neighing coursers daring of the spur,","Their armed staves in charge, their beavers down,","Their eyes of fire sparkling through sights of steel,","And the loud trumpet blowing them together,","Then, then, when there was nothing could have stay'd","My father from the breast of Bolingbroke,","O, when the king did throw his warder down,","His own life hung upon the staff he threw;","Then threw he down himself and all their lives","That by indictment and by dint of sword","Have since miscarried under Bolingbroke."],"id":661,"m":"What honour had my father lost that needed to be revived and breathed into me? The King who loved him was forced, against his will, by the state of things at the time, to banish him. And then, when Henry Bolingbroke and he were mounted and both braced in their saddles, their neighing horses daring the spur, their lances levelled, their visors down, their fiery eyes sparkling through the slits of their steel helmets, and the loud trumpet blowing them together — then, then, when nothing could have stopped my father's lance from reaching Bolingbroke's chest, oh, when the King threw his staff down, his own life hung on the staff he threw. He threw down himself and the lives of all those who since then, by legal charges and by the sword, have been destroyed under Bolingbroke."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["You speak, Lord Mowbray, now you know not what.","The Earl of Hereford was reputed then","In England the most valiant gentleman:","Who knows on whom fortune would then have smiled?","But if your father had been victor there,","He ne'er had borne it out of Coventry:","For all the country in a general voice","Cried hate upon him; and all their prayers and love","Were set on Hereford, whom they doted on","And bless'd and graced indeed, more than the king.","But this is mere digression from my purpose.","Here come I from our princely general","To know your griefs; to tell you from his grace","That he will give you audience; and wherein","It shall appear that your demands are just,","You shall enjoy them, everything set off","That might so much as think you enemies."],"id":662,"m":"You don't know what you're saying now, Lord Mowbray. The Earl of Hereford was then thought the bravest gentleman in England. Who knows which of them fortune would have smiled on? But if your father had won there, he would never have got out of Coventry alive, because the whole country with one voice cried out its hatred of him, and all their prayers and love were set on Hereford, whom they adored and blessed and honoured more than the King. But this is just a digression from my purpose. I've come from our princely general to hear your grievances and to tell you from his grace that he will give you a hearing, and wherever your demands are shown to be just you shall have them, with everything set aside that might make you seem enemies."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["But he hath forc'd us to compel this offer;","And it proceeds from policy, not love."],"id":663,"m":"But he's been forced by us to make this offer, and it comes from cunning, not love."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["Mowbray, you overween to take it so;","This offer comes from mercy, not from fear:","For, lo! within a ken our army lies,","Upon mine honour, all too confident","To give admittance to a thought of fear.","Our battle is more full of names than yours,","Our men more perfect in the use of arms,","Our armour all as strong, our cause the best;","Then reason will our hearts should be as good:","Say you not then our offer is compell'd."],"id":664,"m":"Mowbray, you're being arrogant to take it that way. This offer comes from mercy, not fear. Look, our army lies within sight, upon my honour far too confident to let in a thought of fear. Our army has more famous names in it than yours, our men are more skilled with weapons, our armour as strong, our cause the best; so reason says our courage should be as good. Don't say, then, that our offer is forced."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["Well, by my will we shall admit no parley."],"id":665,"m":"Well, if I have my way we'll have no negotiation."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["That argues but the shame of your offence:","A rotten case abides no handling."],"id":666,"m":"That only proves how shameful your crime is. A rotten case can't bear handling."},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["Hath the Prince John a full commission,","In very ample virtue of his father,","To hear and absolutely to determine","Of what conditions we shall stand upon?"],"id":667,"m":"Does Prince John have full authority, fully representing his father, to hear and to decide absolutely on the conditions we will insist on?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["That is intended in the general's name:","I muse you make so slight a question."],"id":668,"m":"That's implied in the title of general. I'm surprised you ask such a trivial question."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["Then take, my Lord of Westmoreland, this schedule,","For this contains our general grievances:","Each several article herein redress'd,","All members of our cause, both here and hence,","That are insinew'd to this action,","Acquitted by a true substantial form","And present execution of our wills","To us and to our purposes confined,","We come within our awful banks again","And knit our powers to the arm of peace."],"id":669,"m":"Then take this document, my Lord of Westmoreland, because it contains our general grievances. If each separate article in it is put right, and all those who share our cause, both here and elsewhere, who are bound up in this action, are pardoned in a proper, binding form, and our demands, as they concern us and our aims, are carried out at once, we will come back within our respectful bounds and join our forces to the arm of peace."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["This will I show the general. Please you, lords,","In sight of both our battles we may meet;","And either end in peace, which God so frame!","Or to the place of difference call the swords","Which must decide it."],"id":670,"m":"I'll show this to the general. If you please, lords, we can meet in sight of both our armies, and either end in peace, which God grant, or call the swords to the field that must settle our quarrel."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["My lord, we will do so."],"id":671,"m":"My lord, we'll do that."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Westmoreland.","id":672},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["There is a thing within my bosom tells me","That no conditions of our peace can stand."],"id":673,"m":"Something inside me tells me that no terms of peace we make will last."},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["Fear you not that: if we can make our peace","Upon such large terms and so absolute","As our conditions shall consist upon,","Our peace shall stand as firm as rocky mountains."],"id":674,"m":"Don't be afraid of that. If we can make our peace on such broad and absolute terms as our conditions insist on, our peace will stand as firm as rocky mountains."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["Yea, but our valuation shall be such","That every slight and false-derived cause,","Yea, every idle, nice and wanton reason","Shall to the king taste of this action;","That, were our royal faiths martyrs in love,","We shall be winnow'd with so rough a wind","That even our corn shall seem as light as chaff","And good from bad find no partition."],"id":675,"m":"Yes, but we'll be valued in such a way that every trivial, false excuse, yes, every idle, petty and silly reason, will remind the King of this rebellion. Even if we were martyrs in our love and loyalty to the King, we'll be winnowed by such a rough wind that even our grain will seem as light as chaff, and good will not be separated from bad."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["No, no, my lord. Note this; the king is weary","Of dainty and such picking grievances:","For he hath found to end one doubt by death","Revives two greater in the heirs of life,","And therefore will he wipe his tables clean","And keep no tell-tale to his memory","That may repeat and history his loss","To new remembrance; for full well he knows","He cannot so precisely weed this land","As his misdoubts present occasion:","His foes are so enrooted with his friends","That, plucking to unfix an enemy,","He doth unfasten so and shake a friend:","So that this land, like an offensive wife","That hath enraged him on to offer strokes,","As he is striking, holds his infant up","And hangs resolved correction in the arm","That was uprear'd to execution."],"id":676,"m":"No, no, my lord. Notice this: the King is tired of petty, nit-picking grievances, because he has found that ending one fear with a death brings two greater ones to life in the heirs who survive. So he'll wipe his slate clean and keep no informer by him to repeat the story of his losses and remind him of them again. He knows very well he can't weed this land as thoroughly as his suspicions suggest. His enemies are so rooted in among his friends that, in pulling up an enemy, he loosens and shakes a friend. So this land, like a quarrelsome wife who has driven him to the point of hitting her, holds up his baby just as he's about to strike, and keeps the punishment he's decided on hanging in the arm that was raised to carry it out."},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["Besides, the king hath wasted all his rods","On late offenders, that he now doth lack","The very instruments of chastisement:","So that his power, like to a fangless lion,","May offer, but not hold."],"id":677,"m":"Besides, the King has used up all his rods on recent offenders, so that he now lacks the very means of punishment. His power, like a lion without teeth, can threaten but not hold."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["'Tis very true:","And therefore be assured, my good lord marshal,","If we do now make our atonement well,","Our peace will, like a broken limb united,","Grow stronger for the breaking."],"id":678,"m":"That's very true. And so be assured, my good Lord Marshal, if we make our reconciliation well now, our peace will be like a broken limb that has healed, stronger for having been broken."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["Be it so.","Here is return'd my Lord of Westmoreland."],"id":679,"m":"So be it. Here's my Lord of Westmoreland back."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Westmoreland.","id":680},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["The prince is here at hand: pleaseth your lordship","To meet his grace just distance 'tween our armies."],"id":681,"m":"The Prince is close by. If your lordship pleases, meet his grace exactly halfway between our armies."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["Your grace of York, in God's name then, set forward."],"id":682,"m":"Your grace of York, in God's name then, go ahead."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["Before, and greet his grace: my lord, we come."],"id":683,"m":"Go first and greet his grace. My lord, we're coming."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":684},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"Another part of the forest.","id":685},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter, from one side, Mowbray, attended; afterwards, the Archbishop, Hastings, and others; from the other side, Prince John of Lancaster, and Westmoreland; Officers, and others with them.","id":686},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["You are well encounter'd here, my cousin Mowbray:","Good day to you, gentle lord Archbishop;","And so to you, Lord Hastings, and to all.","My Lord of York, it better show'd with you","When that your flock, assembled by the bell,","Encircled you to hear with reverence","Your exposition on the holy text","Than now to see you here an iron man,","Cheering a rout of rebels with your drum,","Turning the word to sword and life to death.","That man that sits within a monarch's heart,","And ripens in the sunshine of his favour,","Would he abuse the countenance of the king,","Alack, what mischiefs might he set abroach","In shadow of such greatness! With you, lord bishop,","It is even so. Who hath not heard it spoken","How deep you were within the books of God?","To us the speaker in his parliament;","To us the imagined voice of God himself;","The very opener and intelligencer","Between the grace, the sanctities of heaven","And our dull workings. O, who shall believe","But you misuse the reverence of your place,","Employ the countenance and grace of heaven,","As a false favourite doth his prince's name,","In deeds dishonourable? You have ta'en up,","Under the counterfeited zeal of God,","The subjects of his substitute, my father,","And both against the peace of heaven and him","Have here up-swarm'd them."],"id":687,"m":"Well met here, my cousin Mowbray. Good day to you, noble lord Archbishop, and to you, Lord Hastings, and to everyone. My Lord of York, you looked better when your congregation, called by the bell, gathered round you to listen reverently to your sermon on the holy text, than now, seeing you here as a man of iron, encouraging a mob of rebels with your drum, turning the Word into the sword and life into death. If a man who sits in a monarch's heart and ripens in the sunshine of his favour abused the King's support, alas, what harm he could set going under cover of such greatness! It's just so with you, lord Bishop. Who hasn't heard how deeply you were in God's books? To us you were the speaker in God's parliament, the imagined voice of God himself, the very interpreter and messenger between the grace and holiness of heaven and our dull actions. Oh, who could believe anything but that you are misusing the respect owed to your position, using the favour and grace of heaven in dishonourable deeds, as a false favourite uses his prince's name? Under a pretended zeal for God, you've raised up the subjects of His deputy, my father, and swarmed them here against both the peace of heaven and the King."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["Good my Lord of Lancaster,","I am not here against your father's peace;","But, as I told my Lord of Westmoreland,","The time misorder'd doth, in common sense,","Crowd us and crush us to this monstrous form","To hold our safety up. I sent your grace","The parcels and particulars of our grief,","The which hath been with scorn shoved from the court,","Whereon this Hydra son of war is born;","Whose dangerous eyes may well be charm'd asleep","With grant of our most just and right desires,","And true obedience, of this madness cured,","Stoop tamely to the foot of majesty."],"id":688,"m":"My good Lord of Lancaster, I'm not here against your father's peace. But, as I told my Lord of Westmoreland, these disordered times, as anyone can see, crowd us and crush us into this monstrous shape to keep ourselves safe. I sent your grace the details and particulars of our grievances, which have been pushed out of court with scorn, and from that this many-headed Hydra, this son of war, is born. Its dangerous eyes may be charmed to sleep by granting our just and proper wishes, and true obedience, cured of this madness, will bow tamely at the feet of majesty."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["If not, we ready are to try our fortunes","To the last man."],"id":689,"m":"If not, we're ready to try our luck to the last man."},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["And though we here fall down,","We have supplies to second our attempt:","If they miscarry, theirs shall second them;","And so success of mischief shall be born","And heir from heir shall hold this quarrel up","Whiles England shall have generation."],"id":690,"m":"And even if we fall here, we have reinforcements to back up our attempt. If they fail, theirs will back them up, and so a succession of trouble will be born, and heir after heir will keep this quarrel going as long as England has children."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["You are too shallow, Hastings, much to shallow,","To sound the bottom of the after-times."],"id":691,"m":"You're too shallow, Hastings, much too shallow, to sound the depths of the future."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["Pleaseth your grace to answer them directly","How far forth you do like their articles."],"id":692,"m":"If your grace pleases, answer them directly: how far do you accept their articles?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["I like them all, and do allow them well,","And swear here, by the honour of my blood,","My father's purposes have been mistook,","And some about him have too lavishly","Wrested his meaning and authority.","My lord, these griefs shall be with speed redress'd;","Upon my soul, they shall. If this may please you,","Discharge your powers unto their several counties,","As we will ours; and here between the armies","Let's drink together friendly and embrace,","That all their eyes may bear those tokens home","Of our restored love and amity."],"id":693,"m":"I like them all and fully accept them, and I swear here by the honour of my blood that my father's intentions have been misunderstood, and that some of those around him have too freely twisted his meaning and his authority. My lord, these grievances will be put right quickly; upon my soul, they will. If this satisfies you, send your troops home to their counties, as we'll send ours; and here between the armies let's drink together in friendship and embrace, so that all their eyes can carry home these signs of our restored love and friendship."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["I take your princely word for these redresses."],"id":694,"m":"I take your princely word for these remedies."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["I give it you, and will maintain my word:","And thereupon I drink unto your grace."],"id":695,"m":"I give it to you, and I'll keep my word. And with that, I drink to your grace."},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["Go, captain, and deliver to the army","This news of peace: let them have pay, and part:","I know it will please them. Hie thee, captain."],"id":696,"m":"Go, captain, and give the army this news of peace. Let them be paid and go. I know it will please them. Hurry, captain."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Officer.","id":697},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["To you, my noble Lord of Westmoreland."],"id":698,"m":"To you, my noble Lord of Westmoreland."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["I pledge your grace; and, if you knew what pains","I have bestow'd to breed this present peace,","You would drink freely: but my love to ye","Shall show itself more openly hereafter."],"id":699,"m":"I drink to your grace. And if you knew what trouble I've taken to bring about this peace, you'd drink heartily. But my love for you will show itself more openly later."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["I do not doubt you."],"id":700,"m":"I don't doubt you."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["I am glad of it.","Health to my lord and gentle cousin, Mowbray."],"id":701,"m":"I'm glad of it. Health to my lord and noble cousin, Mowbray."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["You wish me health in very happy season,","For I am, on the sudden, something ill."],"id":702,"m":"You wish me health at a very good moment, because I've suddenly come over rather ill."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["Against ill chances men are ever merry;","But heaviness foreruns the good event."],"id":703,"m":"Men are always merry just before bad luck, but heaviness comes before a good outcome."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["Therefore be merry, coz; since sudden sorrow","Serves to say thus, \"some good thing comes tomorrow.\""],"id":704,"m":"So be merry, cousin, since sudden sorrow means \"something good will come tomorrow\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["Believe me, I am passing light in spirit."],"id":705,"m":"Believe me, I'm extremely light-hearted."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["So much the worse, if your own rule be true."],"id":706,"m":"All the worse, if your own rule is true."},{"t":"sd","text":"Shouts within.","id":707},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["The word of peace is render'd: hark, how they shout!"],"id":708,"m":"The word of peace has been given. Listen to them shout!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["This had been cheerful after victory."],"id":709,"m":"That would have been cheerful after a victory."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["A peace is of the nature of a conquest;","For then both parties nobly are subdued,","And neither party loser."],"id":710,"m":"A peace is a kind of conquest, because both sides are nobly subdued and neither side loses."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["Go, my lord.","And let our army be discharged too."],"id":711,"m":"Go, my lord, and let our army be dismissed too."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Westmoreland.","id":712},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","cont":1,"lines":["And, good my lord, so please you, let our trains","March by us, that we may peruse the men","We should have coped withal."],"id":713,"m":"And, my good lord, if you please, let your troops march past us so we can look over the men we would have fought."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["Go, good Lord Hastings,","And, ere they be dismiss'd, let them march by."],"id":714,"m":"Go, good Lord Hastings, and let them march past before they're dismissed."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Hastings.","id":715},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["I trust, lords, we shall lie to-night together."],"id":716,"m":"I trust, lords, we'll spend tonight together."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Westmoreland.","id":717},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","cont":1,"lines":["Now, cousin, wherefore stands our army still?"],"id":718,"m":"Now, cousin, why is our army standing still?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["The leaders, having charge from you to stand,","Will not go off until they hear you speak."],"id":719,"m":"The officers, having orders from you to stand, won't leave until they hear you say so."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["They know their duties."],"id":720,"m":"They know their duty."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Hastings.","id":721},{"t":"sp","who":"HASTINGS","lines":["My lord, our army is dispersed already:","Like youthful steers unyoked, they take their courses","East, west, north, south; or, like a school broke up,","Each hurries toward his home and sporting-place."],"id":722,"m":"My lord, our army has already dispersed. Like young bullocks let out of the yoke, they're running off east, west, north and south; or, like a school let out, each one is hurrying home or to his playground."},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["Good tidings, my Lord Hastings; for the which","I do arrest thee, traitor, of high treason:","And you, lord archbishop, and you, Lord Mowbray,","Of capital treason I attach you both."],"id":723,"m":"Good news, my Lord Hastings — and for it I arrest you, traitor, for high treason. And you, lord Archbishop, and you, Lord Mowbray, I arrest you both for treason punishable by death."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["Is this proceeding just and honourable?"],"id":724,"m":"Is this proceeding fair and honourable?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["Is your assembly so?"],"id":725,"m":"Is your gathering?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARCHBISHOP","lines":["Will you thus break your faith?"],"id":726,"m":"Will you break your promise like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["I pawn'd thee none:","I promised you redress of these same grievances","Whereof you did complain; which, by mine honour,","I will perform with a most Christian care.","But for you, rebels, look to taste the due","Meet for rebellion and such acts as yours.","Most shallowly did you these arms commence,","Fondly brought here and foolishly sent hence.","Strike up our drums, pursue the scattr'd stray:","God, and not we, hath safely fought to-day.","Some guard these traitors to the block of death,","Treason's true bed and yielder up of breath."],"id":727,"m":"I pledged you none. I promised you a remedy for the grievances you complained about, which, upon my honour, I will carry out with most Christian care. But as for you, rebels, expect to taste the punishment that fits rebellion and actions like yours. You started this war very shallowly, foolishly brought your forces here and foolishly sent them away. Sound our drums; pursue the scattered stragglers. God, not we, has fought safely today. Some of you, take these traitors to the block, treason's proper bed, where life is given up."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":728},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"III","loc":"Another part of the forest.","id":729},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Excursions. Enter Falstaff and Colevile, meeting.","id":730},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What's your name, sir? of what condition are you, and of","what place, I pray?"],"id":731,"m":"What's your name, sir? What's your rank, and where are you from, may I ask?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COLEVILE","lines":["I am a knight sir; and my name is Colevile of the Dale."],"id":732,"m":"I'm a knight, sir, and my name is Colevile of the Dale."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Well, then, Colevile is your name, a knight is your degree, and","your place the dale: Colevile shall be still your name, a traitor","your degree, and the dungeon your place, a place deep enough; so","shall you be still Colevile of the dale."],"id":733,"m":"Well then, Colevile is your name, a knight is your rank, and your place the dale. Colevile will still be your name, a traitor your rank, and the dungeon your place, a place deep enough, so you'll still be Colevile of the dale."},{"t":"sp","who":"COLEVILE","lines":["Are not you Sir John Falstaff?"],"id":734,"m":"Aren't you Sir John Falstaff?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["As good a man as he, sir, whoe'er I am. Do ye yield, sir? or shall I","sweat for you? If I do sweat, they are the drops of thy lovers, and","they weep for thy death: therefore rouse up fear and trembling,","and do observance to my mercy."],"id":735,"m":"As good a man as him, sir, whoever I am. Do you surrender, sir, or must I sweat for you? If I do sweat, they're the tears of those who love you, weeping for your death. So stir up your fear and trembling and bow to my mercy."},{"t":"sp","who":"COLEVILE","lines":["I think you are Sir John Falstaff, and in that thought yield me."],"id":736,"m":"I think you're Sir John Falstaff, and thinking so, I surrender."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I have a whole school of tongues in this belly of mine, and not a","tongue of them all speaks any other word but my name. An I had but","a belly of any indifferency, I were simply the most active fellow in","Europe: my womb, my womb, my womb undoes me.","Here comes our general."],"id":737,"m":"I have a whole school of tongues in this belly of mine, and not one of them says anything but my name. If only I had a belly of ordinary size, I'd simply be the most active fellow in Europe. My belly, my belly, my belly is my ruin. Here comes our general."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Prince John of Lancaster, Westmoreland, Blunt, and others.","id":738},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["The heat is past; follow no further now:","Call in the powers, good cousin Westmoreland."],"id":739,"m":"The heat of battle is over. Pursue no further now. Call in the troops, good cousin Westmoreland."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Westmoreland.","id":740},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","cont":1,"lines":["Now, Falstaff, where have you been all this while?","When everything is ended, then you come:","These tardy tricks of yours will, on my life,","One time or other break some gallows' back."],"id":741,"m":"Now, Falstaff, where have you been all this time? When everything's over, then you arrive. These late tricks of yours will, I swear, one day or another break some gallows' back."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I would be sorry, my lord, but it should be thus: I never knew yet","but rebuke and check was the reward of valour. Do you think me a","swallow, an arrow, or a bullet? have I, in my poor and old motion,","the expedition of thought? I have speeded hither with the very","extremest inch of possibility; I have foundered nine score and odd","posts: and here, travel-tainted as I am, have, in my pure and","immaculate valour, taken Sir John Colevile of the dale, a most furious","knight and valorous enemy. But what of that? he saw me, and yielded;","that I may justly say, with the hook-nosed fellow of Rome, \"I came,","saw, and overcame.\""],"id":742,"m":"I'd be sorry, my lord, if it were any other way. I've never known anything but scolding and rebuke to be the reward of courage. Do you think I'm a swallow, an arrow or a bullet? Do I, with my poor old movements, have the speed of thought? I've hurried here at the very utmost limit of possibility. I've worn out more than a hundred and eighty post-horses. And here, dirty from the journey as I am, in my pure, spotless courage, I've taken Sir John Colevile of the dale, a most furious knight and brave enemy. But what of it? He saw me and surrendered, so that I can fairly say, with that hook-nosed fellow from Rome, \"I came, saw and conquered.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["It was more of his courtesy than your deserving."],"id":743,"m":"It was more his politeness than your merit."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I know not: here he is, and here I yield him: and I beseech your","grace, let it be booked with the rest of this day's deeds; or, by the","Lord, I will have it in a particular ballad else, with mine own","picture on the top on't, Colevile kissing my foot: to the which","course if I be enforced, if you do not all show like gilt twopences to","me, and I in the clear sky of fame o'ershine you as much as the full","moon doth the cinders of the element, which show like pins' heads to","her, believe not the word of the noble: therefore let me have right,","and let desert mount."],"id":744,"m":"I don't know. Here he is, and here I hand him over. And I beg your grace, let it be recorded with the rest of today's deeds, or else, by the Lord, I'll have it put in a ballad of its own, with my picture at the top and Colevile kissing my foot. And if I'm forced to do that, and you don't all look like cheap gilded coins next to me, and I don't outshine you in the clear sky of fame as much as the full moon outshines the stars, which look like pinheads beside her, then don't believe a nobleman's word. So give me my due, and let merit rise."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["Thine's too heavy to mount."],"id":745,"m":"Yours is too heavy to rise."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Let it shine, then."],"id":746,"m":"Let it shine, then."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["Thine's too thick to shine."],"id":747,"m":"Yours is too dense to shine."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Let it do something, my good lord, that may do me good, and","call it what you will."],"id":748,"m":"Let it do something, my good lord, that will do me some good, and call it whatever you like."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["Is thy name Colevile?"],"id":749,"m":"Is your name Colevile?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COLEVILE","lines":["It is, my lord."],"id":750,"m":"It is, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["A famous rebel art thou, Colevile."],"id":751,"m":"You're a famous rebel, Colevile."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["And a famous true subject took him."],"id":752,"m":"And a famous loyal subject captured him."},{"t":"sp","who":"COLEVILE","lines":["I am, my lord, but as my betters are","That led me hither: had they been ruled by me,","You should have won them dearer than you have."],"id":753,"m":"I am, my lord, only what my superiors are who led me here. If they'd taken my advice, you'd have paid a higher price for them than you have."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I know not how they sold themselves: but thou, like a kind","fellow, gavest thyself away gratis; and I thank thee for thee."],"id":754,"m":"I don't know what price they sold themselves for. But you, like a kind fellow, gave yourself away for free, and I thank you for yourself."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Westmoreland.","id":755},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["Now, have you left pursuit?"],"id":756,"m":"Well, have you stopped the chase?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["Retreat is made and execution stay'd."],"id":757,"m":"The retreat has been sounded and the killing stopped."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["Send Colevile with his confederates","To York, to present execution.","Blunt, lead him hence; and see you guard him sure."],"id":758,"m":"Send Colevile and his allies to York for immediate execution. Blunt, take him away, and make sure you guard him well."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Blunt and others with Colevile.","id":759},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","cont":1,"lines":["And now dispatch we toward the court, my lords:","I hear the king my father is sore sick:","Our news shall go before us to his majesty,","Which, cousin, you shall bear to comfort him,","And we with sober speed will follow you."],"id":760,"m":"And now let's hurry to court, my lords. I hear the King my father is very ill. Our news will go ahead of us to his Majesty; you, cousin, will carry it to comfort him, and we'll follow you with steady speed."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["My lord, I beseech you, give me leave to go through Gloucestershire:","and, when you come to court, stand my good lord, pray, in your good","report."],"id":761,"m":"My lord, please give me permission to go through Gloucestershire, and when you get to court, please be my good lord in your report."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["Fare you well, Falstaff: I, in my condition,","Shall better speak of you than you deserve."],"id":762,"m":"Goodbye, Falstaff. In my position, I'll speak better of you than you deserve."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but Falstaff.","id":763},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I would you had but the wit: 'twere better than your dukedom.","Good faith, this same young sober-blooded boy doth not love me;","nor a man cannot make him laugh; but that's no marvel, he drinks","no wine. There's never none of these demure boys come to any proof;","for thin drink doth so over-cool their blood, and making many","fish-meals, that they fall into a kind of male green-sickness; and","then, when they marry, they get wenches: they are generally fools","and cowards; which some of us should be too, but for inflammation.","A good sherris-sack hath a two-fold operation in it. It ascends me","into the brain; dries me there all the foolish and dull and crudy","vapours which environ it; makes it apprehensive, quick, forgetive,","full of nimble fiery and delectable shapes; which, delivered o'er to","the voice, the tongue, which is the birth, becomes excellent wit.","The second property of your excellent sherris is, the warming of the","blood; which, before cold and settled, left the liver white and pale,","which is the badge of pusillanimity and cowardice; but the sherris","warms it and makes it course from the inwards to the parts extremes:","it illumineth the face, which as a beacon gives warning to all","the rest of this little kingdom, man, to arm; and then the vital","commoners and inland petty spirits muster me all to their captain, the","heart, who, great and puffed up with this retinue, doth any deed of","courage; and this valour comes of sherris. So that skill in the weapon","is nothing without sack, for that sets it a-work; and learning a mere","hoard of gold kept by a devil, till sack commences it and sets it in","act and use. Hereof comes it that Prince Harry is valiant; for the","cold blood he did naturally inherit of his father, he hath, like lean,","sterile and bare land, manured, husbanded and tilled with excellent","endeavour of drinking good and good store of fertile sherris, that he","is become very hot and valiant. If I had a thousand sons, the first","humane principle I would teach them should be, to forswear thin","potations and to addict themselves to sack."],"id":764,"m":"I wish you had the wit to; it would be worth more than your dukedom. Honestly, this sober-blooded boy doesn't love me, and no one can make him laugh. But that's no wonder: he drinks no wine. None of these well-behaved boys ever amount to anything, because weak drink cools their blood so much, and with eating so much fish they fall into a kind of male anaemia, and then when they marry they father girls. They're generally fools and cowards, as some of us would be too, if it weren't for drink heating us up. A good sherry has a double effect. It climbs into the brain and dries up all the foolish, dull, sluggish vapours surrounding it; it makes the brain quick, lively and inventive, full of nimble, fiery, delightful images, which, passed on to the voice, the tongue, which gives them birth, become excellent wit. The second quality of your excellent sherry is that it warms the blood, which, being cold and still before, left the liver white and pale, which is the sign of faint-heartedness and cowardice. But the sherry warms it and makes it run from the inner organs to the extremities. It lights up the face, which like a beacon warns all the rest of this little kingdom, man, to arm; and then all the vital common people and minor spirits inside gather to their captain, the heart, which, great and swollen with this following, will do any brave deed. And this courage comes from sherry. So skill with weapons is nothing without sack, because sack sets it working, and learning is just a hoard of gold guarded by a devil until sack sets it going and puts it to use. That's why Prince Harry is brave: the cold blood he naturally inherited from his father he has manured, farmed and ploughed, like poor, barren, bare land, with the excellent labour of drinking plenty of good fertile sherry, so that he has become very hot and brave. If I had a thousand sons, the first human principle I'd teach them would be to give up weak drinks and devote themselves to sack."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Bardolph.","id":765},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","cont":1,"lines":["How now, Bardolph!"],"id":766,"m":"What's up, Bardolph?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["The army is discharged all and gone."],"id":767,"m":"The whole army has been discharged and gone."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Let them go. I'll through Gloucestershire; and there will I visit","Master Robert Shallow, esquire: I have him already tempering between","my finger and my thumb, and shortly will I seal with him. Come away."],"id":768,"m":"Let them go. I'll go through Gloucestershire, and there I'll visit Master Robert Shallow, esquire. I've already got him softening like wax between my finger and thumb, and soon I'll seal my bargain with him. Come on."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":769},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"IV","loc":"Westminster. The Jerusalem Chamber.","id":770},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the King, the Princes Thomas of Clarence and Humphrey of Gloucester, Warwick, and others.","id":771},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Now, lords, if God doth give successful end","To this debate that bleedeth at our doors,","We will our youth lead on to higher fields","And draw no swords but what are sanctified.","Our navy is address'd, our power collected,","Our substitutes in absence well invested,","And every thing lies level to our wish:","Only, we want a little personal strength;","And pause us, till these rebels, now afoot,","Come underneath the yoke of government."],"id":772,"m":"Now, lords, if God gives a successful end to this conflict that's bleeding at our doors, we will lead our young men to higher battlefields and draw no swords except holy ones. Our navy is ready, our army gathered, our deputies for while we're away properly appointed, and everything is as we'd wish. We only lack a little bodily strength, and we must wait until these rebels who are now in the field are brought back under the yoke of government."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Both which we doubt not but your majesty","Shall soon enjoy."],"id":773,"m":"We have no doubt your Majesty will soon have both of those."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Humphrey, my son of Gloucester,","Where is the prince your brother?"],"id":774,"m":"Humphrey, my son of Gloucester, where is the Prince your brother?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["I think he's gone to hunt, my lord, at Windsor."],"id":775,"m":"I think he's gone hunting at Windsor, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["And how accompanied?"],"id":776,"m":"And who with?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["I do not know, my lord."],"id":777,"m":"I don't know, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Is not his brother, Thomas of Clarence, with him?"],"id":778,"m":"Isn't his brother, Thomas of Clarence, with him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["No, my good lord; he is in presence here."],"id":779,"m":"No, my good lord, he's here with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["What would my lord and father?"],"id":780,"m":"What does my lord and father want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Nothing but well to thee, Thomas of Clarence.","How chance thou art not with the prince thy brother?","He loves thee, and thou dost neglect him, Thomas;","Thou hast a better place in his affection","Than all thy brothers: cherish it, my boy,","And noble offices thou mayst effect","Of mediation, after I am dead,","Between his greatness and thy other brethren:","Therefore omit him not; blunt not his love,","Nor lose the good advantage of his grace","By seeming cold or careless of his will;","For he is gracious, if he be observed.","He hath a tear for pity and a hand","Open as day for melting charity:","Yet notwithstanding, being incensed, he's flint;","As humorous as winter and as sudden","As flaws congealed in the spring of day.","His temper, therefore, must be well observed:","Chide him for faults, and do it reverently,","When you perceive his blood inclined to mirth;","But, being moody, give him line and scope,","Till that his passions, like a whale on ground,","Confound themselves with working. Learn this, Thomas,","And thou shalt prove a shelter to thy friends,","A hoop of gold to bind thy brothers in,","That the united vessel of their blood,","Mingled with venom of suggestion—","As, force perforce, the age will pour it in—","Shall never leak, though it do work as strong","As aconitum or rash gunpowder."],"id":781,"m":"Only good for you, Thomas of Clarence. How is it you're not with the Prince your brother? He loves you, and you neglect him, Thomas. You have a better place in his affection than all your brothers. Value it, my boy, and after I'm dead you may do noble service as a go-between between his greatness and your other brothers. So don't neglect him, don't blunt his love or lose the advantage of his favour by seeming cold or careless of what he wants, because he is gracious if he's treated with respect. He has a tear for pity and a hand as open as daylight for tender charity. And yet, when he's angry, he's flint, as changeable as winter and as sudden as the frozen gusts of early morning. So his temper must be carefully watched. Scold him for his faults, and do it respectfully, when you see he's in a merry mood; but when he's moody, give him line and room, like a fisherman, until his passions, like a stranded whale, wear themselves out with struggling. Learn this, Thomas, and you'll be a shelter to your friends, a gold hoop to bind your brothers together, so that the united vessel of their blood, mixed with the poison of evil suggestion, as the times are bound to pour it in, will never leak, even if it works as strongly as wolfsbane or gunpowder."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["I shall observe him with all care and love."],"id":782,"m":"I'll attend to him with all care and love."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Why art thou not at Windsor with him, Thomas?"],"id":783,"m":"Why aren't you with him at Windsor, Thomas?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["He is not there to-day; he dines in London."],"id":784,"m":"He's not there today. He's having dinner in London."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["And how accompanied? canst thou tell that?"],"id":785,"m":"And who with? Can you tell me that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["With Poins, and other his continual followers."],"id":786,"m":"With Poins and his other constant followers."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Most subject is the fattest soil to weeds;","And he, the noble image of my youth,","Is overspread with them: therefore my grief","Stretches itself beyond the hour of death:","The blood weeps from my heart when I do shape","In forms imaginary the unguided days","And rotten times that you shall look upon","When I am sleeping with my ancestors.","For when his headstrong riot hath no curb,","When rage and hot blood are his counsellors,","When means and lavish manners meet together,","O, with what wings shall his affections fly","Towards fronting peril and opposed decay!"],"id":787,"m":"The richest soil is most prone to weeds, and he, the noble image of my youth, is overgrown with them. So my grief stretches beyond the hour of my death. My heart weeps blood when I imagine the lawless days and rotten times you'll see when I'm asleep with my ancestors. Because when his headstrong rioting has no curb, when rage and hot blood are his advisers, when money and extravagant habits come together, oh, with what wings his desires will fly towards danger ahead and ruin in his path!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["My gracious lord, you look beyond him quite:","The prince but studies his companions","Like a strange tongue, wherein, to gain the language,","'Tis needful that the most immodest word","Be look'd upon and learn'd; which once attain'd,","Your highness knows, comes to no further use","But to be known and hated. So, like gross terms,","The prince will in the perfectness of time","Cast off his followers; and their memory","Shall as a pattern or a measure live,","By which his grace must mete the lives of other,","Turning past evils to advantages."],"id":788,"m":"My gracious lord, you misjudge him completely. The Prince is only studying his companions like a foreign language, in which, to master it, it's necessary to look at and learn the most indecent words; and once he's mastered them, as your Highness knows, they're of no further use except to be recognised and hated. So, like coarse words, the Prince will in the fullness of time cast off his followers, and the memory of them will live on as a pattern or a measure by which his grace will judge other men's lives, turning past evils into advantages."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["'Tis seldom when the bee doth leave her comb","In the dead carrion."],"id":789,"m":"The bee rarely leaves her honeycomb in a dead carcass."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Westmoreland.","id":790},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","cont":1,"lines":["Who's here? Westmoreland?"],"id":791,"m":"Who's here? Westmoreland?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["Health to my sovereign, and new happiness","Added to that that I am to deliver!","Prince John your son doth kiss your grace's hand:","Mowbray, the Bishop Scroop, Hastings and all","Are brought to the correction of your law;","There is not now a rebel's sword unsheathed,","But Peace puts forth her olive every where.","The manner how this action hath been borne","Here at more leisure may your highness read,","With every course in his particular."],"id":792,"m":"Health to my sovereign, and new happiness on top of what I have to tell you! Prince John your son kisses your grace's hand. Mowbray, Bishop Scroop, Hastings and all of them have been brought to the punishment of your law. There isn't a rebel's sword drawn now; Peace holds out her olive branch everywhere. Your Highness can read here at greater leisure how this action was carried out, with every stage in detail."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["O Westmoreland, thou art a summer bird,","Which ever in the haunch of winter sings","The lifting up of day."],"id":793,"m":"Oh Westmoreland, you're a summer bird, who always sings of the dawn at the tail end of winter."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Harcourt.","id":794},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","cont":1,"lines":["Look, here's more news."],"id":795,"m":"Look, here's more news."},{"t":"sp","who":"HARCOURT","lines":["From enemies heaven keep your majesty;","And, when they stand against you, may they fall","As those that I am come to tell you of!","The Earl Northumberland and the Lord Bardolph,","With a great power of English and of Scots,","Are by the sheriff of Yorkshire overthrown:","The manner and true order of the fight","This packet, please it you, contains at large."],"id":796,"m":"May heaven keep your Majesty from enemies, and when they stand against you, may they fall like the ones I've come to tell you about! The Earl of Northumberland and Lord Bardolph, with a great army of English and Scots, have been overthrown by the sheriff of Yorkshire. This packet, if you please, contains the full details of how the fight went."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["And wherefore should these good news make me sick?","Will Fortune never come with both hands full,","But write her fair words still in foulest letters?","She either gives a stomach and no food;","Such are the poor, in health; or else a feast","And takes away the stomach; such are the rich,","That have abundance and enjoy it not.","I should rejoice now at this happy news;","And now my sight fails, and my brain is giddy:","O me! come near me; now I am much ill."],"id":797,"m":"And why should this good news make me ill? Will Fortune never come with both hands full, but always write her fair words in the ugliest letters? She either gives an appetite and no food, as with the poor who are healthy, or else a feast and takes away the appetite, as with the rich, who have plenty and can't enjoy it. I should be rejoicing now at this happy news, and now my sight is failing and my head is spinning. Oh me! Come close to me. I'm very ill now."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Comfort, your majesty!"],"id":798,"m":"Take comfort, your Majesty!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["O my royal father!"],"id":799,"m":"Oh my royal father!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WESTMORELAND","lines":["My sovereign lord, cheer up yourself, look up."],"id":800,"m":"My sovereign lord, cheer up, look up."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Be patient, princes; you do know, these fits","Are with his highness very ordinary.","Stand from him, give him air; he'll straight be well."],"id":801,"m":"Be calm, princes. You know these fits are very common with his Highness. Stand back from him, give him air; he'll be well soon."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["No, no, he cannot long hold out these pangs:","The incessant care and labour of his mind","Hath wrought the mure that should confine it in","So thin that life looks through and will break out."],"id":802,"m":"No, no, he can't endure these pains much longer. The constant worry and work of his mind have worn the wall that should contain it so thin that life shows through and will break out."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["The people fear me; for they do observe","Unfather'd heirs and loathly births of nature:","The seasons change their manners, as the year","Had found some months asleep, and leap'd them over."],"id":803,"m":"The people frighten me, because they've noticed children born without fathers and monstrous births. The seasons have changed their habits, as if the year had found some months asleep and jumped over them."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["The river hath thrice flow'd, no ebb between;","And the old folk, time's doting chronicles,","Say it did so a little time before","That our great-grandsire, Edward, sick'd and died."],"id":804,"m":"The river has flooded three times with no ebb in between, and the old people, time's rambling history books, say it did the same shortly before our great-grandfather Edward fell ill and died."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Speak lower, princes, for the king recovers."],"id":805,"m":"Speak more quietly, princes, because the King is recovering."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["This apoplexy will certain be his end."],"id":806,"m":"This apoplexy will certainly be the end of him."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I pray you, take me up, and bear me hence","Into some other chamber: softly, pray."],"id":807,"m":"Please lift me up and carry me away into another room. Gently, please."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":808},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"V","loc":"Another chamber.","id":809},{"t":"sd","text":"The King lying on a bed: Clarence, Gloucester, Warwick, and others in attendance.","id":810},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Let there be no noise made, my gentle friends;","Unless some dull and favourable hand","Will whisper music to my weary spirit."],"id":811,"m":"Let there be no noise, my gentle friends, unless some kind hand will softly play music to my weary spirit."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Call for the music in the other room."],"id":812,"m":"Call for the musicians in the other room."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Set me the crown upon my pillow here."],"id":813,"m":"Put the crown on my pillow here."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["His eye is hollow, and he changes much."],"id":814,"m":"His eyes are sunken, and he's changing a great deal."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Less noise! less noise!"],"id":815,"m":"Less noise! Less noise!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Prince Henry.","id":816},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Who saw the Duke of Clarence?"],"id":817,"m":"Has anyone seen the Duke of Clarence?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["I am here, brother, full of heaviness."],"id":818,"m":"I'm here, brother, full of sadness."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["How now! rain within doors, and none abroad!","How doth the king?"],"id":819,"m":"What's this? Rain indoors and none outside! How is the King?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Exceeding ill."],"id":820,"m":"Extremely ill."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["Heard he the good news yet? Tell it him."],"id":821,"m":"Has he heard the good news yet? Tell him."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["He alt'red much upon the hearing it."],"id":822,"m":"He changed a great deal when he heard it."},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["If he be sick with joy, he'll recover without physic."],"id":823,"m":"If he's sick with joy, he'll recover without medicine."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Not so much noise, my lords: sweet prince, speak low;","The king your father is disposed to sleep."],"id":824,"m":"Not so much noise, my lords. Sweet prince, speak quietly. The King your father wants to sleep."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["Let us withdraw into the other room."],"id":825,"m":"Let's withdraw into the other room."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Will't please your grace to go along with us?"],"id":826,"m":"Will your grace come with us?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["No; I will sit and watch here by the king."],"id":827,"m":"No, I'll sit here and watch beside the King."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but the Prince.","id":828},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","cont":1,"lines":["Why doth the crown lie there upon his pillow,","Being so troublesome a bedfellow?","O polish'd perturbation! golden care!","That keep'st the ports of slumber open wide","To many a watchful night! sleep with it now!","Yet not so sound and half so deeply sweet","As he whose brow with homely biggen bound","Snores out the watch of night. O majesty!","When thou dost pinch thy bearer, thou dost sit","Like a rich armour worn in heat of day,","That scalds with safety. By his gates of breath","There lies a downy feather which stirs not:","Did he suspire, that light and weightless down","Perforce must move. My gracious lord! my father!","This sleep is sound indeed; this is a sleep","That from this golden rigol hath divorced","So many English kings. Thy due from me","Is tears and heavy sorrows of the blood,","Which nature, love, and filial tenderness,","Shall, O dear father, pay thee plenteously:","My due from thee is this imperial crown,","Which, as immediate from thy place and blood,","Derives itself to me. Lo, here it sits,","Which God shall guard: and put the world's whole strength","Into one giant arm, it shall not force","This lineal honour from me: this from thee","Will I to mine leave, as 'tis left to me."],"id":829,"m":"Why does the crown lie there on his pillow, when it's such a troublesome bedfellow? Oh, polished trouble! Golden worry! You keep the gates of sleep wide open through many wakeful nights. Sleep with it now! And yet not so soundly, and not half so sweetly, as the man who snores the night away with a plain nightcap tied round his head. Oh majesty! When you pinch the one who wears you, you sit like rich armour worn in the heat of the day, which scalds while it protects. At the door of his breath lies a downy feather that doesn't move. If he breathed, that light, weightless down would have to stir. My gracious lord! My father! This sleep is sound indeed. This is the sleep that has divorced so many English kings from this golden circle. What I owe you is tears and the heavy grief of my blood, which nature, love and a son's tenderness will pay you in full, oh dear father. What you owe me is this imperial crown, which comes down to me as next in your place and blood. Look, here it sits (he puts it on), and God will guard it. Even if the whole world's strength were put into one giant arm, it shall not wrench this inherited honour from me. As it's left to me by you, so I'll leave it to my heir."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":830},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Warwick! Gloucester! Clarence!"],"id":831,"m":"Warwick! Gloucester! Clarence!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Warwick, Gloucester, Clarence, and the rest.","id":832},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["Doth the king call?"],"id":833,"m":"Is the King calling?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["What would your majesty? How fares your grace?"],"id":834,"m":"What does your Majesty want? How is your grace?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Why did you leave me here alone, my lords?"],"id":835,"m":"Why did you leave me alone here, my lords?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["We left the prince my brother here, my liege,","Who undertook to sit and watch by you."],"id":836,"m":"We left the Prince my brother here, my lord, who said he'd sit and watch beside you."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["The Prince of Wales! Where is he? let me see him:","He is not here."],"id":837,"m":"The Prince of Wales! Where is he? Let me see him. He isn't here."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["This door is open; he is gone this way."],"id":838,"m":"This door is open. He's gone this way."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["He came not through the chamber where we stay'd."],"id":839,"m":"He didn't come through the room where we were."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Where is the crown? who took it from my pillow?"],"id":840,"m":"Where is the crown? Who took it from my pillow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["When we withdrew, my liege, we left it here."],"id":841,"m":"When we went out, my lord, we left it here."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["The prince hath ta'en it hence: go, seek him out.","Is he so hasty that he doth suppose","My sleep my death?","Find him, my lord of Warwick; chide him hither."],"id":842,"m":"The Prince has taken it away. Go and find him. Is he so hasty that he takes my sleep for my death? Find him, my lord of Warwick, and scold him back here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Warwick.","id":843},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","cont":1,"lines":["This part of his conjoins with my disease,","And helps to end me. See, sons, what things you are!","How quickly nature falls into revolt","When gold becomes her object!","For this the foolish over-careful fathers","Have broke their sleep with thoughts, their brains with care,","Their bones with industry;","For this they have engross'd and piled up","The canker'd heaps of strange-achieved gold;","For this they have been thoughtful to invest","Their sons with arts and martial exercises;","When, like the bee, tolling from every flower","The virtuous sweets,","Our thighs pack'd with wax, our mouths with honey,","We bring it to the hive, and, like the bees,","Are murdered for our pains. This bitter taste","Yields his engrossments to the ending father."],"id":844,"m":"This action of his joins forces with my illness and helps finish me off. See, sons, what you are like! How quickly nature rebels when gold becomes its goal! This is what foolish, over-anxious fathers have broken their sleep with worrying for, broken their brains with care and their bones with work. For this they have hoarded and piled up rusting heaps of gold got by strange means; for this they have taken care to give their sons learning and military training. Like the bee, we collect sweetness from every flower, our thighs packed with wax and our mouths with honey; we bring it to the hive, and, like the bees, we are murdered for our trouble. This is the bitter taste that his hoarding leaves the dying father."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Warwick.","id":845},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","cont":1,"lines":["Now where is he that will not stay so long","Till his friend sickness hath determin'd me?"],"id":846,"m":"Now where is he who can't wait until his friend sickness has finished me off?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["My lord, I found the prince in the next room,","Washing with kindly tears his gentle cheeks,","With such a deep demeanour in great sorrow","That tyranny, which never quaff'd but blood,","Would, by beholding him, have wash'd his knife","With gentle eye-drops. He is coming hither."],"id":847,"m":"My lord, I found the Prince in the next room, washing his gentle cheeks with loving tears, looking so deeply sorrowful that tyranny, which has never drunk anything but blood, would have washed its knife with gentle tears at the sight of him. He's coming here."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["But wherefore did he take away the crown?"],"id":848,"m":"But why did he take away the crown?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-Enter Prince Henry.","id":849},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","cont":1,"lines":["Lo, where he comes. Come hither to me, Harry.","Depart the chamber, leave us here alone."],"id":850,"m":"Look, here he comes. Come here to me, Harry. Leave the room; leave us alone here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Warwick and the rest.","id":851},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["I never thought to hear you speak again."],"id":852,"m":"I never thought I'd hear you speak again."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Thy wish was father, Harry, to that thought:","I stay too long by thee, I weary thee.","Dost thou so hunger for mine empty chair","That thou wilt needs invest thee with my honours","Before thy hour be ripe? O foolish youth!","Thou seek'st the greatness that will overwhelm thee.","Stay but a little; for my cloud of dignity","Is held from falling with so weak a wind","That it will quickly drop: my day is dim.","Thou hast stolen that which after some few hours","Were thine without offence; and at my death","Thou hast seal'd up my expectation:","Thy life did manifest thou lovedst me not,","And thou wilt have me die assured of it.","Thou hidest a thousand daggers in thy thoughts","Which thou hast whetted on thy stony heart,","To stab at half an hour of my life.","What! canst thou not forbear me half an hour?","Then get thee gone and dig my grave thyself,","And bid the merry bells ring to thine ear","That thou art crowned, not that I am dead.","Let all the tears that should bedew my hearse","Be drops of balm to sanctify thy head:","Only compound me with forgotten dust;","Give that which gave thee life unto the worms.","Pluck down my officers, break my decrees;","For now a time is come to mock at form:","Harry the Fifth is crown'd: up, vanity!","Down, royal state! all you sage counsellors, hence!","And to the English court assemble now,","From every region, apes of idleness!","Now, neighbour confines, purge you of your scum:","Have you a ruffian that will swear, drink, dance,","Revel the night, rob, murder, and commit","The oldest sins the newest kind of ways?","Be happy, he will trouble you no more;","England shall double gild his treble guilt,","England shall give him office, honour, might;","For the fifth Harry from curb'd license plucks","The muzzle of restraint, and the wild dog","Shall flesh his tooth on every innocent.","O my poor kingdom, sick with civil blows!","When that my care could not withhold thy riots,","What wilt thou do when riot is thy care?","O, thou wilt be a wilderness again,","Peopled with wolves, thy old inhabitants!"],"id":853,"m":"Your wish was father to that thought, Harry. I stay alive too long for you; I tire you. Are you so hungry for my empty throne that you have to dress yourself in my honours before your time is ripe? Oh, foolish young man! You're seeking the greatness that will overwhelm you. Just wait a little, because my cloud of majesty is held up by so weak a wind that it will soon fall. My day is growing dark. You've stolen what in a few hours would have been yours without wrongdoing, and at my death you've confirmed what I expected of you. Your life showed you didn't love me, and you want me to die sure of it. You're hiding a thousand daggers in your thoughts, sharpened on your stony heart, to stab at half an hour of my life. What! Can't you put up with me for half an hour? Then go and dig my grave yourself, and tell the merry bells to ring in your ear that you're crowned, not that I'm dead. Let all the tears that should fall on my coffin be drops of holy oil to anoint your head. Just mix me with forgotten dust; give the one who gave you life to the worms. Pull down my officials, break my laws, for now the time has come to mock at order. Harry the Fifth is crowned: up with vanity! Down with royal dignity! All you wise counsellors, away! And now gather at the English court from every region, you apes of idleness! Now, neighbouring countries, clear out your scum. Have you got a ruffian who will swear, drink, dance, revel all night, rob, murder and commit the oldest sins in the newest ways? Be happy: he won't trouble you any more. England will double-gild his triple guilt; England will give him office, honour and power. For the fifth Harry takes the muzzle of restraint off licence, and the wild dog will sink his teeth into every innocent. Oh, my poor kingdom, sick with the blows of civil war! When my care could not keep your disorders down, what will you do when disorder is your king's only care? Oh, you'll be a wilderness again, peopled with wolves, your old inhabitants!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["O, pardon me, my liege! but for my tears,","The moist impediments unto my speech,","I had forestall'd this dear and deep rebuke","Ere you with grief had spoke and I had heard","The course of it so far. There is your crown:","And He that wears the crown immortally","Long guard it yours! If I affect it more","Than as your honour and as your renown,","Let me no more from this obedience rise,","Which my most inward true and duteous spirit","Teacheth, this prostrate and exterior bending.","God witness with me, when I here came in,","And found no course of breath within your majesty,","How cold it struck my heart! If I do feign,","O, let me in my present wildness die","And never live to show the incredulous world","The noble change that I have purposed!","Coming to look on you, thinking you dead,","And dead almost, my liege, to think you were,","I spake unto this crown as having sense,","And thus upbraided it: \"The care on thee depending","Hath fed upon the body of my father;","Therefore, thou best of gold art worst of gold:","Other, less fine in carat, is more precious,","Preserving life in medicine potable;","But thou, most fine, most honour'd, most renown'd,","Hast eat thy bearer up.\" Thus, my most royal liege,","Accusing it, I put it on my head,","To try with it, as with an enemy","That had before my face murder'd my father,","The quarrel of a true inheritor.","But if it did infect my blood with joy,","Or swell my thoughts to any strain of pride;","If any rebel or vain spirit of mine","Did with the least affection of a welcome","Give entertainment to the might of it,","Let God for ever keep it from my head","And make me as the poorest vassal is","That doth with awe and terror kneel to it!"],"id":854,"m":"Oh, forgive me, my lord! If it weren't for my tears, the wet obstacles to my speech, I'd have stopped this heartfelt, deep rebuke before you'd spoken in grief and I'd heard so much of it. There's your crown, and may He who wears the crown for ever keep it yours for a long time! If I want it for any other reason than as your honour and your fame, let me never rise again from this kneeling, which my deepest, truest and most dutiful spirit teaches this outward, bowing body. God is my witness how coldly it struck my heart when I came in here and found no breath in your Majesty! If I'm pretending, oh, let me die in my present wildness and never live to show the disbelieving world the noble change I've decided on! Coming to look at you, thinking you were dead, and almost dead myself, my lord, to think you were, I spoke to this crown as if it could understand, and scolded it like this: \"The care that comes with you has eaten up my father's body. So you, the best of gold, are the worst of gold. Other gold, less pure, is more precious, because it preserves life as a medicine you can drink. But you, most pure, most honoured, most famous, have eaten up the man who wore you.\" And so, my most royal lord, accusing it, I put it on my head to fight it, as I would an enemy who had murdered my father in front of me, as a true heir should. But if it filled my blood with joy or swelled my thoughts with any trace of pride, if any rebellious or vain part of me welcomed its power even slightly, let God keep it from my head for ever and make me like the poorest servant who kneels to it in awe and terror!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["O my son,","God put it in thy mind to take it hence,","That thou mightst win the more thy father's love,","Pleading so wisely in excuse of it!","Come hither, Harry, sit thou by my bed;","And hear, I think, the very latest counsel","That ever I shall breathe. God knows, my son,","By what by-paths and indirect crook'd ways","I met this crown; and I myself know well","How troublesome it sat upon my head.","To thee it shall descend with better quiet,","Better opinion, better confirmation;","For all the soil of the achievement goes","With me into the earth. It seem'd in me","But as an honour snatch'd with boisterous hand,","And I had many living to upbraid","My gain of it by their assistances;","Which daily grew to quarrel and to bloodshed,","Wounding supposed peace: all these bold fears","Thou see'st with peril I have answered;","For all my reign hath been but as a scene","Acting that argument: and now my death","Changes the mode; for what in me was purchased,","Falls upon thee in a more fairer sort;","So thou the garland wear'st successively.","Yet, though thou stand'st more sure than I could do,","Thou art not firm enough, since griefs are green;","And all my friends, which thou must make thy friends,","Have but their stings and teeth newly ta'en out;","By whose fell working I was first advanced","And by whose power I well might lodge a fear","To be again displaced: which to avoid,","I cut them off; and had a purpose now","To lead out many to the Holy Land,","Lest rest and lying still might make them look","Too near unto my state. Therefore, my Harry,","Be it thy course to busy giddy minds","With foreign quarrels; that action, hence borne out,","May waste the memory of the former days.","More would I, but my lungs are wasted so","That strength of speech is utterly denied me.","How I came by the crown, O God, forgive;","And grant it may with thee in true peace live!"],"id":855,"m":"Oh, my son, God put it in your mind to take it away, so that you could win your father's love all the more by pleading so wisely in your own defence! Come here, Harry, sit by my bed and hear what I think is the very last advice I'll ever breathe. God knows, my son, by what side paths and indirect, crooked ways I came by this crown, and I myself know well how uneasily it sat on my head. It will come to you more quietly, with better reputation and firmer support, because all the dirt of getting it goes with me into the earth. In me it seemed only an honour snatched with a violent hand, and there were many alive to reproach me for having got it with their help, which every day grew into quarrels and bloodshed, wounding what was meant to be peace. You see how I have met all these bold dangers at great risk, because my whole reign has been like a play acting out that story. Now my death changes the situation, because what I got by my own action comes to you in a fairer way, so you wear the crown by right of succession. Still, though you stand more securely than I could, you're not secure enough while grievances are fresh. All my allies, whom you must make your allies, have only just had their stings and teeth pulled out. Their fierce efforts first raised me up, and I had good reason to fear their power might throw me down again. To avoid that, I cut them down, and I was planning now to lead many of them to the Holy Land, in case rest and idleness made them look too closely at my position. So, my Harry, make it your policy to keep restless minds busy with foreign wars, so that action abroad may wipe out the memory of former days. I would say more, but my lungs are so worn out that I have no strength left to speak. How I came by the crown, oh God, forgive; and grant that it may live in true peace with you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["My gracious liege,","You won it, wore it, kept it, gave it me;","Then plain and right must my possession be:","Which I with more than with a common pain","'Gainst all the world will rightfully maintain."],"id":856,"m":"My gracious lord, you won it, wore it, kept it and gave it to me. Then my possession must be clear and right, and I will defend it rightfully against the whole world with more than common effort."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lord John of Lancaster.","id":857},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Look, look, here comes my John of Lancaster."],"id":858,"m":"Look, look, here comes my John of Lancaster."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["Health, peace, and happiness to my royal father!"],"id":859,"m":"Health, peace and happiness to my royal father!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Thou bring'st me happiness and peace, son John;","But health, alack, with youthful wings is flown","From this bare wither'd trunk: upon thy sight","My worldly business makes a period.","Where is my Lord of Warwick?"],"id":860,"m":"You bring me happiness and peace, son John, but health, alas, has flown on young wings from this bare, withered trunk. Now that I've seen you, my worldly business comes to an end. Where is my Lord of Warwick?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HAL","lines":["My Lord of Warwick!"],"id":861,"m":"My Lord of Warwick!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Warwick, and others.","id":862},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Doth any name particular belong","Unto the lodging where I first did swoon?"],"id":863,"m":"Does the room where I first fainted have any particular name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["'Tis call'd Jerusalem, my noble lord."],"id":864,"m":"It's called Jerusalem, my noble lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Laud be to God! even there my life must end.","It hath been prophesied to me many years,","I should not die but in Jerusalem;","Which vainly I supposed the Holy Land:","But bear me to that chamber; there I'll lie;","In that Jerusalem shall Harry die."],"id":865,"m":"Praise be to God! That's where my life must end. It was prophesied to me many years ago that I would die only in Jerusalem, which I foolishly thought meant the Holy Land. But carry me to that room; I'll lie there. In that Jerusalem Harry will die."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":866},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"Gloucestershire. Shallow's house.","id":867},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Shallow, Falstaff, Bardolph, and Page.","id":868},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["By cock and pie, sir, you shall not away to-night.","What, Davy, I say!"],"id":869,"m":"By cock and pie, sir, you won't go away tonight. Hey, Davy, I say!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["You must excuse me, Master Robert Shallow."],"id":870,"m":"You must excuse me, Master Robert Shallow."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["I will not excuse you; you shall not be excused; excuses","shall not be admitted; there is no excuse shall serve; you shall","not be excused. Why, Davy!"],"id":871,"m":"I won't excuse you. You won't be excused. Excuses won't be allowed. No excuse will do. You won't be excused. Hey, Davy!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Davy.","id":872},{"t":"sp","who":"DAVY","lines":["Here, sir."],"id":873,"m":"Here, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Davy, Davy, Davy, Davy, let me see, Davy; let me see,","Davy; let me see: yea, marry, William cook, bid him come hither.","Sir John, you shall not be excused."],"id":874,"m":"Davy, Davy, Davy, Davy, let me see, Davy; let me see, Davy; let me see. Yes, of course: William the cook, tell him to come here. Sir John, you won't be excused."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAVY","lines":["Marry, sir, thus; those precepts cannot be served; and,","again, sir, shall we sow the headland with wheat?"],"id":875,"m":"Well, sir, it's like this: those legal summonses can't be delivered. And again, sir, shall we sow the edge of the field with wheat?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["With red wheat, Davy. But for William cook: are there no","young pigeons?"],"id":876,"m":"With red wheat, Davy. But as for William the cook: are there any young pigeons?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DAVY","lines":["Yes, sir. Here is now the smith's note for shoeing and","plough-irons."],"id":877,"m":"Yes, sir. Here's the blacksmith's bill for shoeing the horses and for plough blades."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Let it be cast and paid. Sir John, you shall not be excused."],"id":878,"m":"Have it added up and paid. Sir John, you won't be excused."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAVY","lines":["Now, sir, a new link to the bucket must needs be had: and, sir, do","you mean to stop any of William's wages, about the sack he lost the","other day at Hinckley fair?"],"id":879,"m":"Now, sir, we have to have a new link for the well-bucket chain. And, sir, do you mean to stop any of William's wages for the sack he lost the other day at Hinckley fair?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["A' shall answer it. Some pigeons, Davy, a couple of short-legg'd","hens, a joint of mutton, and any pretty little tiny kickshaws,","tell William cook."],"id":880,"m":"He'll pay for it. Some pigeons, Davy, a couple of short-legged hens, a joint of mutton, and any pretty little tiny fancy dishes; tell William the cook."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAVY","lines":["Doth the man of war stay all night, sir?"],"id":881,"m":"Is the soldier staying all night, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Yea, Davy. I will use him well: a friend i' the court is better","than a penny in purse. Use his men well, Davy; for they are","arrant knaves, and will backbite."],"id":882,"m":"Yes, Davy. I'll treat him well. A friend at court is better than a penny in your purse. Treat his men well, Davy, because they're out-and-out rogues and will speak ill of us behind our backs."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAVY","lines":["No worse than they are backbitten, sir; for they have marvellous","foul linen."],"id":883,"m":"No worse than they're bitten in the back themselves, sir, because their shirts are wonderfully lousy."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Well conceited, Davy: about thy business, Davy."],"id":884,"m":"Very witty, Davy. Get on with your work, Davy."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAVY","lines":["I beseech you, sir, to countenance William Visor of Woncot","against Clement Perkes of the hill."],"id":885,"m":"Please, sir, support William Visor of Woncot in his case against Clement Perkes of the hill."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["There is many complaints, Davy, against that Visor: that","Visor is an arrant knave, on my knowledge."],"id":886,"m":"There are a lot of complaints against that Visor, Davy. That Visor is an out-and-out rogue, to my knowledge."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAVY","lines":["I grant your worship that he is a knave, sir; but yet, God forbid,","sir, but a knave should have some countenance at his friend's request.","An honest man, sir, is able to speak for himself, when a knave is not.","I have served your worship truly, sir, this eight years; and if I cannot","once or twice in a quarter bear out a knave against an honest man, I","have but a very little credit with your worship.","The knave is mine honest friend, sir; therefore, I beseech your worship,","let him be countenanced."],"id":887,"m":"I grant your worship that he's a rogue, sir. But still, God forbid, sir, that a rogue shouldn't have a little support when his friend asks for it. An honest man, sir, can speak for himself; a rogue can't. I've served your worship faithfully for eight years, sir, and if I can't once or twice a quarter back a rogue against an honest man, I have very little credit with your worship. The rogue is my honest friend, sir, so I beg your worship, let him be supported."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Go to; I say he shall have no wrong. Look about, Davy."],"id":888,"m":"Go on, I tell you he'll come to no harm. See to things, Davy."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Davy.","id":889},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","cont":1,"lines":["Where are you, Sir John? Come, come, come, off with your boots.","Give me your hand, Master Bardolph."],"id":890,"m":"Where are you, Sir John? Come, come, come, off with your boots. Give me your hand, Master Bardolph."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["I am glad to see your worship."],"id":891,"m":"I'm glad to see your worship."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["I thank thee with all my heart, kind Master Bardolph: and","welcome, my tall fellow [to the Page] Come, Sir John."],"id":892,"m":"I thank you with all my heart, kind Master Bardolph. And welcome, my fine fellow (to the Page). Come, Sir John."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I'll follow you, good Master Robert Shallow."],"id":893,"m":"I'll follow you, good Master Robert Shallow."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Shallow.","id":894},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","cont":1,"lines":["Bardolph, look to our horses."],"id":895,"m":"Bardolph, see to our horses."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Bardolph and Page.","id":896},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","cont":1,"lines":["If I were sawed into quantities, I should make four dozen of such","bearded hermits' staves as Master Shallow. It is a wonderful thing to","see the semblable coherence of his men's spirits and his: they, by","observing of him, do bear themselves like foolish justices: he, by","conversing with them, is turned into a justice-like serving-man:","their spirits are so married in conjunction with the participation of","society that they flock together in consent, like so many wild-geese.","If I had a suit to Master Shallow, I would humour his men with the","imputation of being near their master: if to his men, I would curry","with Master Shallow that no man could better command his servants.","It is certain that either wise bearing or ignorant carriage is","caught, as men take diseases, one of another: therefore let men take","heed of their company. I will devise matter enough out of this Shallow","to keep Prince Harry in continual laughter the wearing out of six","fashions, which is four terms, or two actions; and a' shall laugh","without intervallums.","O, it is much that a lie with a slight oath and a jest with a sad brow","will do with a fellow that never had the ache in his shoulders!","O, you shall see him laugh till his face be like a wet cloak ill laid up!"],"id":897,"m":"If I were sawn into pieces, I'd make four dozen bearded hermits' staffs like Master Shallow. It's a wonderful thing to see how his men's spirits match his. By watching him, they behave like foolish justices; by talking with them, he has turned into a justice-like servant. Their spirits are so married together by keeping company that they flock together in agreement like a lot of wild geese. If I wanted a favour from Master Shallow, I'd flatter his men by suggesting they were close to their master; if I wanted one from his men, I'd flatter Master Shallow that no man could command his servants better. It's certain that wise behaviour or ignorant manners are caught from one another, the way people catch diseases, so men should be careful about the company they keep. I'll make up enough material out of this Shallow to keep Prince Harry laughing for as long as it takes to wear out six fashions, which is four law terms, or two lawsuits, and he'll laugh without a break. Oh, it's amazing what a lie with a little oath, and a joke told with a straight face, will do to a young fellow who's never had aching shoulders! Oh, you'll see him laugh till his face is like a wet cloak badly folded!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["[Within] Sir John!"],"id":898,"m":"(Offstage) Sir John!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I come, Master Shallow; I come, Master Shallow."],"id":899,"m":"I'm coming, Master Shallow; I'm coming, Master Shallow."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":900},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"Westminster. The palace.","id":901},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Warwick and the Lord Chief-Justice, meeting.","id":902},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["How now, my lord chief-justice! whither away?"],"id":903,"m":"Hello, my Lord Chief Justice! Where are you going?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["How doth the king?"],"id":904,"m":"How is the King?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Exceeding well; his cares are now all ended."],"id":905,"m":"Extremely well. His worries are all over now."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["I hope, not dead."],"id":906,"m":"Not dead, I hope."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["He's walk'd the way of nature;","And to our purposes he lives no more."],"id":907,"m":"He's gone the way of nature, and as far as we're concerned he lives no longer."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["I would his Majesty had call'd me with him:","The service that I truly did his life","Hath left me open to all injuries."],"id":908,"m":"I wish his Majesty had called me with him. The loyal service I did him while he lived has left me exposed to every kind of injury."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Indeed I think the young king loves you not."],"id":909,"m":"Indeed, I think the young King doesn't love you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["I know he doth not, and do arm myself","To welcome the condition of the time,","Which cannot look more hideously upon me","Than I have drawn it in my fantasy."],"id":910,"m":"I know he doesn't, and I'm preparing myself to meet the way things are now, which can't look more hideous to me than I've pictured it in my imagination."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lancaster, Clarence, Gloucester, Westmoreland, and others.","id":911},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Here comes the heavy issue of dead Harry:","O that the living Harry had the temper","Of him, the worst of these three gentlemen!","How many nobles then should hold their places,","That must strike sail to spirits of vile sort!"],"id":912,"m":"Here come the sad sons of the dead Harry. Oh, if only the living Harry had the temperament of the worst of these three gentlemen! How many nobles would then keep their positions who will have to lower their sails to men of low character!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["O God, I fear all will be overturn'd!"],"id":913,"m":"Oh God, I'm afraid everything will be overturned!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["Good morrow, cousin Warwick, good morrow."],"id":914,"m":"Good morning, cousin Warwick, good morning."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER AND CLARENCE","lines":["Good morrow, cousin."],"id":915,"m":"Good morning, cousin."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["We meet like men that had forgot to speak."],"id":916,"m":"We meet like men who've forgotten how to speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["We do remember; but our argument","Is all too heavy to admit much talk."],"id":917,"m":"We remember how, but our subject is far too heavy to allow much talk."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["Well, peace be with him that hath made us heavy!"],"id":918,"m":"Well, peace be with him who has made us sad!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Peace be with us, lest we be heavier!"],"id":919,"m":"Peace be with us, in case we're made sadder!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["O, good my lord, you have lost a friend indeed;","And I dare swear you borrow not that face","Of seeming sorrow, it is sure your own."],"id":920,"m":"Oh, my good lord, you've truly lost a friend, and I'd swear that face of sorrow isn't borrowed; it's surely your own."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["Though no man be assured what grace to find,","You stand in coldest expectation:","I am the sorrier; would 'twere otherwise."],"id":921,"m":"Although no one can be sure what favour he'll find, you have the coldest prospects. I'm all the sorrier; I wish it were otherwise."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLARENCE","lines":["Well, you must now speak Sir John Falstaff fair;","Which swims against your stream of quality."],"id":922,"m":"Well, you'll have to be polite to Sir John Falstaff now, which goes against the grain of your character."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Sweet Princes, what I did, I did in honour,","Led by the impartial conduct of my soul;","And never shall you see that I will beg","A ragged and forestall'd remission.","If truth and upright innocency fail me,","I'll to the king my master that is dead,","And tell him who hath sent me after him."],"id":923,"m":"Sweet princes, what I did, I did honourably, guided by the impartial direction of my soul, and you will never see me beg for a shabby pardon that's already been refused. If truth and upright innocence fail me, I'll go to my master the King who is dead and tell him who sent me after him."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Here comes the prince."],"id":924,"m":"Here comes the Prince."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter King Henry the Fifth, attended.","id":925},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Good morrow; and God save your majesty!"],"id":926,"m":"Good morning, and God save your Majesty!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY V","lines":["This new and gorgeous garment, majesty,","Sits not so easy on me as you think.","Brothers, you mix your sadness with some fear:","This is the English, not the Turkish court;","Not Amurath an Amurath succeeds,","But Harry Harry. Yet be sad, good brothers,","For, by my faith, it very well becomes you:","Sorrow so royally in you appears","That I will deeply put the fashion on","And wear it in my heart: why then, be sad;","But entertain no more of it, good brothers,","Than a joint burden laid upon us all.","For me, by heaven, I bid you be assured,","I'll be your father and your brother too;","Let me but bear your love, I'll bear your cares:","Yet weep that Harry's dead, and so will I;","But Harry lives, that shall convert those tears","By number into hours of happiness."],"id":927,"m":"This new and gorgeous garment, majesty, doesn't sit as easily on me as you think. Brothers, you mix your sadness with some fear. This is the English court, not the Turkish one, where one Sultan Amurath succeeds another and kills his brothers; it's Harry succeeding Harry. But be sad, good brothers, because honestly it suits you very well. Sorrow looks so royal on you that I will put the fashion on deeply and wear it in my heart. So be sad; but don't take on more of it, good brothers, than a burden we all share. As for me, by heaven, I want you to be sure that I'll be your father and your brother too. Just let me have your love and I'll carry your cares. Weep that Harry's dead, and so will I; but there's a Harry alive who will turn each of those tears into an hour of happiness."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCES","lines":["We hope no otherwise from your majesty."],"id":928,"m":"We expect nothing else from your Majesty."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY V","lines":["You all look strangely on me: and you most;","You are, I think, assured I love you not."],"id":929,"m":"You all look at me strangely, and you most of all. I think you're sure I don't love you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["I am assured, if I be measured rightly,","Your majesty hath no just cause to hate me."],"id":930,"m":"I'm sure that, if I'm judged fairly, your Majesty has no good reason to hate me."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY V","lines":["No!","How might a prince of my great hopes forget","So great indignities you laid upon me?","What! rate, rebuke, and roughly send to prison","The immediate heir of England! Was this easy?","May this be wash'd in Lethe, and forgotten?"],"id":931,"m":"No! How could a prince with my great prospects forget the great humiliations you heaped on me? What! Scold, rebuke and roughly send to prison the immediate heir to England! Was that a small thing? Can this be washed in the river of forgetfulness and forgotten?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["I then did use the person of your father;","The image of his power lay then in me;","And, in the administration of his law,","Whiles I was busy for the commonwealth,","Your highness pleased to forget my place,","The majesty and power of law and justice,","The image of the king whom I presented,","And struck me in my very seat of judgement;","Whereon, as an offender to your father,","I gave bold way to my authority","And did commit you. If the deed were ill,","Be you contented, wearing now the garland,","To have a son set your decrees at nought,","To pluck down justice from your awful bench,","To trip the course of law and blunt the sword","That guards the peace and safety of your person;","Nay, more, to spurn at your most royal image,","And mock your workings in a second body.","Question your royal thoughts, make the case yours;","Be now the father and propose a son,","Hear your own dignity so much profaned,","See your most dreadful laws so loosely slighted,","Behold yourself so by a son disdain'd;","And then imagine me taking your part","And in your power soft silencing your son:","After this cold considerance, sentence me;","And, as you are a king, speak in your state","What I have done that misbecame my place,","My person, or my liege's sovereignty."],"id":932,"m":"I was then acting as your father's representative; the image of his power was in me. And while I was administering his law, busy for the good of the state, your Highness chose to forget my position, the majesty and power of law and justice, the image of the King whom I represented, and struck me in my very seat of judgement. So, as an offender against your father, I boldly used my authority and sent you to prison. If that was wrong, then be content, now that you wear the crown, to have a son of your own ignore your laws, pull justice down from your awe-inspiring bench, trip up the course of law and blunt the sword that guards the peace and safety of your person — no, more, to kick at your royal image and mock your actions in the person of your deputy. Ask your royal thoughts; make the case your own. Be the father now and imagine a son; hear your own dignity so profaned, see your most dreaded laws so carelessly ignored, see yourself so scorned by a son. And then imagine me taking your side and, using your power, gently silencing your son. After this cool consideration, sentence me, and, as you are a king, say officially what I did that was unfitting for my office, my person or my lord's sovereignty."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY V","lines":["You are right, justice, and you weigh this well;","Therefore still bear the balance and the sword:","And I do wish your honours may increase,","Till you do live to see a son of mine","Offend you and obey you, as I did.","So shall I live to speak my father's words:","\"Happy am I, that have a man so bold,","That dares do justice on my proper son;","And not less happy, having such a son,","That would deliver up his greatness so","Into the hands of justice.\" You did commit me:","For which I do commit into your hand","The unstained sword that you have used to bear;","With this remembrance, that you use the same","With the like bold, just and impartial spirit","As you have done 'gainst me. There is my hand.","You shall be as a father to my youth:","My voice shall sound as you do prompt mine ear,","And I will stoop and humble my intents","To your well-practised wise directions.","And, princes all, believe me, I beseech you;","My father is gone wild into his grave,","For in his tomb lie my affections;","And with his spirit sadly I survive,","To mock the expectation of the world,","To frustrate prophecies and to raze out","Rotten opinion, who hath writ me down","After my seeming. The tide of blood in me","Hath proudly flow'd in vanity till now:","Now doth it turn and ebb back to the sea,","Where it shall mingle with the state of floods,","And flow henceforth in formal majesty.","Now call we our high court of parliament:","And let us choose such limbs of noble counsel,","That the great body of our state may go","In equal rank with the best govern'd nation;","That war, or peace, or both at once, may be","As things acquainted and familiar to us;","In which you, father, shall have foremost hand.","Our coronation done, we will accite,","As I before remember'd, all our state:","And, God consigning to my good intents,","No prince nor peer shall have just cause to say,","God shorten Harry's happy life one day!"],"id":933,"m":"You're right, Justice, and you weigh this up well. So keep holding the scales and the sword. And I hope your honours will grow until you live to see a son of mine offend you and obey you as I did. Then I'll live to speak my father's words: \"I'm lucky to have a man so bold that he dares to do justice on my own son, and no less lucky to have a son who would hand his greatness over to the hands of justice like that.\" You sent me to prison, and for that I now put into your hand the unstained sword you have carried, with this reminder: that you use it with the same bold, just and impartial spirit as you have against me. There's my hand. You'll be like a father to my youth. My voice will say what you prompt in my ear, and I will bend and humble my intentions to your experienced, wise guidance. And, princes all, believe me, I beg you. My father has gone wild into his grave, because my wild impulses lie buried in his tomb, and I survive with his serious spirit to mock the world's expectations, to defeat the prophecies and to wipe out the rotten opinion that has judged me by how I seemed. The tide of blood in me has flowed proudly in vanity until now; now it turns and ebbs back to the sea, where it will mix with the majesty of the ocean and flow from now on in dignified majesty. Now we'll summon our high court of parliament, and let's choose such limbs of noble counsel that the great body of our state may rank equally with the best-governed nation, so that war, or peace, or both at once, may be familiar things to us. In this you, father, will have the leading hand. Once our coronation is over, we'll summon, as I said before, all our nobles. And, God approving my good intentions, no prince or lord shall have good reason to say, \"God shorten Harry's happy life by a single day!\""},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":934},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"III","loc":"Gloucestershire. Shallow's orchard.","id":935},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff, Shallow, Silence, Davy, Bardolph, and the Page.","id":936},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Nay, you shall see my orchard, where, in an arbour, we will eat","a last year's pippin of mine own graffing, with a dish of caraways,","and so forth: come, cousin Silence: and then to bed."],"id":937,"m":"No, you must see my orchard, where we'll sit in an arbour and eat one of last year's apples from a tree I grafted myself, with a dish of caraway seeds and so on. Come, cousin Silence, and then to bed."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["'Fore God, you have here a goodly dwelling and a rich."],"id":938,"m":"By God, you have a fine, rich house here."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Barren, barren, barren; beggars all, beggars all, Sir John:","marry, good air. Spread, Davy; spread, Davy: well said, Davy."],"id":939,"m":"Barren, barren, barren; beggars all, beggars all, Sir John. Mind you, the air's good. Lay the cloth, Davy; lay the cloth, Davy. Well done, Davy."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["This Davy serves you for good uses; he is your serving-man","and your husband."],"id":940,"m":"This Davy is useful to you in many ways. He's your servant and your farm manager."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["A good varlet, a good varlet, a very good varlet, Sir John:","by the mass, I have drunk too much sack at supper: a good","varlet. Now sit down, now sit down: come, cousin."],"id":941,"m":"A good servant, a good servant, a very good servant, Sir John. By the mass, I drank too much sack at supper. A good servant. Now sit down, now sit down. Come, cousin."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["Ah, sirrah! quoth-a, we shall","~Do nothing but eat, and make good cheer,"],"id":942,"m":"Ah, my friend! As the song says, we'll \"do nothing but eat, and make good cheer,\""},{"t":"sd","text":"Singing.","id":943},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","cont":1,"lines":["~And praise God for the merry year;","~When flesh is cheap and females dear,","~And lusty lads roam here and there","~So merrily,","~And ever among so merrily."],"id":944,"m":"\"And praise God for the merry year, when meat is cheap and women dear, and lusty lads roam here and there so merrily, and always so merrily.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["There's a merry heart! Good Master Silence, I'll give you","a health for that anon."],"id":945,"m":"There's a merry heart! Good Master Silence, I'll drink your health for that in a moment."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Give Master Bardolph some wine, Davy."],"id":946,"m":"Give Master Bardolph some wine, Davy."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAVY","lines":["Sweet sir, sit; I'll be with you anon; most sweet sir, sit.","Master page, good master page, sit. Proface!","What you want in meat, we'll have in drink:","but you must bear; the heart's all."],"id":947,"m":"Sweet sir, sit down; I'll be with you shortly. Most sweet sir, sit down. Master page, good master page, sit down. Much good may it do you! What we lack in food we'll make up in drink. But you must put up with it; the welcome is what matters."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":948},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Be merry, Master Bardolph; and, my little soldier there,","be merry."],"id":949,"m":"Be merry, Master Bardolph; and you, my little soldier there, be merry."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["~Be merry, be merry, my wife has all;"],"id":950,"m":"\"Be merry, be merry, my wife has all,\""},{"t":"sd","text":"Singing.","id":951},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","cont":1,"lines":["~For women are shrews, both short and tall;","~'Tis merry in hall when beards wag all;","~And welcome merry Shrove-tide.","~Be merry, be merry."],"id":952,"m":"\"For women are shrews, both short and tall; it's merry in the hall when all the beards wag; and welcome, merry Shrovetide. Be merry, be merry.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I did not think Master Silence had been a man of this mettle."],"id":953,"m":"I didn't think Master Silence was a man of this spirit."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["Who, I? I have been merry twice and once ere now."],"id":954,"m":"Who, me? I've been merry once or twice before now."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Davy.","id":955},{"t":"sp","who":"DAVY","lines":["There's a dish of leather-coats for you. [To Bardolph]"],"id":956,"m":"(To Bardolph) Here's a dish of russet apples for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Davy!"],"id":957,"m":"Davy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DAVY","lines":["Your worship! I'll be with you straight [To Bardolph]","A cup of wine, sir?"],"id":958,"m":"Your worship! I'll be with you at once. (To Bardolph) A cup of wine, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["~A cup of wine that's brisk and fine,"],"id":959,"m":"\"A cup of wine that's brisk and fine,\""},{"t":"sd","text":"Singing.","id":960},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","cont":1,"lines":["~And drink unto the leman mine;","~And a merry heart lives long-a."],"id":961,"m":"\"And drink to my sweetheart, and a merry heart lives long.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Well said, Master Silence."],"id":962,"m":"Well said, Master Silence."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["An we shall be merry, now comes in the sweet o' the night."],"id":963,"m":"If we're going to be merry, now comes the sweetest part of the night."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Health and long life to you, Master Silence!"],"id":964,"m":"Health and long life to you, Master Silence!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["~Fill the cup, and let it come,"],"id":965,"m":"\"Fill the cup and pass it on,\""},{"t":"sd","text":"Singing.","id":966},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","cont":1,"lines":["~I'll pledge you a mile to the bottom."],"id":967,"m":"\"I'll drink to you though it's a mile to the bottom.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Honest Bardolph, welcome: if thou wantest anything and","wilt not call, beshrew thy heart. Welcome, my little tiny thief [to the Page]","and welcome indeed too. I'll drink to Master Bardolph, and to all","the cavaleros about London."],"id":968,"m":"Honest Bardolph, welcome. If you want anything and don't ask for it, curse you. Welcome, my little tiny thief (to the Page), and welcome indeed too. I'll drink to Master Bardolph and to all the fine gentlemen in London."},{"t":"sp","who":"DAVY","lines":["I hope to see London once ere I die."],"id":969,"m":"I hope to see London once before I die."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["An I might see you there, Davy,—"],"id":970,"m":"If I could see you there, Davy —"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["By the mass, you'll crack a quart together, ha! will you not,","Master Bardolph?"],"id":971,"m":"By the mass, you'll down a quart together, ha! Won't you, Master Bardolph?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["Yea, sir, in a pottle-pot."],"id":972,"m":"Yes, sir, from a two-quart pot."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["By God's liggens, I thank thee: the knave will stick by thee, I","can assure thee that. A' will not out; he is true bred."],"id":973,"m":"By God's little eyelids, thank you. The rascal will stick by you, I can assure you of that. He won't back out; he's well bred."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["And I'll stick by him, sir."],"id":974,"m":"And I'll stick by him, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Why, there spoke a king. Lack nothing: be merry."],"id":975,"m":"Why, that's spoken like a king. Want for nothing; be merry."},{"t":"sd","text":"Knocking within.","id":976},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","cont":1,"lines":["Look who's at door there, ho! who knocks?"],"id":977,"m":"Look who's at the door there! Who's knocking?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Davy.","id":978},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Why, now you have done me right."],"id":979,"m":"(To Silence, seeing him empty a full glass) Why, now you've matched me drink for drink."},{"t":"sd","text":"To Silence, seeing him take off a bumper.","id":980},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["~Do me right,"],"id":981,"m":"\"Match me drink for drink,\""},{"t":"sd","text":"Singing.","id":982},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","cont":1,"lines":["~And dub me knight:","~Samingo.","Is't not so?"],"id":983,"m":"\"And dub me knight: Samingo.\" Isn't that right?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["'Tis so."],"id":984,"m":"That's right."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["Is't so? Why then, say an old man can do somewhat."],"id":985,"m":"Is it? Well then, admit that an old man can still do something."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Davy.","id":986},{"t":"sp","who":"DAVY","lines":["An't please your worship, there's one Pistol come from the","court with news."],"id":987,"m":"If it please your worship, there's a man called Pistol come from the court with news."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["From the court? Let him come in."],"id":988,"m":"From the court? Let him come in."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pistol.","id":989},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","cont":1,"lines":["How now, Pistol!"],"id":990,"m":"What's up, Pistol!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Sir John, God save you!"],"id":991,"m":"Sir John, God save you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What wind blew you hither, Pistol?"],"id":992,"m":"What wind blew you here, Pistol?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Not the ill wind which blows no man to good. Sweet knight,","thou art now one of the greatest men in this realm."],"id":993,"m":"Not the ill wind that blows nobody any good. Sweet knight, you are now one of the greatest men in this kingdom."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["By'r lady, I think a' be, but goodman Puff of Barson."],"id":994,"m":"By our Lady, I think he is, except for goodman Puff of Barson."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Puff!","Puff in thy teeth, most recreant coward base!","Sir John, I am thy Pistol and thy friend,","And helter-skelter have I rode to thee,","And tidings do I bring and lucky joys","And golden times and happy news of price."],"id":995,"m":"Puff! Puff in your teeth, you base, cowardly deserter! Sir John, I'm your Pistol and your friend, and I've ridden helter-skelter to you, and I bring news and lucky joys and golden times and precious happy news."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I pray thee now, deliver them like a man of this world."],"id":996,"m":"Please, now, deliver it like a man of this world."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["A foutre for the world and worldlings base!","I speak of Africa and golden joys."],"id":997,"m":"Damn the world and its low worldlings! I speak of Africa and golden joys."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["O base Assyrian knight, what is thy news?","Let King Cophetua know the truth thereof."],"id":998,"m":"Oh, low Assyrian knight, what's your news? Let King Cophetua know the truth of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SILENCE","lines":["~And Robin Hood, Scarlet, and John."],"id":999,"m":"\"And Robin Hood, Scarlet and John.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Shall dunghill curs confront the Helicons?","And shall good news be baffled?","Then, Pistol, lay thy head in Furies' lap."],"id":1000,"m":"Shall dunghill dogs stand up to the Muses? And shall good news be disgraced? Then, Pistol, lay your head in the lap of the Furies."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Honest gentleman, I know not your breeding."],"id":1001,"m":"Honest gentleman, I don't know your upbringing."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Why then, lament therefore."],"id":1002,"m":"Well then, lament it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Give me pardon, sir: if, sir, you come with news from the","court, I take it there's but two ways, either to utter them, or","conceal them.","I am, sir, under the king, in some authority."],"id":1003,"m":"Excuse me, sir. If you've come with news from the court, I take it there are only two ways: either to tell it or to keep it quiet. I have some authority, sir, under the King."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Under which king, Besonian? speak, or die."],"id":1004,"m":"Under which king, you beggar? Speak, or die."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Under King Harry."],"id":1005,"m":"Under King Harry."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Harry the Fourth? or Fifth?"],"id":1006,"m":"Harry the Fourth? Or Fifth?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Harry the Fourth."],"id":1007,"m":"Harry the Fourth."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["A foutre for thine office!","Sir John, thy tender lambkin now is king;","Harry the Fifth's the man. I speak the truth.","When Pistol lies, do this; and fig me, like","The bragging Spaniard."],"id":1008,"m":"Damn your office! Sir John, your tender little lamb is king now. Harry the Fifth's the man. I'm telling the truth. When Pistol lies, do this (he makes a rude gesture), and give me the fig like the bragging Spaniard."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["What, is the old king dead?"],"id":1009,"m":"What, is the old King dead?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["As nail in door: the things I speak are just."],"id":1010,"m":"As a doornail. What I say is true."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Away, Bardolph! saddle my horse. Master Robert Shallow,","choose what office thou wilt in the land, 'tis thine. Pistol, I","will double-charge thee with dignities."],"id":1011,"m":"Away, Bardolph! Saddle my horse. Master Robert Shallow, choose any office you like in the land; it's yours. Pistol, I'll load you with double honours."},{"t":"sp","who":"BARDOLPH","lines":["O joyful day!","I would not take a knighthood for my fortune."],"id":1012,"m":"Oh, joyful day! I wouldn't swap my fortune for a knighthood."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["What! I do bring good news."],"id":1013,"m":"What! Didn't I bring good news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Carry Master Silence to bed. Master Shallow, my Lord Shallow,—","be what thou wilt; I am fortune's steward—get on thy boots:","we'll ride all night. O sweet Pistol! Away, Bardolph!"],"id":1014,"m":"Carry Master Silence to bed. Master Shallow, my Lord Shallow — be whatever you like; I'm fortune's steward — get your boots on. We'll ride all night. Oh, sweet Pistol! Away, Bardolph!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Bardolph.","id":1015},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","cont":1,"lines":["Come, Pistol, utter more to me; and withal devise something to do","thyself good. Boot, boot, Master Shallow: I know the young king is","sick for me. Let us take any man's horses; the laws of England are at","my commandment. Blessed are they that have been my friends; and woe","to my lord chief-justice!"],"id":1016,"m":"Come, Pistol, tell me more, and think of something to do yourself some good. Boots, boots, Master Shallow! I know the young King is longing for me. Let's take anyone's horses; the laws of England are at my command. Blessed are those who have been my friends, and woe to my Lord Chief Justice!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Let vultures vile seize on his lungs also!","\"Where is the life that late I led?\" say they:","Why, here it is; welcome these pleasant days!"],"id":1017,"m":"Let vile vultures tear at his lungs too! \"Where is the life I used to lead?\" they say. Why, here it is. Welcome, these pleasant days!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1018},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"IV","loc":"London. A street.","id":1019},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Beadles, dragging in Hostess Quickly and Doll Tearsheet.","id":1020},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["No, thou arrant knave; I would to God that I might die,","that I might have thee hanged: thou hast drawn my shoulder out","of joint."],"id":1021,"m":"No, you out-and-out rogue. I wish to God I could die so that I could have you hanged. You've pulled my shoulder out of joint."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST BEADLE","lines":["The constables have delivered her over to me; and she shall have","whipping-cheer enough, I warrant her: there hath been a man","or two lately killed about her."],"id":1022,"m":"The constables have handed her over to me, and she'll get a good feast of whipping, I promise her. A man or two have been killed recently over her."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["Nut-hook, nut-hook, you lie. Come on; I'll tell thee what, thou","damned tripe-visaged rascal, an the child I now go with do","miscarry, thou wert better thou hadst struck thy mother, thou","paper-faced villain."],"id":1023,"m":"Nutcracker, nutcracker, you're lying. Come on, I'll tell you what, you damned tripe-faced rascal: if the child I'm carrying now miscarries, you'd have done better to hit your own mother, you paper-faced villain."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["O the Lord, that Sir John were come! he would make this a","bloody day to somebody. But I pray God the fruit of her womb","miscarry!"],"id":1024,"m":"Oh Lord, if only Sir John were here! He'd make this a bloody day for somebody. But I pray to God the fruit of her womb miscarries!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST BEADLE","lines":["If it do, you shall have a dozen of cushions again; you","have but eleven now. Come, I charge you both go with me; for the","man is dead that you and Pistol beat amongst you."],"id":1025,"m":"If it does, you'll have your dozen cushions back again; you've only got eleven now. Come on, I order you both to come with me, because the man you and Pistol beat between you is dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["I'll tell you what, you thin man in a censer, I will have you as","soundly swinged for this,—you blue-bottle rogue, you filthy famished","correctioner, if you be not swinged, I'll forswear half-kirtles."],"id":1026,"m":"I'll tell you what, you thin man in an incense-burner, I'll have you soundly beaten for this — you bluebottle rogue, you filthy starving whipper — if you're not beaten, I'll give up wearing skirts."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST BEADLE","lines":["Come, come, you she knight-errant, come."],"id":1027,"m":"Come on, come on, you female knight-errant, come on."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["O God, that right should thus overcome might! Well, of","sufferance comes ease."],"id":1028,"m":"Oh God, that right should overcome might like this! Well, after suffering comes relief."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["Come, you rogue, come; bring me to a justice."],"id":1029,"m":"Come on, you rogue, come on. Take me to a magistrate."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Ay, come, you starved blood-hound."],"id":1030,"m":"Yes, come on, you starved bloodhound."},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["Goodman death, goodman bones!"],"id":1031,"m":"Mister Death, mister skeleton!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["Thou atomy, thou!"],"id":1032,"m":"You bag of bones, you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DOLL","lines":["Come, you thin thing; come, you rascal!"],"id":1033,"m":"Come on, you thin thing; come on, you rascal!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST BEADLE","lines":["Very well."],"id":1034,"m":"Very well."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1035},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"V","loc":"A public place near Westminster Abbey.","id":1036},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter two Grooms, strewing rushes.","id":1037},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GROOM","lines":["More rushes, more rushes."],"id":1038,"m":"More rushes, more rushes."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GROOM","lines":["The trumpets have sounded twice."],"id":1039,"m":"The trumpets have sounded twice."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GROOM","lines":["'Twill be two o'clock ere they come from the","coronation: dispatch, dispatch."],"id":1040,"m":"It'll be two o'clock before they come back from the coronation. Hurry, hurry."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1041},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Falstaff, Shallow, Pistol, Bardolph, and Page.","id":1042},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Stand here by me, Master Robert Shallow; I will make the","king do you grace: I will leer upon him as a' comes by; and do","but mark the countenance that he will give me."],"id":1043,"m":"Stand here beside me, Master Robert Shallow. I'll make the King show you favour. I'll give him a look as he comes by, and just watch the expression he gives me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["God bless thy lungs, good knight!"],"id":1044,"m":"God bless your lungs, good knight!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Come here, Pistol; stand behind me. O, if I had had to have","made new liveries, I would have bestowed the thousand pound I","borrowed of you. But 'tis no matter; this poor show doth better:","this doth infer the zeal I had to see him."],"id":1045,"m":"Come here, Pistol; stand behind me. Oh, if I'd had time to have new uniforms made, I'd have spent the thousand pounds I borrowed from you. But it doesn't matter. This shabby appearance is better; it shows how eager I was to see him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["It doth so."],"id":1046,"m":"It does."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["It shows my earnestness of affection,—"],"id":1047,"m":"It shows how earnest my affection is —"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["It doth so."],"id":1048,"m":"It does."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["My devotion,—"],"id":1049,"m":"My devotion —"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["It doth, it doth, it doth."],"id":1050,"m":"It does, it does, it does."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["As it were, to ride day and night; and not to deliberate, not to","remember, not to have patience to shift me,—"],"id":1051,"m":"As it were, to ride day and night, and not to stop to think, not to remember, not to have the patience to change my clothes —"},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["It is best, certain."],"id":1052,"m":"That's best, certainly."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["But to stand stained with travel, and sweating with desire to","see him; thinking of nothing else, putting all affairs else in","oblivion, as if there were nothing else to be done but to see him."],"id":1053,"m":"But to stand here stained from travelling and sweating with desire to see him, thinking of nothing else, forgetting all other business, as if there were nothing else to do but see him."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["'Tis \"semper idem,\" for \"obsque hoc nihil est:\" 'tis all in","every part."],"id":1054,"m":"It's \"always the same\", for \"without this there is nothing\". It's all in every part."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["'Tis so, indeed."],"id":1055,"m":"It is indeed."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["My knight, I will inflame thy noble liver,","And make thee rage.","Thy Doll, and Helen of thy noble thoughts,","Is in base durance and contagious prison;","Haled thither","By most mechanical and dirty hand:","Rouse up revenge from ebon den with fell Alecto's snake,","For Doll is in. Pistol speaks nought but truth."],"id":1056,"m":"My knight, I'll set your noble liver on fire and make you rage. Your Doll, the Helen of your noble thoughts, is locked up in a low, infectious prison, dragged there by the dirtiest working-class hands. Rouse revenge from her black den with fierce Alecto's snake, because Doll is inside. Pistol speaks nothing but the truth."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["I will deliver her."],"id":1057,"m":"I'll free her."},{"t":"sd","text":"Shouts, within, and the trumpets sound.","id":1058},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["There roar'd the sea, and trumpet-clangor sounds."],"id":1059,"m":"There roared the sea, and the trumpets blare."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the King and his train, the Lord Chief-Justice among them.","id":1060},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["God save thy grace, King Hal; my royal Hal!"],"id":1061,"m":"God save your grace, King Hal, my royal Hal!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["The heavens thee guard and keep, most royal imp of fame!"],"id":1062,"m":"May the heavens guard and keep you, most royal child of fame!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["God save thee, my sweet boy!"],"id":1063,"m":"God save you, my sweet boy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY V","lines":["My lord chief-justice, speak to that vain man."],"id":1064,"m":"My Lord Chief Justice, speak to that foolish man."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Have you your wits? know you what 'tis you speak?"],"id":1065,"m":"Are you in your right mind? Do you know what you're saying?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["My king! my Jove! I speak to thee, my heart!"],"id":1066,"m":"My king! My Jove! I'm speaking to you, my heart!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING HENRY V","lines":["I know thee not, old man: fall to thy prayers;","How ill white hairs become a fool and jester!","I have long dream'd of such a kind of man,","So surfeit-swell'd, so old, and so profane;","But, being awaked, I do despise my dream.","Make less thy body hence, and more thy grace;","Leave gormandizing; know the grave doth gape","For thee thrice wider than for other men.","Reply not to me with a fool-born jest:","Presume not that I am the thing I was;","For God doth know, so shall the world perceive,","That I have turn'd away my former self;","So will I those that kept me company.","When thou dost hear I am as I have been,","Approach me, and thou shalt be as thou wast,","The tutor and the feeder of my riots:","Till then, I banish thee, on pain of death,","As I have done the rest of my misleaders,","Not to come near our person by ten mile.","For competence of life I will allow you,","That lack of means enforce you not to evils:","And, as we hear you do reform yourselves,","We will, according to your strengths and qualities,","Give you advancement. Be it your charge, my lord,","To see perform'd the tenour of our word.","Set on."],"id":1067,"m":"I don't know you, old man. Say your prayers. How badly white hair suits a fool and a jester! I've long dreamed of a man like this, so swollen with excess, so old and so irreverent, but now that I'm awake I despise my dream. From now on make your body smaller and your virtue greater. Give up gluttony. Know that the grave gapes three times wider for you than for other men. Don't answer me with a jest born of folly. Don't imagine I'm the thing I was, because God knows, and the world will see, that I have turned away my former self, and so I will turn away those who kept me company. When you hear that I'm as I used to be, come to me, and you shall be what you were, the teacher and supplier of my riots. Until then I banish you, on pain of death, as I have the rest of those who led me astray, not to come within ten miles of me. I'll give you enough to live on, so that lack of money doesn't drive you to crime, and when we hear that you've reformed yourselves, we'll promote you according to your abilities and qualities. My lord, it will be your job to see that what we've said is carried out. Move on."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt King, &c.","id":1068},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Master Shallow, I owe you a thousand pounds."],"id":1069,"m":"Master Shallow, I owe you a thousand pounds."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["Yea, marry, Sir John; which I beseech you to let me have","home with me."],"id":1070,"m":"Yes, indeed, Sir John, which I beg you to let me take home with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["That can hardly be, Master Shallow. Do not you grieve at this;","I shall be sent for in private to him: look you, he must seem","thus to the world: fear not your advancements; I will be the man yet","that shall make you great."],"id":1071,"m":"That can hardly be, Master Shallow. Don't be upset by this. I'll be sent for in private. You see, he has to look like this to the world. Don't worry about your promotion; I'll still be the man to make you great."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["I cannot perceive how, unless you give me your doublet","and stuff me out with straw. I beseech you, good Sir John, let me","have five hundred of my thousand."],"id":1072,"m":"I can't see how, unless you give me your jacket and stuff me out with straw. I beg you, good Sir John, let me have five hundred of my thousand."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Sir, I will be as good as my word: this that you heard was","but a colour."],"id":1073,"m":"Sir, I'll be as good as my word. What you heard was only a pretence."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHALLOW","lines":["A colour that I fear you will die in, Sir John."],"id":1074,"m":"A pretence I'm afraid you'll die in, Sir John."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["Fear no colours: go with me to dinner: come, Lieutenant","Pistol; come, Bardolph: I shall be sent for soon at night."],"id":1075,"m":"Don't fear pretences. Come and have dinner with me. Come, Lieutenant Pistol; come, Bardolph. I'll be sent for early tonight."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Prince John, the Lord Chief-Justice; Officers with them.","id":1076},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["Go, carry Sir John Falstaff to the Fleet:","Take all his company along with him."],"id":1077,"m":"Go, take Sir John Falstaff to the Fleet prison. Take all his companions with him."},{"t":"sp","who":"FALSTAFF","lines":["My lord, my lord,—"],"id":1078,"m":"My lord, my lord —"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["I cannot now speak: I will hear you soon.","Take them away."],"id":1079,"m":"I can't speak now. I'll hear you soon. Take them away."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISTOL","lines":["Si fortuna me tormenta, spero me contenta."],"id":1080,"m":"If fortune torments me, hope contents me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but Prince John and the Lord Chief-Justice.","id":1081},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["I like this fair proceeding of the king's:","He hath intent his wonted followers","Shall all be very well provided for;","But all are banish'd till their conversations","Appear more wise and modest to the world."],"id":1082,"m":"I like this fair action of the King's. He intends that his usual followers will all be very well provided for, but they're all banished until their behaviour looks wiser and more decent to the world."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["And so they are."],"id":1083,"m":"And so they are."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["The king hath call'd his parliament, my lord."],"id":1084,"m":"The King has summoned his parliament, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","lines":["He hath."],"id":1085,"m":"He has."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRINCE JOHN","lines":["I will lay odds that, ere this year expire,","We bear our civil swords and native fire","As far as France: I heard a bird so sing,","Whose music, to my thinking, pleased the king.","Come, will you hence?"],"id":1086,"m":"I'll bet that before this year is over we'll carry our swords and our national fire as far as France. I heard a bird singing that song, and its music, I think, pleased the King. Come, will you go?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1087},{"t":"sd","text":"Epilogue. Enter a Dancer.","id":1088},{"t":"sp","who":"EPILOGUE","lines":["First my fear; then my courtesy; last my speech. My fear is, your","displeasure; my courtesy, my duty; and my speech, to beg your","pardons. If you look for a good speech now, you undo me: for","what I have to say is of mine own making; and what indeed I","should say will, I doubt, prove mine own marring. But to the","purpose, and so to the venture. Be it known to you, as it is very","well, I was lately here in the end of a displeasing play, to pray","your patience for it and to promise you a better. I meant indeed to","pay you with this; which, if like an ill venture it come unluckily","home, I break, and you, my gentle creditors, lose. Here I promised","you I would be and here I commit my body to your mercies: bate me","some and I will pay you some and, as most debtors do, promise you","infinitely.","If my tongue cannot entreat you to acquit me, will you command me to","use my legs? and yet that were but light payment, to dance out of","your debt. But a good conscience will make any possible satisfaction,","and so would I. All the gentlewomen here have forgiven me: if the","gentlemen will not, then the gentlemen do not agree with the","gentlewomen, which was never seen before in such an assembly.","One word more, I beseech you. If you be not too much cloy'd with fat","meat, our humble author will continue the story, with Sir John in it,","and make you merry with fair Katharine of France: where, for any","thing I know, Falstaff shall die of a sweat, unless already a' be","killed with your hard opinions; for Oldcastle died a martyr, and this","is not the man.","My tongue is weary; when my legs are too, I will bid you good night:","and so kneel down before you; but, indeed, to pray for the queen."],"id":1089,"m":"First my fear, then my bow, and last my speech. My fear is your displeasure; my bow, my duty; and my speech, to ask your pardon. If you're expecting a good speech now, you'll ruin me, because what I have to say is my own invention, and what I ought to say will, I'm afraid, be my undoing. But to the point, and so to the risk. Let me tell you, as you know very well, I was here recently at the end of an unpopular play, to ask your patience with it and promise you a better one. I meant to pay you back with this one; and if, like a failed trading voyage, it comes home unluckily, I'm bankrupt and you, my kind creditors, lose. I promised you I'd be here, and here I put myself at your mercy. Let me off some of it and I'll pay you some, and, like most debtors, promise you endlessly. If my tongue can't persuade you to let me off, will you order me to use my legs? And yet that would be light payment, to dance my way out of your debt. But a good conscience will make any repayment it can, and so would I. All the ladies here have forgiven me. If the gentlemen won't, then the gentlemen don't agree with the ladies, which has never been seen before in such a gathering. One word more, please. If you're not too sick of fat meat, our humble author will continue the story, with Sir John in it, and amuse you with fair Katharine of France, where, for all I know, Falstaff will die of a fever, unless he's already been killed by your harsh opinions; for Oldcastle died a martyr, and this is not the man. My tongue is tired; when my legs are too, I'll say good night to you. And so I kneel before you — but in fact to pray for the Queen."}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"Prince Hal (later King Henry V)","group":"The royal house","desc":"Henry, Prince of Wales, the hero of Shrewsbury, who has slipped back into Eastcheap with Poins. He is weary and half ashamed of his low company, and says his heart bleeds for his sick father though he cannot show it. He spies on Falstaff disguised as a waiter, then leaves at once when war news comes. At his father's bedside he takes the crown, thinking the King dead, and wins his father's forgiveness with a moving defence. As King Henry V he reassures his brothers, makes the Lord Chief Justice 'a father to my youth', and publicly rejects Falstaff: 'I know thee not, old man.'"},{"name":"King Henry IV","group":"The royal house","desc":"Henry Bolingbroke, now old, sick and sleepless. He cannot rest for the rebellions his usurpation has bred, and he still dreams of a crusade. He fears his son will make England a wilderness of riot. When he finds Hal has taken the crown he accuses him of wishing him dead, then, persuaded of his love, gives him his last advice: the crown came by 'by-paths and indirect crook'd ways', so keep restless lords busy with 'foreign quarrels'. He dies in the room called Jerusalem, fulfilling a prophecy he had misread."},{"name":"Prince John of Lancaster","group":"The royal house","desc":"The King's cool younger son, who leads the royal army against the Archbishop. At Gaultree he promises to redress the rebels' grievances, persuades them to disband their army, then arrests their leaders for treason. Falstaff calls him a 'sober-blooded boy' who never laughs. He approves Hal's banishment of Falstaff and predicts war in France."},{"name":"Thomas, Duke of Clarence, and Humphrey, Duke of Gloucester","group":"The royal house","desc":"The King's other sons. The King urges Clarence, Hal's favourite brother, to keep Hal's love and act as peacemaker among the brothers. With Gloucester he watches over the dying King and later fears what the new King will do."},{"name":"The Lord Chief Justice","group":"The King's side","desc":"England's senior judge, who once sent Prince Hal to prison for striking him. He tries to call Falstaff to account, sees through his evasions and makes him pay the Hostess. When the old King dies he expects to be ruined, but he defends his past actions so well that Henry V keeps him in office and treats him as a father. He carries out the order that sends Falstaff to the Fleet."},{"name":"Earl of Warwick","group":"The King's side","desc":"The King's trusted counsellor. He calms the King's fears about the rebels and about Hal, arguing that the Prince studies his companions 'like a strange tongue' only to cast them off. He finds Hal weeping beside the crown."},{"name":"Earl of Westmoreland","group":"The King's side","desc":"The King's kinsman, who goes between the armies at Gaultree, rebukes the Archbishop for turning his books to graves, and carries out the arrests. He brings the King the news of the rebels' defeat."},{"name":"Earl of Northumberland","group":"The rebels","desc":"Hotspur's father, who 'lies crafty-sick' at Warkworth while his son dies at Shrewsbury. Hearing the truth, he rages for chaos, yet when his wife and his son's widow beg him not to go, he flees to Scotland and fails the rebels a second time. He is defeated by the sheriff of Yorkshire."},{"name":"The Archbishop of York (Richard Scroop)","group":"The rebels","desc":"The leader of the new rising, who turns rebellion into a holy cause by appealing to the blood of King Richard. He presents the rebels' grievances in written articles and trusts Prince John's promise of redress, sends his army home, and is arrested for treason."},{"name":"Lord Mowbray","group":"The rebels","desc":"The Earl Marshal, son of the Duke of Norfolk whom Richard banished. He nurses his father's old quarrel with Bolingbroke and suspects that no peace with the King can last. He is right, and is arrested at Gaultree."},{"name":"Lord Hastings","group":"The rebels","desc":"A rebel lord who counts on Northumberland's support and argues that the King's forces are split three ways. He sends the rebel army home and is the first to be arrested for high treason."},{"name":"Lord Bardolph","group":"The rebels","desc":"A rebel lord who brings Northumberland false news of victory, and later warns the rebels not to repeat Hotspur's mistake of building on hopes, using the image of a man who plans a house he cannot afford."},{"name":"Lady Percy (Kate)","group":"The Percys","desc":"Hotspur's widow. In a passionate speech she reminds Northumberland how he abandoned his son, describes Hotspur as the mirror in which all young men dressed themselves, and persuades him not to join the rebels."},{"name":"Sir John Falstaff","group":"Eastcheap","desc":"The fat old knight, now famous for 'killing' Hotspur and given a command. He is older and sicker than in Part 1, worried about his urine, his gout and his purse, and he has a tiny page to carry his sword. He fobs off the Lord Chief Justice, borrows from the Hostess, flirts with Doll Tearsheet, takes bribes to free recruits, captures Colevile without a blow and praises sherris-sack. When Hal becomes king he rides through the night expecting power, and is banished in front of the crowd."},{"name":"Ned Poins","group":"Eastcheap","desc":"Hal's companion, who mocks Falstaff's letter and plots to spy on him disguised as a waiter. Falstaff says Hal loves him only because they are alike in being empty-headed."},{"name":"Mistress Quickly, the Hostess","group":"Eastcheap","desc":"Landlady of the Boar's Head tavern, a widow who has Falstaff arrested for debt and for breaking his promise to marry her, then lends him more money. She muddles her words ('honey-suckle villain', 'confirmities') and hates 'swaggerers'. At the end she is dragged off by the beadles with Doll."},{"name":"Doll Tearsheet","group":"Eastcheap","desc":"A prostitute who quarrels with Falstaff out of affection, rages at Pistol, sits on Falstaff's knee and tells him to think of heaven. She weeps when he leaves for the war, and is later arrested over a man beaten to death."},{"name":"Ancient Pistol","group":"Eastcheap","desc":"Falstaff's ensign (standard-bearer), a swaggering braggart who speaks in mangled lines from old plays. He is thrown out of the tavern after a fight with Doll, and later brings Falstaff the news that Hal is king."},{"name":"Bardolph","group":"Eastcheap","desc":"Falstaff's red-nosed corporal. He takes bribes from Mouldy and Bullcalf to free them from service and goes with Falstaff to the coronation, where he is arrested with him."},{"name":"Falstaff's Page","group":"Eastcheap","desc":"The tiny boy Hal gave Falstaff, who carries his sword and buckler and is already learning to be witty and cheeky."},{"name":"Justice Robert Shallow","group":"Gloucestershire","desc":"A foolish old country justice who knew Falstaff as a law student. He boasts about his wild youth at Clement's Inn, mourns old friends, helps Falstaff choose recruits, and lends him a thousand pounds in hope of advancement at court. Falstaff sees through him and plans to use him as a joke for Hal."},{"name":"Justice Silence","group":"Gloucestershire","desc":"Shallow's cousin and fellow justice, who says almost nothing until he gets drunk in Shallow's orchard and bursts into song."},{"name":"Davy","group":"Gloucestershire","desc":"Shallow's servant, who really runs the household and asks his master to favour his friend William Visor in a lawsuit, though Visor is a knave."},{"name":"Mouldy, Shadow, Wart, Feeble and Bullcalf","group":"Gloucestershire","desc":"The country recruits. Mouldy and Bullcalf bribe their way out; Wart, Shadow and Feeble are taken. Feeble, the women's tailor, surprises everyone with his courage: 'we owe God a death'."},{"name":"Rumour","group":"Chorus","desc":"The Induction's speaker, 'painted full of tongues', who explains that false reports have spread a story that the rebels won at Shrewsbury. Rumour sets up the play's interest in how news, reputation and report shape belief."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"Rumour, painted with tongues, explains that a false report has reached Warkworth: that Hotspur won at Shrewsbury and Prince Hal is dead. At Warkworth Castle Lord Bardolph brings Northumberland exactly that news. Northumberland's servant Travers says a rider told him the rebels lost, and then Morton arrives from the battle with the truth: Hotspur was killed by Prince Harry, Worcester and Douglas captured, and a royal army under Prince John and Westmoreland is coming north. Northumberland throws away his crutch and calls for chaos. His friends calm him, and Morton reports that the Archbishop of York has risen and turned rebellion into a religious cause.","key":["Rumour's speech makes misinformation the play's starting point and warns us to judge reports carefully.","Northumberland's grief turns into a wish for universal destruction, but his action never matches his words.","The Archbishop's rising gives the rebellion a holy cover, making the conflict more dangerous for the King."],"quiz":[{"q":"What false news does Lord Bardolph bring Northumberland?","o":["That the King has fled to Wales","That Hotspur has won and Prince Harry is dead","That the Archbishop has been killed","That Glendower has come"],"a":1},{"q":"Who brings the true news from Shrewsbury?","o":["Travers","Morton","Rumour","Lord Bardolph"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Morton say the Archbishop has strengthened the rebellion?","o":["By paying Scottish soldiers from the church's own funds","By turning it into a religious cause","By marrying into the Percy family","By capturing Prince John"],"a":1}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"In a London street Falstaff, with a tiny page carrying his sword, hears that his doctor thinks he has more diseases than he knows of and that the mercer won't give him credit. The Lord Chief Justice, who once had Hal imprisoned for striking him, calls Falstaff over about the Gad's Hill robbery. Falstaff pretends to be deaf, talks about the King's apoplexy, and turns every rebuke into a joke about his size and his youth. The Justice says his service at Shrewsbury has gilded over his crime, and tells him the King has separated him from Hal and sent him north with Prince John. He refuses Falstaff a loan. Alone, Falstaff sends letters to Lancaster, the Prince, Westmoreland and Mistress Ursula, and plans to turn his gout to profit.","key":["Falstaff's first words are about his urine and his diseases: in this play his body is old and failing.","The Lord Chief Justice is Falstaff's opposite: law, age and order against wit, appetite and misrule.","We learn that the King has deliberately separated Hal from Falstaff."],"quiz":[{"q":"What did the Lord Chief Justice once do to Prince Hal?","o":["Knighted him on the field after the battle of Shrewsbury","Sent him to prison for striking him","Taught him law","Banished him to Wales"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Falstaff first try to avoid the Lord Chief Justice?","o":["He runs away","He pretends to be deaf","He hides in a tavern","He sends Bardolph instead"],"a":1},{"q":"Where is Falstaff being sent?","o":["To Wales with the King and his army","To France","North with Prince John against the rebels","To Gloucestershire as a justice"],"a":2}]},"a1s3":{"sum":"In York the Archbishop, Mowbray, Hastings and Lord Bardolph discuss their chances. They have about twenty-five thousand men and hope for Northumberland's support. Lord Bardolph warns that Hotspur died because he built on promised help that never came, and compares rash rebels to a man who plans a house he cannot afford. Hastings argues the King's forces are split three ways, against France, Glendower and themselves. The Archbishop scorns the fickle crowd that once cheered Bolingbroke and now longs for Richard, and they set out.","key":["Lord Bardolph's building speech shows the rebels learning from Hotspur's mistake, yet they still depend on Northumberland.","Hastings's argument that the King is divided reveals the instability of Henry's rule.","The Archbishop's speech on the 'fond many' shows the rebels' contempt for the people they claim to speak for."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Lord Bardolph compare a badly planned rebellion to?","o":["A ship without a captain","A house its builder cannot afford to finish","A garden full of weeds","A horse without a rider"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Hastings think the King is weak?","o":["He has no heir that the people will follow","His forces are divided three ways","His nobles are all dead","He has lost the crown jewels"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Archbishop say about the common people?","o":["They are loyal to Henry and will never desert him whatever happens","They are fickle: they cheered Bolingbroke and now miss Richard","They will never fight","They are wiser than nobles"],"a":1}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"Mistress Quickly has two officers, Fang and Snare, arrest Falstaff for debt. Falstaff, Bardolph and the Page fight them off until the Lord Chief Justice arrives. The Hostess says Falstaff owes her money and swore on a gilt goblet to marry her. The Justice orders Falstaff to pay her and make amends. Falstaff talks her round: she withdraws the action, agrees to pawn her plate to lend him more, and invites Doll Tearsheet to supper. Gower brings news that the King and Prince are near and that troops have gone north. Falstaff keeps interrupting with invitations to dinner, and the Justice calls him a great fool.","key":["The tavern scene is a farce of arrest and brawl, but the debt and broken promise are real.","Falstaff's power over the Hostess shows his charm and his willingness to exploit those who love him.","The Lord Chief Justice again judges Falstaff accurately but cannot make him change."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the Hostess have Falstaff arrested?","o":["For stealing her best horse from the stable of the Boar's Head","For debt, and for breaking a promise to marry her","For killing a drawer in a tavern brawl","For treason against the King"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Hostess agree to do by the end of the scene?","o":["Leave London","Pawn her plate to lend Falstaff more money","Marry Bardolph","Testify against Falstaff"],"a":1},{"q":"Who is invited to supper with Falstaff?","o":["The Lord Chief Justice","Doll Tearsheet","Prince John","Justice Shallow"],"a":1}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"Prince Hal, back in London, tells Poins he is tired and wants small beer, which embarrasses his greatness. He says his heart bleeds for his sick father, but that if he wept, everyone would call him a hypocrite because of his company. Bardolph and the Page bring a pompous letter from Falstaff warning Hal that Poins says Hal will marry his sister Nell. Hearing that Falstaff dines at the Boar's Head with Mistress Quickly and Doll Tearsheet, Hal and Poins plan to disguise themselves as waiters and spy on him.","key":["Hal's weariness and shame show him outgrowing the tavern world.","His comment about hypocrisy shows that his reputation now traps him: he cannot show real grief.","The disguise plan repeats the Gad's Hill trick from Part 1, but this time Hal seems less engaged."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Hal say he would be called if he wept for his father?","o":["A coward","A hypocrite","A traitor","A child at heart"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Falstaff's letter warn Hal about?","o":["That Poins says Hal will marry his sister","That the King is already dead and Hal must come home","That Bardolph is a thief","That Doll is in prison"],"a":0},{"q":"How do Hal and Poins plan to spy on Falstaff?","o":["Dressed as musicians","Dressed as waiters","Hidden behind a curtain","Dressed as women"],"a":1}]},"a2s3":{"sum":"At Warkworth Northumberland tells his wife and Lady Percy that his honour is pledged and he must join the rebels. Lady Percy reminds him that he broke his word to his own son at Shrewsbury, praises Hotspur as the model every young man copied, and says he deserves better than to have his father keep faith with others. Lady Northumberland urges him to go to Scotland and wait. Northumberland, torn, decides on Scotland.","key":["Lady Percy's elegy keeps Hotspur alive in the play and condemns Northumberland's past betrayal.","Northumberland abandons his allies for a second time, sealing the rebels' fate.","The scene shows how honour depends on keeping faith, which the rebels do not."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Lady Percy accuse Northumberland of?","o":["Plotting to kill the King and take the crown for himself","Abandoning his son at Shrewsbury","Stealing her dowry","Joining the King's side"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Lady Percy describe Hotspur?","o":["As a hothead nobody liked","As the mirror the noble youth dressed themselves by","As a coward","As a scholar"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does Northumberland decide to go?","o":["To York, to join the Archbishop","To Scotland","To London","To Wales"],"a":1}]},"a2s4":{"sum":"At the Boar's Head the drawers prepare for Hal's disguise. The Hostess fusses over a drunk Doll Tearsheet, and Falstaff and Doll trade insults and affection. Pistol arrives, quarrels with Doll and draws his sword; Falstaff drives him out and wounds him in the shoulder. Doll praises Falstaff's courage and sits on his knee. Hal and Poins, dressed as waiters, overhear Falstaff call Hal a shallow young fellow and Poins a baboon. They reveal themselves; Falstaff claims he insulted Hal before the wicked so they would not love him. Peto brings news of the King and of captains searching for Falstaff. Hal, ashamed of wasting time, leaves at once. Falstaff goes to the wars, and Doll weeps.","key":["The scene is the tavern world at its liveliest and saddest: Falstaff is reminded of death and old age.","Hal's sudden departure shows his sense of duty overtaking the tavern.","Doll's tenderness gives Falstaff a human depth: 'thou'lt forget me when I am gone'."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who does Falstaff drive out of the tavern with his sword?","o":["Bardolph","Pistol","Poins","Peto"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Falstaff say about Hal when he doesn't know Hal is listening?","o":["That he will be a great king one day","That he is a good shallow young fellow","That he is a thief","That he is in love with Doll"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Hal leave the tavern suddenly?","o":["He is called by Peto with news of war","He fights with Falstaff over what he said about him at supper","Doll insults him in front of his friends","The Hostess throws him out"],"a":0}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"At Westminster in the middle of the night the sick King cannot sleep. He envies the poorest subjects and the ship-boy on the mast who can sleep when he cannot: 'Uneasy lies the head that wears a crown.' Warwick and Surrey arrive. The King recalls how Richard predicted that Northumberland, who helped Bolingbroke to the throne, would one day betray him too. Warwick explains that such predictions follow from patterns in men's lives, tells him the rebel numbers are exaggerated by rumour, and reports that Glendower is dead. The King agrees to rest and hopes still to go to the Holy Land.","key":["The King's speech on sleep shows the private cost of power and guilt.","Richard's prophecy reminds us that Henry's troubles come from his usurpation.","Warwick's calm reasoning about 'necessities' contrasts with the King's despair."],"quiz":[{"q":"What can't the King do at the start of the scene?","o":["Eat","Sleep","Walk","Read"],"a":1},{"q":"Whose prophecy does the King remember?","o":["Glendower's","Richard II's","The Archbishop's","Hotspur's"],"a":1},{"q":"What news does Warwick give to comfort the King?","o":["Northumberland has surrendered","Glendower is dead","Hal has reformed","The French have made peace"],"a":1}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"In Gloucestershire Justice Shallow and his cousin Silence talk about Shallow's wild youth at Clement's Inn, where Falstaff was once a page, and about old friends who are dead. Bardolph and Falstaff arrive to collect recruits. Falstaff mocks each man's name: Mouldy, Shadow, Wart, Feeble and Bullcalf. Shallow and Falstaff remember the old days: 'We have heard the chimes at midnight.' While they are inside, Bullcalf and Mouldy bribe Bardolph to be let off, but Feeble says a man can die only once. Falstaff releases Mouldy and Bullcalf, takes Wart, Shadow and Feeble, and defends his choice with comic logic. Alone, he laughs at Shallow's lies about his youth and plans to fleece him.","key":["The recruiting scene shows the corruption of war: the fit buy their freedom and the weak are sent to fight.","Shallow's memories and talk of death give the comedy a melancholy edge: time is running out for these old men.","Feeble's simple courage is a quiet rebuke to Falstaff's dodging."],"quiz":[{"q":"Which two recruits bribe Bardolph to be let off?","o":["Wart and Shadow, the poorest men","Mouldy and Bullcalf","Feeble and Wart","Shadow and Feeble"],"a":1},{"q":"Who says 'we owe God a death'?","o":["Falstaff","Feeble","Shallow","Bullcalf"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Falstaff plan to do to Shallow on his way back?","o":["Arrest him","Fleece him","Make him a knight","Marry his daughter"],"a":1}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"In Gaultree Forest the Archbishop reads a letter: Northumberland has gone to Scotland. A scout reports the royal army of about thirty thousand is close. Westmoreland comes as messenger and rebukes the Archbishop for turning his books into graves and his ink into blood. The Archbishop says the commonwealth is sick and the rebels were denied a hearing. Mowbray recalls how Richard stopped his father's duel with Bolingbroke. Westmoreland says Prince John will hear their grievances, and the Archbishop gives him the articles. Mowbray fears no peace can last, but the Archbishop and Hastings think the King is too weary to punish them. They go to meet Prince John.","key":["Northumberland's desertion leaves the rebels weaker than they hoped.","The Archbishop's image of a diseased state matches the play's sickness imagery.","Mowbray's suspicion of the King's peace is proved right in the next scene."],"quiz":[{"q":"What news does the Archbishop's letter bring?","o":["Northumberland has gone to Scotland","Prince John is dead in an ambush in the forest","The King has surrendered","Glendower has arrived"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Westmoreland accuse the Archbishop of?","o":["Stealing church money from the abbeys of the north to pay for the war","Turning the language of peace into the language of war","Murdering King Richard in Pomfret Castle years ago","Plotting with France against the King"],"a":1},{"q":"Who fears that no peace with the King can last?","o":["Hastings","Mowbray","The Archbishop","Westmoreland"],"a":1}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"Between the armies Prince John rebukes the Archbishop for using the 'counterfeited zeal of God'. He then says he likes all the rebels' articles and swears by the honour of his blood that their grievances will be redressed. If they disband their army, so will he. The rebel leaders drink with him and send their soldiers home. As soon as Hastings reports that the rebel army has scattered, Westmoreland arrests Hastings, the Archbishop and Mowbray for high treason. Prince John says he promised to redress the grievances, not to spare the rebels, and sends them to execution: 'God, and not we, hath safely fought today.'","key":["Prince John wins without a battle through a trick that keeps the letter of his promise and breaks its spirit.","The scene raises the question of whether policy and deceit are acceptable in a king's cause.","The rebellion ends not with heroic combat, as at Shrewsbury, but with arrests."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Prince John promise the rebels?","o":["Their lives","That their grievances will be redressed","Lands in France","A royal pardon for all"],"a":1},{"q":"What happens once the rebel army has gone home?","o":["The rebels are arrested","The King's army also goes home","Prince John gives up his command","A battle begins"],"a":0},{"q":"How does Prince John justify the arrests?","o":["He says the rebels broke the truce first by keeping their army together in the field after the peace","He says he promised redress of grievances, not mercy for the rebels","He says the King ordered it","He says he was misled"],"a":1}]},"a4s3":{"sum":"Falstaff meets the rebel knight Sir John Colevile, who surrenders to him because of his reputation. Prince John arrives, rebukes Falstaff for arriving late, and sends Colevile to execution. Falstaff asks for his deed to be recorded and asks leave to go through Gloucestershire. Alone, he says Prince John is a cold, sober boy who drinks no wine, and praises sherris-sack for warming the brain and the blood: Hal is valiant because he has 'manured' his cold inherited blood with sack. Bardolph says the army is discharged, and Falstaff sets off to visit Shallow.","key":["Falstaff's easy capture of Colevile shows his reputation now does his fighting for him.","The sack speech is a comic hymn to wine that also contrasts Hal's warmth with John's cold policy.","Falstaff plans to exploit Shallow, keeping his comic schemes going."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Colevile surrender to Falstaff?","o":["He is badly wounded and cannot fight on","He believes he is Sir John Falstaff","He is tricked by Bardolph","Prince John orders it"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Falstaff praise in his soliloquy?","o":["Honour, which wins battles for kings","Sherris-sack","Prince John, a sober young man","Peace and quiet in the country"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Falstaff say Prince John lacks?","o":["Courage","Wine, and the warmth it brings","Money","A horse"],"a":1}]},"a4s4":{"sum":"In the Jerusalem Chamber at Westminster the sick King says that once the rebels are crushed he will lead a crusade. He asks after Hal and urges Clarence to keep Hal's love and act as peacemaker among the brothers. Learning that Hal is dining with Poins, he fears the riot that will follow his death. Warwick argues that Hal studies his companions like a foreign language, to cast them off later. Westmoreland brings news that the Gaultree rebels have been brought to justice, and Harcourt that Northumberland and Lord Bardolph have been defeated. The King asks why good news should make him sick, and collapses. His sons talk of omens, and he is carried to another room.","key":["The good news arrives too late for the King: fortune 'writes her fair words still in foulest letters'.","Warwick's 'strange tongue' speech gives the clearest public defence of Hal's tavern years.","The omens and collapse prepare for the King's death and the transfer of the crown."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the King ask Clarence to do?","o":["Lead the army north to York against the Archbishop and his rebels","Keep Hal's love and be a peacemaker among the brothers","Watch Falstaff closely and report back to him","Take the crown when he dies"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Warwick explain Hal's behaviour?","o":["Hal is studying his companions like a foreign language","Hal is ill","Hal is plotting against the King","Hal has been bewitched"],"a":0},{"q":"What happens when the King hears the good news?","o":["He celebrates","He collapses","He leaves for the Holy Land","He knights Westmoreland"],"a":1}]},"a4s5":{"sum":"The King asks for music and for the crown to be set on his pillow. Hal arrives and sits alone by him. Thinking his father dead, Hal speaks to the crown as 'polish'd perturbation', puts it on and leaves. The King wakes, finds the crown gone and accuses Hal of wanting him dead, painting a picture of England under a wild King Harry the Fifth. Hal returns, kneels, and explains that he took the crown as an enemy that had killed his father. The King is convinced, confesses he got the crown by 'by-paths and indirect crook'd ways', and advises Hal to busy giddy minds with foreign quarrels. He learns the room is called Jerusalem and asks to die there.","key":["The crown scene is the heart of the father and son plot: suspicion turns to reconciliation.","Henry's confession and advice reveal how much his reign was built on guilt and policy.","The Jerusalem prophecy is fulfilled ironically: the crusader king dies in a palace room."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Hal take the crown?","o":["He wants to sell it to pay his debts at the tavern","He thinks his father is dead","Falstaff tells him to take it","The King gives it to him"],"a":1},{"q":"What advice does the dying King give Hal?","o":["Make peace with France","Busy giddy minds with foreign quarrels","Pardon all the rebels","Marry quickly"],"a":1},{"q":"What is the 'Jerusalem' in which the King dies?","o":["The Holy City, in the East","A room in the palace","A church in London","A ship"],"a":1}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"Falstaff arrives at Shallow's house in Gloucestershire. Shallow insists he stay and gives orders to his servant Davy, who keeps bringing up farm business and asks his master to favour William Visor, a knave, in a lawsuit. Shallow agrees. Alone, Falstaff mocks how Shallow and his servants have grown alike, and plans to entertain Hal for a long time with stories of Shallow.","key":["The country scenes show small-scale corruption: justice bent for a servant's friend.","Falstaff's plan to amuse Hal shows he still expects to be the Prince's favourite.","The calm of Gloucestershire is a pause before the news of the King's death."],"quiz":[{"q":"What favour does Davy ask of Shallow?","o":["A pay rise","Support for William Visor in a lawsuit","Leave to go to London","A new horse"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Falstaff plan to do with his stories of Shallow?","o":["Write a play","Keep Prince Harry laughing","Sell them","Tell the Lord Chief Justice"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Shallow treat Falstaff?","o":["Coldly and without welcome","Very hospitably","With suspicion","He arrests him"],"a":1}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"At Westminster Warwick tells the Lord Chief Justice the King is dead. The Justice expects the worst from the new King, whom he once imprisoned. The princes arrive, and Clarence says he will have to flatter Falstaff now. The new King Henry V enters, tells his brothers this is the English court, not the Turkish one, and promises to be their father and brother. He reminds the Justice of the indignity of being sent to prison. The Justice says he acted in the King's name and asks Henry to imagine his own son defying the law. Henry agrees, gives him back the sword of justice and asks him to be a father to his youth. He announces that his wildness is buried with his father and calls a parliament.","key":["The Justice's defence persuades the new King, who chooses law over revenge.","Henry V's reassurance of his brothers and the court shows a new, confident king.","'My father is gone wild into his grave' announces the transformation the play has been preparing."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the Lord Chief Justice expect from the new King?","o":["Promotion","Punishment for having imprisoned him","Nothing","Marriage to a princess"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Henry V respond to the Justice's defence?","o":["He banishes him from court and from London, as Falstaff hoped he would","He keeps him in office and calls him a father","He pardons him but dismisses him from office","He has him arrested"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Henry say about his wildness?","o":["It will continue","It has gone into his father's grave","It was a rumour","It was Falstaff's fault"],"a":1}]},"a5s3":{"sum":"In Shallow's orchard Falstaff, Shallow, Silence, Bardolph and the Page eat and drink. The usually silent Silence, drunk, sings snatches of songs. Davy fusses over Bardolph. Pistol arrives with news from court and, after much ranting, announces that the old King is dead and Hal is King Henry V. Falstaff is overjoyed: he promises Shallow any office he likes, declares that the laws of England are at his command, and they ride for London through the night. 'Woe to my lord chief justice!'","key":["The merry orchard scene shows the old men's last happy evening.","Falstaff's response to the news reveals how much he expects from Hal.","'The laws of England are at my commandment' sets up the shock of the rejection."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who sings drunkenly in Shallow's orchard?","o":["Falstaff","Silence","Davy","Pistol"],"a":1},{"q":"What news does Pistol bring?","o":["Doll has been arrested","The old King is dead and Hal is king","Falstaff has been made a lord","The rebels have won"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Falstaff say about the laws of England?","o":["They are unjust","They are at his command","They must be obeyed","They will be rewritten by the Lord Chief Justice"],"a":1}]},"a5s4":{"sum":"In a London street beadles drag the Hostess and Doll Tearsheet to prison, because a man they and Pistol beat has died. Doll claims to be pregnant, and the beadle suggests it is only a cushion. The women hurl insults at the thin beadle and wish Falstaff were there to help them.","key":["The tavern world is being cleaned up even before Falstaff reaches London.","The arrests show the new reign bringing the law down on Eastcheap.","The women's comic abuse hides real danger: they face whipping or worse."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why are the Hostess and Doll arrested?","o":["For debt","Because a man they and Pistol beat has died","For selling meat in Lent","For treason"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Doll claim to protect herself?","o":["That she is a widow","That she is pregnant","That she is Falstaff's wife","That she is French"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom do the women wish were with them?","o":["The Lord Chief Justice","Falstaff","Prince John","Pistol"],"a":1}]},"a5s5":{"sum":"Grooms strew rushes for the coronation procession. Falstaff, Shallow, Pistol and Bardolph wait, still travel-stained, which Falstaff says shows his devotion. Pistol tells him Doll is in prison, and Falstaff promises to free her. The King passes. Falstaff hails him: 'God save thee, my sweet boy!' The King tells the Lord Chief Justice to speak to that 'vain man', then rejects Falstaff: 'I know thee not, old man.' He banishes him and his companions from within ten miles, on pain of death, but grants them enough to live on and promises advancement if they reform. Falstaff tells Shallow he will be sent for in private, but the Lord Chief Justice has him and his friends taken to the Fleet prison. Prince John approves and predicts war in France. In the Epilogue a dancer asks the audience's pardon and promises another play with Sir John in it.","key":["The rejection completes Hal's transformation: the public king must disown the tavern.","Falstaff's hope that Hal will send for him privately shows he still cannot believe the change.","The ending is both triumphant and painful, leaving audiences divided about Hal's coldness."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the new King say to Falstaff?","o":["'Welcome, old friend'","'I know thee not, old man'","'You are my Lord Chancellor'","'Go to Gloucestershire'"],"a":1},{"q":"How far must Falstaff stay from the King?","o":["One mile","Ten miles","A hundred miles","He must leave England"],"a":1},{"q":"Where is Falstaff taken at the end?","o":["To the Tower of London","To the Fleet prison","To France with the army","To the Boar's Head"],"a":1}]}},"notes":{"2":"Rumour was a stock figure in pageants, shown in a robe painted with tongues. The battle of Shrewsbury (1403) ended Part 1; 'Harry Monmouth' is Prince Hal, born at Monmouth. Rumour's lie is that Hotspur won.","17":"Lord Bardolph's report is the exact reverse of Part 1's ending: there, Hal killed Hotspur, Blunt died disguised as the King, and Falstaff was nobody's prisoner.","25":"'Coldspur' plays on Hotspur's nickname: his spur, the sign of a knight, is now cold in death.","32":"Priam was king of Troy; in Virgil's story the ghost of Hector woke Aeneas, but the image of a messenger opening the bed-curtains to tell of Troy burning is Shakespeare's own.","38":"Douglas had killed several men dressed as the King at Shrewsbury (Blunt among them). Worcester was executed after the battle.","39":"Northumberland throws away his crutch and his sick-cap (quoif): his illness at Shrewsbury was 'crafty', as Rumour said. 'Cain' was the first murderer in Genesis.","44":"Richard II was murdered at Pomfret (Pontefract) Castle. The Archbishop shows the people stones stained with his blood, making the rising a holy cause.","49":"Doctors diagnosed illness by examining urine ('water'). Falstaff's first concern in this play is his own failing body.","51":"A mandrake root was thought to look like a little man. Falstaff calls his tiny page a jewel (an agate, often carved with small figures) and jokes about Hal's lack of a beard.","53":"'Achitophel' was the treacherous counsellor of King David. The 'glutton' is the rich man (Dives) of Luke 16, who begged for water to cool his tongue in hell. Horns were the sign of a cuckold.","55":"Proverb: 'Who goes to Westminster for a wife, to St Paul's for a man, and to Smithfield for a horse, may meet with a whore, a knave and a jade.' Hiring servants in St Paul's and buying horses at Smithfield were both risky.","57":"The story that Prince Hal struck the Lord Chief Justice and was sent to prison comes from the chronicles and the old play 'The Famous Victories of Henry V'.","88":"A 'scruple' is a tiny weight used by apothecaries and also a doubt: Falstaff puns on both.","97":"The Gad's Hill robbery is the comic centre of Part 1. Falstaff's service at Shrewsbury included claiming to have killed Hotspur.","102":"Wassail candles were large candles burned at feasts. Tallow (animal fat) was cheaper than wax: Falstaff is all fat.","106":"An 'ill angel' is both an evil spirit and a clipped, underweight coin (an angel was worth ten shillings). 'Costermonger' times are times fit only for street traders.","121":"'Mistress Ursula' is mentioned nowhere else; Falstaff sends her a letter while also promising marriage to the Hostess.","129":"Hastings counts on Northumberland, though the audience already knows from Morton's speech that Northumberland's support is uncertain.","139":"Historically the King's troubles in Wales and with France were real; Shakespeare uses them to make the King look stretched and weak.","144":"Bolingbroke's triumphant entry into London, with Richard following behind, was described in Richard II, Act 5, where dust was thrown on Richard's head.","168":"The Hostess garbles her insults: 'honey-suckle' for homicidal, 'honey-seed' for homicide, 'man-queller' (man-killer) is correct.","172":"The Page's words are made-up insults: 'rampallian' (scoundrel) and 'fustilarian' (fat frump). 'Catastrophe' means backside.","183":"'Wheeson week' is the Hostess's word for Whitsun week. Hal really broke Falstaff's head in some old quarrel; the 'singing-man of Windsor' was a choir member at St George's Chapel.","198":"Falstaff suggests replacing the Hostess's tapestries with cheap painted cloths: the Prodigal Son and a German hunting scene were common subjects.","231":"Small beer was weak beer drunk by servants. 'Holland' is fine linen; Poins's shirts have been pawned to pay for his bastards.","239":"Hal says his low company has made it impossible to show grief for his father without being thought a hypocrite. Compare his first soliloquy in Part 1, 1.2.","255":"In Greek myth Althaea was told her son would live until a log burned out; Hecuba, not Althaea, dreamed she gave birth to a firebrand (Paris). The Page mixes the stories.","263":"Martlemas (11 November) was when cattle were slaughtered and salted for winter: Falstaff is a fat ox ready for killing.","268":"Japhet was one of Noah's sons, from whom Europeans were said to descend: everyone is related to the King if you go back far enough.","271":"Julius Caesar's report 'I came, saw, overcame' was the model of Roman brevity. Falstaff borrows it again in 4.3.","295":"Jove turned himself into a bull to carry off Europa. Hal compares his disguise as a drawer to a god's descent into a beast.","302":"Lady Percy's speech is the play's elegy for Hotspur. 'Speaking thick' means speaking quickly, a habit the young men imitated.","311":"Apple-johns were apples kept for two years until they shrivelled: a joke on Falstaff's age.","322":"Falstaff sings a line from a ballad about Sir Lancelot.","332":"The Hostess's malapropisms: 'temperality' for temper, 'pulsidge' for pulse, 'confirmities' for infirmities. 'The weaker vessel' comes from 1 Peter 3.7.","341":"Pistol's title 'Ancient' means ensign, the officer who carried the company flag. The Hostess hears 'cheater' as escheator, an officer who collected forfeited property.","365":"Pistol speaks in scraps of old plays: Pluto's lake, Erebus and Hiren (Irene) come from popular dramas. He is a walking parody of bombastic tragedy.","367":"A parody of Marlowe's 'Tamburlaine', where the conqueror's chariot is drawn by captured kings: 'Holla, ye pamper'd jades of Asia!'","372":"Calipolis is a character in George Peele's 'Battle of Alcazar'. Pistol's Italian motto is muddled ('if fortune torments me, hope contents me').","383":"The Sisters Three are the Fates, who spin, measure and cut the thread of life; Atropos cuts it.","403":"Bartholomew Fair, held in August at Smithfield, was famous for roast pig.","411":"Falstaff lists foolish tavern skills: drinking off candle-ends floating in brandy (flap-dragons), leaping over stools, wearing smooth boots like a shoemaker's sign.","417":"Saturn (old age) and Venus (love) were rarely in conjunction; Hal mocks an old man courting.","418":"The 'fiery Trigon' was a group of three fire signs in astrology: Bardolph's red face.","449":"Falstaff dodges Hal's charge with a sermon-like division of the company into the damned and the saved.","453":"Selling meat in Lent was illegal; the Hostess admits all innkeepers do it.","483":"The King's speech on sleep sets him beside Richard II, who also reflected on the hollowness of the crown.","492":"The King recalls Richard II, 5.1, where Richard warns Northumberland that he will turn against Bolingbroke. The King was not present in that scene, which Warwick does not point out.","497":"Glendower's death is not historical at this point (he outlived Henry IV), but it removes the Welsh threat from the play.","508":"The Inns of Court and Chancery in London trained young gentlemen in law; Clement's Inn was a lesser inn of Chancery.","510":"Bona-robas were high-class prostitutes. Thomas Mowbray, Duke of Norfolk, was banished by Richard II (Richard II, 1.3); his son is the Mowbray of this play.","518":"John of Gaunt was Henry IV's father. Twelve score and fourteen score are distances in paces (yards): very long shots.","527":"'Accommodated' was a fashionable new word. Bardolph uses it, Shallow admires it as a 'phrase', and neither can define it.","547":"To 'prick' is to mark a name on the list with a pin: Falstaff and Shallow pun on it endlessly.","577":"Falstaff's joke is that Wart's lice are a whole army.","588":"Bell-ringing celebrated the King's coronation; Bullcalf claims he caught his cold doing his duty.","592":"St George's Fields, south of the Thames, was a place of open-air entertainment and brothels.","603":"'We have heard the chimes at midnight' became the title of Orson Welles's film about Falstaff (1965).","606":"Bullcalf offers forty shillings ('four Harry ten shillings') to be released: soldiers who could pay were left at home.","610":"Feeble's 'we owe God a death' echoes Hal's words to Falstaff in Part 1 ('thou owest God a death').","626":"Mile-end Green was where the London militia drilled; Sir Dagonet was King Arthur's fool in a pageant of Arthur's knights.","635":"The 'Vice' was the comic devil-tempter of the old morality plays, who carried a wooden dagger. John of Gaunt's name is a pun on 'gaunt' (thin).","644":"Historically Northumberland's failure to come to Gaultree was decisive. Here it repeats his desertion of Hotspur.","654":"The Archbishop's white robes and learning are contrasted with the military trappings of rebellion.","661":"Mowbray recalls Richard II, 1.3, where Richard stopped the duel between Bolingbroke and Mowbray's father by throwing down his warder (staff).","693":"Prince John's oath is carefully worded: he promises redress of the grievances, not safety for the rebels.","723":"The trick at Gaultree comes from Holinshed, who credits it to Westmoreland; Shakespeare gives the responsibility to Prince John.","742":"'I came, saw, and overcame' is Julius Caesar's boast, 'veni, vidi, vici'. Falstaff calls Caesar 'the hook-nosed fellow of Rome'.","764":"'Green-sickness' was an anaemia thought to afflict young girls. In the sack speech Falstaff mocks the medical theory of humours that he elsewhere relies on.","781":"Thomas of Clarence was Henry's second son; Shakespeare follows the chronicles in making him Hal's favourite.","788":"Warwick's defence of Hal echoes Hal's own explanation in his Part 1 soliloquy.","804":"Edward III, Henry IV's grandfather (the 'great-grandsire' of the princes), died in 1377. The Thames was said to have flooded three times in one day before his death.","829":"A 'rigol' is a circle. Hal's speech on the crown answers his father's speech on sleep in 3.1.","853":"The King's vision of 'Harry the Fifth' is the opposite of what the audience will see: Henry V becomes the model king of Shakespeare's next play.","855":"The King's advice to 'busy giddy minds with foreign quarrels' looks forward to Henry V's war in France.","865":"The Jerusalem Chamber is a real room in the Abbot's house at Westminster. The King had hoped to die on crusade.","887":"Davy's plea shows how justice in the country depends on favours: Shallow agrees to help a knave.","927":"Amurath III, Sultan of Turkey, had his brothers strangled when he came to the throne in 1574; Henry promises not to treat his brothers that way.","933":"'My father is gone wild into his grave' means that his own wildness is buried with his father.","999":"Silence sings a scrap of a Robin Hood ballad.","1004":"'Besonian' is from the Italian bisogno, a needy recruit: a beggar.","1008":"The fig was an obscene gesture with the thumb between the fingers, associated with Spaniards.","1017":"Pistol quotes the first line of a popular song, 'Where is the life that late I led?'","1026":"Beadles wore blue coats, hence 'blue-bottle'. A censer was an incense burner with a thin figure embossed on its lid.","1054":"Pistol's Latin is garbled: 'semper idem' (always the same) and 'absque hoc nihil est' (without this there is nothing).","1056":"Alecto was one of the Furies, with snakes for hair.","1067":"Hal's rejection fulfils his promise in Part 1: 'Banish plump Jack, and banish all the world.' 'I do, I will.'","1077":"The Fleet was a prison in London, often used for debtors and those awaiting judgement.","1089":"The Epilogue mentions Sir John Oldcastle, the name Falstaff originally had in Part 1 until Oldcastle's descendants objected; Oldcastle was a Protestant hero. The promised play with Katharine of France is Henry V, in which Falstaff's death is only reported."},"gloss":{"vent":"opening","post-horse":"horse for carrying messages","covert":"hidden","musters":"gatherings of troops","surmises":"guesses","crafty-sick":"pretending to be ill","posts":"messengers","stratagem":"violent deed, plot","brawn":"fat boar, lump of meat","forspent":"exhausted","jade":"worn-out horse","rowel-head":"spur-wheel","hilding":"good-for-nothing","title-leaf":"title page","woe-begone":"miserable","bruited":"reported","quittance":"repayment, return of blows","outbreathed":"out of breath","physic":"medicine","quoif":"nightcap","complices":"allies","presurmise":"suspicion beforehand","queasiness":"sickness, reluctance","mandrake":"root thought to look like a little man","agate":"small stone carved with tiny figures","juvenal":"youth","slops":"baggy breeches","smooth-pates":"smooth-headed tradesmen","ratsbane":"rat poison","lanthorn":"lantern","apoplexy":"stroke, fit","scruple":"tiny weight; doubt","wassail":"festive","costermonger":"street-seller's, commercial","bear-herd":"bear-keeper","vaward":"vanguard, front","fillip":"flick","beetle":"heavy mallet","groats":"fourpenny coins","puissance":"power","incertain":"uncertain","draw anew the model":"redraw the plan","still-born":"born dead","coffers":"treasure chests","fond":"foolish","disgorge":"vomit up","foin":"thrust","infinitive":"infinite (the Hostess's slip)","continuantly":"continually (the Hostess's slip)","exion":"action, lawsuit (the Hostess's slip)","fubbed off":"put off","malmsey-nose":"red-nosed from wine","quean":"slut","channel":"gutter","man-queller":"murderer","rampallian":"scoundrel","fustilarian":"fat frump","parcel-gilt":"partly gilded","sea-coal":"coal brought by sea","sneap":"rebuke","drollery":"comic picture","loiter":"linger","engraffed":"attached","accites":"prompts","lewd":"low, vulgar","martlemas":"fat ox fattened for slaughter","wen":"swelling, growth","frank":"pigsty","heifers":"young cows","jerkins":"jackets","descension":"descent","apple-johns":"shrivelled apples","temperality":"temper (the Hostess's slip)","pulsidge":"pulse (the Hostess's slip)","canaries":"sweet wine from the Canaries","jordan":"chamber pot","conger":"eel","rheumatic":"irritable (the Hostess means choleric)","confirmities":"infirmities (the Hostess's slip)","hogshead":"large barrel","debuty":"deputy","Tilly-fally":"nonsense","Barbary hen":"guinea fowl","bung":"pickpocket","cuttle":"cut-throat","faitors":"villains","Hiren":"Irene, heroine of an old play; also Pistol's sword","welkin":"sky","neif":"fist","fustian":"bombastic","Quoit":"throw","shove-groat":"shove-ha'penny","imbrue":"draw blood","tirrits":"fits of fright (the Hostess's word)","boar-pig":"roast piglet","death's-head":"skull","pantler":"pantry servant","flap-dragons":"burning raisins or candle-ends drunk off","avoirdupois":"weight","nave":"hub of a wheel (pun on knave)","busses":"kisses","kirtle":"skirt","candle-mine":"store of tallow","malt-worms":"drunkards","victuallers":"innkeepers","smoky cribs":"smoky hovels","pallets":"straw beds","watch-case":"sentry box","'larum-bell":"alarm bell","surge":"wave","beachy":"sandy","intreasured":"stored up","unseason'd":"untimely","ousel":"blackbird","swinge-bucklers":"swashbucklers","bona-robas":"high-class prostitutes","crack":"lively boy","backsword":"broadsword","accommodated":"equipped","Prick":"mark on the list","muster-book":"roll of soldiers","pewterer's":"pewter-maker's","caliver":"light musket","chapt":"wrinkled","tester":"sixpence","thewes":"muscles","assemblance":"appearance","fancies":"songs","good-nights":"serenades","hautboy":"oboe","dace":"small fish","discoverers":"scouts","sortance":"agreement","routs":"mobs","investments":"robes","signories":"estates","beavers":"visors of helmets","warder":"staff of command","overween":"are arrogant","insinew'd":"bound by sinews, joined","winnow'd":"blown to separate grain from chaff","enrooted":"entangled by roots","fangless":"toothless","atonement":"reconciliation","intelligencer":"go-between","Hydra":"many-headed monster","foundered":"lamed","travel-tainted":"dirty from travel","pins' heads":"pinheads","sherris-sack":"sherry","crudy":"thickened, curdled","forgetive":"inventive","pusillanimity":"cowardice","manured":"fertilised","potations":"drinks","flaws":"gusts, flakes of ice","aconitum":"wolfsbane (a poison)","mete":"measure","mure":"wall","Unfather'd heirs":"children without known fathers","biggen":"nightcap","rigol":"circle, ring","canker'd":"rusted","engrossments":"hoarded wealth","potable":"drinkable","by-paths":"side roads","kickshaws":"fancy dishes","precepts":"summonses","headland":"strip of unploughed land at a field's edge","countenance":"support, favour","intervallums":"intervals","Amurath":"a Turkish sultan","Lethe":"river of forgetfulness","graffing":"grafting","caraways":"caraway seeds","Proface":"good appetite!","Shrove-tide":"the feast before Lent","leather-coats":"russet apples","leman":"sweetheart","cavaleros":"gallants","pottle-pot":"two-quart tankard","liggens":"(an oath, meaning unclear)","foutre":"an obscene word of contempt","Besonian":"beggar","fig":"rude gesture","Nut-hook":"constable (who hooks people)","whipping-cheer":"a feast of whipping","tripe-visaged":"tripe-faced","half-kirtles":"skirts","atomy":"skeleton (for anatomy)","liveries":"servants' uniforms","durance":"imprisonment","ebon":"black","gormandizing":"gluttony","competence":"sufficient means","doublet":"close-fitting jacket"}};
const PARTS={"2":[[0,7,"Listen closely, for which of you would block your ears when loud Rumour is speaking? Riding the wind like a post-horse, I carry news from east to west of everything that starts on this earth. Slanders ride constantly on my tongues; I speak them in every language and stuff men's ears with false reports."],[8,13,"I talk of peace while hidden hatred, smiling behind a mask of safety, wounds the world. And who but Rumour, who but I alone, causes frightened gathering of troops and defences, while the swollen year, pregnant with some other trouble, is thought to be carrying a child fathered by that tyrant, war,"],[14,21,"when it is nothing of the kind? Rumour is a pipe blown by guesses, suspicions and assumptions, and its finger-holes are so simple that even the dull many-headed monster, the ever-quarrelling, wavering crowd, can play on it. But why should I dissect my famous body for you, my own household? Why is Rumour here?"],[22,31,"I run ahead of King Henry's victory: at the bloody battle of Shrewsbury he has beaten young Hotspur and his troops and put out the fire of rebellion with the rebels' own blood. But why am I telling the truth at the start? My job is to spread the story that Prince Harry fell to noble Hotspur's sword, and that the King bowed his anointed head as low as death before the furious Douglas."],[32,39,"I have spread this rumour through the country towns between that royal battlefield at Shrewsbury and this worm-eaten fortress of rough stone, where Hotspur's father, old Northumberland, is pretending to be ill. The messengers come riding in exhausted, and not one of them brings any news but what they learned from me. From Rumour's tongues they bring false comfort, which is worse than true bad news."]],"17":[[0,6,"As good as anyone could wish. The King is wounded almost to death. Thanks to your son's success, Prince Harry has been killed outright, and both the Blunts killed by Douglas. Young Prince John, Westmoreland and Stafford ran from the field, and Prince Harry's lump of meat, that hulk Sir John, is your son's prisoner."],[7,10,"Oh, such a day, so well fought, so well followed up and so honourably won, has not graced the times since Caesar's victories!"]],"24":[[0,8,"My lord, Sir John Umfrevile turned me back with happy news and, having a better horse, rode on ahead of me. After him came a gentleman spurring hard, almost worn out with speed, who stopped beside me to rest his bleeding horse. He asked the way to Chester, and I asked him what news there was from Shrewsbury. He told me the rebellion had had bad luck and that young Harry Percy's spur was cold."],[9,14,"With that he gave his horse its head, leaned forward and drove his armoured heels into the panting sides of his poor nag right up to the spur-wheel, and set off so fast he seemed to swallow the road, without waiting for more questions."]],"32":[[0,8,"How are my son and my brother? You're trembling, and the paleness of your cheek tells your message better than your tongue. A man just like you, as faint, as lifeless, as dull, as dreadful in his look and as miserable, opened King Priam's bed-curtains in the dead of night to tell him that half his Troy was burnt. But Priam saw the fire before the man could speak, and I see my Percy's death before you report it."],[9,14,"This is what you'd say: \"Your son did this and this; your brother this; this is how the noble Douglas fought,\" filling my greedy ears with their brave deeds. But in the end, to stop my ears for good, you have a sigh to blow all this praise away, and you finish with \"Brother, son, and all are dead.\""]],"36":[[0,6,"Even so, don't say that Percy's dead. I see a strange confession in your eye: you shake your head as if it were frightening or sinful to speak the truth. If he's been killed, say so. The tongue that reports his death does no wrong. It's a sin to lie about the dead, not to say that the dead are no longer alive."],[7,10,"And yet the first person to bring bad news has a thankless job, and his voice sounds for ever after like a gloomy bell, remembered for tolling a friend's death."]],"38":[[0,6,"I'm sorry I have to make you believe what I wish to God I had never seen. But these eyes of mine saw him covered in blood, fighting back feebly, exhausted and out of breath, against Prince Harry, whose swift fury beat the never-frightened Percy to the ground, and he never rose from it alive."],[7,19,"In short, his death, whose spirit set fire even to the dullest peasant in his camp, once it was known, took the fire and heat from the bravest in his troops. His men took their steel from his metal, and once that was blunted all the rest turned in on themselves like dull, heavy lead. And just as something heavy flies fastest once it's thrown, so our men, made heavy by losing Hotspur, gave their weight such lightness with their fear that arrows never flew faster at their target than our soldiers, aiming at their own safety, fled from the field."],[20,30,"Then that noble Worcester was taken prisoner too soon, and that furious Scot, the bloody Douglas, whose hard-working sword had three times killed men disguised as the King, began to lose heart and joined the shame of those who ran, and as he fled he stumbled in his fear and was captured. The sum of it is that the King has won and has quickly sent an army to meet you, my lord, under the command of young Lancaster and Westmoreland. That's the full news."]],"39":[[0,9,"I'll have plenty of time to mourn this. There's medicine in poison, and this news, which would have made me sick if I'd been well, has, now that I'm sick, made me almost well. And just as a sick wretch, whose fever-weakened joints buckle like feeble hinges under the weight of life, can't bear his fit and breaks out of his nurse's arms like a fire, so my limbs, weakened by grief and now enraged by grief, are three times their strength. Away with you, then, you dainty crutch!"],[10,16,"A scaly steel gauntlet must cover this hand now. And away, you sickly nightcap! You're too soft a protection for a head that princes, flushed with victory, are aiming at. Now bind my forehead with iron, and let the roughest hour that time and malice dare bring come and frown at the enraged Northumberland!"],[17,24,"Let heaven and earth collide! Let Nature's hand no longer hold back the wild sea! Let order die! And let this world no longer be a stage where conflict drags on through a slow act; instead let the spirit of Cain, the first murderer, rule in every heart, so that, with every heart set on bloodshed, the brutal play may end and darkness bury the dead!"]],"42":[[0,8,"The lives of all your loyal allies depend on your health, and if you give yourself up to stormy passion it must fail. You weighed the outcome of the war, my noble lord, and counted up the chances, before you said, \"Let's raise an army.\" You foresaw that, in the sharing out of blows, your son might fall. You knew he was walking on a knife-edge over danger, more likely to fall in than to get across."],[9,16,"You were told his body could be wounded and scarred and that his bold spirit would carry him wherever the danger was thickest. And yet you said \"Go,\" and none of this, though you understood it well, could hold back the stubborn enterprise. So what has happened, what has this bold venture produced, except what was likely to happen?"]],"44":[[0,6,"It's high time. And, my most noble lord, I hear for certain, and I dare say it's true, that the noble Archbishop of York has risen with well-equipped forces. He is a man who binds his followers with a double bond. My lord your son had only the bodies, the mere shadows and shapes of men, to fight with,"],[7,13,"because that word \"rebellion\" divided their bodies' actions from their souls. They fought queasily and under pressure, like men taking medicine, so that only their weapons seemed to be on our side; their spirits and souls were frozen by that word \"rebellion\", like fish in a frozen pond."],[14,22,"But now the Bishop turns rebellion into religion. Since people believe he is sincere and holy in his thoughts, they follow him in body and mind. He adds weight to his rising with the blood of good King Richard, scraped from the stones of Pomfret Castle; he claims his quarrel and cause come from heaven; he tells them he is standing over a bleeding country that is gasping for life under the tyrant Bolingbroke; and high and low flock to follow him."]],"51":[[0,5,"People of every kind take pride in mocking me. The brain of this stupidly made lump of clay, mankind, can't come up with anything funny that isn't invented by me or about me. I'm not only witty myself, I'm the cause of wit in other men. Here I am walking in front of you like a sow that has crushed all her litter except one."],[6,11,"If the Prince put you in my service for any reason except to show me off by contrast, then I have no judgment. You son-of-a-whore mandrake root, you'd be better worn as a badge in my cap than following at my heels. I've never had a dwarf for an attendant till now, but I won't set you in gold or silver; I'll dress you in rags and send you back to your master as a jewel — that juvenile, the Prince your master, whose chin hasn't sprouted any feathers yet."],[12,19,"I'll grow a beard on the palm of my hand before he gets one on his cheek. And yet he'll happily say his face is a royal face. God can finish it whenever He likes; there isn't a hair out of place yet. He can keep it a royal face, because a barber will never earn sixpence from it. And yet he crows as if he'd been a grown man since his father was a bachelor. He can keep his own grace, but he's almost out of mine, I can tell him. What did Master Dombledon say about the satin for my short cloak and my breeches?"]],"53":[[0,6,"Let him be damned like the rich glutton in the Bible! I hope his tongue burns hotter! A son-of-a-whore traitor! A rascally yes-sir knave, to lead a gentleman on and then insist on security! These smooth-headed tradesmen now wear nothing but high shoes and bunches of keys at their belts, and when a man wants to buy honestly on credit, they insist on security. I'd sooner they put rat poison in my mouth than shut it with \"security\"."],[7,11,"I expected him to send me twenty-two yards of satin, as I'm a true knight, and he sends me \"security\". Well, he can sleep in security, because he has the horn of plenty, and his wife's loose behaviour shines through it; and yet he can't see, though he has his own lantern to light him. Where's Bardolph?"]],"106":[[0,5,"Not so, my lord. A bad angel is a light coin, but I hope anyone who looks at me will take me without weighing me. And yet in some ways, I admit, I won't pass as currency; I can't say. Virtue is so little valued in these shopkeeping times that true courage has become a bear-keeper; quick wits have become a barman, wasted on adding up bills. All the other gifts that belong to a man, as this spiteful age shapes them, aren't worth a gooseberry."],[6,9,"You old men don't consider what we young men are capable of. You measure the heat of our passions by the bitterness of your bile, and we who are in the front line of our youth, I must admit, are rascals too."]],"108":[[0,5,"My lord, I was born at about three in the afternoon with a white head and a roundish belly. As for my voice, I lost it shouting and singing hymns. I won't try to prove my youth any further. The truth is, I'm only old in judgement and understanding, and anyone who wants to bet me a thousand marks at dancing, let him lend me the money and I'll take him on!"],[6,9,"As for the box on the ear the Prince gave you, he gave it like a rough prince and you took it like a sensible lord. I've told him off for it, and the young lion is sorry — not in sackcloth and ashes, but in new silk and old sack."]],"112":[[0,4,"Yes, and I have your pretty sweet wit to thank for it. But look, all of you who stay home kissing my lady Peace, pray that our armies don't meet on a hot day, because, by God, I'm only taking two shirts with me and I don't intend to sweat more than usual. If it's a hot day and I brandish anything but a bottle, may I never spit white again."],[5,11,"No dangerous action can poke its head out without my being pushed into it. Well, I can't last for ever. But it's always been a habit of our English nation that when they have a good thing they overuse it. If you insist on saying I'm an old man, you should let me rest. I wish to God my name weren't so terrifying to the enemy. I'd rather rust to death than be scrubbed to nothing by constant motion."]],"136":[[0,5,"Yes, it does, in this kind of war, in this immediate action. A cause that's under way lives on hope the way we see buds appear in early spring: hope gives less guarantee that they'll become fruit than despair gives that the frost will nip them."],[6,12,"When we plan to build, we first survey the site, then draw the plan, and when we see the shape of the house, we have to estimate the cost of building it. If we find it's more than we can afford, what do we do but draw the plan again with fewer rooms, or give up building altogether?"],[13,21,"All the more, in this great work, which is almost to pull a kingdom down and set another up, we should survey the site and the plan, agree on a firm foundation, consult the surveyors, know our own resources and how able we are to take on such a work, and weigh them against the opposition. Otherwise we build our defences on paper and in numbers, using the names of men instead of men,"],[22,26,"like someone who draws the plan of a house beyond his means to build, who gives up halfway and leaves his half-built expense standing bare under the weeping clouds, a ruin for harsh winter to batter."]],"144":[[0,5,"Let's go on and announce the reasons for our taking up arms. The people are sick of their own choice; their over-greedy love has made them overfull. Anyone who builds on the hearts of the common people has a dizzy, unsafe house."],[6,15,"Oh, you foolish crowd, how you beat heaven with loud applause, blessing Bolingbroke before he was what you wanted him to be! And now that he's dressed up in your own desires, you greedy beast, you're so full of him that you're making yourself sick to throw him up. That's how you, common dog, vomited royal Richard out of your gluttonous stomach, and now you'd like to eat your dead vomit up again, and you howl to find it."],[16,23,"What loyalty is there in these times? Those who wanted Richard dead when he was alive have now fallen in love with his grave. You who threw dust on his noble head when he came sighing through proud London behind the admired heels of Bolingbroke now cry, \"Oh earth, give us that king back, and take this one!\" Oh, the cursed thoughts of men! The past and future seem best; the present always worst."]],"163":[[0,6,"I'm ruined if he goes. I promise you, he owes me an infinite amount. Good Master Fang, hold him tight; good Master Snare, don't let him escape. He's always going to Pie Corner — excuse me, gentlemen — to buy a saddle, and he's invited to dinner at the Lubber's Head in Lombard Street, at Master Smooth's the silk merchant's. Please, since my case has been filed and is so publicly known, let him be brought to answer for it."],[7,15,"A hundred marks is a long score for a poor lonely woman to carry, and I've carried it and carried it, and been fobbed off and fobbed off from one day to the next, so it's shameful to think about. There's no honesty in dealing like that, unless a woman is supposed to be a donkey and a beast of burden for every rogue's wrongs. There he comes, and that out-and-out red-nosed rogue Bardolph with him. Do your duty, do your duty, Master Fang and Master Snare. Do me, do me, do me your duty."]],"183":[[0,5,"Indeed, if you were an honest man, yourself and the money too. You swore to me on a partly gilded goblet, sitting in my Dolphin room at the round table by a coal fire, on the Wednesday of Whitsun week, when the Prince cracked your head for comparing his father to a singer from Windsor — you swore to me then, while I was washing your wound, that you'd marry me and make me my lady your wife. Can you deny it?"],[6,14,"Didn't goodwife Keech, the butcher's wife, come in then and call me friend Quickly? She came in to borrow a dish of vinegar and told us she had a good dish of prawns, and you wanted to eat some, and I told you they were bad for a fresh wound. And didn't you, when she'd gone downstairs, tell me not to be so familiar with such poor people, saying that before long they'd be calling me madam? And didn't you kiss me and tell me to fetch you thirty shillings? I put you on your oath now: deny it if you can."]],"231":[[0,7,"Then I suppose my appetite wasn't born royal, because, honestly, I do now remember that poor creature, weak beer. But really, these humble thoughts make me fall out of love with my greatness. What a disgrace it is for me to remember your name! Or to recognise your face tomorrow! Or to keep count of how many pairs of silk stockings you have — these, and the ones that used to be your peach-coloured ones! Or to keep a list of your shirts: one spare and one for wearing!"],[8,14,"But the tennis-court keeper knows that better than I do, because your linen is at a low ebb when you don't play there, as you haven't for a long time, because your lowlands have managed to eat up your fine Dutch linen. And God knows whether the babies who bawl out of the remains of your shirts will inherit His kingdom; but the midwives say the children aren't to blame, and so the world grows and families are mightily increased."]],"271":[[0,4,"(Reads) \"I will imitate the honourable Romans in brevity.\" He must mean shortness of breath, being short-winded. \"I commend myself to you, I commend you, and I leave you. Don't be too familiar with Poins, because he abuses your favours so much that he swears you're going to marry his sister Nell. Repent in your spare time as you can, and so farewell."],[5,9,"Yours, yes and no (which is as much as to say, depending on how you treat him), Jack Falstaff to my friends, John to my brothers and sisters, and Sir John to all Europe.\" My lord, I'll soak this letter in sack and make him eat it."]],"302":[[0,7,"Oh, even so, for God's sake, don't go to these wars! There was a time, father, when you broke your word when you were more bound to keep it than now — when your own Percy, my heart's dear Harry, kept looking north to see his father bring up his army, and waited in vain. Who persuaded you to stay at home then? Two honours were lost there, yours and your son's."],[8,23,"As for yours, may God in heaven polish it bright! As for his, it was fixed on him like the sun in the grey sky, and by its light all the knights of England were moved to brave deeds. He was truly the mirror in which the noble young men dressed themselves. Every young man copied his walk, and his quick, thick speech, a flaw nature gave him, became the way the brave talked, so that those who could speak slowly and quietly spoiled their own perfection to seem like him. In speech, in walk, in diet, in pleasures, in military discipline and in temperament, he was the target and the mirror, the copy and the book, that shaped everyone else."],[24,29,"And him — oh, wonderful him! oh, miracle of men! — you left him, second to none, without your support, to face the hideous god of war at a disadvantage, to hold a battlefield where only the sound of Hotspur's name seemed to give any defence. That's how you left him."],[30,36,"Never, oh never, do his ghost the wrong of keeping your honour more strictly and carefully with others than you did with him! Leave them alone. The Marshal and the Archbishop are strong. If my sweet Harry had had even half their numbers, I might today be hanging on Hotspur's neck and talking about Prince Harry's grave."]],"341":[[0,5,"Fiddlesticks, Sir John, don't tell me. Your ensign swaggerer isn't coming through my doors. I was up before Master Tisick, the deputy, the other day, and he said to me — it was no longer ago than last Wednesday — \"Honestly, neighbour Quickly,\" he says (Master Dumbe, our minister, was there at the time), \"neighbour Quickly,\" he says, \"only let in people who are civil, because,\" he said, \"you have a bad name.\""],[6,9,"Now, he said that, I can tell you why. \"Because,\" he says, \"you're an honest woman and well thought of, so be careful what guests you let in. Don't let in,\" he says, \"any swaggering fellows.\" None come in here. You'd be amazed to hear what he said. No, I won't have swaggerers."]],"483":[[0,4,"How many thousands of my poorest subjects are asleep at this hour! Oh sleep, oh gentle sleep, nature's soft nurse, how have I frightened you, that you'll no longer weigh my eyelids down and soak my senses in forgetfulness?"],[5,13,"Why, sleep, do you prefer to lie in smoky hovels, stretched out on uncomfortable straw beds and lulled to sleep by buzzing night-flies, rather than in the perfumed rooms of great men, under expensive canopies, lulled by the sound of the sweetest music? Oh, you dull god, why do you lie with the lowly in their disgusting beds and leave the king's bed like a sentry box or an alarm bell?"],[14,21,"Will you seal the ship's boy's eyes on top of the high, swaying mast, and rock his brain in the cradle of the rough, overpowering sea, in the onslaught of the winds, which seize the violent waves by the crests, curling their monstrous heads and hanging them in the slippery clouds with such a deafening roar that the uproar wakes death itself?"],[22,27,"Can you, unfair sleep, give your rest to the soaked ship's boy in such a violent hour, and in the calmest, stillest night, with every comfort and aid besides, deny it to a king? Then lie down, you happy humble people! The head that wears a crown lies uneasy."]],"492":[[0,11,"Oh God! If only one could read the book of fate and see how the turning of the times makes mountains flat, and the dry land, tired of being solid, melt into the sea; and at other times see the sandy belt of the shore grow too wide for the sea-god's hips; see how chance mocks us, and how changes fill the cup of alteration with different drinks! Oh, if this could be seen, the happiest young man, looking through the course of his life, what dangers past, what troubles still to come, would shut the book, sit down and die."],[12,21,"It's not ten years ago that Richard and Northumberland were great friends and feasted together, and two years after that they were at war. It's only eight years since this Percy was the man closest to my heart, who worked like a brother on my behalf and laid his love and life at my feet — yes, and for my sake even defied Richard to his face. But which of you was there — you, cousin Nevil, as I remember —"]],"494":[[0,7,"when Richard, his eyes brimming with tears, being rebuked and scolded by Northumberland, spoke these words, which have now proved a prophecy? \"Northumberland, you ladder by which my cousin Bolingbroke climbs to my throne\" — though at that time, God knows, I had no such intention, except that necessity so bent the state that greatness and I were forced to kiss."],[8,12,"\"The time will come,\" he went on, \"the time will come when foul sin, coming to a head, will burst into rottenness.\" And so he went on, foretelling the state of things now and the breaking of our friendship."]],"495":[[0,5,"There's a pattern in every man's life that reflects the nature of times gone by. By observing it, a man can predict fairly accurately the likely course of things that haven't yet come to life, which lie stored up in their seeds and weak beginnings."],[6,12,"Such things are hatched and bred by time. And from this inevitable pattern King Richard could make a perfect guess that great Northumberland, false to him then, would grow from that seed to a greater falseness, which would find no ground to take root in except you."]],"497":[[0,5,"That can't be so, my lord. Rumour doubles the numbers of the people we fear, like a voice and its echo. Please, your grace, go to bed. Upon my soul, my lord, the forces you've already sent out will win this prize very easily."],[6,10,"To comfort you further, I've received reliable news that Glendower is dead. Your Majesty has been ill this fortnight, and these late hours are bound to make your sickness worse."]],"623":[[0,5,"Are you going to tell me how to choose a man, Master Shallow? Do I care about a man's limbs, muscles, height, size and big appearance? Give me spirit, Master Shallow. Here's Wart; you see how scruffy he looks. He'll load and fire for you as fast as a pewterer's hammer, advance and retreat faster than the man who hangs the brewer's buckets on the yoke."],[6,11,"And this half-faced fellow, Shadow: give me this man. He gives the enemy no target; the enemy might as well aim at the edge of a penknife. And as for a retreat, how fast this Feeble the ladies' tailor will run! Oh, give me the thin men and spare me the big ones. Put a musket in Wart's hand, Bardolph."]],"635":[[0,6,"On my way back I'll fleece these justices. I can see right to the bottom of Justice Shallow. Lord, Lord, how prone we old men are to this vice of lying! This starved justice has done nothing but babble to me about the wildness of his youth and the things he did around Turnbull Street, and every third word a lie, paid to the listener more punctually than the Sultan's taxes."],[7,14,"I remember him at Clement's Inn looking like a man carved out of a cheese-rind after supper. When he was naked he was for all the world like a forked radish with a head fantastically carved on it with a knife. He was so scrawny that anyone with poor eyesight couldn't see him at all. He was the very spirit of famine, yet as lecherous as a monkey, and the whores called him \"mandrake\". He was always behind the fashion, and he sang tunes he'd heard the carters whistle to worn-out hussies and swore they were his own compositions or serenades."],[15,22,"And now this stage clown's wooden dagger has become a squire and talks as familiarly about John of Gaunt as if he'd been his sworn brother, and I'll swear he only ever saw him once, at the jousting ground, and then he got his head cracked for pushing in among the marshal's guards. I saw it, and told John of Gaunt he was beating his own name, gaunt, because you could have stuffed Shallow and all his clothes into an eel-skin; the case of a treble oboe was a mansion for him, a palace. And now he has land and cattle."],[23,27,"Well, I'll get to know him if I come back, and I'll make him my philosopher's stone, turning everything to gold for me, or my name's not Falstaff. If the young fish is bait for the old pike, I see no reason in the law of nature why I shouldn't snap at him. Let time decide, and that's the end of it."]],"643":[[0,4,"That's well done. My friends and brothers in this great enterprise, I have to tell you that I've received recently dated letters from Northumberland. Their cold message, gist and substance is this:"],[5,11,"he wishes he were here in person, with an army fitting his rank, which he couldn't raise. So he has withdrawn to Scotland to let his growing fortunes ripen, and he ends with heartfelt prayers that your efforts may survive the danger of a fearful meeting with the enemy."]],"654":[[0,11,"Then, my lord, I address my speech chiefly to your grace. If rebellion came looking like itself, in low, wretched mobs led by violent youths, dressed in rags and supported by boys and beggars — I say, if damned rebellion appeared like that, in its true, natural and proper shape, you, reverend father, and these noble lords wouldn't be here dressing up the ugly shape of low and bloody revolt with your honourable reputations."],[12,22,"You, lord Archbishop, whose diocese is kept up by civil peace, whose beard the silver hand of peace has touched, whose learning and scholarship peace has taught, whose white robes stand for innocence, the dove and the very blessed spirit of peace — why do you so badly translate yourself out of the gracious language of peace into the harsh, noisy tongue of war, turning your books into graves, your ink into blood, your pens into lances and your holy tongue into a loud trumpet sounding the call to battle?"]],"655":[[0,5,"Why am I doing this? That's the question. Briefly, for this reason: we are all diseased, and with our overeating and our wild hours we have brought ourselves into a burning fever, and we must be bled for it. Our late King Richard caught this disease and died of it."],[6,13,"But, my most noble Lord of Westmoreland, I'm not setting myself up here as a doctor, nor do I march among crowds of soldiers as an enemy of peace. Rather, I put on the look of fearful war for a while, to put on a diet minds that are swollen and sick of too much happiness, and to clear the blockages that are beginning to stop our very veins of life. Let me speak more plainly."],[14,26,"I have weighed fairly in the balance the wrongs our army may do and the wrongs we suffer, and I find our grievances heavier than our offences. We see which way the current of the times is running, and we're forced out of our peaceful lives by the rough torrent of events. We have a list of all our grievances, to show in written articles when the time comes, which we offered to the King long ago and could get no hearing for, however we pleaded. When we are wronged and want to set out our grievances, we're refused access to him by the very men who have wronged us most."],[27,34,"The dangers of the days just past, whose memory is still written on the earth in blood that can be seen, and the examples that every minute now presents, have put us in this unfitting armour — not to break the peace or any branch of it, but to set up here a real peace, true both in name and in substance."]],"660":[[0,3,"Oh, my good Lord Mowbray, interpret the times by what they make necessary, and you'll indeed say it's the times, not the King, that are injuring you."],[4,9,"And yet for your part, it doesn't seem to me that either the King or the present times give you an inch of ground to build a grievance on. Weren't you given back all the estates of the Duke of Norfolk, your noble and well-remembered father?"]],"661":[[0,3,"What honour had my father lost that needed to be revived and breathed into me? The King who loved him was forced, against his will, by the state of things at the time, to banish him."],[4,16,"And then, when Henry Bolingbroke and he were mounted and both braced in their saddles, their neighing horses daring the spur, their lances levelled, their visors down, their fiery eyes sparkling through the slits of their steel helmets, and the loud trumpet blowing them together — then, then, when nothing could have stopped my father's lance from reaching Bolingbroke's chest, oh, when the King threw his staff down, his own life hung on the staff he threw. He threw down himself and the lives of all those who since then, by legal charges and by the sword, have been destroyed under Bolingbroke."]],"662":[[0,9,"You don't know what you're saying now, Lord Mowbray. The Earl of Hereford was then thought the bravest gentleman in England. Who knows which of them fortune would have smiled on? But if your father had won there, he would never have got out of Coventry alive, because the whole country with one voice cried out its hatred of him, and all their prayers and love were set on Hereford, whom they adored and blessed and honoured more than the King."],[10,16,"But this is just a digression from my purpose. I've come from our princely general to hear your grievances and to tell you from his grace that he will give you a hearing, and wherever your demands are shown to be just you shall have them, with everything set aside that might make you seem enemies."]],"664":[[0,4,"Mowbray, you're being arrogant to take it that way. This offer comes from mercy, not fear. Look, our army lies within sight, upon my honour far too confident to let in a thought of fear."],[5,9,"Our army has more famous names in it than yours, our men are more skilled with weapons, our armour as strong, our cause the best; so reason says our courage should be as good. Don't say, then, that our offer is forced."]],"669":[[0,9,"Then take this document, my Lord of Westmoreland, because it contains our general grievances. If each separate article in it is put right, and all those who share our cause, both here and elsewhere, who are bound up in this action, are pardoned in a proper, binding form, and our demands, as they concern us and our aims, are carried out at once, we will come back within our respectful bounds and join our forces to the arm of peace."]],"676":[[0,7,"No, no, my lord. Notice this: the King is tired of petty, nit-picking grievances, because he has found that ending one fear with a death brings two greater ones to life in the heirs who survive. So he'll wipe his slate clean and keep no informer by him to repeat the story of his losses and remind him of them again."],[8,17,"He knows very well he can't weed this land as thoroughly as his suspicions suggest. His enemies are so rooted in among his friends that, in pulling up an enemy, he loosens and shakes a friend. So this land, like a quarrelsome wife who has driven him to the point of hitting her, holds up his baby just as he's about to strike, and keeps the punishment he's decided on hanging in the arm that was raised to carry it out."]],"687":[[0,9,"Well met here, my cousin Mowbray. Good day to you, noble lord Archbishop, and to you, Lord Hastings, and to everyone. My Lord of York, you looked better when your congregation, called by the bell, gathered round you to listen reverently to your sermon on the holy text, than now, seeing you here as a man of iron, encouraging a mob of rebels with your drum, turning the Word into the sword and life into death."],[10,16,"If a man who sits in a monarch's heart and ripens in the sunshine of his favour abused the King's support, alas, what harm he could set going under cover of such greatness! It's just so with you, lord Bishop. Who hasn't heard how deeply you were in God's books?"],[17,29,"To us you were the speaker in God's parliament, the imagined voice of God himself, the very interpreter and messenger between the grace and holiness of heaven and our dull actions. Oh, who could believe anything but that you are misusing the respect owed to your position, using the favour and grace of heaven in dishonourable deeds, as a false favourite uses his prince's name? Under a pretended zeal for God, you've raised up the subjects of His deputy, my father, and swarmed them here against both the peace of heaven and the King."]],"688":[[0,5,"My good Lord of Lancaster, I'm not here against your father's peace. But, as I told my Lord of Westmoreland, these disordered times, as anyone can see, crowd us and crush us into this monstrous shape to keep ourselves safe."],[6,12,"I sent your grace the details and particulars of our grievances, which have been pushed out of court with scorn, and from that this many-headed Hydra, this son of war, is born. Its dangerous eyes may be charmed to sleep by granting our just and proper wishes, and true obedience, cured of this madness, will bow tamely at the feet of majesty."]],"693":[[0,6,"I like them all and fully accept them, and I swear here by the honour of my blood that my father's intentions have been misunderstood, and that some of those around him have too freely twisted his meaning and his authority. My lord, these grievances will be put right quickly; upon my soul, they will."],[7,11,"If this satisfies you, send your troops home to their counties, as we'll send ours; and here between the armies let's drink together in friendship and embrace, so that all their eyes can carry home these signs of our restored love and friendship."]],"727":[[0,5,"I pledged you none. I promised you a remedy for the grievances you complained about, which, upon my honour, I will carry out with most Christian care. But as for you, rebels, expect to taste the punishment that fits rebellion and actions like yours."],[6,11,"You started this war very shallowly, foolishly brought your forces here and foolishly sent them away. Sound our drums; pursue the scattered stragglers. God, not we, has fought safely today. Some of you, take these traitors to the block, treason's proper bed, where life is given up."]],"742":[[0,5,"I'd be sorry, my lord, if it were any other way. I've never known anything but scolding and rebuke to be the reward of courage. Do you think I'm a swallow, an arrow or a bullet? Do I, with my poor old movements, have the speed of thought? I've hurried here at the very utmost limit of possibility. I've worn out more than a hundred and eighty post-horses."],[6,9,"And here, dirty from the journey as I am, in my pure, spotless courage, I've taken Sir John Colevile of the dale, a most furious knight and brave enemy. But what of it? He saw me and surrendered, so that I can fairly say, with that hook-nosed fellow from Rome, \"I came, saw and conquered.\""]],"764":[[0,7,"I wish you had the wit to; it would be worth more than your dukedom. Honestly, this sober-blooded boy doesn't love me, and no one can make him laugh. But that's no wonder: he drinks no wine. None of these well-behaved boys ever amount to anything, because weak drink cools their blood so much, and with eating so much fish they fall into a kind of male anaemia, and then when they marry they father girls. They're generally fools and cowards, as some of us would be too, if it weren't for drink heating us up."],[8,12,"A good sherry has a double effect. It climbs into the brain and dries up all the foolish, dull, sluggish vapours surrounding it; it makes the brain quick, lively and inventive, full of nimble, fiery, delightful images, which, passed on to the voice, the tongue, which gives them birth, become excellent wit."],[13,21,"The second quality of your excellent sherry is that it warms the blood, which, being cold and still before, left the liver white and pale, which is the sign of faint-heartedness and cowardice. But the sherry warms it and makes it run from the inner organs to the extremities. It lights up the face, which like a beacon warns all the rest of this little kingdom, man, to arm; and then all the vital common people and minor spirits inside gather to their captain, the heart, which, great and swollen with this following, will do any brave deed. And this courage comes from sherry."],[22,30,"So skill with weapons is nothing without sack, because sack sets it working, and learning is just a hoard of gold guarded by a devil until sack sets it going and puts it to use. That's why Prince Harry is brave: the cold blood he naturally inherited from his father he has manured, farmed and ploughed, like poor, barren, bare land, with the excellent labour of drinking plenty of good fertile sherry, so that he has become very hot and brave. If I had a thousand sons, the first human principle I'd teach them would be to give up weak drinks and devote themselves to sack."]],"772":[[0,6,"Now, lords, if God gives a successful end to this conflict that's bleeding at our doors, we will lead our young men to higher battlefields and draw no swords except holy ones. Our navy is ready, our army gathered, our deputies for while we're away properly appointed, and everything is as we'd wish."],[7,9,"We only lack a little bodily strength, and we must wait until these rebels who are now in the field are brought back under the yoke of government."]],"781":[[0,11,"Only good for you, Thomas of Clarence. How is it you're not with the Prince your brother? He loves you, and you neglect him, Thomas. You have a better place in his affection than all your brothers. Value it, my boy, and after I'm dead you may do noble service as a go-between between his greatness and your other brothers. So don't neglect him, don't blunt his love or lose the advantage of his favour by seeming cold or careless of what he wants, because he is gracious if he's treated with respect."],[12,22,"He has a tear for pity and a hand as open as daylight for tender charity. And yet, when he's angry, he's flint, as changeable as winter and as sudden as the frozen gusts of early morning. So his temper must be carefully watched. Scold him for his faults, and do it respectfully, when you see he's in a merry mood; but when he's moody, give him line and room, like a fisherman, until his passions, like a stranded whale, wear themselves out with struggling."],[23,29,"Learn this, Thomas, and you'll be a shelter to your friends, a gold hoop to bind your brothers together, so that the united vessel of their blood, mixed with the poison of evil suggestion, as the times are bound to pour it in, will never leak, even if it works as strongly as wolfsbane or gunpowder."]],"787":[[0,7,"The richest soil is most prone to weeds, and he, the noble image of my youth, is overgrown with them. So my grief stretches beyond the hour of my death. My heart weeps blood when I imagine the lawless days and rotten times you'll see when I'm asleep with my ancestors."],[8,12,"Because when his headstrong rioting has no curb, when rage and hot blood are his advisers, when money and extravagant habits come together, oh, with what wings his desires will fly towards danger ahead and ruin in his path!"]],"788":[[0,6,"My gracious lord, you misjudge him completely. The Prince is only studying his companions like a foreign language, in which, to master it, it's necessary to look at and learn the most indecent words; and once he's mastered them, as your Highness knows, they're of no further use except to be recognised and hated."],[7,11,"So, like coarse words, the Prince will in the fullness of time cast off his followers, and the memory of them will live on as a pattern or a measure by which his grace will judge other men's lives, turning past evils into advantages."]],"792":[[0,6,"Health to my sovereign, and new happiness on top of what I have to tell you! Prince John your son kisses your grace's hand. Mowbray, Bishop Scroop, Hastings and all of them have been brought to the punishment of your law. There isn't a rebel's sword drawn now; Peace holds out her olive branch everywhere."],[7,9,"Your Highness can read here at greater leisure how this action was carried out, with every stage in detail."]],"797":[[0,6,"And why should this good news make me ill? Will Fortune never come with both hands full, but always write her fair words in the ugliest letters? She either gives an appetite and no food, as with the poor who are healthy, or else a feast and takes away the appetite, as with the rich, who have plenty and can't enjoy it."],[7,9,"I should be rejoicing now at this happy news, and now my sight is failing and my head is spinning. Oh me! Come close to me. I'm very ill now."]],"829":[[0,7,"Why does the crown lie there on his pillow, when it's such a troublesome bedfellow? Oh, polished trouble! Golden worry! You keep the gates of sleep wide open through many wakeful nights. Sleep with it now! And yet not so soundly, and not half so sweetly, as the man who snores the night away with a plain nightcap tied round his head."],[8,13,"Oh majesty! When you pinch the one who wears you, you sit like rich armour worn in the heat of the day, which scalds while it protects. At the door of his breath lies a downy feather that doesn't move. If he breathed, that light, weightless down would have to stir."],[14,19,"My gracious lord! My father! This sleep is sound indeed. This is the sleep that has divorced so many English kings from this golden circle. What I owe you is tears and the heavy grief of my blood, which nature, love and a son's tenderness will pay you in full, oh dear father."],[20,26,"What you owe me is this imperial crown, which comes down to me as next in your place and blood. Look, here it sits (he puts it on), and God will guard it. Even if the whole world's strength were put into one giant arm, it shall not wrench this inherited honour from me. As it's left to me by you, so I'll leave it to my heir."]],"844":[[0,3,"This action of his joins forces with my illness and helps finish me off. See, sons, what you are like! How quickly nature rebels when gold becomes its goal!"],[4,10,"This is what foolish, over-anxious fathers have broken their sleep with worrying for, broken their brains with care and their bones with work. For this they have hoarded and piled up rusting heaps of gold got by strange means; for this they have taken care to give their sons learning and military training."],[11,16,"Like the bee, we collect sweetness from every flower, our thighs packed with wax and our mouths with honey; we bring it to the hive, and, like the bees, we are murdered for our trouble. This is the bitter taste that his hoarding leaves the dying father."]],"853":[[0,8,"Your wish was father to that thought, Harry. I stay alive too long for you; I tire you. Are you so hungry for my empty throne that you have to dress yourself in my honours before your time is ripe? Oh, foolish young man! You're seeking the greatness that will overwhelm you. Just wait a little, because my cloud of majesty is held up by so weak a wind that it will soon fall. My day is growing dark."],[9,20,"You've stolen what in a few hours would have been yours without wrongdoing, and at my death you've confirmed what I expected of you. Your life showed you didn't love me, and you want me to die sure of it. You're hiding a thousand daggers in your thoughts, sharpened on your stony heart, to stab at half an hour of my life. What! Can't you put up with me for half an hour? Then go and dig my grave yourself, and tell the merry bells to ring in your ear that you're crowned, not that I'm dead."],[21,31,"Let all the tears that should fall on my coffin be drops of holy oil to anoint your head. Just mix me with forgotten dust; give the one who gave you life to the worms. Pull down my officials, break my laws, for now the time has come to mock at order. Harry the Fifth is crowned: up with vanity! Down with royal dignity! All you wise counsellors, away! And now gather at the English court from every region, you apes of idleness! Now, neighbouring countries, clear out your scum."],[32,40,"Have you got a ruffian who will swear, drink, dance, revel all night, rob, murder and commit the oldest sins in the newest ways? Be happy: he won't trouble you any more. England will double-gild his triple guilt; England will give him office, honour and power. For the fifth Harry takes the muzzle of restraint off licence, and the wild dog will sink his teeth into every innocent."],[41,45,"Oh, my poor kingdom, sick with the blows of civil war! When my care could not keep your disorders down, what will you do when disorder is your king's only care? Oh, you'll be a wilderness again, peopled with wolves, your old inhabitants!"]],"854":[[0,10,"Oh, forgive me, my lord! If it weren't for my tears, the wet obstacles to my speech, I'd have stopped this heartfelt, deep rebuke before you'd spoken in grief and I'd heard so much of it. There's your crown, and may He who wears the crown for ever keep it yours for a long time! If I want it for any other reason than as your honour and your fame, let me never rise again from this kneeling, which my deepest, truest and most dutiful spirit teaches this outward, bowing body."],[11,16,"God is my witness how coldly it struck my heart when I came in here and found no breath in your Majesty! If I'm pretending, oh, let me die in my present wildness and never live to show the disbelieving world the noble change I've decided on!"],[17,26,"Coming to look at you, thinking you were dead, and almost dead myself, my lord, to think you were, I spoke to this crown as if it could understand, and scolded it like this: \"The care that comes with you has eaten up my father's body. So you, the best of gold, are the worst of gold. Other gold, less pure, is more precious, because it preserves life as a medicine you can drink. But you, most pure, most honoured, most famous, have eaten up the man who wore you.\""],[27,30,"And so, my most royal lord, accusing it, I put it on my head to fight it, as I would an enemy who had murdered my father in front of me, as a true heir should."],[31,38,"But if it filled my blood with joy or swelled my thoughts with any trace of pride, if any rebellious or vain part of me welcomed its power even slightly, let God keep it from my head for ever and make me like the poorest servant who kneels to it in awe and terror!"]],"855":[[0,6,"Oh, my son, God put it in your mind to take it away, so that you could win your father's love all the more by pleading so wisely in your own defence! Come here, Harry, sit by my bed and hear what I think is the very last advice I'll ever breathe."],[7,13,"God knows, my son, by what side paths and indirect, crooked ways I came by this crown, and I myself know well how uneasily it sat on my head. It will come to you more quietly, with better reputation and firmer support, because all the dirt of getting it goes with me into the earth."],[14,24,"In me it seemed only an honour snatched with a violent hand, and there were many alive to reproach me for having got it with their help, which every day grew into quarrels and bloodshed, wounding what was meant to be peace. You see how I have met all these bold dangers at great risk, because my whole reign has been like a play acting out that story. Now my death changes the situation, because what I got by my own action comes to you in a fairer way, so you wear the crown by right of succession."],[25,35,"Still, though you stand more securely than I could, you're not secure enough while grievances are fresh. All my allies, whom you must make your allies, have only just had their stings and teeth pulled out. Their fierce efforts first raised me up, and I had good reason to fear their power might throw me down again. To avoid that, I cut them down, and I was planning now to lead many of them to the Holy Land, in case rest and idleness made them look too closely at my position."],[36,42,"So, my Harry, make it your policy to keep restless minds busy with foreign wars, so that action abroad may wipe out the memory of former days. I would say more, but my lungs are so worn out that I have no strength left to speak. How I came by the crown, oh God, forgive; and grant that it may live in true peace with you!"]],"897":[[0,6,"If I were sawn into pieces, I'd make four dozen bearded hermits' staffs like Master Shallow. It's a wonderful thing to see how his men's spirits match his. By watching him, they behave like foolish justices; by talking with them, he has turned into a justice-like servant. Their spirits are so married together by keeping company that they flock together in agreement like a lot of wild geese."],[7,12,"If I wanted a favour from Master Shallow, I'd flatter his men by suggesting they were close to their master; if I wanted one from his men, I'd flatter Master Shallow that no man could command his servants better. It's certain that wise behaviour or ignorant manners are caught from one another, the way people catch diseases, so men should be careful about the company they keep."],[13,18,"I'll make up enough material out of this Shallow to keep Prince Harry laughing for as long as it takes to wear out six fashions, which is four law terms, or two lawsuits, and he'll laugh without a break. Oh, it's amazing what a lie with a little oath, and a joke told with a straight face, will do to a young fellow who's never had aching shoulders! Oh, you'll see him laugh till his face is like a wet cloak badly folded!"]],"927":[[0,5,"This new and gorgeous garment, majesty, doesn't sit as easily on me as you think. Brothers, you mix your sadness with some fear. This is the English court, not the Turkish one, where one Sultan Amurath succeeds another and kills his brothers; it's Harry succeeding Harry."],[6,11,"But be sad, good brothers, because honestly it suits you very well. Sorrow looks so royal on you that I will put the fashion on deeply and wear it in my heart. So be sad; but don't take on more of it, good brothers, than a burden we all share."],[12,17,"As for me, by heaven, I want you to be sure that I'll be your father and your brother too. Just let me have your love and I'll carry your cares. Weep that Harry's dead, and so will I; but there's a Harry alive who will turn each of those tears into an hour of happiness."]],"932":[[0,10,"I was then acting as your father's representative; the image of his power was in me. And while I was administering his law, busy for the good of the state, your Highness chose to forget my position, the majesty and power of law and justice, the image of the King whom I represented, and struck me in my very seat of judgement. So, as an offender against your father, I boldly used my authority and sent you to prison."],[11,17,"If that was wrong, then be content, now that you wear the crown, to have a son of your own ignore your laws, pull justice down from your awe-inspiring bench, trip up the course of law and blunt the sword that guards the peace and safety of your person — no, more, to kick at your royal image and mock your actions in the person of your deputy."],[18,28,"Ask your royal thoughts; make the case your own. Be the father now and imagine a son; hear your own dignity so profaned, see your most dreaded laws so carelessly ignored, see yourself so scorned by a son. And then imagine me taking your side and, using your power, gently silencing your son. After this cool consideration, sentence me, and, as you are a king, say officially what I did that was unfitting for my office, my person or my lord's sovereignty."]],"933":[[0,10,"You're right, Justice, and you weigh this up well. So keep holding the scales and the sword. And I hope your honours will grow until you live to see a son of mine offend you and obey you as I did. Then I'll live to speak my father's words: \"I'm lucky to have a man so bold that he dares to do justice on my own son, and no less lucky to have a son who would hand his greatness over to the hands of justice like that.\""],[11,19,"You sent me to prison, and for that I now put into your hand the unstained sword you have carried, with this reminder: that you use it with the same bold, just and impartial spirit as you have against me. There's my hand. You'll be like a father to my youth. My voice will say what you prompt in my ear, and I will bend and humble my intentions to your experienced, wise guidance."],[20,31,"And, princes all, believe me, I beg you. My father has gone wild into his grave, because my wild impulses lie buried in his tomb, and I survive with his serious spirit to mock the world's expectations, to defeat the prophecies and to wipe out the rotten opinion that has judged me by how I seemed. The tide of blood in me has flowed proudly in vanity until now; now it turns and ebbs back to the sea, where it will mix with the majesty of the ocean and flow from now on in dignified majesty."],[32,38,"Now we'll summon our high court of parliament, and let's choose such limbs of noble counsel that the great body of our state may rank equally with the best-governed nation, so that war, or peace, or both at once, may be familiar things to us. In this you, father, will have the leading hand."],[39,43,"Once our coronation is over, we'll summon, as I said before, all our nobles. And, God approving my good intentions, no prince or lord shall have good reason to say, \"God shorten Harry's happy life by a single day!\""]],"1067":[[0,7,"I don't know you, old man. Say your prayers. How badly white hair suits a fool and a jester! I've long dreamed of a man like this, so swollen with excess, so old and so irreverent, but now that I'm awake I despise my dream. From now on make your body smaller and your virtue greater. Give up gluttony. Know that the grave gapes three times wider for you than for other men."],[8,15,"Don't answer me with a jest born of folly. Don't imagine I'm the thing I was, because God knows, and the world will see, that I have turned away my former self, and so I will turn away those who kept me company. When you hear that I'm as I used to be, come to me, and you shall be what you were, the teacher and supplier of my riots."],[16,25,"Until then I banish you, on pain of death, as I have the rest of those who led me astray, not to come within ten miles of me. I'll give you enough to live on, so that lack of money doesn't drive you to crime, and when we hear that you've reformed yourselves, we'll promote you according to your abilities and qualities. My lord, it will be your job to see that what we've said is carried out. Move on."]],"1089":[[0,12,"First my fear, then my bow, and last my speech. My fear is your displeasure; my bow, my duty; and my speech, to ask your pardon. If you're expecting a good speech now, you'll ruin me, because what I have to say is my own invention, and what I ought to say will, I'm afraid, be my undoing. But to the point, and so to the risk. Let me tell you, as you know very well, I was here recently at the end of an unpopular play, to ask your patience with it and promise you a better one. I meant to pay you back with this one; and if, like a failed trading voyage, it comes home unluckily, I'm bankrupt and you, my kind creditors, lose. I promised you I'd be here, and here I put myself at your mercy. Let me off some of it and I'll pay you some, and, like most debtors, promise you endlessly."],[13,18,"If my tongue can't persuade you to let me off, will you order me to use my legs? And yet that would be light payment, to dance my way out of your debt. But a good conscience will make any repayment it can, and so would I. All the ladies here have forgiven me. If the gentlemen won't, then the gentlemen don't agree with the ladies, which has never been seen before in such a gathering."],[19,24,"One word more, please. If you're not too sick of fat meat, our humble author will continue the story, with Sir John in it, and amuse you with fair Katharine of France, where, for all I know, Falstaff will die of a fever, unless he's already been killed by your harsh opinions; for Oldcastle died a martyr, and this is not the man."],[25,26,"My tongue is tired; when my legs are too, I'll say good night to you. And so I kneel before you — but in fact to pray for the Queen."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"kingship":["Kingship and the crown","The weight of the crown, the guilt of a usurper, and what Hal must become to wear it."],"fathers":["Fathers and sons","The King and Hal, Northumberland and Hotspur, Falstaff as false father, the Lord Chief Justice as true one."],"time":["Age, time and death","Old men remembering, bodies failing, the chimes at midnight and the grave that gapes."],"sickness":["Sickness and the body politic","A sick king, a sick realm, and Falstaff's diseases."],"rumour":["Rumour, report and reputation","False news, borrowed fame and the gap between seeming and being."],"misrule":["Misrule and reform","The tavern, Falstaff's world of appetite, and Hal's turning away from it."],"justice":["Law and justice","The Lord Chief Justice, country justices and the rule of law."],"rebellion":["Rebellion and policy","The Archbishop's holy cause, Northumberland's failures and Prince John's trick."]},"quotes":[{"id":"2","who":"RUMOUR","text":"Open your ears; for which of you will stop The vent of hearing when loud Rumour speaks?","themes":["rumour"],"note":"The play opens by demanding that we listen, and warning that what we hear may be false."},{"id":"2","who":"RUMOUR","text":"Upon my tongues continual slanders ride,","themes":["rumour"],"note":"Rumour's costume of tongues made into a boast."},{"id":"2","who":"RUMOUR","text":"Rumour is a pipe Blown by surmises, jealousies, conjectures,","themes":["rumour"],"note":"Report is an instrument anyone can play."},{"id":"2","who":"RUMOUR","text":"The still-discordant wavering multitude,","themes":["rumour","rebellion"],"note":"Contempt for the crowd that believes anything."},{"id":"2","who":"RUMOUR","text":"Lies crafty-sick:","themes":["sickness","rebellion"],"note":"Northumberland's illness at Shrewsbury was a pretence."},{"id":"2","who":"RUMOUR","text":"They bring smooth comforts false, worse than true wrongs.","themes":["rumour"],"note":"False comfort hurts more than true bad news."},{"id":"14","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","text":"The times are wild; contention, like a horse Full of high feeding, madly hath broke loose","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"Disorder as an overfed horse."},{"id":"17","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","text":"As good as heart can wish:","themes":["rumour"],"note":"The false good news, delivered with confidence."},{"id":"25","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","text":"Of Hotspur Coldspur?","themes":["rumour","fathers"],"note":"A father hears his son's death in a pun."},{"id":"30","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","text":"Yea, this man's brow, like to a title-leaf, Foretells the nature of a tragic volume:","themes":["rumour"],"note":"A face read like a book."},{"id":"34","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","text":"See what a ready tongue suspicion hath!","themes":["rumour"],"note":"Fear knows the truth before it is told."},{"id":"36","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","text":"Yet the first bringer of unwelcome news Hath but a losing office,","themes":["rumour"],"note":"The messenger of bad news is remembered like a funeral bell."},{"id":"38","who":"MORTON","text":"For from his metal was his party steel'd;","themes":["rebellion","fathers"],"note":"Hotspur was the steel of the rebellion."},{"id":"39","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","text":"In poison there is physic;","themes":["sickness"],"note":"Grief cures Northumberland's pretended sickness."},{"id":"39","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","text":"Let heaven kiss earth! now let not Nature's hand Keep the wild flood confined! let order die!","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"Northumberland calls for the end of all order."},{"id":"39","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","text":"And darkness be the burier of the dead!","themes":["time","rebellion"],"note":"His rage ends in an image of universal death."},{"id":"42","who":"MORTON","text":"You cast the event of war, my noble lord,","themes":["fathers","rebellion"],"note":"Northumberland knew the risk to his son."},{"id":"44","who":"MORTON","text":"But now the bishop Turns insurrection to religion:","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"The Archbishop gives the rising a holy cover."},{"id":"44","who":"MORTON","text":"Of fair King Richard, scraped from Pomfret stones;","themes":["rebellion","kingship"],"note":"Richard's murder becomes a relic for rebels."},{"id":"45","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","text":"Never so few, and never yet more need.","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"A rhyming close that sounds firm but leads to nothing."},{"id":"49","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"what says the doctor to my water?","themes":["sickness","time"],"note":"Falstaff's first line is about his health."},{"id":"51","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I am not only witty in myself, but the cause that wit is in other men.","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Falstaff's famous boast about his wit."},{"id":"51","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I do here walk before thee like a sow that hath overwhelmed all her litter but one.","themes":["misrule"],"note":"A grotesque image of himself beside his tiny page."},{"id":"76","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"some relish of the saltness of time;","themes":["time","justice"],"note":"Falstaff mocks the Justice's age, though he is old himself."},{"id":"86","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"it is the disease of not listening, the malady of not marking,","themes":["sickness","justice"],"note":"Falstaff turns the Justice's charge into a diagnosis."},{"id":"88","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I am as poor as Job, my lord, but not so patient:","themes":["justice"],"note":"Biblical wit against the law."},{"id":"91","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","text":"Well, the truth is, Sir John, you live in great infamy.","themes":["justice","rumour"],"note":"The Justice states plainly what Falstaff's reputation really is."},{"id":"92","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"He that buckles himself in my belt cannot live in less.","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Infamy becomes a joke about his size."},{"id":"93","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","text":"Your means are very slender, and your waste is great.","themes":["justice","misrule"],"note":"The Justice can pun as well as Falstaff."},{"id":"95","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","text":"You have misled the youthful prince.","themes":["misrule","fathers"],"note":"The charge against Falstaff, put directly."},{"id":"96","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"The young prince hath misled me:","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Falstaff reverses the accusation."},{"id":"101","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","text":"What! you are as a candle, the better part burnt out.","themes":["time"],"note":"Falstaff's life is almost spent."},{"id":"104","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"His effect of gravy, gravy, gravy.","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Gravity becomes gravy: everything leads back to food."},{"id":"106","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"You that are old consider not the capacities of us that are young;","themes":["time"],"note":"An old man claiming youth."},{"id":"107","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","text":"and will you yet call yourself young?","themes":["time","justice"],"note":"The Justice lists the signs of age."},{"id":"108","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"My lord, I was born about three of the clock in the afternoon, with a white head and something a round belly.","themes":["time","misrule"],"note":"Falstaff claims he was born old."},{"id":"109","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","text":"Well, God send the prince a better companion!","themes":["misrule","justice"],"note":"The Justice hopes for what Hal will finally do."},{"id":"110","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"God send the companion a better prince!","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Falstaff's instant reversal."},{"id":"112","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I were better to be eaten to death with a rust than to be scoured to nothing with perpetual motion.","themes":["time"],"note":"Falstaff pretends he is worn out by too much service."},{"id":"117","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"A man can no more separate age and covetousness than 'a can part young limbs and lechery:","themes":["time","misrule"],"note":"Falstaff's view of human nature."},{"id":"121","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I can get no remedy against this consumption of the purse:","themes":["sickness","misrule"],"note":"Debt described as a wasting disease."},{"id":"123","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I will turn diseases to commodity.","themes":["sickness","misrule"],"note":"Falstaff will profit even from his gout."},{"id":"134","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","text":"Eating the air on promise of supply,","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"Hotspur's mistake: living on promises."},{"id":"136","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","text":"When we mean to build, We first survey the plot, then draw the model;","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"The rebellion compared to building a house."},{"id":"136","who":"LORD BARDOLPH","text":"Using the names of men instead of men;","themes":["rebellion","rumour"],"note":"Armies on paper are not armies."},{"id":"144","who":"ARCHBISHOP","text":"An habitation giddy and unsure Hath he that buildeth on the vulgar heart.","themes":["rebellion","kingship"],"note":"Popularity is an unsafe foundation."},{"id":"144","who":"ARCHBISHOP","text":"O thou fond many,","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"The Archbishop's contempt for the crowd."},{"id":"144","who":"ARCHBISHOP","text":"And now thou wouldst eat thy dead vomit up,","themes":["rebellion","kingship"],"note":"The people now long for the Richard they rejected."},{"id":"144","who":"ARCHBISHOP","text":"Past and to come seems best; things present worst.","themes":["time","rebellion"],"note":"A line about political nostalgia."},{"id":"146","who":"HASTINGS","text":"We are time's subjects, and time bids be gone.","themes":["time","rebellion"],"note":"Even the rebels feel ruled by time."},{"id":"168","who":"HOSTESS","text":"Ah, thou honey-suckle villain!","themes":["misrule"],"note":"The Hostess's mangled insults."},{"id":"179","who":"HOSTESS","text":"He hath eaten me out of house and home;","themes":["misrule"],"note":"The Hostess gives English a phrase."},{"id":"183","who":"HOSTESS","text":"Thou didst swear to me upon a parcel-gilt goblet,","themes":["misrule","justice"],"note":"The Hostess's long, detailed charge of a broken promise."},{"id":"185","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","text":"I am well acquainted with your manner of wrenching the true cause the false way.","themes":["justice"],"note":"The Justice sees through Falstaff."},{"id":"187","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","text":"Pay her the debt you owe her, and unpay the villany you have done her:","themes":["justice"],"note":"Justice in money and in repentance."},{"id":"198","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Come, an 'twere not for thy humours, there's not a better wench in England.","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Falstaff charms the Hostess into lending him more."},{"id":"223","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","text":"thou art a great fool.","themes":["justice"],"note":"The Justice's last word on Falstaff in this scene."},{"id":"227","who":"HAL","text":"Before God, I am exceeding weary.","themes":["misrule","time"],"note":"Hal's first line in the play: weariness."},{"id":"229","who":"HAL","text":"Doth it not show vilely in me to desire small beer?","themes":["misrule","kingship"],"note":"A prince ashamed of his low appetite."},{"id":"231","who":"HAL","text":"these humble considerations make me out of love with my greatness.","themes":["kingship","misrule"],"note":"Hal feels his low company diminishing him."},{"id":"239","who":"HAL","text":"let the end try the man.","themes":["kingship","misrule"],"note":"Hal asks to be judged by how he ends."},{"id":"239","who":"HAL","text":"my heart bleeds inwardly that my father is so sick:","themes":["fathers","sickness"],"note":"Hal's private grief for his father."},{"id":"242","who":"POINS","text":"I would think thee a most princely hypocrite.","themes":["rumour","fathers"],"note":"Reputation stops Hal from showing true feeling."},{"id":"265","who":"POINS","text":"Marry, the immortal part needs a physician;","themes":["sickness","misrule"],"note":"Falstaff's soul is sicker than his body."},{"id":"271","who":"POINS","text":"JACK FALSTAFF with my familiars, JOHN with my brothers and sisters, and SIR JOHN with all Europe.","themes":["rumour","misrule"],"note":"Falstaff's grand self-image in his letter."},{"id":"274","who":"HAL","text":"Well, thus we play the fools with the time, and the spirits of the wise sit in the clouds and mock us.","themes":["time","misrule"],"note":"Hal knows he is wasting time."},{"id":"276","who":"HAL","text":"doth the old boar feed in the old frank?","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Falstaff as a pig in a sty."},{"id":"295","who":"HAL","text":"From a prince to a prentice? a low transformation!","themes":["kingship","misrule"],"note":"Hal's disguise as a drawer."},{"id":"295","who":"HAL","text":"for in everything the purpose must weigh with the folly.","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Hal's folly always has a purpose."},{"id":"301","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","text":"Alas, sweet wife, my honour is at pawn;","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"Honour as a debt to be redeemed."},{"id":"302","who":"LADY PERCY","text":"There were two honours lost, yours and your son's.","themes":["fathers","rebellion"],"note":"Lady Percy's charge against Northumberland."},{"id":"302","who":"LADY PERCY","text":"he was indeed the glass Wherein the noble youth did dress themselves:","themes":["fathers"],"note":"Hotspur as the mirror of chivalry."},{"id":"302","who":"LADY PERCY","text":"He was the mark and glass, copy and book, That fashion'd others.","themes":["fathers"],"note":"Hotspur shaped a generation."},{"id":"302","who":"LADY PERCY","text":"O miracle of men! him did you leave,","themes":["fathers","rebellion"],"note":"The climax of Lady Percy's accusation."},{"id":"306","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","text":"'Tis with my mind As with the tide swell'd up unto his height, That makes a still-stand, running neither way:","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"Northumberland's paralysis."},{"id":"318","who":"HOSTESS","text":"your pulsidge beats as extraordinarily as heart would desire;","themes":["misrule","sickness"],"note":"The Hostess's medical malapropisms."},{"id":"324","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"So is all her sect; an they be once in a calm, they are sick.","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Tavern bawdy about women and sickness."},{"id":"332","who":"HOSTESS","text":"you two never meet but you fall to some discord:","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Doll and Falstaff's affectionate quarrels."},{"id":"333","who":"DOLL","text":"Come, I'll be friends with thee, Jack: thou art going to the wars;","themes":["misrule","time"],"note":"Doll's sudden tenderness."},{"id":"337","who":"HOSTESS","text":"I'll no swaggerers:","themes":["misrule"],"note":"The Hostess's comic horror of swaggerers."},{"id":"360","who":"DOLL","text":"Captain! thou abominable damned cheater, art thou not ashamed to be called captain?","themes":["rumour","misrule"],"note":"Doll attacks Pistol's false title."},{"id":"367","who":"PISTOL","text":"Shall packhorses And hollow pamper'd jades of Asia,","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Pistol's ranting parody of stage heroes."},{"id":"370","who":"PISTOL","text":"Die men like dogs! give crowns like pins!","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Pistol's bombast."},{"id":"383","who":"PISTOL","text":"Then death rock me asleep, abridge my doleful days!","themes":["time","misrule"],"note":"Pistol borrows tragic language for a tavern brawl."},{"id":"397","who":"DOLL","text":"thou art as valorous as Hector of Troy,","themes":["misrule","rumour"],"note":"Doll's mock-heroic praise of Falstaff."},{"id":"403","who":"DOLL","text":"when wilt thou leave fighting o' days and foining o' nights, and begin to patch up thine old body for heaven?","themes":["time","misrule"],"note":"Doll tells Falstaff to think of death."},{"id":"405","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"do not speak like a death's-head; do not bid me remember mine end.","themes":["time"],"note":"Falstaff refuses to think about dying."},{"id":"407","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"A good shallow young fellow:","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Falstaff's verdict on Hal, overheard by Hal."},{"id":"415","who":"POINS","text":"Is it not strange that desire should so many years outlive performance?","themes":["time","misrule"],"note":"Poins mocks the old lover."},{"id":"421","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I am old, I am old.","themes":["time"],"note":"Falstaff's rare admission."},{"id":"423","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Thou'lt forget me when I am gone.","themes":["time","misrule"],"note":"A line that foreshadows his rejection."},{"id":"428","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Ha! a bastard son of the king's?","themes":["kingship","misrule"],"note":"Falstaff jokes when Hal is revealed."},{"id":"429","who":"HAL","text":"Why, thou globe of sinful continents, what a life dost thou lead!","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Falstaff's body as a world of sin."},{"id":"446","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I dispraised him before the wicked, that the wicked might not fall in love with him;","themes":["misrule","rumour"],"note":"Falstaff's quick-witted escape."},{"id":"449","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"his face is Lucifer's privy-kitchen,","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Bardolph's red face in hell."},{"id":"463","who":"HAL","text":"By heaven, Poins, I feel me much to blame, So idly to profane the precious time,","themes":["time","kingship"],"note":"Hal's turn from tavern to duty."},{"id":"469","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"the undeserver may sleep, when the man of action is called on.","themes":["misrule","rumour"],"note":"Falstaff enjoys being in demand."},{"id":"473","who":"HOSTESS","text":"I have known thee these twenty-nine years, come peascod-time;","themes":["time","misrule"],"note":"The Hostess's long attachment to Falstaff."},{"id":"483","who":"KING","text":"How many thousands of my poorest subjects Are at this hour asleep!","themes":["kingship","sickness"],"note":"The King envies his poorest subjects."},{"id":"483","who":"KING","text":"O sleep, O gentle sleep, Nature's soft nurse, how have I frighted thee,","themes":["kingship","sickness"],"note":"The opening of the sleep speech."},{"id":"483","who":"KING","text":"Uneasy lies the head that wears a crown.","themes":["kingship"],"note":"The play's best-known line about kingship."},{"id":"490","who":"KING","text":"Then you perceive the body of our kingdom How foul it is;","themes":["sickness","kingship"],"note":"The kingdom as a diseased body."},{"id":"492","who":"KING","text":"O God! that one might read the book of fate,","themes":["time","kingship"],"note":"The King longs to see the future."},{"id":"492","who":"KING","text":"Would shut the book, and sit him down and die.","themes":["time"],"note":"Knowledge of the future would kill hope."},{"id":"494","who":"KING","text":"\"Northumberland, thou ladder by the which My cousin Bolingbroke ascends my throne;\"","themes":["kingship","rebellion"],"note":"Richard's prophecy, remembered."},{"id":"494","who":"KING","text":"That I and greatness were compell'd to kiss:","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Henry claims he never meant to take the crown."},{"id":"495","who":"WARWICK","text":"There is a history in all men's lives,","themes":["time"],"note":"Warwick's view of history as a pattern."},{"id":"496","who":"KING","text":"Are these things then necessities? Then let us meet them like necessities:","themes":["kingship","rebellion"],"note":"The King accepts what he cannot change."},{"id":"497","who":"WARWICK","text":"Rumour doth double, like the voice and echo, The numbers of the fear'd.","themes":["rumour"],"note":"Rumour again, this time on the King's side."},{"id":"510","who":"SHALLOW","text":"I was called any thing; and I would have done any thing indeed too,","themes":["time","misrule"],"note":"Shallow's invented wild youth."},{"id":"512","who":"SHALLOW","text":"Jesu, Jesu, the mad days that I have spent! and to see how many of my old acquaintance are dead!","themes":["time"],"note":"Comedy and melancholy mixed."},{"id":"514","who":"SHALLOW","text":"death, as the Psalmist saith, is certain to all; all shall die. How a good yoke of bullocks at Stamford fair?","themes":["time"],"note":"Shallow moves from death to cattle prices in one breath."},{"id":"518","who":"SHALLOW","text":"Jesu, Jesu, dead!","themes":["time"],"note":"Shallow's lament for old Double."},{"id":"529","who":"BARDOLPH","text":"Accommodated; that is, when a man is, as they say, accommodated;","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Bardolph cannot define his fashionable word."},{"id":"545","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"'Tis the more time thou wert used.","themes":["misrule","justice"],"note":"Falstaff's joke as he presses Mouldy."},{"id":"559","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"for we have a number of shadows to fill up the muster-book.","themes":["rumour","justice"],"note":"Falstaff's corrupt recruiting: names without men."},{"id":"575","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"thou wilt be as valiant as the wrathful dove or most magnanimous mouse.","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Mock praise of Feeble."},{"id":"589","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Come, thou shalt go to the wars in a gown;","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Falstaff mocks Bullcalf's cold."},{"id":"601","who":"SILENCE","text":"That's fifty-five year ago.","themes":["time"],"note":"Silence measures the years."},{"id":"603","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"We have heard the chimes at midnight, Master Shallow.","themes":["time","misrule"],"note":"Falstaff's tender, melancholy line about youth."},{"id":"604","who":"SHALLOW","text":"Jesus, the days that we have seen!","themes":["time"],"note":"Shallow echoes Falstaff's nostalgia."},{"id":"610","who":"FEEBLE","text":"a man can die but once; we owe God a death:","themes":["time","justice"],"note":"Feeble's plain courage."},{"id":"610","who":"FEEBLE","text":"he that dies this year is quit for the next.","themes":["time"],"note":"A soldier's simple philosophy."},{"id":"623","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Give me the spirit, Master Shallow.","themes":["misrule","justice"],"note":"Falstaff's excuse for choosing the weakest men."},{"id":"623","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"O, give me the spare men, and spare me the great ones.","themes":["misrule"],"note":"A pun that hides his bribes."},{"id":"635","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Lord, Lord, how subject we old men are to this vice of lying!","themes":["time","misrule"],"note":"Falstaff mocks Shallow's lies, unaware of his own."},{"id":"635","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"if the young dace be a bait for the old pike, I see no reason in the law of nature but I may snap at him.","themes":["misrule","justice"],"note":"Falstaff's predatory plan."},{"id":"644","who":"MOWBRAY","text":"Thus do the hopes we have in him touch ground And dash themselves to pieces.","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"Northumberland fails the rebels again."},{"id":"654","who":"WESTMORELAND","text":"Turning your books to graves, your ink to blood,","themes":["rebellion","justice"],"note":"Westmoreland's rebuke to the Archbishop."},{"id":"655","who":"ARCHBISHOP","text":"we are all diseased,","themes":["sickness","rebellion"],"note":"The Archbishop diagnoses the realm."},{"id":"655","who":"ARCHBISHOP","text":"And find our griefs heavier than our offences.","themes":["rebellion","justice"],"note":"The rebels' justification."},{"id":"662","who":"WESTMORELAND","text":"Who knows on whom fortune would then have smiled?","themes":["rebellion","time"],"note":"Westmoreland answers Mowbray's grievance about the past."},{"id":"666","who":"WESTMORELAND","text":"A rotten case abides no handling.","themes":["rebellion","justice"],"note":"A bad cause cannot bear examination."},{"id":"675","who":"MOWBRAY","text":"We shall be winnow'd with so rough a wind That even our corn shall seem as light as chaff","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"Mowbray foresees the King's revenge."},{"id":"678","who":"ARCHBISHOP","text":"Our peace will, like a broken limb united, Grow stronger for the breaking.","themes":["rebellion","sickness"],"note":"The Archbishop's misplaced optimism."},{"id":"687","who":"PRINCE JOHN","text":"Turning the word to sword and life to death.","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"Prince John's charge against the Archbishop."},{"id":"687","who":"PRINCE JOHN","text":"Under the counterfeited zeal of God,","themes":["rebellion","rumour"],"note":"Prince John calls the rebels' piety a counterfeit."},{"id":"693","who":"PRINCE JOHN","text":"My lord, these griefs shall be with speed redress'd;","themes":["rebellion","justice"],"note":"The carefully worded promise."},{"id":"703","who":"ARCHBISHOP","text":"Against ill chances men are ever merry;","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"The Archbishop's proverb is about to come true."},{"id":"710","who":"ARCHBISHOP","text":"A peace is of the nature of a conquest;","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"Dramatic irony: the peace is a trap."},{"id":"723","who":"WESTMORELAND","text":"I do arrest thee, traitor, of high treason:","themes":["rebellion","justice"],"note":"The trick is sprung."},{"id":"727","who":"PRINCE JOHN","text":"I pawn'd thee none:","themes":["rebellion","justice"],"note":"Prince John's defence: he promised redress, not mercy."},{"id":"727","who":"PRINCE JOHN","text":"God, and not we, hath safely fought to-day.","themes":["rebellion"],"note":"Prince John gives credit to God for a victory won by deceit."},{"id":"737","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I have a whole school of tongues in this belly of mine, and not a tongue of them all speaks any other word but my name.","themes":["rumour"],"note":"Falstaff's fame does his fighting for him."},{"id":"742","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"\"I came, saw, and overcame.\"","themes":["rumour","misrule"],"note":"Falstaff borrows Caesar's boast."},{"id":"762","who":"PRINCE JOHN","text":"Shall better speak of you than you deserve.","themes":["rumour","justice"],"note":"Prince John's cold farewell."},{"id":"764","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"this same young sober-blooded boy doth not love me;","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Falstaff on Prince John."},{"id":"764","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"A good sherris-sack hath a two-fold operation in it.","themes":["misrule"],"note":"The start of Falstaff's hymn to sack."},{"id":"764","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"to forswear thin potations and to addict themselves to sack.","themes":["misrule","fathers"],"note":"Falstaff's mock advice to his imaginary sons."},{"id":"781","who":"KING","text":"For he is gracious, if he be observed.","themes":["fathers","kingship"],"note":"The King's insight into Hal's nature."},{"id":"781","who":"KING","text":"A hoop of gold to bind thy brothers in,","themes":["fathers","kingship"],"note":"Clarence's role as peacemaker."},{"id":"787","who":"KING","text":"Most subject is the fattest soil to weeds;","themes":["fathers","misrule"],"note":"The King's fear for Hal."},{"id":"787","who":"KING","text":"When I am sleeping with my ancestors.","themes":["time","fathers"],"note":"The King imagines his death."},{"id":"788","who":"WARWICK","text":"The prince but studies his companions Like a strange tongue,","themes":["misrule","kingship"],"note":"Warwick's defence of Hal."},{"id":"788","who":"WARWICK","text":"Cast off his followers;","themes":["misrule","kingship"],"note":"A prediction of the rejection."},{"id":"789","who":"KING","text":"'Tis seldom when the bee doth leave her comb In the dead carrion.","themes":["fathers","misrule"],"note":"The King doubts Hal can leave his habits."},{"id":"797","who":"KING","text":"And wherefore should these good news make me sick?","themes":["sickness","kingship"],"note":"Victory comes too late for the King."},{"id":"797","who":"KING","text":"Will Fortune never come with both hands full,","themes":["time","kingship"],"note":"Fortune gives and takes away."},{"id":"802","who":"CLARENCE","text":"So thin that life looks through and will break out.","themes":["sickness","time"],"note":"The King's body worn thin by care."},{"id":"829","who":"HAL","text":"O polish'd perturbation! golden care!","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Hal addresses the crown."},{"id":"829","who":"HAL","text":"Like a rich armour worn in heat of day, That scalds with safety.","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Majesty protects and burns."},{"id":"829","who":"HAL","text":"My due from thee is this imperial crown,","themes":["kingship","fathers"],"note":"Hal claims his inheritance."},{"id":"844","who":"KING","text":"See, sons, what things you are!","themes":["fathers"],"note":"The King's bitter outburst."},{"id":"844","who":"KING","text":"Are murdered for our pains.","themes":["fathers"],"note":"Fathers as bees killed for their honey."},{"id":"853","who":"KING","text":"Thy wish was father, Harry, to that thought:","themes":["fathers","kingship"],"note":"The King accuses Hal of wishing him dead."},{"id":"853","who":"KING","text":"Harry the Fifth is crown'd: up, vanity!","themes":["kingship","misrule"],"note":"The King's nightmare of Hal's reign."},{"id":"853","who":"KING","text":"O, thou wilt be a wilderness again, Peopled with wolves, thy old inhabitants!","themes":["kingship","rebellion"],"note":"England returned to savagery."},{"id":"854","who":"HAL","text":"O, pardon me, my liege!","themes":["fathers"],"note":"Hal's plea begins."},{"id":"854","who":"HAL","text":"Hast eat thy bearer up.","themes":["kingship","sickness"],"note":"The crown has devoured his father."},{"id":"855","who":"KING","text":"By what by-paths and indirect crook'd ways I met this crown;","themes":["kingship","fathers"],"note":"Henry's confession of usurpation."},{"id":"855","who":"KING","text":"For all the soil of the achievement goes With me into the earth.","themes":["kingship","fathers"],"note":"The stain of usurpation will die with Henry."},{"id":"855","who":"KING","text":"Be it thy course to busy giddy minds With foreign quarrels;","themes":["kingship","rebellion"],"note":"The King's political advice."},{"id":"855","who":"KING","text":"How I came by the crown, O God, forgive;","themes":["kingship"],"note":"The King's last prayer."},{"id":"856","who":"HAL","text":"You won it, wore it, kept it, gave it me;","themes":["kingship","fathers"],"note":"Hal's simple claim to rightful possession."},{"id":"865","who":"KING","text":"In that Jerusalem shall Harry die.","themes":["kingship","time"],"note":"The prophecy fulfilled."},{"id":"871","who":"SHALLOW","text":"I will not excuse you; you shall not be excused;","themes":["misrule"],"note":"Shallow's comic repetition."},{"id":"882","who":"SHALLOW","text":"a friend i' the court is better than a penny in purse.","themes":["justice"],"note":"Shallow's motive for hospitality."},{"id":"887","who":"DAVY","text":"An honest man, sir, is able to speak for himself, when a knave is not.","themes":["justice"],"note":"Davy's comic logic for corruption."},{"id":"897","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"therefore let men take heed of their company.","themes":["misrule","justice"],"note":"Falstaff's advice, which applies to Hal too."},{"id":"905","who":"WARWICK","text":"Exceeding well; his cares are now all ended.","themes":["kingship","time"],"note":"The King is dead."},{"id":"912","who":"WARWICK","text":"Here comes the heavy issue of dead Harry:","themes":["fathers","kingship"],"note":"The princes in mourning."},{"id":"923","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","text":"Sweet Princes, what I did, I did in honour,","themes":["justice"],"note":"The Justice's integrity."},{"id":"927","who":"KING HENRY V","text":"This new and gorgeous garment, majesty, Sits not so easy on me as you think.","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Henry V's first words as king."},{"id":"927","who":"KING HENRY V","text":"This is the English, not the Turkish court;","themes":["kingship","fathers"],"note":"He will not murder his brothers."},{"id":"927","who":"KING HENRY V","text":"I'll be your father and your brother too;","themes":["fathers","kingship"],"note":"Henry reassures his brothers."},{"id":"931","who":"KING HENRY V","text":"How might a prince of my great hopes forget So great indignities you laid upon me?","themes":["justice","kingship"],"note":"Henry tests the Justice."},{"id":"932","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","text":"I then did use the person of your father;","themes":["justice","fathers"],"note":"The Justice acted as the King's image."},{"id":"932","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","text":"Be now the father and propose a son,","themes":["justice","fathers"],"note":"The Justice asks Henry to imagine himself as a father."},{"id":"933","who":"KING HENRY V","text":"You are right, justice, and you weigh this well;","themes":["justice","kingship"],"note":"Henry accepts the rule of law."},{"id":"933","who":"KING HENRY V","text":"You shall be as a father to my youth:","themes":["justice","fathers"],"note":"The Justice replaces Falstaff as Hal's father figure."},{"id":"933","who":"KING HENRY V","text":"My father is gone wild into his grave,","themes":["fathers","misrule"],"note":"Hal buries his wildness with his father."},{"id":"933","who":"KING HENRY V","text":"To mock the expectation of the world,","themes":["rumour","kingship"],"note":"Henry will prove report wrong."},{"id":"933","who":"KING HENRY V","text":"Now doth it turn and ebb back to the sea,","themes":["kingship","misrule"],"note":"The tide image of reformation."},{"id":"942","who":"SILENCE","text":"Do nothing but eat, and make good cheer,","themes":["misrule","time"],"note":"Silence's song in the orchard."},{"id":"954","who":"SILENCE","text":"I have been merry twice and once ere now.","themes":["time","misrule"],"note":"Silence's comic boast."},{"id":"993","who":"PISTOL","text":"thou art now one of the greatest men in this realm.","themes":["rumour","misrule"],"note":"Pistol announces Falstaff's supposed rise."},{"id":"1008","who":"PISTOL","text":"Sir John, thy tender lambkin now is king;","themes":["kingship","misrule"],"note":"Pistol's news of Hal's accession."},{"id":"1011","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"choose what office thou wilt in the land, 'tis thine.","themes":["misrule","justice"],"note":"Falstaff gives away offices he does not have."},{"id":"1016","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I know the young king is sick for me.","themes":["misrule","sickness"],"note":"Falstaff's fatal confidence."},{"id":"1016","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"the laws of England are at my commandment.","themes":["misrule","justice"],"note":"The line that the rejection answers."},{"id":"1016","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"woe to my lord chief-justice!","themes":["justice","misrule"],"note":"Falstaff expects revenge on the law."},{"id":"1028","who":"HOSTESS","text":"O God, that right should thus overcome might!","themes":["justice","misrule"],"note":"The Hostess reverses the proverb."},{"id":"1053","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"But to stand stained with travel, and sweating with desire to see him;","themes":["misrule","fathers"],"note":"Falstaff's love for Hal, on show."},{"id":"1061","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"God save thy grace, King Hal; my royal Hal!","themes":["misrule","kingship"],"note":"Falstaff's greeting."},{"id":"1063","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"God save thee, my sweet boy!","themes":["misrule","fathers"],"note":"Falstaff speaks to the King like a father to a son."},{"id":"1064","who":"KING HENRY V","text":"My lord chief-justice, speak to that vain man.","themes":["justice","kingship"],"note":"Henry first answers through the Justice."},{"id":"1067","who":"KING HENRY V","text":"I know thee not, old man: fall to thy prayers;","themes":["kingship","misrule","time"],"note":"The rejection."},{"id":"1067","who":"KING HENRY V","text":"How ill white hairs become a fool and jester!","themes":["time","misrule"],"note":"Henry sees Falstaff as an old fool."},{"id":"1067","who":"KING HENRY V","text":"I have long dream'd of such a kind of man,","themes":["misrule","kingship"],"note":"The tavern years as a dream."},{"id":"1067","who":"KING HENRY V","text":"know the grave doth gape For thee thrice wider than for other men.","themes":["time"],"note":"A last joke about Falstaff's size, and a warning of death."},{"id":"1067","who":"KING HENRY V","text":"That I have turn'd away my former self;","themes":["kingship","misrule"],"note":"Henry's transformation, stated publicly."},{"id":"1067","who":"KING HENRY V","text":"Till then, I banish thee, on pain of death,","themes":["justice","misrule"],"note":"The sentence."},{"id":"1069","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"Master Shallow, I owe you a thousand pounds.","themes":["misrule","justice"],"note":"Falstaff's first words after the rejection."},{"id":"1071","who":"FALSTAFF","text":"I shall be sent for in private to him:","themes":["misrule","rumour"],"note":"Falstaff cannot believe the change."},{"id":"1074","who":"SHALLOW","text":"A colour that I fear you will die in, Sir John.","themes":["time","misrule"],"note":"Shallow's unintended prophecy."},{"id":"1077","who":"CHIEF JUSTICE","text":"Go, carry Sir John Falstaff to the Fleet:","themes":["justice"],"note":"The law finally catches Falstaff."},{"id":"1082","who":"PRINCE JOHN","text":"I like this fair proceeding of the king's:","themes":["kingship","justice"],"note":"Prince John approves the rejection."},{"id":"1086","who":"PRINCE JOHN","text":"We bear our civil swords and native fire As far as France:","themes":["kingship","rebellion"],"note":"War abroad, as the old King advised."},{"id":"1089","who":"EPILOGUE","text":"our humble author will continue the story, with Sir John in it,","themes":["misrule"],"note":"A promise not kept: Falstaff dies offstage in Henry V."},{"id":"1089","who":"EPILOGUE","text":"for Oldcastle died a martyr, and this is not the man.","themes":["rumour"],"note":"A disclaimer about Falstaff's original name."}]};
const EX={"intro":["The Second Part of King Henry the Fourth picks up the story at the moment Part 1 ended. Hotspur is dead at Shrewsbury, but a new rebellion is gathering in the North under the Archbishop of York, and Hotspur's father, Northumberland, still wavers. King Henry is old and ill, and he cannot sleep. His son Prince Hal, the hero of Shrewsbury, has drifted back to Eastcheap, and Sir John Falstaff, now famous for a victory that was not his, is fatter, older and sicker than ever.","Where Part 1 built up to a battle, Part 2 is a play of waiting, remembering and dying. The rebellion ends not in combat but in a trick at Gaultree Forest, where Prince John promises to redress the rebels' grievances and then arrests them. Falstaff recruits ragged soldiers in Gloucestershire, takes bribes, and spends merry evenings with his old friend Justice Shallow, who remembers wild nights that never happened. In the tavern Doll Tearsheet tells Falstaff to think of heaven, and he answers, 'I am old, I am old.'","At the dying King's bedside Hal takes the crown too soon, is accused of wishing his father dead, and wins him back with a speech of love and grief. Crowned King Henry V, he keeps the Lord Chief Justice who once imprisoned him and calls him a father. When Falstaff rides through the night to greet him, Henry rejects him in front of the crowd: 'I know thee not, old man.' The play ends with the law in charge, Falstaff in prison, and England looking towards France."],"keyfacts":[["Full title","The Second Part of Henry the Fourth, Continuing to His Death, and Coronation of Henry the Fifth (title of the quarto, 1600); usually called Henry IV, Part 2"],["Written","About 1597 to 1598, soon after Part 1; printed in quarto in 1600"],["Genre","History: one of the Histories in the 1623 First Folio. The second play of the second tetralogy (Richard II, 1 and 2 Henry IV, Henry V)"],["Setting","England in 1403 to 1413: Warkworth Castle in Northumberland, London taverns and streets, York, Gaultree Forest in Yorkshire, Justice Shallow's house in Gloucestershire, and the palace at Westminster"],["Protagonist","Prince Hal, who becomes King Henry V"],["Antagonists","The rebels (the Archbishop of York, Mowbray, Hastings, Northumberland); in another sense Falstaff, whose world Hal must reject"],["Inciting incident","Morton's report of Hotspur's death and the Archbishop's new rising (1.1)"],["Turning point","Hal leaves the tavern at the news of war (2.4); the rebels are tricked and arrested at Gaultree (4.2)"],["Climax","The crown scene, where the dying King accuses Hal and is reconciled with him (4.5)"],["Resolution","Henry V keeps the Lord Chief Justice, rejects Falstaff and turns towards France (5.2, 5.5)"],["Time span","About ten years of history, from Shrewsbury (1403) to Henry IV's death (1413), compressed into what feels like a few weeks"],["Major conflicts","The King against the rebels; the King's fear of his son against Hal's private loyalty; Falstaff and misrule against the Lord Chief Justice and the law"],["Themes","Kingship and the crown; fathers and sons; age, time and death; sickness of the body and the realm; rumour and reputation; misrule and reform; law and justice; rebellion and policy"]],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"Rumour spreads false news that Hotspur won at Shrewsbury","id":2},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Morton tells Northumberland that Hotspur is dead","id":38},{"s":"a1s1","t":"The Archbishop of York raises a new rebellion","id":44},{"s":"a1s2","t":"The Lord Chief Justice confronts Falstaff in the street","id":59},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Falstaff learns he is sent north with Prince John","id":111},{"s":"a1s3","t":"The rebels debate whether to rely on Northumberland","id":136},{"s":"a2s1","t":"The Hostess has Falstaff arrested for debt","id":166},{"s":"a2s1","t":"The Justice makes Falstaff pay; the Hostess pawns her plate for him","id":187},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Hal confesses his grief for his sick father","id":239},{"s":"a2s3","t":"Lady Percy persuades Northumberland to flee to Scotland","id":302},{"s":"a2s4","t":"Falstaff drives Pistol out of the tavern","id":387},{"s":"a2s4","t":"Hal and Poins, disguised as waiters, spy on Falstaff","id":407},{"s":"a2s4","t":"Hal leaves at the news of war","id":463},{"s":"a3s1","t":"The sleepless King: 'Uneasy lies the head that wears a crown'","id":483},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Warwick reports Glendower's death","id":497},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Falstaff recruits soldiers at Shallow's house","id":540},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Mouldy and Bullcalf bribe their way out","id":606},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Northumberland deserts the rebels for Scotland","id":643},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Prince John promises redress; the rebels disband","id":693},{"s":"a4s2","t":"The rebel leaders are arrested for treason","id":723},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Colevile surrenders to Falstaff","id":736},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Falstaff praises sherris-sack","id":764},{"s":"a4s4","t":"News of victory reaches the King, who collapses","id":797},{"s":"a4s5","t":"Hal takes the crown from his sleeping father","id":829},{"s":"a4s5","t":"Father and son are reconciled; the King gives his last advice","id":855},{"s":"a5s2","t":"Henry V keeps the Lord Chief Justice as 'a father to my youth'","id":933},{"s":"a5s3","t":"Pistol brings Falstaff news that Hal is king","id":1008},{"s":"a5s4","t":"The Hostess and Doll are arrested","id":1021},{"s":"a5s5","t":"Henry V rejects Falstaff: 'I know thee not, old man'","id":1067},{"s":"a5s5","t":"Falstaff is sent to the Fleet; war with France is predicted","id":1077}],"irony":{"2":"Rumour tells the audience the truth before Northumberland hears the lie: we watch him believe what we know is false.","17":"Every detail of Lord Bardolph's good news is the reverse of what happened at Shrewsbury.","45":"Northumberland ends with a rousing rhyme about need, and then fails his allies again.","108":"Falstaff tells the Justice he has rebuked Hal for striking him: the Justice will outlast Falstaff in Hal's favour.","110":"'God send the companion a better prince!' The prince will indeed be 'better', and it will ruin the companion.","136":"Lord Bardolph warns against building on promises of support, and the rebels go on counting on Northumberland.","407":"Falstaff calls Hal 'a good shallow young fellow' while Hal stands behind him.","423":"'Thou'lt forget me when I am gone' is said to Doll, but it is Hal who will forget him.","497":"Warwick soothes the King's fears of rumour, as Rumour itself had promised to deceive.","678":"The Archbishop promises that their peace will grow stronger for the breaking; it breaks within the hour.","710":"'A peace is of the nature of a conquest': for Prince John, it is exactly that.","788":"Warwick predicts that Hal will 'cast off his followers', which he does in the final scene.","797":"Fortune brings the King victory just as his health fails.","853":"The King's picture of Harry the Fifth as a wild tyrant is the opposite of the king Hal becomes.","865":"The King expected to die in Jerusalem on crusade; he dies in a room of that name at Westminster.","1016":"Falstaff cries 'woe to my lord chief-justice!' while the new King is making the Justice his father.","1071":"Falstaff insists he will be sent for in private; he is sent for by the Justice, to prison."},"devices":{"sick":"Sickness and the body","time":"Time, age and death","tongue":"Tongues, report and rumour","debt":"Money, debt and pawning","grow":"Gardens, weeds and building","beasts":"Beasts and birds"},"dev":{"2":[["tongue","Rumour wears a costume of tongues and calls report a pipe that anyone can play.","Rumour is a pipe"]],"30":[["tongue","Morton's face is read like the title page of a tragedy.","like to a title-leaf"]],"39":[["sick","Grief works on Northumberland like poison that becomes medicine.","In poison there is physic"]],"49":[["sick","Falstaff's first concern is his urine sample.","what says the doctor to my water?"]],"101":[["time","Falstaff's life is a candle almost burnt out.","as a candle, the better part burnt out"]],"121":[["debt","Debt as a wasting disease of the purse.","consumption of the purse"]],"136":[["grow","A rebellion planned like a house: survey, model, cost.","We first survey the plot, then draw the model"],["grow","Early hopes are buds the frost may bite.","in an early spring We see the appearing buds"]],"144":[["beasts","The common people as a dog eating its own vomit.","thou common dog"]],"276":[["beasts","Falstaff as a boar in his sty.","doth the old boar feed in the old frank?"]],"301":[["debt","Honour as a pledge in pawn.","my honour is at pawn"]],"302":[["time","Hotspur as a sun in the grey vault of heaven.","as the sun In the grey vault of heaven"]],"403":[["time","Doll reminds Falstaff of his old body and heaven.","patch up thine old body for heaven"]],"405":[["time","A skull as a reminder of death.","do not speak like a death's-head"]],"463":[["time","Precious time profaned by idleness.","So idly to profane the precious time"]],"483":[["sick","Sleep as nature's nurse, frightened away from the king.","Nature's soft nurse"]],"490":[["sick","The kingdom as a diseased body.","the body of our kingdom"]],"492":[["time","The future as a book that would make men die if they read it.","read the book of fate"]],"495":[["grow","Future events lie in seeds and weak beginnings.","And weak beginning lie intreasured"]],"603":[["time","The chimes at midnight: youth remembered.","the chimes at midnight"]],"610":[["debt","Death as a debt owed to God.","we owe God a death"]],"655":[["sick","The realm sick from surfeiting, needing to be bled.","we are all diseased"]],"675":[["grow","Rebels winnowed like grain in a rough wind.","even our corn shall seem as light as chaff"]],"678":[["sick","Peace as a broken limb that heals stronger.","like a broken limb united"]],"764":[["grow","Hal's cold blood farmed and manured with sack.","manured, husbanded and tilled"]],"787":[["grow","Hal's rich nature overgrown with weeds.","Most subject is the fattest soil to weeds"]],"789":[["beasts","The bee rarely leaves its comb in a carcass.","the bee doth leave her comb"]],"829":[["sick","The crown as an armour that scalds.","Like a rich armour worn in heat of day"]],"844":[["beasts","Fathers as bees murdered for their honey.","like the bees, Are murdered for our pains"]],"853":[["beasts","England left a wilderness of wolves.","Peopled with wolves, thy old inhabitants"]],"933":[["time","Hal's reformation as a tide ebbing back to the sea.","Now doth it turn and ebb back to the sea"]]},"stage":{"castle":"Warkworth Castle, Northumberland's stronghold in the North","street":"A London street","church":"The Archbishop's palace at York","tavern":"The Boar's Head tavern in Eastcheap","palace":"The royal palace at Westminster","house":"Justice Shallow's house in Gloucestershire","forest":"Gaultree Forest in Yorkshire","room":"A chamber in the palace: the Jerusalem Chamber","orchard":"Justice Shallow's orchard","abbey":"A street near Westminster Abbey"},"stagerules":[["archbishop","church"],["tavern","tavern"],["castle","castle"],["orchard","orchard"],["shallow","house"],["forest","forest"],["jerusalem|chamber","room"],["abbey","abbey"],["palace","palace"],["street","street"]],"place":{"a1s1":"Warkworth Castle, Northumberland","a1s2":"A street, London","a1s3":"The Archbishop's palace, York","a2s1":"A street, London","a2s2":"Another street, London","a2s3":"Warkworth Castle","a2s4":"The Boar's Head, Eastcheap","a3s1":"The palace, Westminster","a3s2":"Before Justice Shallow's house, Gloucestershire","a4s1":"Gaultree Forest, Yorkshire","a4s2":"Gaultree Forest, between the armies","a4s3":"Gaultree Forest, after the rout","a4s4":"The Jerusalem Chamber, Westminster","a4s5":"Another chamber, Westminster","a5s1":"Shallow's house, Gloucestershire","a5s2":"The palace, Westminster","a5s3":"Shallow's orchard, Gloucestershire","a5s4":"A street, London","a5s5":"Near Westminster Abbey, London"},"arc":{"Hal's standing":[2,2,2,2,3,3,4,4,4,4,4,5,3,7,7,9,9,9,10],"Falstaff's standing":[6,6,6,5,5,5,6,6,7,7,7,8,7,7,7,6,10,8,0]},"arcnote":"Hal's standing begins low: Rumour says he is dead, the Justice and his father think him lost to Falstaff, and he himself feels ashamed and unable to show grief. It rises a little when he leaves the tavern for duty, dips in 4.4 when his father fears for England under him, jumps in 4.5 when he wins his father's love, and climbs to its peak as he keeps the Lord Chief Justice and is crowned. Falstaff's standing starts fairly high on his false fame from Shrewsbury and rises as his reputation captures Colevile and his hopes swell at Shallow's. It peaks in 5.3 when he hears Hal is king and thinks the laws of England are his, falls as Doll and the Hostess are arrested, and collapses to nothing when the new King rejects him. The two lines cross at the end: one man's coronation is the other's banishment.","iago":[["a1s1","Rumour reports Hal dead; the truth is that he killed Hotspur."],["a1s2","The Justice reveals the King has separated Hal from Falstaff."],["a2s2","Hal admits his heart bleeds for his sick father but he cannot show it."],["a2s2","He feels his low company has made him 'out of love with my greatness'."],["a2s4","He spies on Falstaff disguised as a waiter, one last tavern game."],["a2s4","At news of war he blames himself for profaning time and leaves at once."],["a4s3","Falstaff credits Hal's valour to sack: the tavern still claims him."],["a4s4","The King fears Hal's rule; Warwick predicts he will cast off his followers."],["a4s5","Thinking his father dead, Hal takes up the crown as his due."],["a4s5","Accused of wishing his father dead, he wins him back with his defence."],["a4s5","He receives the King's confession and his advice on foreign quarrels."],["a4s5","He claims the crown: 'You won it, wore it, kept it, gave it me'."],["a5s2","As King Henry V he reassures his frightened brothers."],["a5s2","He keeps the Lord Chief Justice and makes him a father to his youth."],["a5s2","He declares his wildness buried with his father and calls a parliament."],["a5s5","He rejects Falstaff in public: 'I know thee not, old man'."],["a5s5","He banishes his old companions but grants them a living."],["a5s5","Prince John predicts the war in France that his father advised."]],"rel":{"1":[["NORTHUMBERLAND","LORD BARDOLPH","trust"],["ARCHBISHOP","NORTHUMBERLAND","trust"],["CHIEF JUSTICE","FALSTAFF","hate"],["FALSTAFF","CHIEF JUSTICE","deceive"],["FALSTAFF","HAL","love"],["MOWBRAY","KING","hate"],["HASTINGS","NORTHUMBERLAND","trust"]],"2":[["HOSTESS","FALSTAFF","love"],["FALSTAFF","HOSTESS","deceive"],["HAL","POINS","trust"],["HAL","FALSTAFF","deceive"],["DOLL","FALSTAFF","love"],["DOLL","PISTOL","hate"],["LADY PERCY","NORTHUMBERLAND","hate"],["NORTHUMBERLAND","ARCHBISHOP","deceive"]],"3":[["KING","WARWICK","trust"],["KING","NORTHUMBERLAND","hate"],["SHALLOW","FALSTAFF","love"],["FALSTAFF","SHALLOW","deceive"],["BARDOLPH","FALSTAFF","trust"]],"4":[["PRINCE JOHN","ARCHBISHOP","deceive"],["WESTMORELAND","MOWBRAY","deceive"],["MOWBRAY","PRINCE JOHN","hate"],["KING","HAL","hate"],["HAL","KING","love"],["KING","CLARENCE","trust"],["WARWICK","HAL","trust"]],"5":[["KING HENRY V","CHIEF JUSTICE","trust"],["CHIEF JUSTICE","KING HENRY V","love"],["FALSTAFF","KING HENRY V","love"],["KING HENRY V","FALSTAFF","hate"],["SHALLOW","FALSTAFF","trust"],["PISTOL","FALSTAFF","love"],["PRINCE JOHN","KING HENRY V","trust"]]},"context":[["Date","Written about 1597 to 1598, straight after Part 1, and printed in a single quarto in 1600. Falstaff's success in Part 1 clearly shaped it: he has more scenes, and new comic figures (Shallow, Silence, Pistol, Doll) are added around him."],["Sources","Shakespeare's main source was Raphael Holinshed's Chronicles (1587). He also used Samuel Daniel's poem The Civil Wars (1595) and the anonymous play The Famous Victories of Henry the Fifth, which includes the box on the ear given to the Lord Chief Justice and the crown taken from the sleeping King."],["The real history","The play compresses ten years. Archbishop Scroop and Mowbray were executed after Gaultree (Shipton Moor) in 1405. Northumberland was killed at Bramham Moor in 1408, defeated by the sheriff of Yorkshire. Henry IV died in the Jerusalem Chamber at Westminster in March 1413. Glendower in fact outlived the events of the play."],["The Lord Chief Justice legend","The story that Prince Henry struck the Chief Justice (Sir William Gascoigne) and accepted imprisonment was told by Sir Thomas Elyot in The Governor (1531) as a model of princely self-control. Shakespeare turns it into the test of the new King."],["The Henriad","Richard II, the two parts of Henry IV and Henry V tell a continuous story of usurpation, guilt and the making of a king. The dying King's confession of 'by-paths and indirect crook'd ways' looks back to Richard II, and his advice about 'foreign quarrels' looks forward to Henry V's war in France."],["Falstaff and Oldcastle","In Part 1 Falstaff was first called Sir John Oldcastle, after a real Lollard martyr. Oldcastle's descendants, the Cobham family, objected, and the name was changed. The Epilogue's 'Oldcastle died a martyr, and this is not the man' is a public apology. A speech prefix 'Old.' survives in the quarto of Part 2."],["Rumour","Rumour, 'painted full of tongues', comes from the figure of Fama in Virgil's Aeneid and from court pageants. Opening the play with a lie sets up its interest in report, reputation and how people come to believe what they are told."],["Gloucestershire and the Inns of Court","Shallow's Gloucestershire is full of real local detail: Woncot, Stamford fair, Hinckley fair. Young gentlemen like Shallow went to the Inns of Court and Chancery in London to learn law and enjoy themselves."],["Soldiers and the press","In the 1590s England was raising troops for Ireland and the Netherlands. Captains like Falstaff could take bribes to release men and keep the pay of 'shadows'. The recruiting scene would have been familiar to Shakespeare's audience."],["Holiday and misrule","Festive custom crowned a Lord of Misrule for a season and then dismissed him. The rejection of Falstaff follows that pattern: holiday must end when the real king is crowned."],["Staging","The rejection scene has been played many ways, from triumph to tragedy. Orson Welles's film Chimes at Midnight (1965) combined both parts of Henry IV and made Falstaff's rejection the centre of the story."]],"critics":{"misrule":[["C. L. Barber, 'Shakespeare's Festive Comedy' (1959)","Paraphrased: Barber read Falstaff through holiday customs, as a Lord of Misrule whose reign of licence must end. The rejection, he argued, is a ritual expulsion that clears the way for order, though Part 2 makes the misrule sourer and older."],["A. C. Bradley, 'The Rejection of Falstaff' in 'Oxford Lectures on Poetry' (1909)","Paraphrased: Bradley argued that Falstaff's comic freedom wins the audience's sympathy so completely that the rejection feels painful, and that Henry's coldness in that moment is hard to like even if it is politically right."]],"kingship":[["E. M. W. Tillyard, 'Shakespeare's History Plays' (1944)","Paraphrased: Tillyard saw the plays as teaching a Tudor idea of order, with Hal as the ideal prince who learns about every level of his people and chooses justice over vanity."],["Jan Kott, 'Shakespeare Our Contemporary' (1964)","Paraphrased: Kott read the histories as a 'grand mechanism' in which power endlessly repeats itself through betrayal and killing; Prince John's trick at Gaultree shows its cold workings."]],"fathers":[["Ernst Kris, 'Prince Hal's Conflict' (1948)","Paraphrased: Kris gave a psychoanalytic reading in which Hal splits his feelings between two fathers, the guilty King and the loving, disreputable Falstaff, and must reject one to accept the other."],["Harold Bloom, 'Shakespeare: The Invention of the Human' (1998)","Paraphrased: Bloom treated Falstaff as the greatest life in the plays and Hal's rejection of him as a betrayal, the cold act of a politician turning on the man who had loved him."]],"justice":[["Samuel Johnson, notes to his edition of Shakespeare (1765)","Paraphrased: Johnson found Falstaff a man of vices whose wit makes him necessary to the Prince, and drew the moral that no one is safe who keeps such company, however entertaining."],["John Dover Wilson, 'The Fortunes of Falstaff' (1943)","Paraphrased: Dover Wilson read the two parts as a morality play in which the Prince chooses between Vanity (Falstaff) and Government (the Lord Chief Justice), so that the rejection is the expected and proper ending."]],"rebellion":[["Stephen Greenblatt, 'Invisible Bullets' (1985)","Paraphrased: Greenblatt argued that the Henry IV plays show power producing and then containing subversion; Hal's tavern years and Prince John's broken faith at Gaultree reveal the deceit on which authority rests."]],"time":[["L. C. Knights, 'Time's Subjects' in 'Some Shakespearean Themes' (1959)","Paraphrased: Knights showed that Part 2 is dominated by age, disease and the passing of time, linking it to the Sonnets, and found its comedy darker than Part 1's."],["Harold Jenkins, 'The Structural Problem in Shakespeare's Henry the Fourth' (1956)","Paraphrased: Jenkins argued that Part 2 is both a sequel and a second version of Part 1's pattern, with Hal's reformation shown again and completed."]]},"symbols":{"Crown":"\\bcrown\\w*","Sleep":"\\bsleep\\w*|\\bslumber\\w*","Sickness":"\\bsick\\w*|\\bdisease\\w*|\\bphysic\\w*","Time":"\\btime\\b|\\bold\\b|\\bage\\b","Rumour":"\\brumou?r\\w*|\\btongues?\\b|\\bnews\\b","Sack":"\\bsack\\b|\\bsherris\\b"},"symbols2":[["The crown","The 'golden care' that keeps the King awake, that Hal takes too soon, and that he finally wears by right. It stands for both power and the guilt of how it was won."],["Sleep","What the King cannot have. The ship-boy and the poorest subject sleep; the King lies awake; when the King sleeps, Hal mistakes it for death."],["Sickness","The King's illness, Falstaff's gout and urine, the Archbishop's 'we are all diseased': the body and the realm are both unwell."],["Rumour's tongues","The Induction's costume, Falstaff's 'whole school of tongues' that capture Colevile, and the report that shapes every reputation."],["The chimes at midnight","Falstaff's phrase for youth remembered: the sound of a past that is gone."],["Jerusalem","The holy city the King never reaches, and the palace room where he dies."],["The Lord Chief Justice's sword","The sword of justice that Henry V hands back, choosing law as his new father."],["Sack","Falstaff's wine, praised in his soliloquy as the source of wit and courage; the tavern's comfort that Hal leaves behind."]],"solo":{"2":1,"123":1,"483":1,"635":1,"764":1,"829":1,"897":1},"why":["Opens the play by asking us to listen, and warns that what we hear may be false.","Shows rumour as a costume of tongues spreading slander.","Makes report an instrument anyone can play.","Shows contempt for the easily fooled crowd.","Reveals Northumberland's illness at Shrewsbury was a pretence.","Sums up the cruelty of false comfort.","Pictures civil conflict as a horse broken loose.","The false good news that the play opens with.","A father hears his son's death in a pun.","Reads a face like a book: the messenger's look tells the story.","Suspicion knows the truth before it is spoken.","Shows how messengers of bad news are remembered.","Shows how much the rebellion depended on Hotspur.","Grief cures pretended sickness.","Northumberland calls for the end of order.","His rage ends in universal death.","Reminds Northumberland that he knew the risk to his son.","Shows the Archbishop turning rebellion into religion.","Richard's murder used as a rebel relic.","A firm-sounding rhyme from a man who will not act.","Falstaff's first concern is his failing body.","Falstaff's famous claim to inspire wit in others.","A grotesque comic picture of Falstaff and his page.","Falstaff mocks the Justice's age while denying his own.","Turns the Justice's charge into a diagnosis.","Biblical wit used to dodge the law.","The Justice names Falstaff's real reputation.","Turns infamy into a joke about his belt.","Shows the Justice can pun too.","The central charge against Falstaff.","Falstaff reverses the charge.","Falstaff's life nearly burnt out.","Gravity becomes gravy: appetite wins.","An old man claims to be young.","The Justice lists the signs of age.","Falstaff claims he was born old.","The Justice hopes for the change that will come.","Falstaff's quick reversal.","Falstaff pretends to be worn out by service.","Falstaff's cynical view of age and youth.","Debt described as a disease.","Falstaff will profit from his illness.","Hotspur's mistake: living on promises.","The rebellion as a house that must be planned.","Armies on paper are not armies.","Popularity is unsafe ground.","The Archbishop's scorn for the people.","The people long for the king they rejected.","Nostalgia: past and future always seem better.","Even rebels obey time.","The Hostess's garbled insults.","The Hostess's famous phrase.","The broken promise of marriage, in vivid detail.","The Justice sees through Falstaff.","Justice in money and in repentance.","Falstaff charms his way out of debt.","The Justice's verdict on Falstaff.","Hal enters weary.","A prince ashamed of his low tastes.","Hal falls out of love with his greatness.","Hal asks to be judged by how he ends.","Hal's hidden grief for his father.","Reputation traps Hal: real grief would look false.","Falstaff's soul is sicker than his body.","Falstaff's grand view of himself.","Hal knows he is wasting time.","Falstaff as a pig in a sty.","Hal's disguise as a fall from prince to apprentice.","Hal's folly always has a purpose.","Honour as a pledge Northumberland must redeem.","Lady Percy's charge against Northumberland.","Hotspur as the mirror of the young nobility.","Hotspur shaped a generation.","The climax of Lady Percy's accusation.","Northumberland's paralysis.","The Hostess's medical muddle.","Tavern bawdy about women and sickness.","Doll and Falstaff's affectionate quarrels.","Doll's sudden tenderness before the war.","The Hostess's horror of swaggerers.","Doll attacks Pistol's false title.","Pistol's parody of stage heroics.","Pistol's empty bombast.","Tragic language in a tavern brawl.","Doll's mock-heroic praise.","Doll tells Falstaff to prepare for death.","Falstaff refuses to think of death.","Falstaff's verdict on Hal, overheard.","Poins mocks the old lover.","Falstaff admits his age.","Foreshadows his rejection.","Falstaff jokes even when caught.","Falstaff's body as a world of sin.","Falstaff's quick escape from Hal's charge.","Bardolph's red face in hell.","Hal turns from tavern to duty.","Falstaff enjoys being sought after.","The Hostess's long attachment.","The King envies the sleeping poor.","The opening of the great sleep speech.","The play's best-known line about kingship.","The kingdom as a diseased body.","The King longs to know the future.","Knowledge of the future would kill hope.","Richard's prophecy remembered.","Henry claims he never meant to take the crown.","Warwick's idea of history as a pattern.","The King accepts necessity.","Rumour again: report doubles fear.","Shallow's invented wild youth.","Comedy and melancholy together.","Death and cattle prices in one breath.","Shallow's lament for old Double.","Bardolph cannot define his fashionable word.","Falstaff's pun as he presses Mouldy.","Falstaff's corruption: names without men.","Mock praise of Feeble.","Falstaff mocks Bullcalf's cold.","Silence measures the years.","Falstaff's tender line about youth.","Shallow echoes the nostalgia.","Feeble's plain courage.","A soldier's simple philosophy.","Falstaff's excuse for choosing the weakest.","A pun that hides his bribes.","Falstaff mocks Shallow's lies, unaware of his own.","Falstaff's predatory plan for Shallow.","Northumberland fails the rebels again.","Westmoreland's rebuke to the Archbishop.","The Archbishop diagnoses the realm.","The rebels' justification.","Westmoreland answers the old grievance.","A bad cause cannot bear examination.","Mowbray foresees the King's revenge.","The Archbishop's misplaced hope.","Prince John's charge against the Archbishop.","Prince John calls the rebels' piety counterfeit.","The carefully worded promise.","A proverb about to come true.","Dramatic irony: the peace is a trap.","The trick is sprung.","Prince John's legalistic defence.","Credit to God for a victory won by deceit.","Falstaff's fame fights for him.","Falstaff borrows Caesar's boast.","Prince John's cold farewell.","Falstaff on Prince John's coldness.","The start of the hymn to sack.","Falstaff's mock advice to imaginary sons.","The King's insight into Hal's nature.","Clarence's role as peacemaker.","The King's fear for Hal.","The King imagines his death.","Warwick's defence of Hal.","A prediction of the rejection.","The King doubts Hal can change.","Victory comes too late.","Fortune gives and takes away.","The King's body worn thin.","Hal addresses the crown.","Majesty protects and burns.","Hal claims his inheritance.","The King's bitter outburst.","Fathers killed for their honey.","The King accuses Hal of wishing him dead.","The King's nightmare of Hal's reign.","England returned to savagery.","Hal's plea begins.","The crown has eaten his father up.","Henry confesses how he got the crown.","The stain of usurpation dies with Henry.","The King's political advice.","The King's last prayer.","Hal's claim to rightful possession.","The Jerusalem prophecy fulfilled.","Shallow's comic repetition.","Shallow's motive for hospitality.","Davy's comic logic for corruption.","Falstaff's advice applies to Hal too.","The King is dead.","The princes in mourning.","The Justice's integrity.","Henry V's first words as king.","He will not murder his brothers.","Henry reassures his brothers.","Henry tests the Justice.","The Justice acted as the King's image.","The Justice asks Henry to imagine himself a father.","Henry accepts the rule of law.","The Justice replaces Falstaff as Hal's father.","Hal buries his wildness with his father.","Henry will prove report wrong.","The tide image of reformation.","Silence's song in the orchard.","Silence's comic boast.","Pistol announces Falstaff's supposed rise.","Pistol's news of Hal's accession.","Falstaff gives away offices he does not have.","Falstaff's fatal confidence.","The line the rejection answers.","Falstaff expects revenge on the law.","The Hostess reverses the proverb.","Falstaff's love for Hal, on show.","Falstaff's greeting.","Falstaff speaks to the King like a father.","Henry answers through the Justice.","The rejection.","Henry sees Falstaff as an old fool.","The tavern years as a dream.","A last joke and a warning of death.","Henry's transformation stated publicly.","The sentence.","Falstaff's first words after the rejection.","Falstaff cannot believe the change.","Shallow's unintended prophecy.","The law finally catches Falstaff.","Prince John approves.","War abroad, as the old King advised.","A promise of more Falstaff.","A disclaimer about Falstaff's original name."],"insults":[{"id":"51","f":"Thou whoreson mandrake","who":"Falstaff","n":"Falstaff abuses his tiny page affectionately."},{"id":"53","f":"A whoreson Achitophel! a rascally yea-forsooth knave!","who":"Falstaff","n":"Falstaff curses the mercer who refused him credit."},{"id":"101","f":"you are as a candle, the better part burnt out","who":"Chief Justice","n":"The Justice's cool put-down of Falstaff's age."},{"id":"168","f":"thou bastardly rogue!","who":"Hostess","n":"The Hostess's fury at Falstaff."},{"id":"168","f":"thou honey-suckle villain!","who":"Hostess","n":"Her insults come out garbled."},{"id":"172","f":"Away, you scullion! you rampallian! you fustilarian!","who":"Page","n":"The Page has learned his master's art of abuse."},{"id":"223","f":"thou art a great fool","who":"Chief Justice","n":"The Justice's final judgement."},{"id":"254","f":"Away, you whoreson upright rabbit","who":"Bardolph","n":"Bardolph and the Page swap abuse."},{"id":"255","f":"Away, you rascally Althaea's dream","who":"Page","n":"The Page's learned insult about Bardolph's fiery face."},{"id":"325","f":"You muddy rascal","who":"Doll","n":"Doll's affectionate abuse of Falstaff."},{"id":"331","f":"you muddy conger","who":"Doll","n":"Doll calls Falstaff a slimy eel."},{"id":"354","f":"you poor, base, rascally, cheating, lack-linen mate!","who":"Doll","n":"Doll's real contempt for Pistol."},{"id":"356","f":"you filthy bung","who":"Doll","n":"Doll calls Pistol a pickpocket."},{"id":"360","f":"thou abominable damned cheater","who":"Doll","n":"Doll attacks Pistol's false rank."},{"id":"409","f":"hang him, baboon!","who":"Falstaff","n":"Falstaff on Poins, overheard."},{"id":"429","f":"thou globe of sinful continents","who":"Hal","n":"Hal's insult turns Falstaff into a whole world."},{"id":"436","f":"You whoreson candle-mine","who":"Hal","n":"Hal calls Falstaff a store of tallow."},{"id":"448","f":"Answer, thou dead elm, answer.","who":"Poins","n":"Poins calls Falstaff a dead tree."},{"id":"1004","f":"Under which king, Besonian?","who":"Pistol","n":"Pistol calls Shallow a beggar."},{"id":"1023","f":"thou damned tripe-visaged rascal","who":"Doll","n":"Doll abuses the beadle."},{"id":"1026","f":"you blue-bottle rogue","who":"Doll","n":"An insult about the beadle's blue coat."},{"id":"1031","f":"Goodman death, goodman bones!","who":"Doll","n":"Doll mocks the beadle's thinness."},{"id":"1067","f":"How ill white hairs become a fool and jester!","who":"King Henry V","n":"The cruellest insult of all, from a friend."}],"pronouns":[{"id":"113","f":"God bless your expedition!","n":"The Justice uses polite 'you' to Falstaff: distance and authority."},{"id":"183","f":"Thou didst swear to me","n":"The Hostess uses 'thou' to Falstaff: intimacy and anger."},{"id":"237","f":"I could tell to thee","n":"Hal uses 'thee' to Poins as a familiar companion."},{"id":"429","f":"what a life dost thou lead!","n":"Hal and Falstaff still use 'thou' in the tavern."},{"id":"853","f":"Thy wish was father, Harry, to that thought:","n":"The King uses 'thou' to his son: intimate and angry."},{"id":"854","f":"There is your crown:","n":"Hal answers his father with respectful 'you'."},{"id":"933","f":"You shall be as a father to my youth:","n":"The new King gives the Justice the respect of 'you'."},{"id":"1067","f":"I know thee not, old man:","n":"Henry uses 'thee' to Falstaff one last time, now as a king to an inferior."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["Northumberland rides to join the Archbishop","Northumberland hesitates and later flees to Scotland"],"a1s2":["Falstaff goes to prison for the robbery","Falstaff is sent north with Prince John"],"a1s3":["The rebels march at once","They set out but depend on Northumberland, who fails them"],"a2s1":["Falstaff pays the Hostess","He borrows more from her instead"],"a2s2":["Hal goes straight to his sick father","Hal spies on Falstaff in disguise first"],"a2s3":["Northumberland joins the rebels","Northumberland flees to Scotland"],"a2s4":["Hal punishes Falstaff for his insults","War news calls Hal away"],"a3s1":["The King leads his army north","The King stays at Westminster, ill"],"a3s2":["Falstaff picks the strongest recruits","He takes bribes and picks the weakest"],"a4s1":["A battle at Gaultree","Talks between the armies"],"a4s2":["The rebels win their demands","The rebels are tricked and arrested"],"a4s3":["Falstaff fights bravely","Falstaff's reputation captures a knight for him"],"a4s4":["The good news cures the King","The King collapses"],"a4s5":["The King dies cursing Hal","Father and son are reconciled"],"a5s1":["Falstaff tells Shallow about Hal","Falstaff plans to make Hal laugh at Shallow"],"a5s2":["Henry V punishes the Lord Chief Justice","He keeps him and calls him a father"],"a5s3":["Falstaff stays in Gloucestershire","Falstaff rides through the night to London"],"a5s4":["Falstaff rescues the women","The women go to prison without him"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Falstaff and the Lord Chief Justice","pts":["Both are old men, and each mocks the other's age.","Falstaff stands for appetite and misrule; the Justice for law and order.","Hal chooses the Justice as 'a father to my youth' and sends Falstaff to the Fleet."],"ids":[107,933]},{"t":"King Henry IV and Northumberland","pts":["Both are fathers who fail their sons in some way.","Northumberland abandons Hotspur; the King misjudges Hal.","The King is reconciled with his son; Northumberland's son is dead."],"ids":[302,855]},{"t":"Hal and Prince John","pts":["Both brothers end the play as public figures of order.","John wins Gaultree by a cold trick; Hal wins hearts by speeches and choices.","Falstaff says John is 'sober-blooded' while Hal was warmed with sack."],"ids":[727,764]},{"t":"Falstaff and Shallow","pts":["Old friends from the Inns of Court who remember the same nights.","Shallow invents a wild youth; Falstaff mocks him but lives on his own myths.","Each hopes to use the other: Falstaff for money, Shallow for advancement."],"ids":[603,635]},{"t":"The King's sleep and Hal's crown","pts":["The King envies the sleeping poor; Hal sees the crown as 'golden care'.","Both speeches see power as a burden that kills.","Hal's speech answers his father's, showing he already understands kingship."],"ids":[483,829]}],"analysis":{"HAL":["Hal enters this play weary and ashamed. He has won glory at Shrewsbury, yet his father still thinks him lost, and his own first words are a complaint of tiredness and a longing for small beer. He tells Poins that his heart bleeds for his sick father, but that if he wept everyone would call him a hypocrite. The reputation he built on purpose in Part 1 now traps him.","His last tavern game, spying on Falstaff dressed as a waiter, is half-hearted, and he abandons it at once when news of war arrives: 'I feel me much to blame, So idly to profane the precious time.' From then on he is absent from the comic plot until the end, and the audience hears about him through others: his father's fears, Warwick's defence that he studies his companions 'like a strange tongue', and Falstaff's claim that sack made him valiant.","The crown scene is his real test. He takes the crown believing his father dead and speaks to it as 'polish'd perturbation' and 'golden care', showing he already understands what it costs. When the King wakes and accuses him of wishing his death, Hal's long reply, full of grief and duty, convinces him. 'You won it, wore it, kept it, gave it me' is his simple claim to the throne.","As Henry V he moves fast and surely. He reassures his brothers, keeps the Lord Chief Justice who once imprisoned him, and declares that his wildness is buried with his father. Then he publicly rejects Falstaff with 'I know thee not, old man'. Audiences divide over that moment: some see a king doing what he must, others a cold betrayal of the man who loved him."],"FALSTAFF":["Falstaff is still the play's great talker, but he has aged. His first line asks what the doctor says about his urine, and he is troubled by gout, debt and a 'consumption of the purse'. His fame as the man who killed Hotspur has given him a command and a page, and he uses it to borrow, dodge the law and impress country justices.","With the Lord Chief Justice he fights a duel of words he cannot quite win. He pretends to be deaf, claims to be young, and turns every charge into a joke, but the Justice sees through him: 'I am well acquainted with your manner of wrenching the true cause the false way.' The Justice is the one figure in the play he cannot charm.","In the tavern and in Gloucestershire his comedy becomes more melancholy. Doll tells him to patch up his old body for heaven, and he answers, 'I am old, I am old.' With Shallow he hears the chimes at midnight. He also shows his worst side: he takes bribes from fit recruits and takes the weakest men to war, and plans to 'snap at' Shallow like a pike at a dace.","When he hears Hal is king he believes 'the laws of England are at my commandment'. He rides all night and greets the new King as 'my sweet boy', and is rejected and imprisoned. His last hope, that he will be sent for in private, shows that he never imagined Hal could really change."],"KING":["King Henry IV is a sick and sleepless man. In his great soliloquy he envies the poorest subjects and the ship-boy on the mast who can sleep when he cannot: 'Uneasy lies the head that wears a crown.' His illness is both bodily and moral, the result of the guilt of usurpation.","He is haunted by the past. He remembers Richard's prophecy that Northumberland would betray him, and in the crown scene he confesses that he came by the crown through 'by-paths and indirect crook'd ways'. He hopes that all its 'soil' will go with him into the grave.","His fear for Hal is intense. He imagines England under 'Harry the Fifth' as a wilderness of wolves, and accuses his son of wishing him dead. Yet he is quickly moved by Hal's defence and blesses him.","His last advice is shrewd and political: keep restless lords busy 'with foreign quarrels'. He dies in the Jerusalem Chamber, fulfilling a prophecy that he had taken to mean a crusade."],"CHIEF JUSTICE":["The Lord Chief Justice represents the law. He once sent Prince Hal to prison for striking him, and in the play he is the only person who consistently tells Falstaff the truth.","His exchanges with Falstaff are a contest between two old men: the Justice tells him he lives 'in great infamy' and is a candle 'burnt out'; Falstaff mocks his age. The Justice makes him pay the Hostess and refuses him a loan.","When Henry IV dies the Justice expects ruin, but he will not beg. Asked by the new King to explain himself, he argues that he acted as the King's own image, and asks Henry to imagine his own son defying the law.","Henry accepts his argument and makes him 'a father to my youth'. In the last scene it is the Justice who carries Falstaff to the Fleet: the law has replaced misrule at the King's side."],"SHALLOW":["Justice Shallow is a foolish, garrulous country gentleman who lives in his memories. He repeats himself ('Jesu, Jesu, dead!'), muddles death with the price of bullocks, and boasts about wild days at Clement's Inn.","Falstaff sees through him at once: Shallow was a skinny, forgotten student who never saw John of Gaunt except once in the Tilt-yard. His boasts are, as Falstaff says, the old man's 'vice of lying'.","He is also a small example of corrupt justice. He lets Davy talk him into favouring the knave William Visor, and he lends Falstaff a thousand pounds in the hope of advancement at court.","His last line, that Falstaff will 'die in' the colour of his pretence, is an unwitting prophecy. Shallow is comic, but his presence deepens the play's sense of age and lost time."],"ARCHBISHOP":["Richard Scroop, Archbishop of York, gives the second rebellion a religious cover. Morton says he 'turns insurrection to religion', using the blood of King Richard to win followers.","He is eloquent and contemptuous of the common people, whom he calls a dog returning to its vomit. He presents the rebels' case as medicine for a diseased state.","Westmoreland and Prince John accuse him of turning books to graves and ink to blood, abusing the trust of his office.","He trusts Prince John's oath, sends his army home and is arrested. His misplaced confidence ('a peace is of the nature of a conquest') makes him a figure of dramatic irony."],"LADY PERCY":["Lady Percy, Hotspur's widow, has one great scene. When Northumberland says his honour requires him to join the rebels, she reminds him that he broke his word to his own son at Shrewsbury.","Her elegy for Hotspur describes him as the mirror in which all the young nobles dressed themselves, so that even his fast speech became the fashion.","Her speech is both love and accusation. It is the main reason Northumberland flees to Scotland and fails the rebels.","She keeps Hotspur alive in the play's memory, and her grief contrasts with the rumours of the opening scene."]},"motifs":[["Sickness","The King is dying, Falstaff is diseased, and the Archbishop says 'we are all diseased'. The realm and its people are both sick.",[20,40,41,101,129,156,158]],["Sleep and the crown","The King cannot sleep; Hal mistakes his sleep for death and takes the crown, the 'golden care' that keeps kings awake.",[98,99,100,159,160,168]],["Rumour and report","From Rumour's tongues to Falstaff's 'school of tongues', reputations are made by report, true or false.",[0,1,2,108,143,191]],["Age and time","Old men remember, count the years and face death: the chimes at midnight, 'I am old', the grave that gapes.",[31,35,89,111,118,119,209]],["Fathers","The King, Northumberland, Falstaff and the Lord Chief Justice are all fathers in some sense; Hal must choose among them.",[61,70,164,173,184,189,190]],["Law and justice","The Lord Chief Justice confronts Falstaff, country justice bends for favours, and the new King chooses the law.",[26,53,177,188,199,215]]],"bigq":[["Is the rejection of Falstaff right?",["Hal promised it in Part 1 and Warwick predicted it","Falstaff has shown corruption and expects the law to serve him","Henry provides for his old friends and promises advancement if they reform","But it is public, cold and cruel to a man who loves him"],[198,199,206,207,210,211]],["Is Prince John's trick at Gaultree justified?",["It ends the rebellion without bloodshed","He keeps the letter of his promise","But it breaks the spirit of an oath sworn on his blood","It shows how power relies on deceit"],[137,140,141,142]],["Is the King a good king?",["He is burdened by guilt and illness","He confesses the crooked ways he gained the crown","His advice to Hal is political, not moral","He fears for England and loves his son"],[100,105,169,171,172]],["Does Hal really change?",["His weariness in 2.2 shows him outgrowing the tavern","He leaves at once when duty calls","The crown scene shows deep feeling","His final speeches announce the change publicly"],[59,60,95,159,190,192]],["Is Part 2 darker than Part 1?",["Battles are replaced by tricks and arrests","Falstaff is older and sicker","Death and memory run through the comedy","But Silence's songs and Shallow's orchard keep comic warmth"],[89,111,119,141,193]],["Who is Hal's true father?",["The King, by blood and inheritance","Falstaff, as companion and teacher in the tavern","The Lord Chief Justice, whom Henry chooses","Each represents a different idea of authority"],[164,173,189,190,204]]],"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present kingship in Henry IV, Part 2?",[98,100,159,169,171,182]],["Explore the presentation of Falstaff in the play.",[20,21,89,119,147,199]],["How does Shakespeare present the relationship between Hal and his father?",[61,164,166,167,173,190]],["How is the theme of time and age presented in the play?",[31,35,89,111,119,209]],["Explore how Shakespeare presents justice and the law.",[26,53,186,188,189,215]],["How does Shakespeare present rebellion and policy?",[17,45,130,137,141,142]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short quotations, built into my own sentences","Explained what the quotation suggests, not just what it says","Zoomed in on a single word or image","Named a method (soliloquy, imagery, prose and verse, parallel plots, irony, structure) and its effect","Linked to context where it helps the argument","Considered more than one interpretation, especially of Hal and the rejection","Referred to the beginning, middle and end of the play","Ended with a conclusion that answers the question"],"models":[[100,"Shakespeare uses the King's soliloquy on sleep to show the private cost of power.","He envies the poorest subjects and the ship-boy who sleep while he lies awake.","'Uneasy lies the head that wears a crown' turns the crown into a source of discomfort, not glory.","The guilt of usurpation lies behind his sleeplessness."],[119,"Falstaff's 'We have heard the chimes at midnight' gives the comedy a melancholy edge.","He and Shallow remember nights of youth that are long gone.","The midnight chimes suggest both revelry and the passing of time.","The line prepares for the end of Falstaff's world when Hal becomes king."],[169,"The King's confession in 4.5 reveals the guilt behind his reign.","'By-paths and indirect crook'd ways' admits that he took the crown dishonestly.","The image of crooked roads contrasts with the straight succession he hopes for Hal.","It explains why Henry's reign has been full of rebellion."],[206,"The rejection of Falstaff is the play's most shocking moment.","'I know thee not, old man' denies their whole friendship in five words.","'Old man' reduces Falstaff to his age and weakness.","Shakespeare leaves us divided between admiration for the new King and pity for Falstaff."],[141,"Prince John's 'I pawn'd thee none' shows policy replacing honour.","He promised to redress grievances, not to spare the rebels.","The legal quibble wins the war without a battle.","The audience may admire the efficiency but question the morality."],[189,"Henry V's 'You shall be as a father to my youth' completes his change.","By choosing the Lord Chief Justice he chooses the law.","The word 'father' replaces Falstaff, who greeted him as 'my sweet boy'.","It shows Hal's reformation as a choice between different kinds of authority."]],"lang":{"un":[["483","Uneasy lies the head that wears a crown.","The man who wears the crown cannot rest easily."],["855","By what by-paths and indirect crook'd ways","By what side roads and crooked, dishonest methods"],["933","My father is gone wild into his grave,","My wildness has gone into my father's grave."],["1067","I know thee not, old man:","I don't know you, old man."]],"pun":[["93","Your means are very slender, and your waste is great.","waste","pun","Waste (spending) and waist (belly)."],["104","His effect of gravy, gravy, gravy.","gravy","pun","Gravity and gravy: the Justice's seriousness becomes food."],["547","Prick him.","Prick","double meaning","To mark a name on the list, and a bawdy meaning."],["545","'Tis the more time thou wert used.","used","double meaning","Mouldy things lack use; and Mouldy will be used as a soldier."],["623","O, give me the spare men, and spare me the great ones.","spare","pun","Spare means thin, and to spare means to let off."],["1074","A colour that I fear you will die in, Sir John.","colour","pun","Colour as pretence, and collar as the hangman's noose."]],"st":[["483","Uneasy lies the head that wears a crown.",[["Uneasy","The stressed opening word makes discomfort the point."],["crown","The cause of the unease."]]],["603","We have heard the chimes at midnight, Master Shallow.",[["chimes","The sound of youth and time passing."],["midnight","The hour of revelry and of endings."]]],["1067","I know thee not, old man: fall to thy prayers;",[["know","A friendship denied."],["old","The cruellest word: age and death."]]]]},"dir":[["2","Open your ears","How should Rumour begin the play?",[["Loudly, directly to the audience","Rumour treats the audience as the crowd it deceives."],["Whispering, like gossip","Rumour spreads quietly and slyly."],["Mockingly, enjoying the lie","Rumour delights in the confusion to come."]]],["483","Uneasy lies the head that wears a crown.","How should the King speak this line?",[["Wearily, to himself","A sick man's exhausted conclusion."],["Bitterly, to the audience","A guilty king's accusation of fate."],["Calmly, as a proverb","He has accepted his burden."]]],["603","We have heard the chimes at midnight, Master Shallow.","How should Falstaff say this?",[["Merrily, as a boast","He enjoys the memory of youth."],["Quietly and sadly","He feels the time that has passed."],["Ironically, humouring Shallow","He knows Shallow's memories are lies."]]],["829","O polish'd perturbation! golden care!","How should Hal speak to the crown?",[["With awe, holding it up","He feels its power."],["With grief, looking at his father","The crown has killed his father."],["With hunger, ready to put it on","The King's suspicion is justified."]]],["933","My father is gone wild into his grave,","How should Henry V deliver this to the court?",[["Solemnly, as a public vow","He announces his new self."],["Warmly, reassuring his brothers","He comforts the frightened court."],["Coldly, to warn any doubters","He makes clear who is master now."]]],["1067","I know thee not, old man:","How should Henry V reject Falstaff?",[["Coldly, without looking at him","The King has truly put the past away."],["With visible pain","It costs him to do this."],["Loudly, for the crowd","It is a public performance of reform."]]]],"ev":{"ctx":[["1064","1089"],["2","931","1064"],["492","723","865"],["931","109","1064"],["855","494","1089"],["1089","1069"],["2","25","34"],["508","512","603"],["559","219"],["1061","1064","276"],["603","1064","1069"]],"cr":{"misrule":[["1061","1064","276"],["603","1064","1069"]],"kingship":[["788","931","1064"],["723","855","1082"]],"fathers":[["855","856","1064"],["1061","1064","603"]],"justice":[["1064","931","110"],["931","1064","1077"]],"rebellion":[["723","727","2"]],"time":[["101","421","603"],["274","495","603"]]}}};
